ralph/progress.txt:223:- Success-envelope soft-limit alerts are structured `yalla.output.v1.warnings`, not endpoint `data`: emit them through `apienvelope.WriteDataWithWarnings` with stable codes plus `entitlement_key`, `usage`, `threshold`, `limit`, optional `period`, and recovery/upgrade `hint`; derive usage warnings only from already-authorized tenant-scoped rows so denied or cross-tenant responses never leak warning metadata.
ralph/progress.txt:254:- Backoffice runtime configuration uses global `admin_config_sets` plus `admin_config_versions`, not tenant tables: create editable drafts, publish immutable effective-at versions, archive instead of deleting, and make runtime readers select only `status='published'` rows whose `effective_at` has passed. Bump the set `revision` on version mutations so future cache layers have a monotonic invalidation token.
ralph/progress.txt:262:- Traefik metering source collection lives under `internal/controlplane/metering` and should stay source-only: query bounded Prometheus windows, normalize counters into deterministic unattributed samples, attach `query_version` plus raw-sample checksum metadata, and leave tenant attribution/quarantine plus `usage_events` writes to later attribution/aggregation layers.
ralph/progress.txt:264:- Billing-grade usage events are append-only and written through `store.UsageEventRepository.Append`: validate the tenant-owned organization/project/environment/service scope before insert, redact metadata at the repository boundary, assign the active subscription period with a UTC calendar-month fallback, and use `(organization_id, source, idempotency_key)` for replay-safe idempotency; corrections are new `adjusted` rows, never updates.
ralph/progress.txt:269:- Pricing plan catalog rows are versioned, global configuration rather than tenant data: `plans` uses immutable accepted versions keyed by `(slug, billing_period, version)` plus one active version per `(slug, billing_period)`, and `PricingPlanRepository.Update` archives the addressed row before inserting the next version and cloning entitlements. Do not update an accepted plan row in place.
ralph/progress.txt:279:  - Added `docs/development/security-review-checklist.md` with the backend security review scope, exact commands, required redacted environment placeholders, expected outputs, contract checks, redaction requirements, tenant-isolation expectations, fake-Dokploy-by-default guidance, opt-in live Dokploy smoke warning, and failure recovery guidance.
ralph/progress.txt:329:- Admin organization-rooted read endpoints with `{org_id}` in the path should authorize through `organizationIDResolver` against the real path organization and use a store-backed reader that verifies the parent organization before listing child rows. For `GET /v1/admin/organizations/{org_id}/dokploy-refs`, `admin.read` remains a CapSupport action, support can inspect cross-tenant mappings through the policy exception, non-support callers are denied before persistence, invalid `org_id` is a 400, and an unknown organization is a 404 instead of an empty list.
ralph/progress.txt:330:- Missing authorization scope is a request-shape error, not a normal denial: `policy.Decide` returns `ReasonDeniedScopeRequired` for non-self actions whose `policy.Resource.Scope.OrganizationID` is empty, and `policy.Authorize` maps that to typed `apierr.ScopeRequired()` / `E_SCOPE_REQUIRED` (HTTP 400). Route resolvers should still provide explicit organization scope; this path catches missing path/query scope and resolver wiring mistakes before they collapse into misleading cross-tenant `E_FORBIDDEN`.
ralph/progress.txt:333:- Admin unpathed mutation endpoints that target a tenant selected by request data should expose `organization_id` in the query string so `RequireAuth` authorizes the real target before the handler reads the body. The body may repeat `organization_id` for typed clients but must match the query target; body-only cross-tenant targets are validation failures. Store-backed admin job starters should verify the target organization, insert the durable job, and append the allowed audit event in one `Store.Write` transaction so no import/reconcile job exists without its audit trail.
ralph/progress.txt:336:- Preview environment state-machine stories use the same audited transition shape as the other resource state machines: `preview_environments.status` has the lifecycle table `pending -> provisioning/deleting/failed`, `provisioning -> ready/deleting/failed`, `ready -> deleting/failed`, `deleting -> deleted/failed`, `failed -> provisioning/deleting`, and `deleted -> terminal`; `PreviewEnvironmentRepository.Transition` locks the tenant-scoped `(organization_id, project_id, id)` row, rejects invalid edges with `E_INVALID_STATE_TRANSITION` before any write, updates `status` plus `deletion_scheduled_at` when entering `deleting`, and appends a redacted `preview_environment_events` row with actor/request/previous/next/reason metadata in the same transaction. The legacy `ScheduleDeletion` helper also routes through `Transition` so it cannot bypass the lifecycle timeline.
ralph/progress.txt:337:- Quota reservation state-machine stories use a pure `ReservationStatus.CanTransitionTo` table plus an atomic repository `Transition` method: lock the tenant-scoped reservation row, reject impossible edges with `E_INVALID_STATE_TRANSITION` before any write, update `quota_reservations.status` and `settled_at`, and append the matching `quota_reservation_events` row in the same transaction with actor/request/previous/next/reason metadata. Redact free-text reason text before persisting event message/metadata.
ralph/progress.txt:338:- Admin Dokploy reconcile mutation endpoints (BE-0286 pattern, POST `/v1/admin/dokploy/reconcile`) stay org-rooted for policy: `organization_id` is taken from the query parameter or defaults to the principal home org so `RequireAuth` authorizes `admin.reconcile` against the real target before the handler runs. The JSON body may repeat `organization_id` for typed clients, but it must match the query target; cross-tenant body-only targets are rejected before the runner port is called. Add the runner as a route-option dependency to avoid sweeping every existing `NewHandler` test call site.
ralph/progress.txt:339:- Admin Dokploy drift list endpoints (BE-0283 pattern, GET `/v1/admin/dokploy/drift`) use an unpathed admin route with query-scoped targeting: `organization_id` defaults to the principal home org, while optional `project_id`, `environment_id`, `service_id`, `status`, and `limit` filters are mirrored into `policy.Scope` by the resolver before `admin.reconcile` authorization. The handler validates hierarchy shape (`environment_id` requires `project_id`; `service_id` requires both project and environment) before store access. The store-backed `DriftFindingReader` verifies the target organization and any supplied parent resources tenant-scoped, then delegates to `DriftFindingRepository.List` whose SQL uses constant optional predicates so caller input never alters the statement shape.
ralph/progress.txt:340:- Drift finding state-machine stories use an explicit `drift_findings.status` lifecycle (`open -> resolved`, with `resolved` terminal) in addition to the existing `resolved_at` / `resolved_by_actor_id` stamps. `DriftFindingRepository.Transition` locks the tenant-scoped row, rejects invalid edges with `E_INVALID_STATE_TRANSITION` before any write, updates the resolution columns for terminal resolution, and appends a redacted `drift_finding_events` row with actor/request/previous/next/reason metadata in the same transaction. The legacy `MarkResolved` helper also writes the lifecycle event so it cannot bypass the timeline.
ralph/progress.txt:341:- Bare-id job retry endpoints (BE-0277 pattern, POST `/v1/jobs/{job_id}/retry`) reuse `jobIDResolver` so policy is evaluated against the persisted job's owning scope before the mutating handler runs. The store-backed retry path leaves terminal source rows immutable and inserts a fresh queued clone for `failed`, `cancelled`, or `dead_letter` sources only; it scopes the caller's `idempotency_key` as `retry:<source_job_id>:<caller_key>`, copies non-secret payload refs, adds `retried_job_id` / `retry_requested_by`, appends a redacted `job.retry` audit event in the same transaction, and returns the queued retry job with `202 Accepted`. `succeeded`, `queued`, `running`, and `retrying` sources are stable conflicts; cross-tenant/unknown source ids remain tenant-scoped `E_NOT_FOUND`.
ralph/progress.txt:342:- Bare-id job cancel endpoints (BE-0280 pattern, POST `/v1/jobs/{job_id}/cancel`) reuse `jobIDResolver` so action `job.cancel` is authorized against the persisted job's owning scope before mutation. The store-backed cancel path calls `JobRepository.Transition(..., JobStatusCancelled, ...)` inside `Store.Write`, so queued/running/retrying jobs follow the shared state-machine bookkeeping, terminal jobs fail with `E_INVALID_STATE_TRANSITION`, and cross-tenant/unknown ids remain tenant-scoped `E_NOT_FOUND`. Append the redacted `job.cancel` audit event in the same transaction as the lifecycle event and return the cancelled source row with `202 Accepted`.
ralph/progress.txt:343:- Break-glass session state-machine stories use explicit `break_glass_sessions.status` plus the existing time/revocation stamps: `active -> revoked|expired`, with `revoked` and `expired` terminal. `BreakGlassRepository.Transition` locks the tenant-scoped session row, rejects invalid edges with `E_INVALID_STATE_TRANSITION` before any write, updates status/revocation columns, and appends a redacted `break_glass_session_events` row with actor/request/previous/next/reason metadata in the same transaction. Customer-facing revoke routes through this transition path while preserving the existing public conflict response for duplicate revocation attempts.
ralph/progress.txt:344:- API key state-machine stories use explicit `api_keys.status` plus the existing lifecycle stamps: `active -> revoked|expired`, with `revoked` and `expired` terminal. `APIKeyRepository.Transition` locks the tenant-scoped key row, rejects invalid edges with `E_INVALID_STATE_TRANSITION` before any write, updates status, stamps `revoked_at` for revoked transitions, and appends a redacted `api_key_events` row with actor/request/previous/next/reason metadata in the same transaction. Customer-facing revoke should call the transition path before appending its separate audit event so lifecycle timeline and audit timeline commit atomically.
ralph/progress.txt:345:- Project state-machine stories use the same audited transition shape as environment/service state machines: `projects.status` has the lifecycle table `pending -> active/deleting`, `active -> suspended/deleting`, `suspended -> active/deleting`, `deleting -> active/deleted`, `deleted -> terminal`; `ProjectRepository.Transition` locks the tenant-scoped project row, rejects invalid edges with `E_INVALID_STATE_TRANSITION` before any write, updates status, and appends a redacted `project_events` row with actor/request/previous/next/reason metadata in the same transaction.
ralph/progress.txt:346:- Bare-id job read endpoints (BE-0274 pattern, GET `/v1/jobs/{job_id}`) should authorize against the persisted job row's owning scope, not an org-root placeholder: the resolver reloads the tenant-scoped job through `store.JobReader.GetJob`, mirrors `ProjectID` / `EnvironmentID` / `ServiceID` into `policy.Scope`, then the handler performs the same tenant-scoped read for the response. This preserves project/environment/service-scoped grant containment while unknown or cross-tenant ids still collapse to the repository's `E_NOT_FOUND` path.
ralph/progress.txt:347:- Job list endpoints (BE-0271 pattern, GET `/v1/jobs`) use an organization-root route with optional query filters `(project_id, environment_id, service_id, status, limit)`: `jobListResolver` mirrors supplied filters into `policy.Scope` before auth, the handler validates hierarchy shape before store access (`environment_id` requires `project_id`, `service_id` requires both), and `store.JobReader` verifies filtered parents tenant-scoped before delegating to `JobRepository.List` with a constant SQL statement whose optional predicates never interpolate caller input.
ralph/progress.txt:348:- Environment state-machine stories use a pure `EnvironmentStatus.CanTransitionTo` table plus an atomic repository `Transition` method: lock the tenant-scoped environment row, reject impossible edges with `E_INVALID_STATE_TRANSITION` before any write, update `environments.status`, and append the matching `environment_events` row in the same transaction with actor/request/previous/next/reason metadata. Redact free-text reason text before persisting event message/metadata.
ralph/progress.txt:349:- Service state-machine stories use a pure `ServiceStatus.CanTransitionTo` table plus an atomic repository `Transition` method: lock the tenant-scoped service row, reject impossible edges with `E_INVALID_STATE_TRANSITION` before any write, update `services.status`, and append the matching `service_events` row in the same transaction with actor/request/previous/next/reason metadata. Redact free-text reason text before persisting event message/metadata.
ralph/progress.txt:350:- Preview delete endpoints (BE-0268 pattern, DELETE `/v1/projects/{project_id}/previews/{preview_id}`) should stay parent-project scoped: route through `projectIDResolver` with action `preview.delete`, handler forwards principal org + path project/preview ids plus optional `If-Match`, store service verifies the tenant-scoped parent project and preview row before side effects, rejects stale/already-scheduled state, calls `PreviewEnvironmentRepository.ScheduleDeletion`, enqueues `delete_preview_environment`, and appends a redaction-safe `preview.delete` audit event in the same transaction. Return `202 Accepted` with the scheduled preview snapshot and ETag because teardown is durable but worker-driven.
ralph/progress.txt:351:- Provisioning job state-machine stories use a pure `JobStatus.CanTransitionTo` table plus an atomic repository `Transition` method: lock the tenant-scoped job row, reject impossible edges with `E_INVALID_STATE_TRANSITION` before any write, derive lease/attempt/timestamp bookkeeping from the target status, and append the matching `provisioning_job_events` row in the same transaction with actor/request/previous/next/reason metadata. Redact free-text reason/error summaries before persisting event message/metadata.
ralph/progress.txt:352:- Deployment state-machine stories use a pure `DeploymentStatus.CanTransitionTo` table plus an atomic repository `Transition` method: lock the tenant-scoped deployment row, reject impossible edges with `E_INVALID_STATE_TRANSITION` before any write, update lifecycle timestamps/error fields, and append the matching `deployment_events` row in the same transaction with actor/request/previous/next/reason metadata. Redact free-text reason/error summaries before persisting event message/metadata.
ralph/progress.txt:353:- Worker Dokploy import jobs (BE-0502 pattern for `import_dokploy_resource`) are organization-scoped only and use a typed payload `(organization_id, yalla_organization_id, dokploy_organization_id[, assignment_dokploy_org_id])`. The worker reloads the Yalla organization from Postgres, rejects stale desired versions before scanning Dokploy, then delegates classification/apply to `migrateimport.Importer` through a store-backed repository adapter. Each imported project/environment/service row and its `dokploy_refs` mapping are inserted in the same transaction, so replay after partial success classifies existing refs as `skip_already_imported` and creates only missing descendants. Scanner/Dokploy failures stay retryable unless cancellation interrupted the context; malformed/cross-tenant/stale desired state is `worker.Terminal`.
ralph/progress.txt:354:- Worker service reconciliation jobs (BE-0501 pattern for `reconcile_service`) use one service-scoped payload `(organization_id, project_id, environment_id, service_id[, engine])`, reload the service and all dependent desired state through tenant-scoped repositories, verify payload scope plus `DesiredVersion`, reject deletion-scheduled services, resolve the parent environment `dokploy_refs` row, call typed Dokploy `EnsureService`, persist the service ref idempotently, render effective variables through the variables resolver, call typed `SyncVariables`, then ensure each desired `service_domain` with idempotent per-domain refs. A job already marked `succeeded` is a no-op; retryable upstream errors stay plain so the durable queue can persist a redacted `error_summary`.
ralph/progress.txt:355:- Worker preview teardown jobs (BE-0500 pattern for `delete_preview_environment`) extend `worker.Provisioner` with a typed scoped payload `(organization_id, project_id, environment_id, preview_id)`, load the `preview_environments` wrapper and wrapped `environments.kind=preview` clone through tenant-scoped repositories, verify payload scope and preview-row `DesiredVersion`, resolve the clone's `environment` `dokploy_refs` row, call the typed Dokploy `RemoveEnvironment` intent only when an upstream mapping exists, then delete the mapping and hard-delete the preview wrapper in one transaction. A job already marked `succeeded` is a no-op; a replay after cleanup treats a missing preview wrapper as success and does not issue another Dokploy call. Retryable upstream errors stay plain so the durable queue can persist a redacted `error_summary`; stale/cross-tenant/malformed desired state is `worker.Terminal`.
ralph/progress.txt:356:- Worker preview create jobs (BE-0499 pattern for `create_preview_environment`) extend `worker.Provisioner` with a typed scoped payload `(organization_id, project_id, environment_id, preview_id)`, load the `preview_environments` wrapper and wrapped `environments.kind=preview` row through tenant-scoped repositories, verify payload scope and preview-row `DesiredVersion`, reject deletion-scheduled previews before any Dokploy call, resolve the parent project Dokploy ref, call the typed Dokploy `EnsureEnvironment` intent for the wrapped clone, and persist exactly one `dokploy_refs` row as `yalla_kind=environment` / `yalla_id=<wrapped environment_id>`. A job already marked `succeeded` is a no-op replay; a crash replay with an existing ref fetches the upstream environment and leaves the mapping table unchanged. Retryable upstream errors stay plain so the durable queue can persist a redacted `error_summary`.
ralph/progress.txt:357:- Worker backup restore jobs (BE-0498 pattern for `restore_backup`) extend `worker.Provisioner` with a typed scoped payload `(organization_id, project_id, environment_id, service_id, backup_id)`, load both the parent service and `service_backups` row through tenant-scoped repositories, require the backup row to be enabled and already `succeeded`, reject stale desired versions and deletion-scheduled services before any Dokploy call, resolve the service-kind-specific Dokploy service ref, then call the typed Dokploy `RestoreBackup` intent. A job already marked `succeeded` is a replay no-op and must not issue another upstream POST; retryable upstream errors stay plain so the durable queue can persist a redacted `error_summary`.
ralph/progress.txt:358:- Worker backup run jobs (BE-0497 pattern for `run_backup`) extend `worker.Provisioner` with a typed scoped payload `(organization_id, project_id, environment_id, service_id, backup_id)`, load both the parent service and `service_backups` row through tenant-scoped repositories, reject stale backup versions plus disabled/running/deletion-scheduled state before any Dokploy call, resolve the service-kind-specific Dokploy service ref, mark the backup row `running` before calling the typed Dokploy `RunBackup` intent, then project the result to `succeeded` or `failed`. Replays of an already-succeeded backup row are no-op and must not issue another upstream POST; retryable upstream errors stay plain so the durable queue can persist a redacted `error_summary`.
ralph/progress.txt:359:- Worker variable sync jobs (BE-0496 pattern for `sync_variables`) extend `worker.Provisioner` with a typed scoped payload `(organization_id, project_id, environment_id, service_id[, engine])`, load the service through the tenant-scoped repository, verify payload scope and `DesiredVersion`, resolve the service-kind-specific Dokploy service ref, fetch all four variable scopes in one read transaction, merge them with `internal/controlplane/variables.Resolver` and the configured secrets provider, render deterministic dotenv content, and call the typed Dokploy `SyncVariables` intent. The typed client maps application to `/application.saveEnvironment`, compose to `/compose.update`, and database engines to `/<engine>.saveEnvironment`; fake Dokploy request recording must redact the entire rendered `env` field because it contains plaintext effective values.
ralph/progress.txt:360:- Worker domain sync jobs (BE-0495 pattern for `sync_domains`) extend `worker.Provisioner` with a typed scoped payload `(organization_id, project_id, environment_id, service_id)`, load the service through the tenant-scoped repository, verify payload scope and `DesiredVersion`, resolve the service-kind-specific Dokploy service ref, list desired `service_domains`, and call the typed Dokploy `EnsureDomain` intent once per desired row. Persist mappings as `dokploy_refs` rows with `yalla_kind='service_domain'`, `yalla_id=service_domains.id`, and `dokploy_resource='domain'`; replays pass `ExistingID` to `EnsureDomain` and must not insert duplicate domain mappings. Retryable upstream errors stay plain for the durable queue to persist redacted `error_summary`; malformed/stale/cross-tenant desired state is `worker.Terminal`.
ralph/progress.txt:361:- Worker project teardown jobs (BE-0494 pattern for `project.delete` / PRD alias `delete_project`) extend `worker.Provisioner` with a typed scoped payload `(organization_id, project_id)`, load the project through the tenant-scoped repository, verify payload scope and `DesiredVersion` against the persisted row, resolve the `project` `dokploy_refs` row, and call the typed Dokploy `RemoveProject` intent. A job already marked `succeeded` is a replay no-op; a prior upstream deletion is idempotent because the typed Dokploy client treats remove 404 as success. Like environment/service delete jobs, project delete jobs do not reject `deletion_scheduled_at` because scheduled deletion is the expected lifecycle path.
ralph/progress.txt:362:- Worker environment teardown jobs (BE-0493 pattern for `environment.delete` / PRD alias `delete_environment`) extend `worker.Provisioner` with a typed scoped payload `(organization_id, project_id, environment_id)`, load the environment through the tenant-scoped repository, verify payload scope and `DesiredVersion` against the persisted row, resolve the `environment` `dokploy_refs` row, and call the typed Dokploy `RemoveEnvironment` intent. A job already marked `succeeded` is a replay no-op; a prior upstream deletion is idempotent because the typed Dokploy client treats remove 404 as success. Like service delete jobs, environment delete jobs do not reject `deletion_scheduled_at` because scheduled deletion is the expected lifecycle path.
ralph/progress.txt:363:- Worker teardown jobs (BE-0492 pattern for `service.delete` / PRD alias `delete_service`) extend `worker.Provisioner` with a typed scoped payload `(organization_id, project_id, environment_id, service_id)`, load the service through the tenant-scoped repository, verify payload scope and `DesiredVersion` against the persisted row, resolve the service-kind-specific `dokploy_refs` row, and call the typed Dokploy `RemoveService` intent. A job already marked `succeeded` is a replay no-op; a prior upstream deletion is also idempotent because the typed Dokploy client treats remove 404 as success. Unlike runtime-control jobs, delete jobs do not reject `deletion_scheduled_at`; scheduled deletion is the normal lifecycle path.
ralph/progress.txt:364:- Worker runtime-control jobs (BE-0488/BE-0489/BE-0490/BE-0491 pattern for `service.restart` / PRD alias `restart_service`, `service.stop` / PRD alias `stop_service`, `service.start` / PRD alias `start_service`, and `service.rollback` / PRD alias `rollback_service`) extend `worker.Provisioner` with a typed payload parser carrying `(organization_id, project_id, environment_id, service_id)`, load the service through the tenant-scoped repository, verify payload scope against the persisted row, refuse stale `DesiredVersion` and `deletion_scheduled_at` before any Dokploy call, resolve the concrete service Dokploy ID from the service-kind-specific `dokploy_refs` row, and call a typed Dokploy runtime intent such as `RestartService`, `StopService`, `StartService`, or `RollbackService`. Keep accepting PRD underscore aliases (`restart_service`, `stop_service`, `start_service`, `rollback_service`, etc.) for compatibility with manually seeded jobs. Jobs with `Status == succeeded` should be no-op replays and must not issue another Dokploy POST; retryable upstream errors stay plain so the durable queue can persist a redacted `error_summary`.
ralph/progress.txt:365:- Worker deployment jobs (BE-0487 pattern for `service.deploy` / PRD alias `deploy_service`) extend `worker.Provisioner` with a typed payload parser that includes `deployment_id`, load the deployment and parent service through tenant-scoped repositories, verify payload scope against the persisted deployment row, resolve the concrete service Dokploy ID from the service-kind-specific `dokploy_refs` row, mark the deployment `running` before calling `DeployService`, mark it `succeeded` only after a succeeded upstream deployment, and return retryable errors while the upstream deployment is still pending/running or unavailable. Replays of already-succeeded deployments are no-op and must not issue another Dokploy POST; retryable upstream failures leave the deployment running without `finished_at` so the durable queue can retry. The existing API enqueues `service.deploy`; keep accepting the PRD `deploy_service` alias for compatibility.
ralph/progress.txt:366:- Worker child ensure jobs (BE-0482/BE-0483/BE-0484 pattern for `ensure_project`, `ensure_environment`, and `ensure_application_service`) extend `worker.Provisioner` with a typed payload parser, validate the job scope fields against payload IDs, load the source-of-truth row through the tenant-scoped repository, check `DesiredVersion` before Dokploy calls and again before persisting refs, resolve the parent Dokploy ID from tenant-scoped `dokploy_refs`, call the typed Dokploy client through mapper-produced intents, and persist exactly one child `dokploy_refs` row. Replay should read the existing child ref, call the typed client with `ExistingID`, and leave the mapping table unchanged; malformed/stale/cross-tenant/missing desired state are `worker.Terminal`, retryable Dokploy errors stay plain, and queue-level tests should verify persisted `error_summary` stays redacted. Service-kind-specific jobs must also verify the source-of-truth `services.kind` before calling Dokploy and persist the matching Dokploy resource kind (`application`, `compose`, or `database`). `ensure_database_service` additionally validates the concrete engine in the durable job payload because the current `services` row stores only the broad `database` kind.
ralph/progress.txt:367:- Worker provisioning jobs are implemented through `internal/controlplane/worker.Provisioner`: parse `provisioning_jobs.payload` into a typed schema, mark malformed/stale/cross-tenant desired state with `worker.Terminal`, load source-of-truth state from Postgres before Dokploy calls, and persist resolved upstream IDs through tenant-scoped `dokploy_refs`; replay should verify an existing ref instead of inserting a duplicate mapping.
ralph/progress.txt:368:- Repository tenant-isolation tests for a **project-scoped preview lifecycle wrapper with Insert + GetByID + ListByProject + Update + ScheduleDeletion + DeleteByID, database-owned version/timestamps, and soft-delete state visible on read paths** (BE-0480 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/preview_environment_repository_tenant_isolation_test.go for preview_environments). Seed two complete Organization -> Project -> source Environment + preview-kind wrapped Environment chains, use overlapping-looking DisplayName/ChangeRef fixtures, assert ListByProject renders only the caller's stable page and returns a non-nil empty slice for `(orgA, projB)`, assert Get/Update/ScheduleDeletion/Delete using orgA with orgB's `(project_id, preview_id)` return typed `E_NOT_FOUND`, byte-compare both orgA and orgB bystanders after failed cross-tenant writes, and explicitly schedule deletion on orgB to prove soft-deleted preview rows remain tenant-scoped on both list and direct-id read paths. Add a global-PK collision probe across tenants because `preview_environments.id` is globally unique even though all normal reads/writes are tenant-scoped; the conflict must leave the prior owner byte-identical and create no row for the requester.
ralph/progress.txt:369:- Repository CRUD-and-invariants tests for a **project-scoped preview lifecycle table whose row points at both a preview-kind environment clone and a source environment, carries soft-delete lifecycle state, and exposes Insert + GetByID + ListByProject + Update + ScheduleDeletion + DeleteByID** (BE-0479 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/preview_environment_repository_invariants_test.go for preview_environments). The table is tenant-scoped by direct `(organization_id, project_id)` predicates and composite FKs to `environments (organization_id, project_id, id)`, uses `penv_` IDs via `domain.KindPreviewEnvironment`, and adds a trigger to enforce that the wrapped environment row has `kind='preview'` because a FK cannot express referenced-row-body predicates. It keeps status/deletion state out of customer metadata Update, and uses ScheduleDeletion (`status='deleting'`, `deletion_scheduled_at=COALESCE(..., now())`) for API teardown while preserving hard DeleteByID for worker cleanup after Dokploy teardown. Forward-applicable to future project-scoped lifecycle rows that wrap a concrete hierarchy resource with worker-owned status: test row shape/default status, deterministic `(created_at,id)` list ordering, optimistic conflicts, soft-delete lifecycle, hard-delete cleanup, constraint conflicts, referenced-row-body trigger conflicts, rollback, nil-Tx guards, and database-owned version/timestamps.
ralph/progress.txt:370:- Preview create endpoint/service pattern (BE-0262): parent-scoped preview API routes use `projectIDResolver`, a thin HTTP handler, and `store.PreviewEnvironmentService`. The store unit of work verifies the path project exists under the principal org, reloads the source environment tenant-scoped, requires `source.ProjectID == projectID` before auth/quota/job/audit side effects, creates a real `environments.kind='preview'` clone plus the `preview_environments` wrapper in the same transaction, reserves both `environments` and `preview_environments`, enqueues `create_preview_environment` with the preview id through the current `JobEnqueuer` port, and audits `preview.create` against `domain.KindPreviewEnvironment`. Wrong-project or cross-tenant source ids should collapse to typed `E_NOT_FOUND`.
ralph/progress.txt:372:- Repository tenant-isolation tests for a **service-scoped hard-delete leaf table with Insert + GetByID + ListByService + Update + DeleteByID and optimistic version triggers** (BE-0476 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/service_domain_repository_tenant_isolation_test.go for service_domains). Seed two full Organization -> Project -> Environment -> Service chains, use overlapping-looking but globally unique (hostname, path) fixtures, assert ListByService returns only the caller tenant's stable page and a non-nil empty slice for a cross-tenant service_id, assert Get/Update/Delete using orgA with orgB's service/domain ids return typed E_NOT_FOUND, and byte-compare both orgA and orgB bystanders after failed cross-tenant mutations so missing organization predicates show up as version/updated_at drift. For service_domains specifically, an empty create path is intentionally normalized by the service layer to "/" and the database allows it; do not treat blank path as a DB CHECK conflict in repository constraint tests.
ralph/progress.txt:373:- Repository CRUD-and-invariants tests for a **tenant-scoped service leaf table whose public surface is Insert + GetByID + ListByService + Update + DeleteByID, whose row has database-owned version/created_at/updated_at, and whose parent link is the composite `(organization_id, service_id)` FK** (BE-0475 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/service_domain_repository_invariants_test.go for service_domains). The reusable shape is: seed the Organization -> Project -> Environment -> Service chain with existing helpers, insert through the typed repository rather than raw SQL, assert version=1 and created_at==updated_at on INSERT, assert Update bumps version and advances updated_at while preserving created_at and identity fields, assert matching If-Match succeeds while stale If-Match on Update/Delete returns typed `E_CONFLICT`, assert DeleteByID returns the final row and removes it, and cover CHECK/FK/UNIQUE violations, transaction rollback, parent cascade, deterministic ListByService ordering, and nil-Tx guards. Forward-applicable to future service-scoped leaf tables with hard-delete lifecycle and optimistic concurrency.
ralph/progress.txt:374:- Repository CRUD-and-invariants tests for a **TABLE WHOSE *_repository.go + PRIMARY *_test.go + INTERNAL *_internal_test.go + CANONICAL *_canonical_test.go ALL ALREADY EXIST FROM A PRIOR (NON-PRD) SUBSYSTEM STORY + VALIDATOR-EXHAUSTIVENESS STORY, AND WHOSE LIFECYCLE IS APPEND + READ + ONE STATE-TRANSITION (MarkRevoked, *Tx-bound) WITH A REPOSITORY-LEVEL LIMIT CAP, AND WHOSE UPDATE SURFACE IS GUARDED BY THREE STACKED BEFORE-UPDATE TRIGGERS (set_updated_at + bump_version + a per-column rewrite-rejecting trigger), AND WHOSE Append FILLS A ZERO StartedAt SERVER-SIDE IN THE REPOSITORY (not via DB DEFAULT)** (BE-0473 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/break_glass_repository_invariants_test.go for break_glass_sessions). Subspecies of the BE-0469 (idempotency_keys) gap-fill pattern with five load-bearing deltas: (a) the THREE-trigger UPDATE surface is unique in store — the rewrite-rejecting trigger (break_glass_sessions_reject_rewrite) raises SQLSTATE 23001 (restrict_violation) for any UPDATE that mutates a non-revocation column AND for any UPDATE that blanks the reason column. The test file walks the FULL trigger predicate matrix (id, organization_id, actor_id, actor_kind, reason, started_at, expires_at, request_id, correlation_id) via raw-SQL UPDATEs and asserts BOTH the typed error AND byte-identical row re-load AFTER the rejection. A regression that demoted any single column from the trigger predicate would surface as a per-case failure on its first iteration. The existing primary _test.go's TestBreakGlassSchemaRejectsUpdateToImmutableColumns covers ONLY reason rewrite, so the per-column-matrix invariants probe is genuinely new coverage. (b) The Append fills a zero StartedAt server-side via `if s.StartedAt.IsZero() { s.StartedAt = time.Now().UTC() }` BEFORE the INSERT — the column has NO DEFAULT in the migration (vs created_at/updated_at which DO have DEFAULT now()). A regression that removed the IsZero branch would NOT surface as a CHECK violation (expires_at > started_at is satisfied with zero started_at + any future expires_at) — it would silently store year-0001 rows. TestBreakGlassRepositoryAppendStartedAtDefaultsToNow is the regression backstop: it asserts the filled StartedAt falls within a ±2s wallclock bracket. (c) MarkRevoked's UPDATE filters on `revoked_at IS NULL` (column predicate, NOT row-existence predicate) — same shape as DriftFindingRepository.MarkResolved (BE-0471). The zero-rows branch falls through to a tenant-scoped follow-up Get inside the SAME *Tx to distinguish "missing" (typed apierr.NotFound, caller-supplied id) from "already revoked" (typed apierr.Conflict). This means TestBreakGlassRepositoryMarkRevokedCrossTenantIsNotFound is NOT redundant with TestBreakGlassRepositoryGetCrossTenantIsNotFound: a regression that dropped tenant scoping from BOTH the UPDATE and the follow-up Get would silently succeed in revoking another tenant's session. The cross-tenant Get + cross-tenant MarkRevoked pair pins both halves of the contract; either alone is insufficient. (d) The CHECK / FK violation matrix for break_glass_sessions has FOUR distinct probes that surface as typed apierr.Conflict via mapWriteError: unknown organization (FK), blank actor_kind + unknown actor_kind (both fail the CHECK actor_kind IN ('usr','sa')), blank reason (CHECK length(reason) > 0), and expires_at <= started_at (CHECK expires_at > started_at — exercised with both equal-to-started_at AND predates-started_at sub-cases). The repository does NOT pre-validate these fields (the service buildSessionToCreate is the chokepoint for validation), so the constraint violation flows through the database CHECK and surfaces via mapWriteError. (e) The `twoOrgs(t, db, f, label)` helper is genuinely new at package scope (verified via grep on `^func twoOrgs` across all store_test files — no prior declaration exists) — it names the two-organization fixture pattern (a TARGET tenant + a SUPPORT actor's home tenant) that every break_glass test needs. The 28-test shape this file lands: Append-row-shape (raw-SQL byte-equal + bgs_ prefix + server-side timestamps within ±2s + version=1 + RevokedAt nil), Append-respects-caller-supplied-id, Append-duplicate-id-Conflict (PRIMARY KEY), Append-StartedAt-defaults-to-now (the load-bearing server-side-fill probe), four CHECK/FK-violation Conflicts (unknown-org, blank-actor_kind, unknown-actor_kind, blank-reason), expires_at-not-after-started_at-Conflict (equal + predate sub-cases), Append-nil-Tx-Internal, Append-transaction-rollback, Append-peer-row-byte-identical, Get-reads-back, Get-unknown-NotFound, Get-cross-tenant-NotFound, Get-expired-returns-row-as-is (Active(t) observation, NOT Get filtering), List-newest-first-ordering (started_at DESC, id DESC), List-limit-clamping matrix (non-positive / negative / above-cap / in-range), List-unknown-org-empty-slice, MarkRevoked-happy-path (RETURNING byte-equal raw-SQL + version+1 via bump_version + updated_at advance via set_updated_at), MarkRevoked-already-revoked-Conflict (does not move revoked_at/revoked_by_*), MarkRevoked-unknown-NotFound, MarkRevoked-cross-tenant-NotFound (does not touch row), MarkRevoked-nil-Tx-Internal, MarkRevoked-peer-row-byte-identical, append-mostly-trigger-rejects-column-rewrites (per-column matrix), append-mostly-trigger-rejects-blank-reason (trigger's leading guard), organization-DELETE-cascade. New helpers introduced (per-file BreakGlass-suffix to avoid store_test collisions): rawBreakGlassRow, loadBreakGlassRowByID, countBreakGlassRowsForOrg, mintBreakGlassSessionID, breakGlassSessionFixture, seedBreakGlassSession, assertBreakGlassRowByteIdentical, breakGlassTxRollbackSentinel, sanitizeTestNameForBreakGlassID, AND the package-new twoOrgs helper for the two-org fixture shape. REUSED verbatim from sibling files: assertTimePtrEqual (drift_finding_repository_invariants_test.go), seedOrg (schema_test.go), newStore (store_test.go), wantErrCode (idempotency_test.go). NO migration added (0019_break_glass_sessions landed in an early migration). Forward-applicable to ANY future repository CRUD-and-invariants story whose table carries a three-trigger UPDATE surface (set_updated_at + bump_version + rewrite-rejecting) AND whose Append fills a zero timestamp server-side in the repository rather than via DB DEFAULT — the per-column trigger-predicate matrix and the ±2s wallclock-bracket pattern are the chokepoints that catch the two most common regression shapes (a demoted trigger column or a removed IsZero default-fill branch).
ralph/progress.txt:375:- Repository tenant-isolation tests for a **TABLE WHOSE *_repository.go + *_repository_invariants_test.go ALREADY EXIST IN THE IMMEDIATELY PRIOR PRD STORY, WHOSE PUBLIC SURFACE IS THE FULL Append + GetByID + ListByOrganization + MarkResolved (state-transition) MATRIX, WHOSE OPTIONAL RESOURCE LEGS ARE NULLABLE COMPOSITE FKs MATCH SIMPLE, AND WHOSE ROW BODY HAS A TENANT-SHARED TEXT COLUMN (e.g. dokploy_resource_id with NO UNIQUE constraint cross-tenant)** (BE-0472 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/drift_finding_repository_tenant_isolation_test.go). Subspecies of the BE-0468 (audit_events) full-surface template with three load-bearing deltas: (a) the public mutation surface is TWO writes (Append + MarkResolved) rather than one (Append), so the tenant-isolation file must add a deepened cross-tenant MarkResolved probe with a MULTI-ROW orgB pool (target + (kind, reason, level)-sharing sibling + already-resolved row) — the existing invariants-file cross-tenant MarkResolved probe targets only one row inline and does not exercise the bystander byte-identity loop. (b) The tenant-shared text shape probe uses dokploy_resource_id (NOT NULL DEFAULT '' with NO UNIQUE cross-tenant) — orgA and orgB seed rows with the SAME dokploy_resource_id value across both Append-bystander-byte-identity AND ListByOrganization-cross-tenant probes. A regression that confused tenants by dokploy_resource_id rather than organization_id would surface as a foreign-tenant row in the list result or a bystander row drift. (c) The optional resource legs (project_id / environment_id / service_id / service_domain_id) are NULLABLE composite FKs MATCH SIMPLE, so the two-tenant fixture seeds per-tenant parent chains (orgA+projA+envA+svcA, orgB+projB+envB+svcB) — a single shared chain would not satisfy the composite FK on the cross-tenant probes, and a missing chain would force every test into the organization-level (every-leg-NULL) shape. The fixture helper seedTwoTenantDriftFindingFixture lands the full per-tenant chain by default; tests opt into organization-level findings inline by leaving the legs unset. The 9-test shape this file lands: Append-on-orgA-does-not-touch-orgB-rows (multi-row orgB bystander pool covering kind/reason/level/lifecycle spread + tenant-shared dokploy_resource_id), Append-on-orgA-does-not-change-orgB-row-count, Append-cross-tenant-id-collision (global PK collision + foreign-column-leak audit on the Conflict payload), ListByOrganization-cross-tenant-returns-only-own-rows (the load-bearing list probe with tenant-shared dokploy_resource_id AND identical (kind, reason, level) tuples), ListByOrganization-limit-clamping-is-tenant-scoped (raw-SQL seed 205 orgA rows above the 200-cap + orgB-only above-cap list returns exactly orgB's count), ListByOrganization-unknown-tenant-empty-and-side-effect-free, GetByID-cross-tenant-bystander-byte-identical (deepens existing invariants-file probe with bystander row + per-tenant count + error-payload foreign-column-leak audit), MarkResolved-cross-tenant-deep-bystander-byte-identical (deepens existing invariants-file probe with multi-row orgB pool including (kind, reason, level)-sharing sibling), and organization-delete-cascade-is-tenant-scoped (deepens existing invariants-file probe with three-row orgB pool covering pending + resolved + shared-dokploy_resource_id + post-cascade ListByOrganization probe). New helpers introduced: twoTenantDriftFindingFixture (full per-tenant parent chain), seedTwoTenantDriftFindingFixture, totalDriftFindingRows (global counterpart to countDriftFindingRowsForOrg for the cross-tenant id-collision global-count probe). Reused verbatim from sibling files: rawDriftFindingRow, loadDriftFindingRowByID, countDriftFindingRowsForOrg, mintDriftFindingID, driftFindingFixture, seedDriftFinding, assertDriftFindingByteIdentical (drift_finding_repository_invariants_test.go); seedOrg, seedProject, seedEnvironment, seedService (schema_test.go); newStore (store_test.go); wantErrCode (idempotency_test.go); containsValue (organization_variable_tenant_isolation_test.go); itoa (audit_repository_tenant_isolation_test.go). The itoa helper from audit_repository_tenant_isolation_test.go is reused for the raw-SQL seed loop in TestDriftFindingRepositoryListByOrganizationLimitClampingIsTenantScoped to avoid importing strconv for one call site; a duplicate top-level itoa in this file would fail compilation because all *_test.go files share package store_test. Forward-applicable to ANY future repository tenant-isolation story whose surface is Append + GetByID + ListByOrganization + a single state-transition (MarkResolved-like) mutation AND whose row body carries a tenant-shared text column without a UNIQUE constraint cross-tenant — the dokploy_resource_id-shared-text-shape probe shape is the chokepoint that catches a regression switching tenant-discrimination from organization_id to any other column.
ralph/progress.txt:376:- Repository CRUD-and-invariants tests for a **TABLE WHOSE *_repository.go + PRIMARY *_test.go + INTERNAL *_internal_test.go + READER ADAPTER + READER ADAPTER TEST ALL ALREADY EXIST FROM A PRIOR (NON-PRD) SUBSYSTEM STORY, AND WHOSE LIFECYCLE IS APPEND-ONLY WITH ONLY ONE WRITE SURFACE (Append) AND ONE READ SURFACE (ListByOrganization, no GetByID) AND A REPOSITORY-LEVEL LIMIT CAP, AND WHOSE INSERT COLUMN LIST DELIBERATELY OMITS occurred_at + created_at (so DEFAULT now() applies and caller-supplied timestamps are SILENTLY DISCARDED)** (BE-0467 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/audit_repository_invariants_test.go for audit_events). Subspecies of the BE-0461 (provisioning_jobs) gap-fill pattern with five load-bearing deltas: (a) the typed repository is the NARROWEST in the store package — Append + ListByOrganization only, NO GetByID, NO Update, NO Delete (row removal reachable only through organizations ON DELETE CASCADE on tenant teardown). The acceptance-criteria phrase "create typed repository methods for creating, reading, updating, and deleting ... where the lifecycle requires it" narrows to "Append + List only; the audit log lifecycle structurally rejects per-row Update / Delete" and the file header MUST document this as load-bearing so a future reader does not mistake the absence for a coverage gap; (b) the audit_events INSERT column list (auditEventColumns minus occurred_at/created_at) deliberately omits both timestamp columns, so DEFAULT now() applies regardless of caller. A test `TestAuditRepositoryAppendIgnoresCallerSuppliedTimestamps` is the regression backstop — a future refactor that added occurred_at to the INSERT (so a caller could back-date an audit record) would silently break the "audit timestamps are server-side" guarantee unless this test was also updated. The probe seeds OccurredAt=year-2000 and asserts the persisted row's OccurredAt is within [now-1s, now+1s], NOT the back-dated value; (c) CHECK constraints that the application does NOT pre-validate (blank action / blank resource_kind / blank reason / actor_kind outside the closed-set ('', 'usr', 'sa', 'system')) MUST be exercised: Append does NOT pre-check these fields (the audit service is the chokepoint for the principal-mapping inputs), so the constraint violation flows through the database CHECK and surfaces as typed `apierr.Conflict` via mapWriteError. Each test asserts BOTH the error code AND `countAuditRowsForOrg(org.ID) == 0` to pin "the rejection left no row behind"; (d) the repository-level limit cap (`auditEventListMaxLimit = 200`) is a public invariant of the ListByOrganization contract and MUST be exercised in three branches — non-positive (limit <= 0 -> 200), above-cap (limit > 200 -> 200), in-range (limit in (0, 200] honoured verbatim). A regression that returned the empty slice when limit <= 0 (or every row when limit was unbounded) would surface here. The seeding helper `seedAuditRowsRawSQL` bypasses the repository and uses direct INSERT with caller-controlled occurred_at so 201 rows can be planted in O(n) without sleeping; (e) the secondary-sort ORDER BY id DESC tiebreaker on tied occurred_at is the load-bearing list-ordering invariant: a UI rendering a pair of audit events written in the same Postgres microsecond would flicker without it. The probe seeds TWO rows with the SAME occurred_at (via raw INSERT — Append's DEFAULT now() would advance between calls) and asserts listed[0].ID > listed[1].ID lexicographically. The 16-test shape this file lands: Append-row-shape (raw-SQL byte-equal + aud_ prefix + server-side timestamps within [before, after]), Append-respects-caller-supplied-id, Append-ignores-caller-supplied-timestamps (the load-bearing server-side-timestamp probe), four CHECK-violation Conflicts (blank-action, blank-resource_kind, blank-reason, unknown-actor_kind), duplicate-id-Conflict (PRIMARY KEY), Append-transaction-rollback (sentinel error + countAuditRowsForOrg == 0), Append-cascades-from-organization-delete (orgA wipe + orgB bystander byte-identity across id/organization_id/action/reason/occurred_at/created_at), Append-peer-row-byte-identical (the BE-0468 byte-identity bystander predecessor — peer Append must NOT touch a prior row), List-orders-id-DESC-on-timestamp-tie, three List-limit-clamping branches (non-positive, above-cap, in-range), List-unknown-org-returns-empty. New helpers (per-file Audit-suffix to avoid store_test collisions): rawAuditRow, loadAuditRowByID, countAuditRowsForOrg (the per-org counter — organization is the only parent FK for audit_events, so this is also the natural per-parent counter), mintAuditID, auditTxRollbackSentinel, seedAuditRowsRawSQL (the raw-INSERT batch seeder for cap-boundary tests). Reused verbatim from sibling audit_test.go: auditEventFixture, appendAudit, assertAuditEventByteIdentical; from schema_test.go: seedOrg; from store_test.go: newStore. NO migration added. Forward-applicable to any future first-typed-repository story whose underlying table is append-only with a single (Append, List) surface and a repository-level limit cap (a future security_events / system_audit table would inherit the same template, including the server-side-timestamp invariant if the INSERT column list omits the timestamp legs).
ralph/progress.txt:377:- Repository CRUD-and-invariants tests for a **TABLE WHOSE *_repository.go + PRIMARY *_test.go ALREADY EXIST FROM A PRIOR (NON-PRD) SUBSYSTEM STORY, AND WHOSE LIFECYCLE HAS A CLAIM PROTOCOL (Claim + Complete + Release + Find — three writes, one read) DRIVEN BY AN UPSERT WITH AN ON-CONFLICT TARGET ON A NON-PK UNIQUE TUPLE (organization_id, principal_id, idempotency_key), AND WHOSE ROW BODY CARRIES A MULTI-COLUMN COMPLETION-CONSISTENT CHECK** (BE-0469 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/idempotency_repository_invariants_test.go for idempotency_keys). Subspecies of the BE-0467 (audit_events) gap-fill pattern with five load-bearing deltas: (a) the Claim INSERT uses `ON CONFLICT (organization_id, principal_id, idempotency_key) DO UPDATE` rather than the canonical `ON CONFLICT (id)` -- the PK is the secondary disambiguator, not the conflict target. So a duplicate id on a DIFFERENT (organization_id, principal_id, idempotency_key) tuple is NOT caught by ON CONFLICT; the PK violation flows through the database and surfaces through mapWriteError as apierr.Conflict. The TestIdempotencyRepositoryClaimDuplicateIDIsConflict probe MUST force the collision through caller-supplied ids on different (principal, key) tuples; a regression that switched the ON CONFLICT target to (id) would swallow the collision and silently rewrite a peer row. (b) The `idempotency_keys_completion_consistent` CHECK requires (response_status, response_body, completed_at) to be either ALL NULL (pending) or ALL NOT NULL (completed). The Complete method coerces a nil `body []byte` argument to `[]byte{}` (zero-length but non-nil) BEFORE the UPDATE so the CHECK's `response_body IS NOT NULL` clause holds for empty envelopes. TestIdempotencyRepositoryCompleteCoercesNilBodyToEmpty pins both the application-layer coercion AND the database-side non-NULL empty-bytea storage. The pending-side of the CHECK is pinned by TestIdempotencyRepositoryClaimMintsRowShape (a fresh Claim must leave all three completion columns NULL); the completed-side by TestIdempotencyRepositoryCompleteRowShape (after Complete, all three must be non-NULL AND a raw-SQL re-load must byte-equal the returned struct, AND created_at + expires_at must NOT have drifted across the Complete UPDATE -- only the four completion columns transition). (c) The Complete method's `now time.Time` argument is the only application-controlled timestamp on the table: created_at + expires_at are caller-supplied via Claim and completed_at can be caller-supplied via Complete's now arg (zero defaults to time.Now().UTC()). TestIdempotencyRepositoryCompleteHonoursExplicitNow uses a specific 2024 timestamp far from time.Now() so a regression that ignored the arg and wrote time.Now() would fail with an obvious delta. This is a structurally distinct invariant from BE-0467 (audit_events) where ALL timestamps are server-side via DEFAULT now() and a caller-supplied OccurredAt must be discarded. (d) The id minter newIdempotencyID emits `idk_<32hex>` (16 bytes of crypto/rand entropy hex-encoded — 4 + 32 = 36 chars total). The row-shape probe MUST pin BOTH the prefix AND the total length so a regression that shortened the entropy or reformatted the prefix surfaces here. Test-local mintIdempotencyID(t, suffix) follows the established per-file suffixed-helper pattern (`idk_`+t.Name()+`_`+suffix) so parallel tests that need a deterministic caller-supplied id (to force a PK collision) cannot collide. (e) The Release method has NO pre-validation coverage in the primary idempotency_test.go's TestIdempotencyClaimValidation (which only covers Claim nil-tx + Claim missing-fields + Complete nil-tx + Complete out-of-range-status). The invariants file fills the Release gap with TestIdempotencyRepositoryReleaseRejectsNilTx so all three mutating methods (Claim / Complete / Release) have a pinned nil-tx guard. The 15-test shape this file lands: Claim-row-shape (raw-SQL byte-equal + idk_ prefix + 36-char length + server-side created_at + ExpiresAt round-trip + pending-side completion-consistent), Claim-respects-caller-supplied-id, Claim-duplicate-id-Conflict (PK collision on different (org, principal, key)), Claim-unknown-organization-Conflict (FK violation), Complete-row-shape (completed-side completion-consistent + created_at/expires_at non-drift), Complete-honours-explicit-now (specific 2024 timestamp), Complete-coerces-nil-body-to-empty (non-NULL empty bytea), Release-rejects-nil-tx, Release-on-completed-is-noop (row stays in place), Release-on-missing-key-is-noop, Claim-transaction-rollback (sentinel + countIdempotencyRowsForOrg == 0), Cascade-from-organization-delete (orgA wipe + orgB bystander byte-identity across id/route/request_hash/request_id/status/created_at/expires_at), Claim-peer-row-byte-identical, Find-returns-expired-row-as-is (Find does NOT filter on expires_at > now()), ExpiresAt-round-trip (microsecond-precise far-future timestamp). Note: idempotency_keys has NO updated_at column and NO BEFORE UPDATE trigger -- the peer-row byte-identity probe relies on the per-column projection across all 13 observable columns without a trigger-managed drift anchor (vs BE-0466 dokploy_refs which anchors on the set_updated_at trigger). Note: the response_status BETWEEN 100 AND 599 CHECK is unreachable from the typed Complete API (it pre-rejects out-of-range as apierr.Internal); same for the status IN ('pending', 'completed') CHECK (Claim hard-codes 'pending', Complete hard-codes 'completed'); same for the length() > 0 CHECKs on principal_id/idempotency_key/route/request_hash (Claim pre-validates these as apierr.InvalidInput). These CHECKs are pinned at the DB level only and NOT probed from Go in the invariants file -- the file header MUST document this delegation so a future reader does not mistake the absence for a coverage gap. New helpers introduced (per-file Idempotency-suffix to avoid store_test collisions): rawIdempotencyRow (with NULLABLE *int / *time.Time fields so pending NULLs are distinguishable from zero-value scalars), loadIdempotencyRowByID, countIdempotencyRowsForOrg, mintIdempotencyID, idempotencyTxRollbackSentinel. REUSED verbatim from sibling idempotency_test.go: idemFixture, claimIdem, completeIdem, findIdem, wantErrCode, newIdempotencyStore; from schema_test.go: seedOrg; from store_test.go: newStore. NO migration added. The byte-identical-bystander tenant-isolation gap-fill is the paired BE-0470 story. Forward-applicable to any future PRD CRUD-and-invariants story whose underlying table has a claim-protocol-style upsert on a non-PK UNIQUE tuple with a multi-column completion-consistent CHECK (e.g. a future webhook-delivery-claims or rate-limit-burst-tokens table).
ralph/progress.txt:378:- Repository tenant-isolation tests for a **TABLE WHOSE SCHEMA LANDED IN AN EARLY MIGRATION, WHOSE TYPED REPOSITORY + INVARIANTS TESTS LANDED IN THE IMMEDIATELY PRIOR PRD STORY, AND WHOSE ROW BODY HAS NO CUSTOMER-MUTABLE COLUMN (Insert + Get + List + Delete only, NO Update / NO state-transition surface) AND CARRIES TWO UNIQUE CONSTRAINTS — ONE GLOBAL across tenants AND ONE PER-TENANT** (BE-0466 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/dokploy_ref_tenant_isolation_test.go for dokploy_refs). Subspecies of the BE-0464 pattern (append-only no-Update tenant-isolation) with five load-bearing deltas: (a) the table HAS an updated_at column maintained by a set_updated_at BEFORE UPDATE trigger (vs BE-0464's no-updated_at + BEFORE-UPDATE-rejection trigger), so assertXxxByteIdentical anchors on BOTH created_at AND updated_at as bystander drift probes; a foreign-tenant UPDATE that snuck past the organization_id predicate would fire the trigger and surface as an updated_at drift, even if every other column happened to match. (b) The GLOBAL UNIQUE leg (e.g. dokploy_refs UNIQUE (dokploy_resource, dokploy_id)) demands a dokploy_refs-specific cross-tenant probe not present in any prior tenant-isolation file: orgA Inserting at a (resource, dokploy_id) ALREADY OWNED by orgB must surface as typed apierr.Conflict AND leave orgB's row byte-identical — this is the cross-tenant mirror of the same-tenant duplicate-target Conflict pinned in the BE-0465 invariants file, with the added byte-identity invariant. A regression that widened the GLOBAL UNIQUE to (organization_id, dokploy_resource, dokploy_id) would let orgA's row through (orgA.count would go to 1); a regression that silently UPDATEd orgB's row before the UNIQUE fired would surface as yalla_kind/yalla_id drift on the bystander. (c) GetByXxxTarget (e.g. dokploy_refs.GetByDokployTarget(orgID, resource, dokploy_id)) is a SECOND tenant-scoped read surface beyond the per-id Get — and because the (resource, dokploy_id) tuple is GLOBAL-UNIQUE, the row DOES exist for orgB's foreign-target call, so the tenant predicate is the ONLY thing hiding it. The cross-tenant test is structurally load-bearing: a regression that dropped the organization_id predicate from GetByXxxTarget would surface a successful read of orgA's row from orgB's caller (worst-case cross-tenant data leak). This file must also include a SANITY clause that calls GetByXxxTarget(orgA, resource, alphaDokployID) and expects success; without it the entire test could silently pass if the repository broke for both tenants. (d) Delete is *Tx-bound and returns NotFound via the tag.RowsAffected() == 0 path (not via pgx.ErrNoRows). The cross-tenant probe must run inside an s.Write closure and assert BOTH typed NotFound AND orgA's row byte-identity after — a regression that dropped the organization_id leg from the DELETE predicate would surface as either tag.RowsAffected() == 1 (orgB's call returns nil and orgA's row vanishes — the worst-case data-loss bug) OR as an orgA bystander column drift. The closest sibling (BE-0464 job_attempts) has no Delete surface, so this is a BE-0466-unique probe. (e) The positive-direction "same X in two tenants both persist" test must NOT use the dokploy_id column (the GLOBAL UNIQUE rejects same dokploy_id across tenants) — it instead probes the (yalla_kind, dokploy_resource) pair under DIFFERENT yalla_id/dokploy_id per tenant. The probe pins that there is NO phantom UNIQUE on (yalla_kind, dokploy_resource) alone — without it, a regression resolving such a phantom UNIQUE would prevent orgA's Insert and every byte-identical bystander assertion would silently pass against an unmutated row that was never written. The 10-test shape this file lands: Insert-bystander-byte-identity / Insert-row-count-invariance / Insert-cross-tenant-GLOBAL-UNIQUE-Conflict-AND-orgB-byte-identical / same-(yalla_kind,dokploy_resource)-in-two-tenants-positive-direction with per-tenant ListByYallaResource read-side proof / Get-cross-tenant-NotFound-with-no-leak (substring checks on yalla_id / dokploy_id / dokploy_resource — the 3 caller-controllable identifiers that a regression could echo) / GetByDokployTarget-cross-tenant-NotFound-with-no-leak + side-effect-free read counts + orgA-sanity-success / ListByYallaResource-cross-tenant-empty + per-tenant COUNT(*) invariance for BOTH tenants / ListByOrganization-cross-tenant-returns-only-own-rows + orgA sanity / Delete-cross-tenant-NotFound + orgA bystander byte-identity + orgA count invariance / organizations DELETE cascade is tenant-scoped across multiple bystander kinds. NEW helpers all carry the per-file DokployRef suffix: assertDokployRefByteIdentical, twoTenantDokployRefFixture + seedTwoTenantDokployRefFixture (4-deep hierarchy seeder: org/proj/env/svc per tenant — needed because dokploy_refs maps to services as the most common yalla_kind), firstDokployRefDokployID; REUSED verbatim from invariants file: mintDokployID, runInsertDokployRefOrFail, rawDokployRefRow, loadDokployRefRowByID, countDokployRefRowsForOrg; from sibling tenant-isolation: seedOrg/seedProject/seedEnvironment/seedService/containsValue. NO migration added. Forward-applicable to any future tenant-isolation story whose table has a GLOBAL UNIQUE on (kind, external_id) — a future external-reference/billing-target table would inherit the same template, including the structurally-load-bearing GetByXxxTarget cross-tenant probe and the GLOBAL-UNIQUE cross-tenant-Conflict + bystander byte-identity pair.
ralph/progress.txt:379:- Repository tenant-isolation tests for a **TABLE WHOSE TYPED REPOSITORY IS THE NARROWEST IN THE STORE PACKAGE — APPEND (write) + ListByOrganization (read) ONLY, with NO GetByID, NO Update, NO Delete (row removal reachable only through organizations ON DELETE CASCADE on tenant teardown), AND WHOSE PRIMARY KEY IS GLOBAL (no UNIQUE on (organization_id, id) because the id minter emits 128-bit-entropy opaque tokens that are globally unique by construction)** (BE-0468 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/audit_repository_tenant_isolation_test.go for audit_events). Subspecies of the BE-0466 (dokploy_refs) tenant-isolation pattern with five load-bearing deltas that collapse the surface back to the narrowest viable cross-tenant shape: (a) the GetByID / GetByXxxTarget / Delete cross-tenant probes from BE-0466 are STRUCTURALLY ABSENT (no such methods exist on the repository), so the file header MUST document this as load-bearing so a future reader does not mistake the absence for a coverage gap. The BE-0466 9-probe shape collapses to a 7-probe shape: Append-bystander-byte-identity / Append-bystander-row-count-invariance / Append-cross-tenant-id-collision-Conflict-with-orgA-byte-identity-and-no-foreign-column-echo / ListByOrganization-cross-tenant-returns-only-own-rows-byte-identical + orgA-COUNT-invariance / ListByOrganization-above-cap-limit-clamping-is-tenant-scoped / ListByOrganization-unknown-tenant-empty-and-preserves-real-tenants-COUNT / organizations DELETE cascade is tenant-scoped (3-row orgB pool spanning the decision/metadata spread, plus post-cascade ListByOrganization probe). (b) The cross-tenant id-collision Conflict probe is the load-bearing audit-events-specific contribution: orgA writes a row carrying distinctive business columns (alpha_distinctive_* strings, a unique IP, a unique user-agent, a unique metadata key/value pair) and orgB attempts an Append with the SAME id under its own tenant; the PRIMARY KEY (id) global UNIQUE makes this a Conflict regardless of organization_id, AND the Conflict payload must echo ONLY the supplied id (which orgB has) and never any foreign column from orgA's row (action, resource_kind, resource_id, reason, request_id, correlation_id, ip_address, user_agent, or metadata values). A regression that widened the conflict resolution by organization_id (e.g. an ON CONFLICT (organization_id, id) DO UPDATE clause) would silently allow orgB to mint a row carrying orgA's id under its own tenant — the worst-case audit-log correlation-corruption shape. The not-foreign-substring assertions (containsValue against each distinctive value) are the second-line defence behind the apierr.Conflict code probe. (c) The above-cap-limit-clamping-is-tenant-scoped probe is the load-bearing read-side contribution: a regression that dropped the organization_id predicate from ListByOrganization but kept the LIMIT clause would surface here as a returned-row count well above orgB's seeded count (min(alphaSeed + bravoSeed, auditEventListMaxLimit) instead of just bravoSeed). The test seeds orgA's pool ABOVE the cap (auditEventListMaxLimit + 5 = 205 rows via raw-SQL INSERT — a 205-row Append-loop through the store would multiply the transaction setup cost too much for a single probe) and orgB's pool at a small fixed count (4); orgB's ListByOrganization with an above-cap limit request (1000) must resolve to exactly 4 rows, NOT the 200 cap. (d) assertAuditRowByteIdentical is structurally distinct from the application-level assertAuditEventByteIdentical in audit_test.go: the AuditEvent struct comparator would silently round-trip a metadata jsonb byte drift (the marshalled bytes differ but the unmarshalled map equals) or a sub-second occurred_at rewrite (the application struct's time.Time has Go-native nanosecond precision but the database column's microsecond precision can mask a rewrite within the same microsecond). The raw-row helper compares the persisted bytes exactly as the database stores them — the second-line defence the BE-0468 acceptance criteria require. (e) The unknown-tenant-empty-and-preserves-real-tenants-COUNT probe is the cross-tenant variant of the BE-0467 List-unknown-org-returns-empty: the invariants file seeds a single tenant and asserts the empty slice; the BE-0468 file seeds TWO real tenants and asserts BOTH (i) the empty slice for the phantom org-id AND (ii) BOTH real tenants' per-org COUNT(*) is unchanged after the read. A regression that silently auto-vacuumed orphan rows or counted rows owned by ANY tenant into a global cache would surface here. New helpers introduced (per-file Audit-suffix already established by BE-0467 to avoid store_test collisions): assertAuditRowByteIdentical (raw-row byte-level snapshot comparator), twoTenantAuditFixture + seedTwoTenantAuditFixture (two-organization fixture, NARROWER than the BE-0466 dokploy_refs equivalent because audit_events has no project/environment/service parent chain), itoa (tiny local int-to-string converter for the raw-INSERT seed loop in the above-cap-limit-clamping test — keeps the import graph free of strconv at this site). REUSED verbatim from sibling audit_test.go: auditEventFixture, appendAudit; from audit_repository_invariants_test.go: rawAuditRow, loadAuditRowByID, countAuditRowsForOrg, mintAuditID; from schema_test.go: seedOrg; from store_test.go: newStore; from organization_variable_tenant_isolation_test.go: containsValue. NO migration added. Forward-applicable to any future tenant-isolation story whose table has the (Append, ListByOrganization) narrow surface and a global PRIMARY KEY: a future security_events / system_audit / webhook_events table would inherit the same template, including the cross-tenant id-collision Conflict + foreign-column-no-echo invariant and the above-cap-limit-clamping-is-tenant-scoped invariant.
ralph/progress.txt:380:- Repository CRUD-and-invariants tests for a **TABLE WHOSE SCHEMA ALREADY EXISTS FROM AN EARLY MIGRATION BUT WHOSE TYPED REPOSITORY IS THE FIRST STORY-LEVEL CALLER** -- i.e. the table landed in an ancient migration (dokploy_refs in 0002_tenant_hierarchy) and has been exercised only by raw-SQL schema_test.go tests; the CRUD-and-invariants PRD story creates BOTH the *_repository.go file AND the *_repository_invariants_test.go file from scratch (BE-0465 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/dokploy_ref.go + internal/controlplane/store/dokploy_ref_repository_invariants_test.go). Five load-bearing differences from a first-story-for-table CRUD vertical (BE-0457 deployments, BE-0463 job_attempts, etc.): (a) NO migration is added because the table already exists -- grep the schema_test.go assertions BEFORE writing to learn the column shape and the CHECK / UNIQUE / FK invariants the raw-SQL tests already pin; (b) the typed repository MUST NOT add columns the table lacks even if a comment in another migration suggests otherwise -- migration 0011_optimistic_versions's preamble claims to cover dokploy_refs 'for parity' but its actual ALTER statements only touch organizations / projects / environments / services, so the DokployRef struct MUST NOT carry a Version field and the repository MUST NOT include version in dokployRefColumns or scan it (the single fastest way to discover this kind of drift is the error-chain unwrap debug pattern: write a one-shot test that prints %T:%s for each `cur.(unwrapper).Unwrap()` layer, which surfaces the underlying *pgconn.PgError with the SQLSTATE 42703 'column X does not exist' message in one round trip); (c) if the table has NO customer-mutable column the typed repository surface is INSERT + GET + LIST + DELETE only -- no Update method, no optimistic-concurrency If-Match path -- and the file header MUST document this as load-bearing so a future reader does not mistake the absence for a coverage gap (the 'optimistic versioning where applicable' acceptance-criteria phrase narrows to 'not applicable here'); (d) when the table has a bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY (the FIRST and ONLY int64 PK in the store package), the typed struct's ID field is int64 (not string), the INSERT statement OMITS id from the column list entirely (any value supplied -- even NULL or DEFAULT -- trips SQLSTATE 428C9 'cannot insert a non-DEFAULT value into column "id"'), and the typed error payload renders the int64 id via a private package-local helper rather than importing strconv -- keeping the store package's 'no strconv' invariant intact. A test InsertIgnoresCallerSuppliedID is the regression backstop (a caller that smuggles ID: 999_999_999 must still see the database-minted id in the returned struct); (e) when the table has TWO UNIQUE constraints, one GLOBAL across tenants (e.g. (dokploy_resource, dokploy_id) on dokploy_refs -- a Dokploy object cannot be claimed twice across tenants) and one PER-TENANT (e.g. (organization_id, yalla_id, dokploy_resource, dokploy_id)), BOTH must be exercised explicitly by the invariants tests -- the global-UNIQUE test must seed orgA's row then try to insert orgB's row with the SAME (dokploy_resource, dokploy_id) and assert countRowsForOrg(orgB) == 0 after the rejection. The 19-test shape this file lands: Insert-row-shape (raw-SQL byte-equal), Insert-ignores-caller-supplied-id, four typed-Internal rejections (nil-tx + blank-closed-set-args), four CHECK/FK-violation Conflicts (unknown-yalla_kind, unknown-dokploy_resource, blank-yalla_id, blank-dokploy_id, unknown-organization_id), both UNIQUE violations (global + per-tenant), positive-direction 'multiple domains for same service all persist' (pins the per-tenant UNIQUE includes dokploy_id), Insert-rollback, Get-happy + Get-unknown, GetByDokployTarget-happy + unknown + blank-resource-Internal, ListByYallaResource-ordered + empty + blank-kind-Internal, ListByOrganization-ordered + empty, Delete-happy + unknown + nil-tx, organization-delete-cascade, and a raw-SQL set_updated_at trigger pin (no bump_version trigger exists despite migration 0011's preamble). New helpers introduced (per-file DokployRef-suffix to avoid store_test collisions): rawDokployRefRow, loadDokployRefRowByID, countDokployRefRowsForOrg, mintDokployID, runInsertDokployRefOrFail, dokployRefTxRollbackSentinel. Reused verbatim: seedOrg/seedProject/seedEnvironment/seedService (schema_test.go), newStore (store_test.go). The byte-identical-bystander tenant-isolation gap-fill is the paired BE-0466 story. Forward-applicable to any future first-typed-repository story whose underlying table landed in an early migration and has been carried by raw-SQL schema tests since (a future idempotency_keys repository would inherit the same template, though without the bigint identity PK twist).
ralph/progress.txt:381:- Repository tenant-isolation tests for an APPEND-ONLY TABLE whose *_repository.go + invariants tests already exist (BE-0464 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/job_attempt_repository_tenant_isolation_test.go for job_attempts). Subspecies of the BE-0462 pattern with three load-bearing deltas: (a) NO updated_at column AND a BEFORE UPDATE trigger that rejects every update, so the trigger-managed-anchor invariant used in BE-0458/BE-0462's byte-identity bystander helper does NOT apply -- assertXxxByteIdentical compares created_at as the sole timestamp anchor and every business column individually (the bystander proof now probes the trigger AND the predicate, not the trigger-managed updated_at refresh). (b) Repository surface is narrower: ONE write surface (Append, *Tx-bound), TWO read surfaces (GetByID per-id, ListByParent per-parent-and-org), NO Update/Delete/Transition. So the test set drops from BE-0462's 8 to 7: no cross-tenant-Transition test exists -- the "foreign-tenant write through a state-machine surface silently UPDATEd a peer row" probe has no analogue. (c) The invariants file already exports a per-(org, parent) counter (countXxxRowsForParent) but NOT a per-org counter -- the tenant-isolation file MUST add countXxxRowsForOrg because the org-cascade test needs to probe "did orgB's TOTAL count change across all parents?" across multiple parent rows, not just per-parent. Forward-applicable to any future append-only-attempt/event table whose schema follows the (composite-FK-to-parent + BEFORE-UPDATE-rejection-trigger + no-updated_at + per-tenant-UNIQUE) shape (e.g. future audit-log tables, future immutable timeline tables). Tests pinned: Append(orgA) bystander byte-identity across orgB peers spanning every status the closed-set carries / per-tenant row-count invariance / same-(parent_id, sequence_number)-in-two-tenants positive-direction pair with per-tenant ListByParent read-side proof / GetByID cross-tenant NotFound + no-leak substring check on every non-id business column carrying caller-controllable content (worker_id, error_summary, error_code, request_id, correlation_id, parent_id) / ListByParent cross-tenant empty + per-tenant COUNT(*) invariance for BOTH tenants / parent-row delete cascade tenant-scoped / parent-organization delete cascade tenant-scoped. NEW helpers all carry the per-file Xxx suffix (BE-0464: assertJobAttemptByteIdentical, twoTenantJobAttemptFixture, seedTwoTenantJobAttemptFixture, countJobAttemptRowsForOrg, firstAttemptID); REUSED verbatim from invariants file: rawXxxRow, loadXxxRowByID, countXxxRowsForParent, mintXxxID, runAppendXxxOrFail, seedXxx; from sibling tenant-isolation: seedOrg/seedProject/seedQueuedJob/containsValue. NO migration added.
ralph/progress.txt:382:- Repository tenant-isolation tests for a **TABLE WHOSE *_repository.go, PRIMARY *_test.go AND *_repository_invariants_test.go ALREADY EXIST FROM A PRIOR (NON-PRD) SUBSYSTEM STORY + BE-046x INVARIANTS STORY** -- i.e. the tenant-isolation PRD story follows the same gap-fill pattern as the invariants story did (BE-0462 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/job_repository_tenant_isolation_test.go for provisioning_jobs). Five load-bearing differences from a first-story-for-table tenant-isolation vertical (BE-0458 deployments, BE-0456 quota_reservations, etc.): (a) NO migration is added -- the table was already exercised by both the subsystem story AND the BE-046x invariants story; (b) the rawXxxRow/loadXxxRowByID/countXxxRowsForOrg/mintXxxID/xxxTxRollbackSentinel helpers ALREADY EXIST in the invariants_test file in the SAME package store_test -- grep BEFORE writing and REUSE verbatim; (c) the lightweight cross-tenant smoke tests in the subsystem _test.go are kept (do NOT delete them) and this file is a GAP-FILL against the canonical *_tenant_isolation_test.go template (deployment_tenant_isolation_test.go is the closest template), adding the byte-identity bystander proofs / no-leak substring checks / per-tenant COUNT(*) invariance / cascade-tenant-scope proofs the smoke tests deliberately did not pin; (d) helpers introduced here MUST carry the per-file suffix (`Job`, `Dep`, `QR`, ...) and ARE distinct from the rawXxxRow/loadXxxRowByID family already exported by the invariants file (those are in the same package); the new ones to add are timePtrEqualXxx, fmtTimePtrXxx, strPtrEqualXxx, fmtStrPtrXxx (only if the table has nullable *string columns), assertXxxByteIdentical, twoTenantXxxFixture+seedTwoTenantXxxFixture, plus one or two opinionated `seedYyyXxx` wrappers (e.g. seedQueuedJob/seedRunningJob); (e) when the table has NO `version` column (provisioning_jobs is the canonical example, vs deployments/projects/services which DO have one), the `assertXxxByteIdentical` MUST NOT compare a version column and the file header MUST call out `updated_at` as the SOLE trigger-managed drift anchor (the BEFORE UPDATE set_updated_at trigger is the load-bearing anchor; a missing organization_id predicate would surface as updated_at drift even when every other column happened to look right). Mandatory test shape (the 8 tests this file lands, adapted from BE-0458): Insert(orgA)DoesNotTouchOrgB (byte-identity over queued + running-with-live-lease + idempotency-collide bystanders), Insert(orgA)DoesNotChangeOrgBRowCount, InsertSameIdempotencyKeyInTwoTenantsBothPersist (positive direction + per-tenant FindByIdempotencyKey round-trip), GetCrossTenantReturnsNotFoundAndDoesNotEcho (typed apierr.NotFound + substring no-leak on idempotency_key/request_id/correlation_id/project_id), FindByIdempotencyKeyCrossTenantDoesNotLeak (returns (zero, false, nil) + zero-value field-by-field no-leak), <StateMutation>CrossTenantLeavesOrgABystandersByteIdentical (the table's single mutating surface attempted by orgB on orgA's id -- Cancel for deployments, Transition(..., cancelled, mut) for jobs -- MUST surface as apierr.NotFound NOT Conflict, because the FOR UPDATE SELECT under (organization_id, id) returns ErrNoRows BEFORE the state-machine validator runs; bystanders include the named target + queued sibling + running sibling carrying a live lease), ListByOrganizationCrossTenantReturnsEmptyAndDoesNotLeakTotal (empty slice + side-effect-free read + per-tenant COUNT(*) invariance), OrganizationDeleteCascadeIsTenantScoped (orgA deletion cascades only to orgA's rows; orgB rows survive byte-identical -- include a row carrying a live lease to anchor lease_owner/lease_deadline/started_at). Always document in the file header what the file DELIBERATELY DELEGATES (composite-FK cross-tenant-parent path pinned by invariants story, closed-set / lifecycle CHECKs / state-machine matrix / lease bookkeeping / claimer pinned by subsystem story, redactor + payload marshalling pinned by internal_test) so a future reader does not mistake the omission for a coverage gap. When the table's worker-side claimer is INTENTIONALLY NOT tenant-scoped (e.g. ClaimNext SELECT ... FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED across all tenants), call it out explicitly in the header rationale. Forward-applicable to any future tenant-isolation story whose table follows the (subsystem-tests + invariants-tests + table-has-no-version-column) shape (e.g. future job_attempts / dokploy_refs if their repos land before their PRD stories).
ralph/progress.txt:383:- Repository CRUD-and-invariants tests for a **TABLE WHOSE *_repository.go AND PRIMARY *_test.go ALREADY EXIST FROM A PRIOR (NON-PRD) SUBSYSTEM STORY** — i.e. the CRUD-and-invariants PRD story is NOT the first story for the table (BE-0461 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/job_repository_invariants_test.go for provisioning_jobs). Structurally distinct from a "first-story-for-table" CRUD-and-invariants vertical (BE-0457 deployments, BE-0455 quota_reservations, etc.) in three load-bearing ways: (a) NO migration is added — the table landed in an earlier migration (0008_provisioning_jobs.up.sql for provisioning_jobs) and is already exercised by worker-subsystem tests; (b) NO existing helpers may be re-introduced — grep for `jobFixture / insertJob / transitionJob` (or the analogous resource fixtures) BEFORE writing and reuse them verbatim; collisions inside `package store_test` are compile errors; (c) the new file is a GAP-FILL pass against the canonical *_repository_invariants_test.go pattern (deployment_repository_invariants_test.go is the closest template), NOT a reimplementation. Mandatory gap-fill checklist (the 7-test shape this file lands): InsertMintsRowShape (byte-equal raw-SQL re-load vs Insert's RETURNING projection across every column, INCLUDING nullable resource-target legs which must round-trip as SQL NULL not empty string), InsertDuplicateIDReturnsConflict (PRIMARY KEY collision with a DIFFERENT idempotency key — distinct from the UNIQUE (organization_id, idempotency_key) violation already pinned), InsertRollbackPersistsNoRow (sentinel-error rollback + countXxxRowsForOrg == 0 after), GetUnknownReturnsNotFound (same-tenant unknown id, NOT the cross-tenant variant — pgx.ErrNoRows must map to typed apierr.NotFound), ListByOrganizationReturnsReverseChronological (explicit sleeps + 3 rows + assert ordered created_at DESC, id DESC), ListByOrganizationEmptyReturnsEmptySlice (pin len == 0 rather than nil-vs-empty because some repos legitimately return either), ListByOrganizationClampsNonPositiveLimit (probe BOTH limit=0 AND limit=-1 — if the implementation used the caller's value as a literal LIMIT, both would return 0 rows). New helpers introduced (unique to this file, with a per-file `Job` suffix to avoid the package-shared `store_test` collisions): rawJobRow, loadJobRowByID, countJobRowsForOrg, mintJobID, jobTxRollbackSentinel. Reused verbatim: jobFixture, insertJob, transitionJob (from job_test.go), seedOrg/seedProject (schema_test.go), newStore (store_test.go). The file header MUST document what it deliberately does NOT re-cover (validation guards / nil-Tx / state-machine paths / lease-claim / cross-tenant reads / closed-set status / redactor / payload marshalling are pinned by job_test.go and job_internal_test.go) so a future reader does not mistake the absence for a coverage gap. Special-case: the provisioning_jobs row body has THREE concurrent CHECK constraints (finished_consistent, lease_consistent, attempts_bounded) but Insert's coercion of Status='queued' / Attempts=0 / no lease columns makes them UNREACHABLE from public Insert input — they are Transition-time invariants, already pinned by job_test.go's Transition* suite; the invariants file does NOT try to drive them via Insert. Forward-applicable to any future "Repository: <existing-table> CRUD and invariants" story whose table already has worker-subsystem tests (e.g. future audit_events / job_attempts / dokploy_refs / idempotency_keys if their repos land before their PRD CRUD stories).
ralph/progress.txt:384:- Repository tenant-isolation tests for an **APPEND-ONLY CHILD table whose ONLY public mutation surface is Append() inside a *Tx, anchored by a composite FK (organization_id, deployment_id) to a tenant-scoped parent table with ON DELETE CASCADE, whose row body has NO version column AND NO updated_at column AND NO Update/Delete surface (BEFORE UPDATE trigger rejects every UPDATE at the SQL level), AND whose tenant teardown is reachable only TRANSITIVELY via parent-deployment-cascade OR parent-organization-cascade** (BE-0460 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/deployment_event_tenant_isolation_test.go). Structurally distinct from BE-0458 (deployments — lifecycle-bearing table with version + updated_at trigger pair and a Cancel state-transition surface) in five load-bearing ways: (a) there is NO bump_version trigger AND NO set_updated_at trigger to anchor a cross-row UPDATE detection, because the BEFORE UPDATE trigger rejects every UPDATE outright; the bystander byte-identity assertion is therefore really proving Append's INSERT statement never even ATTEMPTS a write outside the supplied (organization_id, deployment_id) tuple. (b) The cross-tenant guard test must seed a SYMMETRIC PAIR of forged Append attempts — (orgA, bravoDep) AND (orgB, alphaDep) — and probe deployment_events by the forged ids to assert count = 0 for BOTH; a single direction would miss a regression that derived organization_id from the deployment row at write time, AND a missing forged-id-was-never-persisted probe would miss a buggy retry loop that swallowed the FK error after a phantom INSERT. (c) The cascade variants are TRANSITIVE and must be tested at BOTH depths separately — parent-deployment delete cascade (composite FK ON DELETE CASCADE on deployments(organization_id, id)) AND parent-organization delete cascade (the transitive chain organizations -> deployments -> deployment_events) — because each FK leg's organization_id scoping must be proven independently; a regression that dropped the organization_id leg of either FK would surface as either a bravo row vanishing or its byte content drifting. (d) The "soft-deleted rows where applicable" acceptance-criteria phrase narrows to "where applicable": deployment_events has no soft-delete column and no Delete repository surface, so cascade is the analogous proof and is the substitute test surface — the file header MUST document this delegation explicitly so a future reader does not mistake the absence for a coverage gap. (e) The metadata column is jsonb stored as []byte; bystander byte-identity uses bytes.Equal not json equivalence (a regression that stripped or reordered keys would defeat json equivalence but survive bytes.Equal — and the test wants to catch ANY rewrite, semantic or representational). The 8-test shape (Append-bystander-byte-identity across queued+running+progress peers / Append-row-count-invariant / Append-foreign-parent-deployment-rejected-BOTH-directions-with-forged-id-not-persisted+bystander-byte-identity / GetByID-cross-tenant-NotFound-with-no-leak (message/metadata key/value/request_id/correlation_id/event_type/deployment_id) / ListByDeployment-cross-tenant-empty-and-counts-unchanged across per-org AND per-deployment probes / ListByDeployment-foreign-deployment-id-of-SAME-tenant-returns-empty (composite (organization_id, deployment_id) probe distinct from "foreign org+foreign deployment") / parent-deployment-delete-cascade-is-tenant-scoped across queued+running+progress orgB peers / parent-organization-delete-cascade-is-tenant-scoped) leaves BE-0459's invariants surface (CRUD/append-only-trigger/closed-set-event_type/composite-FK/duplicate-id/nil-tx/rollback/metadata-redaction/caller-supplied-occurred_at/cascade-on-deployment) untouched. Helpers reused from BE-0459: rawDeploymentEventRow, loadDeploymentEventRowByID, countDeploymentEventRowsForDeployment, mintDeploymentEventID, runAppendDeploymentEventOrFail, seedDeploymentEvent; from BE-0458: seedTwoTenantDeploymentFixture, twoTenantDeploymentFixture type; from BE-0457: seedQueuedDeployment; from store_test: seedOrg/seedProject/seedEnvironment/seedService, newStore, containsValue. New helpers introduced (unique to this file): twoTenantDeploymentEventFixture + seedTwoTenantDeploymentEventFixture (the two-tenant boilerplate that ALSO seeds one parent deployment per tenant, because events need a parent deployment as the FK target — a pure two-tenant org/proj/env/svc fixture is insufficient for events), assertDeploymentEventByteIdentical (anchored on every column INCLUDING metadata []byte via bytes.Equal; NO version/updated_at columns to anchor cross-row UPDATE detection), countDeploymentEventRowsForOrg (per-tenant aggregate variant of countDeploymentEventRowsForDeployment — the cascade tests need both, per-tenant is the cascade-scoped probe, per-deployment is the Append-scoped probe). Forward-applicable to any tenant-isolation story whose table is an append-only child anchored by a composite FK to a tenant-scoped parent with cascade (e.g. future audit_events / job_events / log lines stories).
ralph/progress.txt:385:- Repository tenant-isolation tests for a **TENANT-SCOPED LIFECYCLE-BEARING table whose Insert is caller-id, whose state-transition surface is a single Cancel() with optimistic-concurrency disambiguation, whose row body carries a lifecycle CHECK and a bump_version + set_updated_at trigger pair, AND whose global PRIMARY KEY (id) makes deployment ids strictly per-tenant-distinct even though every OTHER per-row anchor (UNIQUE (organization_id, idempotency_key), source_ref, source/status taxonomies) is per-tenant** (BE-0458 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/deployment_tenant_isolation_test.go). Structurally distinct from BE-0456 (quota_reservations — INSERT-only mutation surface, no Cancel, the row body has nullable string columns) in five load-bearing ways: (a) the bystander byte-identity assertion is anchored on TWO trigger-managed columns simultaneously — set_updated_at refreshes updated_at on any matched UPDATE AND the bump_version trigger increments version on the same UPDATE — so a regression that swept a peer tenant's row through a missing organization_id predicate surfaces as either an updated_at drift OR a version bump, not just one. (b) The Insert positive-direction pair is "same-(idempotency_key, source_ref) in two tenants both persist" instead of "same-resource in two tenants both persist", because the only per-tenant UNIQUE on this table is (organization_id, idempotency_key); the test must also assert FindByIdempotencyKey resolves the SAME key to the per-tenant row never cross-tenant — this is the read-side proof of the positive-direction shape. (c) The cross-tenant Cancel probe MUST seed BOTH a target row and an UNRELATED sibling for the same orgA, then byte-identity-assert BOTH after orgB's Cancel — without the sibling, a regression that widened the predicate by status alone (status IN ('queued','running')) would touch every queued row across both tenants and the target-only assertion would miss it. (d) The not-found payload for cross-tenant GetByID must name ONLY the deployment_id the caller supplied — explicit no-leak substring assertions against the foreign source_ref / idempotency_key / service_id pin this; the apierr.NotFound("deployment", deploymentID) call site is the chokepoint. (e) The acceptance criterion "List queries return stable pagination without leaking total counts from other tenants" requires asserting BOTH the empty slice AND that the per-tenant COUNT(*) for the foreign tenant is unchanged after the read — the read must not leak into a write. The 9-test shape (Insert-bystander-byte-identity across queued+running+idem-collide peers / Insert-row-count-invariant / Insert-same-idempotency-key-in-two-tenants-both-persist with cross-FindByIdempotencyKey read-side proof / GetByID-cross-tenant-NotFound-with-no-leak / FindByIdempotencyKey-cross-tenant-returns-zero-no-leak / Cancel-cross-tenant-leaves-target+sibling-byte-identical / ListByService-cross-tenant-empty-and-counts-unchanged / ListByService-foreign-service-id-of-SAME-tenant-returns-empty (composite (organization_id, service_id) probe distinct from "foreign org+foreign svc") / organization-delete-cascade-is-tenant-scoped across queued+running+extra orgB rows) leaves BE-0457's invariants surface (CHECK/FK/duplicate/nil-tx/rollback/lifecycle disambiguation) untouched. Helpers reused from BE-0457: rawDeploymentRow, loadDeploymentRowByID, countDeploymentRowsForOrg, mintDeploymentID, runInsertDeploymentOrFail, seedQueuedDeployment, seedRunningDeployment; from store_test: seedOrg/seedProject/seedEnvironment/seedService, newStore, containsValue. New helpers introduced (unique to this file): twoTenantDeploymentFixture + seedTwoTenantDeploymentFixture (the two-tenant boilerplate is centralised because every test in this file needs both halves), assertDeploymentByteIdentical (anchored on updated_at + version simultaneously), timePtrEqualDep / fmtTimePtrDep (per-file Dep-suffix is mandatory — store_test already exposes QR, QP, and "" variants; collisions block compilation). Note that rawDeploymentRow has NO nullable string columns, so stringPtrEqualDep is deliberately not added (would be U1000 dead code). GOTCHA: when two tenants need to share a row "shape" in the cascade test, the suffixes MUST be per-tenant-unique because mintDeploymentID hashes `t.Name() + suffix` and the GLOBAL PRIMARY KEY on deployments(id) means two tenants cannot share an id even though every OTHER column is per-tenant unique — a naive same-suffix-for-both-tenants call trips mapWriteError's generic "a deployment with this idempotency key already exists" message which LOOKS like an idem-key conflict but is actually an id collision. Forward-applicable to any tenant-isolation story whose table carries a global PRIMARY KEY AND a per-tenant UNIQUE on a different column AND lifecycle triggers (e.g. future BE-0461 provisioning_jobs).
ralph/progress.txt:386:- Repository CRUD-and-invariants tests for a **TENANT-SCOPED LIFECYCLE-BEARING table whose Insert is caller-id (no per-row id minting in the repository), whose only public state-transition surface is a single Cancel() with optimistic-concurrency disambiguation, and whose row body carries a lifecycle CHECK (terminal-status <-> finished_at) plus a bump_version trigger** (BE-0457 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/deployment_repository_invariants_test.go). Structurally distinct from BE-0455 (quota_reservations — the repository mints qres_<token> ids and has no Update/Cancel surface) in three load-bearing ways: (a) the test must mint deployment ids itself (DeploymentService.Create owns domain.NewID(domain.KindDeployment); the repo does NOT mint). A test-local mintDeploymentID(t, suffix) helper using `"dep_"+t.Name()+"_"+suffix` avoids global PK collisions across parallel cases without a shared atomic counter — every *_test.go under internal/controlplane/store/ shares one package, so t.Name() is the natural disambiguator. (b) The terminal-status-on-Insert probe leverages the deployments_finished_consistent CHECK: Insert's INSERT column list omits finished_at, so any terminal Status passed in violates the CHECK by construction (status terminal AND finished_at NULL). This is the one-line proof that this surface can only mint non-terminal rows; no separate trigger test is needed. (c) Cancel exercises THREE disambiguation legs against the SAME *Tx — nil expectedVersion + terminal row -> apierr.Conflict (no current_version attached), non-nil expectedVersion + version mismatch -> apierr.ConflictStale (current_version attached, verified via apierr.CurrentVersionOf), unknown id -> apierr.NotFound. The cross-tenant Cancel probe MUST include a byte-identity assertion on the foreign-tenant row (status unchanged, finished_at still NULL, version not bumped) — without it, a regression that scoped the UPDATE by id alone and disambiguated by organization_id would silently mutate the foreign tenant's row before failing the lookup. The 31-test shape (Insert: row-shape / status-default / non-queued-status-persists / dup-id / dup-(org,idem) / blank-id / blank-requested_by / blank-idem / unknown-source / terminal-without-finished / unknown-org / cross-tenant-service / nil-tx / rollback; GetByID: persisted-row / unknown / cross-tenant; FindByIdempotencyKey: present / missing / cross-tenant; Cancel: queued / running / matching-version / stale-version-ConflictStale / terminal-row-Conflict / unknown-NotFound / cross-tenant-NotFound-with-byte-identity / nil-tx; ListByService: reverse-chronological / empty-non-nil-slice / cross-tenant-empty) leaves the BE-0458 tenant-isolation file to add the bystander byte-identical proof + organization-delete-cascade probes. Helpers reused: seedOrg/seedProject/seedEnvironment/seedService (schema_test.go), newStore (store_test.go). New helpers introduced (unique to this file): rawDeploymentRow, loadDeploymentRowByID, countDeploymentRowsForOrg, mintDeploymentID, runInsertDeploymentOrFail, seedQueuedDeployment, seedRunningDeployment, deploymentTxRollbackSentinel.
ralph/progress.txt:387:- Repository tenant-isolation tests for a **TENANT-SCOPED EVENT-LIKE LEDGER table whose ONLY public mutation surface is an unbounded INSERT (no UNIQUE on (organization_id, resource) — only PRIMARY KEY (id) and a composite-FK target UNIQUE (organization_id, id)), whose ONLY read aggregate is a SUM filtered by tenant + resource + status + expires_at predicates, and whose row body carries nullable timestamp/text columns** (BE-0456 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/quota_reservation_tenant_isolation_test.go). Structurally distinct from BE-0454 (quota_usage — partial UNIQUE (organization_id, resource) and the ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING / SELECT FOR UPDATE primitive) in three load-bearing ways: (a) the bystander byte-identity assertion must cover the nullable job_id and settled_at columns (rawQuotaReservationRow declares them *string/*time.Time; the per-file stringPtrEqualQR/timePtrEqualQR/fmtStringPtrQR/fmtTimePtrQR helpers MUST get a per-file QR suffix because store_test already exposes QP-suffixed variants from BE-0451 and a stringPtrEqual variant from BE-0440); (b) the same-resource-in-two-tenants positive-direction pair is INSERT-and-both-persist (no conflict at all — no UNIQUE constraint to defeat) instead of LockUsage-and-both-succeed; (c) a NEW cross-tenant probe is mandatory — global PRIMARY KEY collision across tenants must surface as typed apierr.Conflict (yerr.CodeConflict) AND leave the prior owner's row byte-identical AND insert no row for the requester; this guards against a regression that swapped the INSERT for an ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE (a copy-paste of quota_policies' upsert pattern). The 7-test shape (insert-bystander-byte-identity across active+settled peers / insert-row-count-invariant / same-resource-two-tenants-both-persist / global-PK-collision-cross-tenant-conflict with byte-identical prior-owner / SumActiveReservations-tenant-scoped (10-vs-300 chosen so a cross-tenant fold shows as 310) / SumActiveReservations-on-empty-tenant-returns-zero-even-when-others-have-rows / organization-delete-cascade-is-tenant-scoped) leaves the BE-0455 invariants tests (status / time / resource predicate filters, CHECK / DOMAIN / FK violations, row-shape, id-provenance, rollback, nil-tx) untouched. Helpers reused: seedOrg, insertReservationRow, insertSettledReservationRow, loadQuotaReservationRowByID, countQuotaReservationRowsForOrg, runInsertReservationOrFail, newStore. New helpers introduced (unique to this file): assertQuotaReservationByteIdentical (anchored on updated_at via the BEFORE UPDATE quota_reservations_set_updated_at trigger).
ralph/progress.txt:388:- Repository CRUD invariants test file for a **TENANT-SCOPED COUNTER table whose ONLY public mutation surface is an INSERT...ON CONFLICT (organization_id, resource) DO NOTHING + SELECT...FOR UPDATE primitive (LockUsage), with NO per-row Update/Delete, NO version column, NO service_id pointer, and a database-managed used_value mutated by an EXTERNAL code path (quota.Checker, not the repository), set_updated_at trigger fires BEFORE UPDATE only** (BE-0453 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/quota_usage_repository_invariants_test.go). Structurally distinct from BE-0451 (quota_policies — INSERT...ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE upsert) in four load-bearing ways: (a) the second-call byte-identity proof must anchor updated_at TOO — ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING is not an UPDATE and the set_updated_at trigger must not fire (a regression that switched to ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE with even a no-op SET would silently bump updated_at and a missing anchor would let that slip past); (b) the peer-row proof is a SAME-TENANT-DIFFERENT-RESOURCE check (UNIQUE (organization_id, resource) isolates rows by resource within a tenant); (c) the row-already-exists-with-non-zero-value leg has no policies analog — pre-seed via raw SQL at a sentinel value (e.g. used_value=17, id=`qu_seed_<resource>`) and prove BOTH the seeded id AND the seeded used_value survive a LockUsage call (this is the ONLY way to prove the discarded INSERT's minted prefix id does not replace the row's id — a regression to ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE SET id=EXCLUDED.id would be a catastrophic data-loss path that typed reader projections would not catch); (d) the DOMAIN-violation test casts an arbitrary string to the typed Resource (e.g. `store.QuotaResource("not-a-resource")`) to bypass Go's compile-time check and exercise the DOMAIN quota_resource CHECK at the database layer — surfacing as yerr.CodeConflict through mapWriteError. The 8-new-test shape (row-shape, second-call-byte-identity, peer-row-byte-identity, persisted-used-value, unknown-org-Conflict, unknown-resource-Conflict, tx-rollback, nil-tx-Internal) leaves the pre-existing TestQuotaRepositoryLockUsageCreatesAndReturnsCounter in quota_repository_test.go alone as the load-bearing two-call-idempotency probe. Helper symbols introduced: `lockUsageOrFail`, `loadQuotaUsageRow`, `countQuotaUsageRowsForOrg`, `rawQuotaUsageRow`, `quotaUsageTxRollbackSentinel`. Reused symbols from sibling files: `seedOrg` (schema_test.go), `seedQuotaUsage` (usage_test.go), `newStore` (store_test.go) — note `seedQuotaUsage` is the ONLY way to set used_value to a non-zero value through a test fixture because the repository surface has no equivalent (LockUsage cannot increment).
ralph/progress.txt:389:- Repository tenant-isolation tests for a **DUAL-SCOPE ledger-style table whose ONLY mutation surface is an INSERT...ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE upsert and whose row body is observable EXCLUSIVELY through typed resolvers (no per-id Get/Update/Delete, no ListByParent)** (BE-0452 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/quota_policy_tenant_isolation_test.go). quota_policies has TWO scope_kind values ('plan_default' with NULL organization_id; 'organization' with non-NULL) anchored by partial unique indexes (e.g. `quota_policies_org_resource_idx (organization_id, resource) WHERE scope_kind='organization'`) and NO version column — so the byte-identical-bystander anchor reduces to the trigger-managed updated_at ALONE. The 9-test shape diverges from BE-0440 (project_grant, ListByParent-only) and BE-0436 (api_key_scope, per-id Get + per-id mutations) in four load-bearing ways: (a) cross-tenant projection is proved through EVERY resolver leg independently — one test per resolver method (EffectiveLimit per-resource tenant-scoped; ListEffectiveLimits count isolation; ListOrganizationUsage two-CTE-leg isolation) — because each resolver has its own WHERE clause and a regression dropping any one leg leaks a different column; (b) a NEW plan-isolation leg is added: orgA on 'starter' must not see 'pro' plan defaults even though both rows live in the SAME table with NULL organization_id — the plan column is the second tenant boundary on the plan_default scope and a missing plan predicate is a distinct regression surface from a missing organization_id predicate; (c) the byte-identical-bystander proof is observed via a RAW-SQL load of the underlying quota_policies row (reusing BE-0451's loadOrganizationPolicyRow + rawQuotaPolicyRow), because the typed reader projections deliberately omit id/created_at/updated_at/scope_kind/plan — a regression that stamped correct-looking limit_value and enforcement_mode onto the peer's row would slip past every typed-resolver projection if asserted only via EffectiveLimit; (d) ListOrganizationUsage has TWO independent tenant-predicate legs (one in the policies CTE on organization_id, one in the usage CTE on organization_id) — they need INDEPENDENT proofs because either leg dropping alone leaks a different column (limit_value vs used_value), so the file has BOTH a "policies-CTE leg isolated" test (orgA has an override, orgB has overrides on different resources, no counters) AND a "usage-CTE leg isolated" test (both tenants have a quota_usage counter on the SAME resource with DISTINCT used_value). NO per-id Get => NO (Code, Hint) existence-leak projection: the cross-tenant probe surfaces as an EMPTY projection (resolver returns ok=false, or len(rows)==0), NOT a typed apierr.NotFound — the empty-slice / ok=false IS the existence-indistinguishability proof. The shared-conflict-tuple fixture for the Upsert-UPDATE bystander test MUST be built via upsertOrganizationPolicy (not seedOrgPolicy raw SQL), because using the repository to build the fixture both proves the partial unique index lets the peer row land at all AND aligns baseline.updated_at timestamps so a wrong-row UPDATE shows up as sub-second drift. The corollary "same-resource-in-two-tenants-both-succeed" test is the load-bearing positive-direction pair — without it, a regression that resolved the conflict by (resource,) alone would have prevented the bystander INSERT, and every byte-identical assertion in the UPDATE-branch test would silently pass against a non-existent row. Helper naming caveat: nullable-column helpers MUST get a per-file suffix (stringPtrEqualQP / fmtStringPtrQP) — BE-0440's project-grant file already defines a stringPtrEqual variant in store_test and same-named helpers collide at compile time. Forward-applicable to any future dual-scope ledger-style table (a hypothetical KindFeatureFlagDefault + KindFeatureFlagOverride pairing, or KindPriceTier defaults + organization overrides, would inherit this template).
ralph/progress.txt:390:- Repository tenant-isolation tests for a **multi-table resource FAMILY whose physical realization is one table per scope in the Organization -> Project -> Environment -> Service hierarchy AND whose row body carries SECRET-BEARING columns (value plaintext + sealed-secret tuple)** (BE-0450 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/{organization,project,environment,service}_variable_tenant_isolation_test.go). Mirrors the BE-0449 per-scope split of the invariants tests: when the family is hierarchical with diverging mutation surfaces (organization variant exposes per-key GetByKey + DeleteByKey + UpdateMutable on top of the bulk Upsert + DeleteByXxxExceptKeys pair; project/environment/service variants expose ONLY the bulk pair), ship one *_tenant_isolation_test.go per physical table. The project/environment/service files converge on a uniform 8-test shape: ListByX-correct-across-tenants, ListByX-cross-tenant-scope-returns-empty, ListByX-isolates-shared-key, Upsert-INSERT-bystander-byte-identical, Upsert-UPDATE-bystander-byte-identical, DeleteByXExceptKeys-clear-all-bystander, DeleteByXExceptKeys-keep-subset-bystander, Upsert-same-key-in-two-tenants-both-succeed. The organization file ADDS four per-key tests that have no analog in the other three: GetByKey-cross-tenant-returns-NotFound (with error-surface no-value-echo), DeleteByKey-on-orgA-bystander (plus second-DeleteByKey-on-now-gone-key-returns-NotFound-no-value-echo), UpdateMutable-on-orgA-bystander (Version bumps on orgA, orgB stays byte-identical), UpdateMutable-cross-tenant-key-returns-NotFound-no-value-echo-bystander-byte-identical. THE LOAD-BEARING DIVERGENCE from the BE-0448 grant tenant-isolation template is the byte-identical-bystander projection: variables MUST anchor BOTH Value AND the (SecretProvider, SecretKeyID, SecretCiphertext) tuple AND the IsSecret flag, because variables carry secret-bearing columns that grants do not — a regression that stamped orgA's payload onto orgB's row or that re-sealed orgB's row with orgA's secret bytes would otherwise slip past every identity-column check. bytes.Equal (stdlib) is the right SecretCiphertext compare helper; no custom one needed. Helper REUSE strategy across the family: organization/environment/service tenant-isolation files reuse newXVariableID / upsertXVariable / listXVariablesOrFail defined in their sibling BE-0449 invariants files (same store_test package); the project file DEFINES its own newProjectVariableID(f, org, label) / upsertProjectVariable / listProjectVariablesOrFail because project_variable_repository_invariants_test.go inlines its helpers (no shared symbols to reuse). The project helper signature takes f + org because f.Project requires an org to synthesise the id — the org/env/service helpers use a factory-counter-only or fake-parent scheme. Picking the wrong helper shape at call sites is the most common compile failure when porting tests across the family. Each file defines getXVariableFromListOrFail and assertXVariableByteIdentical with distinct names to avoid same-package collisions across store_test. The containsValue substring helper for the error-surface no-value-echo assertions is defined in the organization file (only organization-scope per-key methods can RETURN errors with message/hint text); env/service/project paths surface their failures via FK Conflict or PK Conflict, both of which are invariants-file legs (not tenant-isolation legs). The "Upsert-same-key-in-two-tenants-both-succeed" test is structurally necessary as the positive-direction pair for the bystander byte-identical proofs — without it, a regression that resolved the conflict on (scope_id, key) alone would still pass every bystander check (the bystander INSERTs first, the peer Upsert then hits PK uniqueness or no conflict at all). OrganizationVariableRepository.ListByOrganization returns a NIL slice on no-rows (unlike its project/environment/service siblings which use make([]T, 0)) — the tenant-isolation contract is "len == 0", not "non-nil"; the org-scope test documents this divergence in a comment and DOES NOT assert non-nil. Forward-applicable to any future multi-table secret-bearing family at this hierarchy.
ralph/progress.txt:391:- Repository CRUD invariants for a **multi-table resource FAMILY whose physical realization is one table per scope in the Organization -> Project -> Environment -> Service hierarchy, sharing identical CHECK / FK / CASCADE / bump_version / set_updated_at invariants but with per-scope mutation surfaces** (BE-0449 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/{organization,project,environment,service}_variable_repository_invariants_test.go). A PRD note that reads "Database table: <name>" can refer to such a family (variables = 4 scope tables, quota_* = 3 ledger tables) — when the family is hierarchical, ship one *_repository_invariants_test.go per physical table rather than collapsing into a single mega-file, because per-scope mutation surfaces diverge (organization_variables uniquely exposes UpdateMutable + DeleteByKey on top of the bulk-replace surface; project / environment / service variables expose only the bulk Upsert + DeleteByXxxExceptKeys pair). Each file in the family extends the BE-0447 grant-table invariants template with three uniform substitutions: (a) the Upsert payload struct is the scope's XxxVariableUpsert (Value, IsSecret, SecretProvider, SecretKeyID, SecretCiphertext) instead of role + principal_kind; (b) a brand-new secret_columns_consistent CHECK-violation test is added (IsSecret=true with non-empty Value triggers the secret branch's `value=''` clause and surfaces as typed apierr.Conflict — exhaustively probing the OTHER drift directions is needless because the repository's nullableSecretColumns helper makes them structurally impossible from the caller side); (c) the leaf-scope variants (env / service) add a cross-tenant composite-FK test mirroring BE-0447's service-grant cross-tenant proof. Every *_test.go file under internal/controlplane/store/ shares the same store_test package, so the per-scope helpers MUST get distinct names — newOrganizationVariableID / newEnvironmentVariableID / newServiceVariableID, upsertOrganizationVariable / upsertEnvironmentVariable / upsertServiceVariable, listOrganizationVariablesOrFail / listEnvironmentVariablesOrFail / listServiceVariablesOrFail, and organizationVariableTxRollbackSentinel / projectVariableTxRollbackSentinel / environmentVariableTxRollbackSentinel / serviceVariableTxRollbackSentinel. The project-variables file is the smallest because project_variable_test.go already covers Upsert INSERT/UPDATE happy paths, cross-tenant rejection, the bulk delete surface, and the nil-Tx guards — its invariants file pins ONLY the row-shape and lifecycle invariants the existing tests do not anchor (version=1 default, byte-equal created_at == updated_at, ON CONFLICT id+created_at preservation, PK reuse -> Conflict, secret CHECK violation, ON DELETE CASCADE, Upsert tx-rollback). Forward-applicable to any future multi-table family at this hierarchy (a hypothetical KindOrganizationSetting / KindProjectSetting / KindEnvironmentSetting / KindServiceSetting feature would inherit this 4-file template structure).
ralph/progress.txt:392:- Repository CRUD invariants test file for a **tenant-scoped LEAF-grant table whose ONLY mutation surface is an INSERT...ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE upsert PLUS a bulk DELETE-by-exclusion, with a database-managed version column, NO nullable scope pointer below the parent FK, and a composite FK to a leaf-entity parent (organization_id, service_id) -> services** (BE-0447 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/service_grant_repository_invariants_test.go). Structural reduction of the BE-0443 environment_grant_repository_invariants_test.go template: every leg that exercised a nullable *string scope pointer collapses or drops out — the round-trip pointer test is replaced by a service-account-principal-kind test (the closed-set CHECK leg still needs proving), the NULLS-FIRST ordering assertion in ListByService is gone (two-column ORDER BY principal_id, id), the empty-string-COALESCE dedup equivalence leg is gone (no COALESCE in the unique index), and the upsert/replace input struct drops the trailing *string field. The Upsert signature is six-positional `(id, organization_id, parent_id, principal_id, principal_kind, role)`; the ON CONFLICT branch keys on the three-tuple `(organization_id, parent_id, principal_id)`. The principal-kind cross-smuggling invariant MUST be preserved verbatim (re-upsert with a different caller-supplied principal_kind for the same principal_id; assert persisted kind = baseline). The duplicate-PK test must use a DIFFERENT principal_id so the ON CONFLICT branch does NOT match and the PRIMARY KEY (id) uniqueness violation is exercised instead of the upsert path. The ON DELETE CASCADE proof must delete the parent via raw SQL (the ServiceRepository.ScheduleDeletion is a SOFT delete and would NOT trigger CASCADE). The Upsert rollback proof reduces to ListByService-only because no per-id Get exists; ListByService is the only observation surface for a grant row. NEW Kind constant in domain/id.go MUST be added to BOTH the const block AND the canonical `kinds` map at the bottom — Valid() and the ID parser read from the map, not the const list, so a missing map entry silently breaks NewID(KindFoo) with ErrUnknownKind. For brand-new tables, services already exposes UNIQUE (organization_id, id) from migration 0002 so NO companion ALTER TABLE is needed on the parent (UNLIKE BE-0435 api_keys parent). Forward-applicable to any future grant table whose parent IS the leaf of the Organization -> Project -> Environment -> Service hierarchy or whose parent is otherwise structurally leaf-like (a hypothetical KindDeploymentGrant / KindJobGrant would inherit this template).
ralph/progress.txt:393:- Repository CRUD invariants test file for a **tenant-scoped child table whose ONLY mutation surface is an INSERT...ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE upsert PLUS a bulk DELETE-by-exclusion (no per-id Get, no per-id Delete, no traditional UPDATE), with a database-managed version column, optional nullable scoping pointers, and a composite FK to a parent (organization_id, project_id) -> projects** (BE-0439 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/project_grant_repository_invariants_test.go). Two structural divergences from BE-0435 (api_key_scope_repository_invariants_test.go) are load-bearing: (1) the Upsert ON CONFLICT branch must be tested with a DIFFERENT caller-supplied id AND a DIFFERENT caller-supplied principal_kind to prove BOTH the caller-id-is-ignored guarantee AND the cross-kind-smuggling-impossible guarantee — the principal_kind column is NOT part of the scope tuple, so an existing row stays at its current principal_kind even when the caller passes a different kind for the same principal_id; a single Role-only update test would silently pass while a regression that promoted principal_kind to a SET clause still went undetected. (2) The Upsert rollback proof reduces to ListByProject-only because the repository exposes no per-id Get for grants — ListByProject is the ONLY observation surface for a grant row, so it is the load-bearing leak channel a partial commit would surface through, and an absent Get assertion is a deliberate file boundary, not a gap. Insert duplicate-id test must use a DIFFERENT principal_id so the ON CONFLICT (org, proj, principal, env, svc) branch does NOT match and the PRIMARY KEY (id) uniqueness violation is exercised — otherwise the upsert path silently swallows the duplicate id. DeleteByProjectExceptIDs has TWO codepaths in the repository (len(keepIDs)==0 -> unconditional DELETE; len>0 -> DELETE WHERE id NOT IN (ANY $3::text[])), so BOTH must be exercised independently — the empty-keep test must additionally use a fresh seed after the nil-keep clear AND assert the empty-slice ([]string{}) form is identical to nil at the wire (the repository documents nil and empty as identical, so a regression that diverged them would surface here). ON DELETE CASCADE from projects is the project-grant analogue of api_key_scope's ON DELETE CASCADE from api_keys: use raw `DELETE FROM projects WHERE id=$1` (NOT the repository's ScheduleDeletion, which is a SOFT delete and would NOT trigger CASCADE) and observe via ListByProject. Helper `newProjectGrantID(f, label)` synthesises a `pgrnt_<token_n>` id by rewriting f.Project's `prj_<token_n>` prefix — the testutil.Factory only stamps known domain prefixes and there is no f.ProjectGrant, so prefix-rewriting is the cleanest way to mint factory-unique ids without reaching into domain.NewID(domain.KindProjectGrant). The sibling project_grant_test.go's `seedProjectGrant` raw-SQL helper is kept intentionally separate: that file's tests exercise the read path against rows the schema (not the repository) inserted, so a regression that broke INSERT would still let the read tests pass — by contrast THIS file's `upsertProjectGrant` exercises the repository's INSERT path, so the two seed surfaces are complementary, not duplicates.
ralph/progress.txt:394:- Repository CRUD invariants test file for a **tenant-scoped table whose mutation surface spans FOUR distinct UPDATE paths (Update / UpdateDisplayName / ScheduleDeletion / Restore) PLUS Insert, with a database-managed version column AND a nullable soft-delete column (deletion_scheduled_at)** (BE-0437 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/project_repository_invariants_test.go): this is the BE-0425 organization template extended with the second-UPDATE-path projection (UpdateDisplayName must run the dual-anchor AND prove the path-specific WHERE/SET isolation — the row's slug must be untouched since the SET clause is `SET display_name = $3` only) and the BE-0431 inverse-soft-delete projection (Restore must dual-anchor AND clear deletion_scheduled_at back to NULL — the trigger fires on any UPDATE so a forgotten `SET deletion_scheduled_at = NULL` clause would still pass the version/timestamp anchors, making the "deletion_scheduled_at is nil after Restore" assertion the load-bearing one). Insert rollback proof must span ALL read surfaces the rolled-back row touches: by-id Get, by-org ListByOrganization, AND CountByOrganization (the count the quota layer reads), so a future refactor that switched Insert to a SAVEPOINT or autonomous transaction would leak the row into a quota check even though Get reports NotFound. The nil-Tx typed-Internal guard only needs to close the ONE mutation path the existing tests did not cover (here UpdateDisplayName) — duplicating Insert/Update/ScheduleDeletion/Restore guards already proved elsewhere is noise. Test fixtures compose seedOrg + projectFixture(f.Project(org, label)) + insertProject helpers from project_test.go; do not re-implement them at package scope.
ralph/progress.txt:395:- Repository CRUD invariants test file for a **tenant-scoped grandchild table whose mutation surface is Insert + Update + ScheduleDeletion ONLY (NO UpdateDisplayName, NO Restore), with a database-managed version column, a CHECK-constrained closed-set Kind column, a nullable soft-delete column (deletion_scheduled_at), and a composite FK to a parent (organization_id, project_id) -> projects** (BE-0441 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/environment_repository_invariants_test.go): the BE-0437 project_repository_invariants_test.go template MINUS the UpdateDisplayName and Restore tests (the EnvironmentRepository exposes neither method — adding empty stubs that reference store.EnvironmentRepository.UpdateDisplayName / Restore would fail to compile), so the invariants matrix shrinks from six DB-backed tests to four (Insert dual-fields-anchor, Update dual-anchor, Update matching-If-Match positive, ScheduleDeletion dual-anchor) PLUS the Insert-rollback and Insert-nil-Tx pair. Update's preserved-identity assertion gains a Kind-preservation check (the SET clause is `SET slug = $3, display_name = $4` only, so kind is structurally untouched even though the column exists on this table) that the projects template did not need (projects has no kind column); ScheduleDeletion's preserved-identity assertion ALSO gains a Kind check for the same reason. The nil-Tx typed-Internal matrix-closer is Insert (NOT UpdateDisplayName as in projects) because environment_update_test.go and environment_delete_test.go already cover Update and ScheduleDeletion respectively — the matrix-closer convention is "fill the missing path", and the missing path is table-specific. New file-scope helpers `environmentFixture` and `insertEnvironment` mirror projectFixture/insertProject but environmentFixture MUST default Kind to store.EnvironmentKindStandard (the migration 0025 CHECK constraint requires "standard" or "preview" — empty string fails) — the raw-SQL seedEnvironment in schema_test.go relies on the database column default, but the repository's INSERT path supplies kind explicitly. Rollback proof scope is by-id GetByID + parent-scoped ListByProject (there is no CountByOrganization equivalent on environments — quota counts environments per-project, not per-org, and per-project counts are not yet exposed on the repository surface; do NOT extend the rollback assertion to a non-existent method). Rollback sentinel type is environmentTxRollbackSentinel — every *_repository_invariants_test.go in store_test contributes its own uniquely-named sentinel.
ralph/progress.txt:396:- Repository tenant-isolation test file for a **tenant-scoped child table with a database-managed version column, a composite FK to a parent api_keys row, and NO secret-bearing column** (BE-0436 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/api_key_scope_tenant_isolation_test.go). The BE-0430 membership_tenant_isolation_test.go shape is the closest template; four substitutions are mandatory: (a) the cross-tenant indistinguishability probe uses the SAME (Code, Hint) anchor pattern, but on a single id column rather than a composite key — both the cross-tenant scope id and an unknown scope id under the prober's tenant must render byte-identical typed NotFound; (b) the byte-identical-bystander assertion carries TWO independent trigger anchors (api_key_scopes_set_updated_at AND api_key_scopes_bump_version) instead of memberships' one (memberships_set_updated_at + role_version-only-via-UpdateRole). Both must be asserted because a regression could drift one without the other; (c) the load-bearing worst-case fixture is the shared-scope-text fixture (both tenants own a scope row with the SAME scope text on their OWN respective keys) because the (organization_id, api_key_id, scope) UNIQUE constraint is per-tenant, so the WHERE filter is the only thing keeping the rows apart at the read path — every byte-identical-bystander test uses this fixture as a result; (d) ListByAPIKey is a SECOND tenant-scoped list path (the parent api_key_id is the secondary tenant-scoping anchor) and must be tested both for cross-tenant api_key_id (returns empty non-nil slice) AND indistinguishable from an unknown api_key_id under the prober's tenant. The api_key_scopes table has NO secret-bearing column (the scope text itself is the customer-facing capability string), so the BE-0434 raw-secret_hash probe has no analogue and the byte-identical-bystander assertion alone covers every observable column. Helpers seedAPIKeyForScopes / newAPIKeyScope / insertAPIKeyScope are reused from api_key_scope_repository_invariants_test.go in the same store_test package — sibling-file test name collisions (e.g. TestAPIKeyScopeRepositoryListByAPIKeyIsTenantScoped from BE-0435) MUST be checked with `grep '^func Test'` on the sibling invariants file before adding new test names. Future tenant-scoped child tables with a version column and no secret-bearing column (variables.value when is_secret=false, future per-scope capability tables, scoped grant link tables not yet sharded out) should reuse THIS file as the template; if the row body carries a secret-bearing column add the BE-0434 raw-column probe on top.
ralph/progress.txt:397:- Repository tenant-isolation test file for a **tenant-scoped table with FOUR distinct mutation paths (Update / UpdateDisplayName / ScheduleDeletion / Restore), a nullable soft-delete column (deletion_scheduled_at), and a count surface the quota layer reads (CountByOrganization)** (BE-0438 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/project_tenant_isolation_test.go): this is the BE-0436 api_key_scope_tenant_isolation template extended with three projections api_key_scopes had no analogue for — (a) UpdateDisplayName needs its own OnOrgADoesNotTouchOrgB / CrossTenantBystanderIsByteIdentical pair because no other repo file proves the cross-tenant invariant on this surface; (b) the byte-identical-bystander helper carries DeletionScheduledAt as a fourth observable column on top of (version, updated_at, slug, display_name), so a cross-tenant ScheduleDeletion that stamped a peer nil→non-nil OR a cross-tenant Restore that cleared a peer non-nil→nil is caught even when triggers happened to look untouched (same-second match); (c) one composite SoftDeletedRowIsTenantScopedOnReadPaths test proves cross-tenant invisibility across ALL THREE read surfaces (Get / ListByOrganization / CountByOrganization) AND owner-tenant visibility across the same three in a single fixture — the projections collapse into one test because the SAME WHERE organization_id filter is what isolates the row on every read surface. Force BOTH fixtures to share slug text via direct `.Slug = "shared-slug"` overrides AFTER projectFixture(f.Project(...)), because the per-factory token in f.Project derives distinct slug text per tenant and a WHERE-on-slug-only regression would otherwise stay invisible. Two shared helpers at file scope (`getProjectOrFail`, `assertProjectByteIdentical`) mirror BE-0436's helper names — same shape, DeletionScheduledAt column added. The file's docstring MUST call out which legs are already proved elsewhere (cross-tenant NotFound code for Update / ScheduleDeletion / Restore is in project_test.go / project_delete_test.go / project_restore_test.go) and which are NEW HERE (byte-identical bystander on version + updated_at + DeletionScheduledAt, Code+Hint indistinguishability of cross-tenant vs unknown probes, per-tenant UNIQUE-shared-column coexistence, soft-delete tenant-scoping across all three read surfaces, Count tenant-scoping on the quota path) so a future reader does not look for missing tests where the proof is intentionally elsewhere.
ralph/progress.txt:398:- Repository tenant-isolation test file for a **tenant-scoped grandchild table whose ONLY read surface is a parent-scoped ListByProject (no per-id Get, no per-id Delete), with TWO mutation paths (Upsert with ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE PLUS DeleteByProjectExceptIDs whose len(keepIDs)==0 and len>0 codepaths must each be exercised), a database-managed version column, optional nullable scoping pointers (environment_id, service_id), and a composite FK to a parent (organization_id, project_id) -> projects** (BE-0440 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/project_grant_tenant_isolation_test.go). This is the BE-0436 api_key_scope_tenant_isolation template adapted for a grandchild table whose conflict target itself includes (organization_id, project_id) AND a multi-column scope tuple. Five projections api_key_scopes had no analogue for are load-bearing here: (a) the byte-identical-bystander proof is observed through ListByProject (the only read surface) — there is no per-id Get like api_key_scopes had, so a helper getGrantFromListOrFail must scan the list response for the bystander id and fatal on absence; the implementation favours correctness over efficiency because the bystander lists are 1-2 rows by construction. (b) the byte-identical-bystander assertGrantByteIdentical helper must compare TWO nullable *string pointer columns (environment_id, service_id) in addition to the scalar columns — pair-helper stringPtrEqual / fmtStringPtr emit "<nil>" vs quoted string for failing assertions so nullability and value drift surface side-by-side; api_key_scopes had no pointer columns, so this is the first tenant-isolation file with a *string comparison. (c) the load-bearing worst-case fixture is the shared-scope-tuple fixture (both tenants own a grant row with the SAME (principal_id, environment_id, service_id) tuple on their OWN projects) AND the Upsert UPDATE-branch bystander test re-upserts with a DIFFERENT role + a fresh caller-supplied id to BOTH fire the ON CONFLICT branch on orgA AND prove the caller-id is ignored — a regression that resolved the conflict by scope-tuple alone (without organization_id and project_id in the conflict target) would have UPDATEd orgB on the peer-trigger fire. (d) DeleteByProjectExceptIDs needs TWO bystander tests because the repository has two SQL codepaths (one unconditional DELETE for nil/empty keepIDs, one DELETE WHERE id NOT IN ANY for non-empty keepIDs); the len>0 test must seed orgB grants whose id is NOT in keepIDs so a regression that dropped the tenant predicate would have deleted them through the "NOT IN ANY" branch. (e) ListByProject cross-tenant projectID returns an empty non-nil slice, so the cross-tenant indistinguishability projection is len==0 + non-nil + unknown-projectID-under-own-tenant yields the SAME empty-non-nil shape (NOT the Code+Hint NotFound shape from api_key_scopes / projects, because grants are read through a list path, not a per-id Get). Helpers seedOrg / seedProject / newProjectGrantID / upsertProjectGrant / listGrantsOrFail are reused from project_grant_test.go and project_grant_repository_invariants_test.go in the same store_test package — sibling-file test name collisions (TestProjectGrantRepo* in project_grant_test.go, TestProjectGrantRepository* in project_grant_repository_invariants_test.go) MUST be checked with `grep '^func Test'` on BOTH sibling files before adding new test names; this file uses the TestProjectGrantRepository* prefix (matching the invariants file convention) with unique suffixes (...OnOrgADoesNotTouchOrgB, ...IsolatesSharedPrincipal, ...InTwoTenantsBothSucceed). The file's docstring MUST call out which legs are already proved elsewhere (cross-tenant FK Conflict, duplicate-PK Conflict, ON DELETE CASCADE, tx-rollback, nil-Tx guards in BE-0439; ListByProject ordering and basic cross-tenant empty-list property in project_grant_test.go) and which are NEW HERE (byte-identical bystander on version + updated_at + role + pointer columns across BOTH Upsert branches AND BOTH DeleteByProjectExceptIDs codepaths, indistinguishability of cross-tenant vs unknown projectID at the list path, per-tenant scope-tuple coexistence, shared-principal_id read isolation) so a future reader does not look for missing tests where the proof is intentionally elsewhere.
ralph/progress.txt:399:- Repository tenant-isolation test file for a **tenant-scoped great-grandchild table with a composite (organization_id, project_id, environment_id) FK to environments, all SIX mutation surfaces of the projects template (Insert / GetByID / ListByEnvironment / Update / ScheduleDeletion / Restore), a database-managed version column, a nullable soft-delete column (deletion_scheduled_at), AND a per-parent UNIQUE (environment_id, slug) — NOT per-organization** (BE-0446 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/service_tenant_isolation_test.go). This is the BE-0438 project_tenant_isolation template extended with TWO load-bearing projections that have no analogue in the projects/environments tenant-isolation files: (1) `Restore CrossTenant bystander byte-identical` requires TWO baselines on the peer tenant — a live peer (DeletionScheduledAt nil) and a soft-deleted peer (DeletionScheduledAt non-nil) — because a regression that mis-handled the WHERE clause on Restore could clear a peer's stamp from non-nil to nil ONLY when the peer had a stamp to clear; a single state would silently pass a regression that only mis-fired in the other direction. The OnOrgADoesNotTouchOrgB Restore test seeds orgA→ScheduleDeletion (so Restore has something to undo), reads orgB live baseline, runs Restore on orgA, asserts orgB live bystander; THEN ScheduleDeletion(orgB), re-ScheduleDeletion(orgA), reads orgB soft-deleted baseline, runs Restore(orgA) again, asserts orgB soft-deleted bystander. The cross-tenant Restore test only needs the soft-deleted-peer baseline because its hostile probe touches the bystander row at most once. (2) The composite FK key surface widens to FOUR identity columns (id, organization_id, project_id, environment_id) — every GetByIDReturnsCorrectRowAcrossTenants assertion verifies all four anchors are DISTINCT between the two tenants (not just (id, organization_id)) so a WHERE-on-id-only mistake that collapses both lookups onto the same row is caught even if both tenants happen to be on the same project_id or environment_id by fixture accident. The `assertServiceByteIdentical` helper extends `assertEnvironmentByteIdentical` with environment_id and adds a Restore-direction error message to the DeletionScheduledAt switch (`cross-tenant Restore (or ScheduleDeletion-clear) cleared a peer tenant's soft-delete stamp`). Seed strategy: use raw-SQL `seedOrg`/`seedProject`/`seedEnvironment` for parents (they return testutil.* types directly) and `insertService` (repo path) for the target services rows — avoids the `projATuple`/`envFromStore` conversion needed in BE-0442 because that file's targets WERE environments and it had to feed `store.Project` into `f.Environment`. The shared-slug fixture works because UNIQUE is (environment_id, slug) — two tenants always have distinct environment_ids so they CAN legitimately share service slug text, making the WHERE-filter the only isolation and the byte-identical-bystander projection load-bearing. The file's docstring MUST call out which legs are already proved elsewhere (cross-tenant NotFound at the typed-error level by service_test.go `TestServiceRepoGetByIDIsTenantScoped`/`TestServiceRepoListByEnvironmentIsTenantScoped`, service-layer cross-tenant by `serviceservice_update_test.go` `TestServiceServiceUpdateCrossTenantIsNotFound`, Restore tenant-scope by `service_restore_test.go` `TestServiceRepositoryRestoreIsTenantScoped`) so a future reader does not look for missing tests where the proof is intentionally elsewhere.
ralph/progress.txt:401:- New customer-data resource = full vertical when it is the FIRST story for that table (BE-0435 pattern, api_key_scopes migration 0033, store/api_key_scope.go, api_key_scope_repository_invariants_test.go): this story is allowed to land migration + repository + first-pass tests because no prior story touched the table (no migration, no repo, no port, no handler, no wiring). The migration declares the table tenant-scoped at the database via the composite FK pattern established by project_grants / environment_grants — and when the parent table never exposed UNIQUE (organization_id, id) (api_keys was such a table) the SAME migration adds the constraint to the parent so the child's composite FK has a target; this is a safe additive change because the parent's PK already makes id unique and the new UNIQUE merely pairs it with organization_id. Repository follows the membership.go / project_grant.go pattern: stateless struct, *Tx for mutations with nil-Tx -> typed apierr.Internal, Querier for reads, pgx.ErrNoRows -> apierr.NotFound, mapWriteError for constraint-violation -> apierr.Conflict, tag.RowsAffected() == 0 -> apierr.NotFound on Delete. Tests combine CRUD + row-shape invariants in one file since no prior tests exist; tenant-isolation byte-identical-bystander proof is deferred to the paired BE-0436 (tenant-isolation) story that always follows.
ralph/progress.txt:402:- Test fixture caveat for api_keys-related seeding (BE-0435 learning, internal/controlplane/store/api_key_scope_repository_invariants_test.go seedAPIKeyForScopes): testutil.Factory.APIKey stamps a per-FACTORY prefix `yk_<token[:8]>`, NOT a per-key prefix. Two keys minted from the same factory get the same Prefix value, which collides with the api_keys_prefix_key UNIQUE constraint when two are inserted in the same test. Always derive the persisted prefix as `key.Prefix + "_" + key.ID` (or similar per-row salt) — never use `key.Prefix + "_" + label` because the same label is reused across orgs in tenant-isolation fixtures and the label is not unique.
ralph/progress.txt:403:- Repository for a **global identity table** with no organization_id and no version column (BE-0427 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/user.go): follow OrganizationRepository's shape (Get-by-own-id, scanX helper, nil-Tx guard → apierr.Internal) but drop the optimistic-concurrency If-Match path entirely; add a separate GetInOrganization(organizationID, userID) that JOINs against memberships so tenant-scoped reads return NotFound (the same shape an unknown id produces) for non-members and cross-tenant requesters. Delete uses RowsAffected() == 0 → apierr.NotFound (mirrors api_keys.Revoke). Conflict mapping for citext UNIQUE columns goes through mapWriteError with a non-sensitive user-facing message.
ralph/progress.txt:405:- Tenant-isolation tests for a **global identity table** (BE-0428 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/user_tenant_isolation_test.go): byte-identical-snapshot proof for a table that has no organization_id and no version column. The BE-0426 organization template still applies, but three substitutions are mandatory: (a) drop the stale-If-Match/Update-with-mismatched-version test — there is no version column on the row, so optimistic-concurrency leak surfaces do not exist at this layer (session-token revocation lives on memberships.role_version and is covered by membership_test.go). (b) Add a CONFLICT-PATH BYTE-IDENTICAL test that snapshots BOTH the conflicting target AND the bystander — because the citext UNIQUE constraint on email fires AFTER the UPDATE row is locked and the BEFORE-UPDATE trigger has run, a regression where the trigger stamps updated_at before the constraint rolls back the write would surface on EITHER row; in BE-0426 there is no equivalent "global UNIQUE column outside the WHERE-id" surface to test. (c) Replace "list-by-parent must NotFound for cross-tenant parent ids" with "GetInOrganization-with-unknown-id and GetInOrganization-with-non-member-id produce indistinguishable NotFound shapes (same Code, same Hint)" — the membership join replaces the parent-id-WHERE filter, and existence-leak via Code/Hint divergence is the analogous failure mode. The DELETE-cascade test should snapshot BOTH userB AND userB's memberships count (asymmetric across tenants: the deleted user's memberships in EVERY tenant cascade away via memberships.user_id ON DELETE CASCADE, while bystander users in any tenant keep theirs). Document the deliberate ABSENCE of list-by-parent and soft-delete tests in the file's package-doc comment so a future reader does not look for a missing test (mirrors BE-0426's "tenant-root tables have no ListByParent" note).
ralph/progress.txt:406:- Repository CRUD invariants test file for a **composite-key tenant-scoped link table** with a database-managed version column (BE-0429 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/membership_repository_invariants_test.go). The BE-0427 user_repository_invariants_test.go shape is the closest template, but four substitutions are mandatory because the row has no own-id, has a (organization_id, user_id) composite primary key, and carries role_version + the memberships_set_updated_at trigger: (a) the duplicate-Insert Conflict test asserts on the PK collision (a second Insert with the same (org, user) pair) instead of the citext-UNIQUE-email collision — the schema has no UNIQUE column outside the PK that an INSERT could violate; (b) the existing membership_test.go's UpdateRole case already asserts the role_version atomic +1 increment, so the invariants file's UpdateRole test is dedicated to the timestamp dual-anchor (updated_at refreshed by the BEFORE-UPDATE trigger, created_at preserved) — do NOT duplicate the role_version increment here; (c) NO Delete-cascades-to-children test (the membership row is the SINK of two ON-DELETE-CASCADE FKs from users and organizations, not a SOURCE — cascades INTO memberships are proved in the parent table's invariants file, e.g. TestUserRepositoryDeleteCascadesToMemberships in user_repository_invariants_test.go); (d) the local rollback sentinel type MUST use a distinct name from user_repository_invariants_test.go's `errSentinel` because every *_test.go file under internal/controlplane/store/ shares the same store_test package — `membershipTxRollbackSentinel struct{}` is the canonical naming choice. The Insert-returns-row-with-timestamps test additionally asserts (a) role_version == 1 (the schema default that backs session-token revocation in BE-0020 auth), (b) Email + UserDisplayName populated from the JOINed users row (Insert returns OrganizationMember, not raw Membership — the httpapi layer renders the joined shape directly), and (c) created_at.Equal(updated_at) on the fresh row (the trigger has not fired yet). Seed pattern: seedOrg + seedUserRow first, then call repo.Insert through the repository (NOT seedMembership, which is the raw-INSERT helper for tenant-isolation tests). Forward-applicable to BE-0431/0432 (service_accounts CRUD + tenant-isolation) and BE-0433/0434 (api_keys CRUD + tenant-isolation) — both are tenant-scoped tables with their own version-tracking columns, so the rule "split the version-increment assertion (canonical CRUD test) from the timestamp-dual-anchor assertion (invariants test)" should also apply.
ralph/progress.txt:407:- Repository CRUD invariants test file for a **tenant-scoped table whose only customer mutation is an idempotent lifecycle flip** (BE-0431 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/serviceaccount_repository_invariants_test.go). The BE-0427 user_repository_invariants_test.go and BE-0429 membership_repository_invariants_test.go shapes are the templates, but three substitutions are mandatory because service_accounts has no generic Update and no version column — only Insert + Disable (COALESCE-idempotent): (a) the trigger-dual-anchor test drives through the **lifecycle mutation** (Disable) instead of a generic Update — created_at preserved, updated_at moves forward via the BEFORE UPDATE trigger, plus the lifecycle field (disabled_at) is set and IsDisabled() flips true; (b) the idempotent-second-call semantics are NOT re-asserted here (they belong in the canonical CRUD test in serviceaccount_test.go); (c) the rollback-leaves-zero-footprint test asserts BOTH Get-is-NotFound AND CountByOrganization == 0 (the quota reader is the second observable surface a half-written row could surface through, and a future refactor that switched Insert to a SAVEPOINT or autonomous transaction would silently inflate it without ever surfacing on Get). Distinct sentinel type name (`serviceAccountTxRollbackSentinel`) to avoid the store_test package-level collision with `errSentinel` (user) and `membershipTxRollbackSentinel` (membership). Forward-applicable to BE-0433/0434 (api_keys CRUD + tenant-isolation): api_keys has an Update path (rotate / revoke / patch metadata), so the invariants file should re-introduce the full Update-trigger dual-anchor test from the user/membership template alongside the rollback + nil-Tx guards; the secret-bearing nature of api_keys means the rollback test must additionally assert that no secret_hash needle leaks into post-rollback observable state.
ralph/progress.txt:408:- Repository tenant-isolation test file for a **composite-key tenant-scoped link table** with NO own id, NO version column independent of role_version, and NO soft-delete column (BE-0430 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/membership_tenant_isolation_test.go). The BE-0428 user_tenant_isolation_test.go shape is the closest template, but four substitutions are mandatory because the link table is keyed by (organization_id, user_id) and the load-bearing cross-row failure mode is "the SAME user is a legitimate member of two tenants, and a WHERE-on-user-only mutation scrubs/bumps the WRONG tenant's row": (a) every byte-identical-snapshot test seeds the SAME user_id into BOTH tenants with deliberately distinct (role, role_version) tuples — a WHERE-on-user-only mistake would return one row for both Get calls and the disjoint role_version values would catch it even when the role values coincide; (b) the UpdateRole bystander test snapshots the OTHER tenant's row (not the unrelated user's row) — the BE-0428 "different user, different tenant" framing is too weak for a link table, because the disjoint-id check that user identity gets for free here is the WHOLE THING the test is proving; (c) the duplicate-Insert Conflict bystander test snapshots BOTH the conflicting target tenant's row AND the SAME-user-different-tenant bystander row — the PK collision fires AFTER any row-level CHECKs and the BEFORE-INSERT triggers, so a regression that ever touched the wrong row would leak through updated_at on either side; (d) the cross-tenant-NotFound-vs-unknown-pair indistinguishability test must run for BOTH Get AND GetMember (the JOIN-bearing read) — divergence in either Code or Hint is an existence leak surface, and the JOIN-bearing path is a separate code path from the bare-row Get. The List isolation test takes TWO shapes that must both run: (i) disjoint-user-base count isolation (orgA has 1 member, orgB has 3, List(orgA) returns 1 — proves the WHERE-organization_id filter is present at all); (ii) shared-user-row isolation (Ada is a member of BOTH tenants, List(orgA) returns ONE row containing only orgA's view of Ada, List(orgB) returns ONE row containing only orgB's view of Ada — proves the JOIN does not produce a row-per-(membership × every memberships row for the same user_id) duplication). Document the deliberate ABSENCE of soft-delete tests (memberships has no deletion_scheduled_at) AND the cross-table cascade tests (cascades INTO memberships from users/organizations are proved by the parent-table tests TestUserRepositoryDeleteCascadesToMemberships and TestTenantHierarchyCascadeDelete, not here) in the file's package-doc comment. CRITICAL SEED-VALUE GOTCHA: the memberships_role_check CHECK constraint on memberships restricts role to {owner, admin, member} ONLY — the broader {owner, admin, developer, viewer, ci, support} set is the GRANTS-table role enum (project_grants/environment_grants), not memberships. Tests that seed "developer" or "viewer" into memberships will fail with `new row for relation "memberships" violates check constraint "memberships_role_check" (SQLSTATE 23514)`. Forward-applicable to BE-0432 (service_accounts tenant isolation: a tenant-scoped table with an organization_id COLUMN and an own id, so the BE-0426 organizations template is the closer fit — but the "same logical entity across two tenants" angle does not apply to service_accounts the way it does to memberships, because a service_account row has no shared-identity column the way memberships shares user_id with the global users table) and BE-0434 (api_keys tenant isolation: same shape as service_accounts).
ralph/progress.txt:409:- Repository tenant-isolation test file for a **tenant-scoped row with its own id, organization_id COLUMN, NO version column, NO generic Update, and NO soft-delete column** — only Insert + Disable lifecycle flip (BE-0432 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/serviceaccount_tenant_isolation_test.go). The BE-0428 user_tenant_isolation_test.go and BE-0430 membership_tenant_isolation_test.go shapes are the templates, but four substitutions are mandatory because service_accounts has its OWN id (no shared-identity column the way memberships shares user_id with the global users table) and the load-bearing cross-row failure mode is "two tenants happen to share a slug (UNIQUE (organization_id, slug) permits this by design) and a WHERE-on-slug-only or WHERE-on-id-only mutation swaps the WRONG tenant's row": (a) every byte-identical-snapshot test seeds BOTH tenants with service accounts holding the SAME slug via a `seedServiceAccountWithSlug(t, db, f, org, label, slug)` helper that overrides the factory's slug — a WHERE-on-slug mistake would resolve to whichever row the planner found first and the per-tenant id assertion would catch it (the BE-0430 "same user, distinct role/role_version tuples" framing translates here to "different ids, SAME slug"; the inverse is the only cross-row leak surface a tenant-root-with-its-own-id table exposes); (b) the Disable bystander test is the analogue of BE-0430's UpdateRole bystander — Disable is the only mutation beyond Insert, so the BE-0428 generic Update test does not apply; the trigger dual-anchor (created_at preserved + updated_at refreshed) PLUS the lifecycle field (disabled_at == nil) on the bystander row catch any WHERE-on-slug-only Disable that ever matched the wrong row; (c) the duplicate-Insert Conflict bystander test snapshots BOTH the conflicting target tenant's row AND the same-slug-different-tenant bystander row AND a post-state CountByOrganization == 1 for each (since the trigger is BEFORE-UPDATE only, a rolled-back INSERT that resolved its UNIQUE check against the cross-tenant row would have no trigger anchor — Count is the second observable surface a row landing in the wrong tenant could leak through, mirroring the BE-0431 invariants-file Count-after-rollback rule); (d) the cross-tenant-NotFound-vs-unknown-id indistinguishability test must run for BOTH Get AND Disable (the only two NotFound-emitting paths the repository exposes) — divergence in Code or Hint is an existence leak surface, and the mutation path is a separate code path from the read; afterward, re-read the foreign tenant's row and assert disabled_at == nil so the cross-tenant Disable probe did not silently flip the lifecycle. The List isolation test takes TWO shapes that must both run (mirrors BE-0430): (i) disjoint-fixture count isolation (orgA=1 SA, orgB=3 SAs, gotA must contain only saA's id, gotB must contain exactly {saB1, saB2, saB3} — proves no JOIN scrambled which rows land where); (ii) shared-slug-row isolation (saA and saB both with slug "shared-ci-slug", List(orgA) returns exactly one row with id=saA, List(orgB) returns exactly one row with id=saB — proves a SELECT disambiguating by slug instead of by organization would have either swapped or merged the two rows). One additional CountByOrganization-specific test seeds three tenants (orgA=2, orgB=3, orgC=0 empty bystander) and asserts the three distinct counts; this is the quota-layer leak surface (Count is the read the quota engine consults to enforce per-organization limits, so a leak inflates one tenant's effective quota with another's usage). Document the deliberate ABSENCE of soft-delete tests (service_accounts has no deletion_scheduled_at — only disabled_at, which the byte-identical Disable snapshot treats as the analogue) AND the deliberate ABSENCE of generic-Update / DELETE / cascade tests (Disable is the only mutation; the api_keys.service_account_id cross-tenant FK leak proof lives in serviceaccount_test.go's TestServiceAccountAPIKeyCannotCrossTenantBoundary; the cascade-on-org-delete proof lives in TestServiceAccountBelongsToOneOrganization and TestTenantHierarchyCascadeDelete) in the file's package-doc comment so a future reader does not look for a missing test (mirrors BE-0428/BE-0430's deliberate-absence note). Note on apierr.NotFound message vs Hint: NotFound("service_account", id) Newf-builds a Message containing the caller-supplied id verbatim, but never sets Hint — so the cross-tenant-vs-unknown-id indistinguishability check MUST compare Code and Hint only (Message would diverge by caller input, which is not a leak). Forward-applicable to BE-0434 (api_keys tenant-isolation: same shape with the same "two tenants share a slug-equivalent" angle — api_keys has UNIQUE (organization_id, prefix), so the shared-prefix fixture is the structural counterpart; plus a secret-bearing extension — the cross-tenant probes must additionally assert no secret_hash needle reaches any observable surface) and BE-0436 (api_key_scopes tenant-isolation).
ralph/progress.txt:410:- Repository CRUD invariants test file for a **tenant-scoped row with its own id, organization_id COLUMN, NO version column, THREE distinct UPDATE paths (UpdateMutable / Revoke / RotateCredential), an additional UPDATE-but-not-mutation-state path (TouchLastUsed), and a secret-bearing row body (secret_hash + globally-UNIQUE prefix)** (BE-0433 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/api_key_repository_invariants_test.go). The BE-0427 user_repository_invariants_test.go, BE-0429 membership_repository_invariants_test.go, and BE-0431 serviceaccount_repository_invariants_test.go shapes are the templates, but five substitutions are mandatory because api_keys has no version column, three UPDATE paths instead of one, a separate touch-style UPDATE for the auth path, and a secret-bearing row body: (a) the trigger-dual-anchor (created_at preserved + updated_at refreshed by the BEFORE UPDATE trigger) must run ONCE PER UPDATE PATH — UpdateMutable, Revoke, RotateCredential, and TouchLastUsed each get their own dedicated dual-anchor test, so a refactor that dropped the trigger from one path but not the others trips a path-specific gate (the BE-0431 single-Disable pattern is too weak here); (b) the UpdateMutable dual-anchor test additionally pins the **mutable surface** to exactly {name, scopes} by snapshotting every non-mutable column (prefix, secret_hash, created_by, service_account_id, expires_at, revoked_at, last_used_at) as byte-identical to the baseline — UpdateMutable is the first repository method in the store package whose SET-clause is a true subset of the table's mutable columns, so the non-mutation assertion is the only structural defense against a future refactor that quietly widened the SET-clause; (c) RotateCredential gets a dedicated test that pins the **credential swap** invariant: prefix and secret_hash flip to the new values, every identity/ownership/scopes/lifecycle column stays byte-identical, the OLD prefix no longer FindByPrefix-resolves (must surface yerr.CodeNotFound — rotation is the only mutation that orphans a previous lookup key), and the NEW prefix re-binds to the SAME key id (rotation is a credential swap, not a re-mint, so audit trails and outstanding access grants keep pointing at the same id); (d) the rollback-leaves-zero-footprint test asserts the standard Get-NotFound + ListByOrganization-empty, AND adds two **secret-bearing redaction probes** specific to api_keys: SELECT COUNT(*) FROM api_keys WHERE secret_hash = $1 must return 0 AND SELECT COUNT(*) FROM api_keys WHERE prefix = $1 must return 0 — a SAVEPOINT-or-autonomous-transaction refactor that survived the outer rollback would surface here even when every per-id read reports NotFound, because the credential primitives (the secret hash an attacker would need, and the public prefix that drives the authentication lookup) are the load-bearing observables the table owes its caller; (e) typed-Internal nil-Tx guards run for ALL FIVE mutation paths (Insert / UpdateMutable / Revoke / RotateCredential / TouchLastUsed) — the pre-existing TestAPIKeyRepositoryInsertRejectsNilTx in apikey_test.go only asserts err != nil (weaker than the typed-Internal contract every other repository follows), so the typed-code test is additive, not a replacement. Distinct sentinel type name (`apiKeyTxRollbackSentinel`) to avoid the store_test package-level collision with `errSentinel` (user), `membershipTxRollbackSentinel` (membership), and `serviceAccountTxRollbackSentinel` (service account). Local `equalTimePtr(a, b *time.Time) bool` helper is the canonical way to express "lifecycle stamp untouched" concisely without dragging in a third-party deep-equality helper — both-nil OR both-non-nil-and-time.Equal. Forward-applicable to BE-0434 (api_keys tenant isolation: the cross-tenant probes must additionally assert no secret_hash needle reaches any observable surface — same secret-bearing redaction probe pattern documented above for the rollback test, but applied to the cross-tenant-Get / cross-tenant-FindByPrefix / cross-tenant-List read paths and to the typed error surfaces Code/Hint/Message) and to ANY future repository whose row body carries a secret-bearing column (api_key_scopes does NOT — the scope-name column is non-secret — but variables.value when is_secret=true IS, so the BE-0451+ variables invariants stories should reuse this secret-bearing-rollback-probe rule).
ralph/progress.txt:411:- Repository tenant-isolation test file for a **tenant-scoped row whose mutation surface spans FIVE distinct methods (UpdateMutable / Revoke / RotateCredential / TouchLastUsed plus the Insert path) AND whose row body carries TWO load-bearing credential primitives (a globally-UNIQUE prefix and a secret-bearing secret_hash)** (BE-0434 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/api_key_tenant_isolation_test.go; the cross-tenant analogue of BE-0433 and the secret-bearing variant of the BE-0432 service_accounts shape). Two schema-level facts make this pattern structurally distinct from BE-0432: (i) api_keys.prefix is GLOBALLY UNIQUE (NOT UNIQUE (organization_id, prefix) — the prefix authentication lookup runs BEFORE the caller's tenant is known) so the BE-0432 "two tenants share a slug" worst-case fixture has no row-level analogue here; the cross-tenant prefix-conflict path is still load-bearing (orgA Insert with orgB's already-persisted prefix AND orgA RotateCredential to orgB's prefix) and BOTH paths must surface CodeConflict, leave orgB's bystander row byte-identical, and leave NO row anywhere carrying the rolled-back secret_hash needle (raw-SQL probe). (ii) api_keys carries a secret-bearing column — secret_hash — so the cross-tenant byte-identical-bystander assertion includes a `assertRawSecretHashUnchanged` raw-SQL probe AGAINST the typed-read SecretHash field (the typed read path is already covered by the struct snapshot, but a regression that scanned a non-secret column into the struct or skipped the field on Get would silently pass the struct check while the underlying row drifted — the raw probe is the load-bearing safety net for THAT class of regression specifically), and the cross-tenant indistinguishability test additionally asserts the secret_hash needle does NOT appear in any of the typed-error surfaces yeCross.Error() / yeCross.Message / yeCross.Hint across ALL five mutation methods (the secret-bearing redaction probe extension to the BE-0432 Code+Hint indistinguishability rule). Skeleton: one t.Parallel test per (mutation × leg) plus a 5-row indistinguishability-and-redaction table test that iterates (Get, UpdateMutable, Revoke, RotateCredential, TouchLastUsed). Three load-bearing rules: (1) Every byte-identical-bystander test asserts BOTH the trigger-managed updated_at (the api_keys_set_updated_at BEFORE-UPDATE trigger refreshes it on every matched UPDATE, including a WHERE-on-id-only UPDATE that touched the wrong tenant) AND the lifecycle column the OWNING method writes (revoked_at for Revoke, last_used_at for TouchLastUsed, prefix+secret_hash for RotateCredential, name+scopes for UpdateMutable) so a WHERE-on-id-only regression surfaces independently on EITHER anchor. (2) The Insert cross-tenant prefix-conflict test must add a SELECT COUNT(*) FROM api_keys global-table-size probe AFTER the conflicting Insert — a rolled-back Insert that landed its row in some OTHER tenant's slot would show up there even if the per-id probe missed it; the per-id probe alone is insufficient because the failing row's id is not known to the probe. (3) FindByPrefix is INTENTIONALLY NOT a cross-tenant probe surface — the signature carries no caller-tenant parameter, the prefix column is globally unique, and the resolved row carries its own organization_id; document this absence in the test file's package-doc comment so a future reader does not look for a missing test (mirrors BE-0426's "tenant-root tables have no ListByParent" note). Helper shape worth pinning forward: a `getXOrFail(ctx, t, s, repo, orgID, id, label) X` and a `writeX(ctx, s, fn) (X, error)` pair keep byte-identical snapshot tests free of Read/Write closure boilerplate; combined with a single `assertBystanderByteIdentical(t, label, baseline, after)` helper they reduce a 12-column equality check to two lines per (mutation × leg). Forward-applicable to BE-0436 (api_key_scopes tenant isolation — a child-table variant with NO secret-bearing column and only one mutation surface, so the secret-bearing redaction probe and most of the conflict-rollback shape collapse; the LIST-by-parent isolation rule remains) and to BE-0450 (variables tenant isolation, where the secret-bearing column is value WHEN is_secret=true, so the rolled-back-secret probe rule applies CONDITIONALLY on is_secret — the same shape extended with a per-row classification check). Use docker compose up -d postgres + YALLA_TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgres://yalla:yalla@127.0.0.1:5432/yalla go test -race -count=1 -run 'TestAPIKeyRepository.*OrgADoesNotTouchOrgB|TestAPIKeyRepositoryGetReturnsCorrectRowAcrossTenants|TestAPIKeyRepositoryInsertCrossTenantPrefixConflictDoesNotTouchOrgB|TestAPIKeyRepositoryRotateCredentialCrossTenantPrefixConflictDoesNotTouchOrgB|TestAPIKeyRepositoryListByOrganizationCountsAreIsolated|TestAPIKeyRepositoryListByServiceAccountIsTenantScoped|TestAPIKeyRepositoryCrossTenantErrorIsIndistinguishableFromUnknownId' ./internal/controlplane/store/ to verify; the suite skips cleanly when the env var is unset. Same pre-existing -race-with-DB unrelated regressions noted under BE-0426/BE-0432 still apply — confirmed by stashing the new file and re-running, the failing tests are environment-service / api-key-service Create paths, organization_variable_patch audit metadata, and TestSQLArgsAreConstantExpressions; none are touched by this pattern.
ralph/progress.txt:413:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, pure-function-chokepoint instance (BE-0407 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_service_desired_state_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/dokploy/service_desired_state_canonical_test.go's new canonical pair TestServiceDesiredStateCoversCallSites + TestServiceDesiredStatePreservesContractUnderContention + SECURITY.md "## Service Desired-State Golden Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: service desired-state golden tests (BE-0407)" subsection; TWENTY-NINTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0406 verification-suite mega-pattern. Canonical command `go test -run TestServiceDesiredState ./...`, CI step name `Service desired-state golden tests`, verify.sh prefix #31 inserted between BE-0406's #30 and the trailing optionals renumbered #31-#35 → #32-#36 in the same edit (with the BE-0400 external-smoke static-test constants `# 35 → # 36` and synthetic-fixture `# 34 → # 35` updated to match). Two forward-applicable extensions specific to the FIRST pure-no-context-no-port chokepoint instance — the chokepoint is `(*dokploy.Renderer).Render(in RenderInput) (RenderedSpec, error)` which is a pure function of its input alone (no context, no I/O, no port interfaces, no Repository): (1) `THE CONTENTION BURST BUILDS A FRESH RENDERER PER ITERATION VIA dokploy.NewRenderer() — NOT A SHARED INSTANCE — BECAUSE CONSTRUCTION IS CHEAP AND THE CONTRACT IS "NewRenderer THEN Render"`. The renderer has no port interfaces at construction time, so a shared instance would also work, but the per-iteration construction pins the documented API contract verbatim and surfaces a regression that smuggled in a sync.Once mutating a per-call map, a sync.Pool reused without resetting, or any cached per-instance view as a per-iteration mismatch even when an aggregate pass count matched. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint is a constructed-instance method (`pkg.NewX().Method(args)` style) where the constructor is cheap and takes no ports: build a fresh chokepoint per iteration via the documented constructor; the burst's value is the assertion that the package's method-internal pure logic has no package-level shared state AND that the documented constructor stays cheap and reusable. (2) `THE VALUE-FREE PROJECTION CANARY SEEDS THE SECRET MARKER INTO EVERY LEVEL OF THE VARIABLE HIERARCHY (org/project/env/service) AND ASSERTS MARKER-ABSENCE IN THREE REDACTED SURFACES: Summary JSON, slog LogValue group, AND Summary.String() form`. Unlike BE-0406's single-surface canary (ProposedDisplay survival + closed-set-tag absence), the renderer's redaction contract spans three concrete projection surfaces — the JSON-marshalled Summary, the slog handler's text output, and the single-line String form — because each is a separate code path the redactor must enforce. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint emits a type that implements slog.LogValuer alongside an explicit Summary projection: drive the marker-absence canary through ALL THREE projection surfaces (encoding/json output, slog.NewTextHandler captured output, and any documented String form), not just the JSON; a regression in any one surface ships to a different operator audience (audit pipeline vs structured log handler vs ad-hoc fmt.Println) and pinning all three end-to-end keeps the redaction contract bound to the public API, not just the wire format. One renderer-canonical gotcha worth pinning forward: `domain.ID FIXTURES IN THE dokploy_test PACKAGE MUST USE A 26-CHAR CROCKFORD-BASE32-LOWERCASE SUFFIX EXCLUDING i, l, o, u; the canonical pair uses serviceDesiredStateFixedID(domain.KindXxx, last byte ∈ {a, b, c, d}) — last bytes from the safe set keep determinism self-check #6's reflect.DeepEqual stable across runs without invented IDs that LOOK 26-char-valid but carry excluded letters. The canonical pair file lives in `package dokploy_test` (mirrors BE-0406's `package migrateimport_test`) — the chokepoint is exported (Render is a public method on Renderer) so the canonical pair stays in the _test package next to renderer_test.go rather than the package-internal store_test convention used by the BE-0404 break-glass pair. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
ralph/progress.txt:415:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, port-driven-pure-classifier instance (BE-0406 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_import_dry_run_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/migrateimport/import_dry_run_canonical_test.go's new canonical pair TestImportDryRunCoversCallSites + TestImportDryRunPreservesContractUnderContention + SECURITY.md "## Import Dry-Run Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: import dry-run tests (BE-0406)" subsection; TWENTY-EIGHTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0405 verification-suite mega-pattern. Canonical command `go test -run TestImportDryRun ./...`, CI step name `Import dry-run tests`, verify.sh prefix #30 inserted between BE-0405's #29 and the trailing optionals renumbered #30-#34 → #31-#35 in the same edit (with the BE-0400 external-smoke static-test constants `# 34 → # 35` and synthetic-fixture `# 33 → # 34` updated to match). Three forward-applicable extensions specific to the FIRST port-driven-pure-classifier instance — the chokepoint is `(*migrateimport.Importer).Plan(ctx, PlanInput) (Plan, error)` which is deterministic but reads from port interfaces (Scanner, Repository) rather than being a package-level pure function: (1) `THE CONTENTION BURST BUILDS A FRESH IMPORTER PER ITERATION — NOT A SHARED INSTANCE — BECAUSE THE CHOKEPOINT TAKES PORT INTERFACES AT CONSTRUCTION TIME` — unlike BE-0405 (which has no shared receiver because Diff is a package-level function), the import-dry-run chokepoint is a method on Importer whose fake Scanner/Repository are scenario-specific. Each goroutine therefore builds its own (Snapshot, fake Repository, Importer) triple per iteration. The burst's value shifts from "package has no shared state" to "the planner's internal pure logic (classify, pendingOwnerItems, proposeSlug, indexProjectsByID, indexEnvironmentsByID, indexServicesByID) has no package-level shared state" — a regression that smuggled in a cached owner table or a sync.Once mutating a per-action map would still surface as a per-iteration mismatch. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint is a constructed-from-ports classifier (`pkg.New(cfg).Method(args)` style): build a fresh chokepoint per iteration; the burst's value is the assertion that the package's pure helpers have no package-level shared state. (2) `THE DRY-RUN PURITY INVARIANT IS A POSITIVE ASSERTION ON THE FAKE REPOSITORY'S RECORDED CALLS, NOT THE ABSENCE OF AN EFFECT — SCAN repo.calls FOR EVERY CREATE PREFIX AFTER EVERY Plan CALL` — the importDryRunCheckOutcome predicate scans the fakeRepo.calls slice for any string with prefix `CreateProject:`, `CreateEnvironment:`, or `CreateService:` and returns a failure when one is found; pending-owner rows additionally assert len(repo.calls) == 0 (the no-owner short-circuit must not touch the repository at all). A regression that started writing through Plan would fail the prefix-scan on its first ready scenario; a regression that started reading the repository under a pending-owner short-circuit would fail the count assertion on its first pending-owner scenario. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint is documented read-only on some paths and read-write on others: pin the read-only contract as a positive assertion over the fake's call recorder, not as the absence of an observable side-effect (a state diff would silently pass when the planner reads but does not write, which is itself a regression). (3) `THE PER-ROW REDACTION CANARY ASSERTS MARKER-SURVIVAL IN ProposedDisplay AND MARKER-ABSENCE IN STATUS / REASON / LEVEL — NOT IN ProposedSlug, BECAUSE SLUG IS DERIVED FROM DISPLAY` — the value-free classification contract says the importer's classification surface (Reason, Status, slug, display name) is value-free for sensitive content, but ProposedSlug is itself derived from ProposedDisplay via domain.NormalizeSlug and will deliberately carry alphanumeric content from the human-authored name. The canary therefore seeds importsecretmarker into untrusted Dokploy display names and (a) asserts the marker survives in at least one item's ProposedDisplay (operator review surface MUST render the human name), (b) asserts the marker is absent from string(Level), string(Status), and string(Reason) of every item (closed-set tags MUST stay value-free). Asserting on slug too would over-tighten: a slug derived from a name containing the marker WILL carry the marker by design. Watch out for MaxSlugLen=30 truncation: marker-bearing test names MUST stay ≤ 30 chars so the duplicate-collision scenario's pre-seeded slug matches the planner's normalized slug exactly (a longer name truncates and the dominance test silently flips to StatusReady). Forward-applicable to every verification story whose value-free contract has a deliberate exception field (display, label, raw-name): enumerate the closed-set tag fields the marker MUST be absent from; do not include the deliberate-exception field in the absence predicate; pin marker survival in the exception field too so the canary's positive direction is also pinned. Two store-layer gotchas worth pinning forward: (A) `domain.ID FIXTURES MUST USE CROCKFORD BASE32 LOWERCASE EXCLUDING i, l, o, u` — the importer's fake repository accepts any string but human-facing IDs in test fixtures follow the canonical Crockford suffix; `proj_01hz00000000000000000000dp` and `proj_01hz00000000000000000000ap` are valid (4 + 20 + 2 = 26 chars), while invented IDs like `proj_…colp` or `proj_…linp` are NOT (they contain `l` or `o`). (B) `NormalizeSlug TRUNCATES TO MaxSlugLen=30 AND TRIMS TRAILING HYPHENS` — keep marker-bearing fixture names short enough that their normalized slug equals their raw input verbatim; the duplicate-collision scenario's pre-seeded slug uses the same shortened name as the snapshot to avoid silent slug-mismatch drift. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
ralph/progress.txt:418:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, policy-engine pure-function instance (BE-0403 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_admin_endpoint_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/policy/admin_endpoint_policy_test.go's new canonical pair TestAdminEndpointCoversCallSites + TestAdminEndpointPreservesContractUnderContention + SECURITY.md "## Admin Endpoint Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: admin endpoint tests (BE-0403)" subsection; TWENTY-FIFTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0402 verification-suite mega-pattern. Canonical command `go test -run TestAdminEndpoint ./...`, CI step name `Admin endpoint tests`, verify.sh prefix #27 inserted between BE-0402's #26 and the trailing optionals renumbered #27-#31 → #28-#32 in the same edit (with the BE-0400 external-smoke static-test constants `# 31 → # 32` and synthetic-fixture `# 30 → # 31` updated to match). Three forward-applicable extensions specific to the FIRST policy-engine pure-function instance: (1) `CLOSED-SET SCENARIO TABLE IS GENERATED FROM CARTESIAN PRODUCT, NOT HANDWRITTEN` — adminEndpointScenarios() builds 90 rows from 6 endpoints × (12 role rows + 3 grant rows) by nested for-loops over four structural axes (endpoint × role × {same-tenant, cross-tenant} × grant scope); each row carries its own predicted (Allow, Reason) so a failure points the operator at the exact axis that drifted. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose scenario table is the cartesian product of two or more closed enumerations — generate the table, never hand-list it; the per-row predicted verdict is the contract. (2) `THREE STRUCTURAL SELF-CHECKS FIRE FAST AT THE HEAD OF THE COVERS-CALL-SITES TEST, BEFORE THE TABLE WALK` — (a) every endpoint in adminEndpointCallSites binds to one of the four ActionAdmin* constants AND every ActionAdmin* is bound to ≥1 endpoint (exhaustiveness in both directions), (b) every admin action is catalogued as CapSupport via Engine.ActionCapability, (c) builtinRoleCaps admits CapSupport for exactly RoleSupport via BuiltinRoles(). Each fires before the row walk so a regression that drifted the catalog or role matrix gives a fast targeted failure at the seam, NOT a confusing per-row mismatch many lines later. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose closed set is constructed from registered enumerations the engine looks up by name — assert exhaustiveness in BOTH directions at the head of the test, then walk the table. (3) `REDACTION CANARY VIA SECRET MARKER IN PRINCIPAL ID, ASSERTED ON EVERY CONTENTION ITERATION` — adminEndpointPrincipal seeds the literal `ADMINENDPOINTSECRETMARKER` into every Principal ID, and the contention burst's per-iteration check calls reasonContainsSubstring(got.Reason, marker) to assert the Reason never echoes principal-supplied data. The Reason set is closed today so the check is structurally redundant, but it catches a future engine change that widened Reason to caller-supplied data on its FIRST iteration, NOT after a real credential leaks into a log. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint produces a structured outcome (Reason, Code, etc.) and is fed caller-supplied identifiers — seed a marker in the caller-supplied input and scan the output on every burst iteration; the marker is the chokepoint between "decides via" and "echoes the input". Two engine gotchas worth pinning forward: (A) Principal struct uses `Disabled bool` (default false = enabled), NOT an `Enabled` field — invented `Enabled:true` is a compile error; the zero-value-is-enabled invariant matches the existing principalIn helper. (B) Grant-only principals (Role="" + non-empty Grants) skip the unknown-role guard at engine.go:109 because the guard is conditional on `principal.Role != ""`; leave Role unset rather than setting a sentinel string the engine would reject. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
ralph/progress.txt:423:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, tenant-isolation instance (BE-0398 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_tenant_isolation_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/policy/tenant_isolation_test.go's new canonical pair TestTenantIsolationCoversCallSites + TestTenantIsolationPreservesScopeUnderContention + SECURITY.md "## Tenant Isolation Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: tenant isolation tests (BE-0398)" subsection; TWENTIETH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0397 verification-suite mega-pattern, canonical command `go test -run TestTenantIsolation ./...`, CI step name `Tenant isolation tests`, verify.sh prefix #23 inserted between BE-0397's #22 and the trailing optionals renumbered 23->24/24->25/25->26/26->27 in the same edit. Per-story knobs unchanged from BE-0397's bullet — (a) canonical command from PRD, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (next number after the previous required gate), (d) SECURITY.md section substring set (here 20 substrings: internal/controlplane/policy, tenant_isolation_test.go, deterministic, policy.Engine, NewEngine, ReasonDeniedCrossTenant, ReasonAllowedBySupport, ReasonAllowedSelf, ReasonDeniedNoPrincipal, ReasonDeniedPrincipalDisabled, ReasonDeniedUnknownAction, request_id, yalla.output.v1, yalla.error.v1, tenant isolation, the two function names, Every push and PR, redacted, and the self-locating static-test filename), (e) AGENTS.md subsection mirroring SECURITY.md but speaking to maintainers. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six. Three BE-0398-specific extensions to the verification-suite template, all forward-applicable to ANY future verification story whose subject is an authorization / policy / RBAC chokepoint in a Go package with an existing matrix-style test fixture (BE-0399 backup-restore rehearsal, BE-0400 external-dokploy-smoke): (1) `SINGLE-PACKAGE INTERNAL TEST REUSING THE EXISTING TENANT-CONSTANT FIXTURES — NO NEW STORE / FAKE / RECORDER NEEDED` — the canonical pair lives in `package policy` (the SAME internal test package as the existing `policy_test.go`) so the coverage member reuses the existing `orgA` / `orgB` constants, the `principalIn` / `resourceIn` / `assertDecision` helpers, and the package-private `builtinRoleCaps` matrix verbatim. Unlike BE-0395..BE-0397 (which each introduced a new in-file concurrent fixture for the burst member because the chokepoint had observable mutable state), policy.Engine.Decide is a documented pure function with no I/O and no shared mutable state; the contention burst therefore needs no fixture at all — every goroutine constructs its own (Principal, Action, Resource) tuple from the closed-set coverage table and asserts the verdict matches the table's prediction for ITS OWN tuple. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint is a pure function: skip the concurrent-fixture pattern entirely; let the goroutines share the immutable Engine and assert per-iteration verdict-vs-prediction equality. (2) `THE COVERAGE TABLE AND THE BURST PREDICTION SHARE ONE SOURCE-OF-TRUTH FUNCTION` — buildTenantIsolationScenarios(t) returns a []tenantIsolationScenario where each entry pairs the input tuple with the predicted (wantAllow, wantReason). The coverage member iterates the table in t.Run subtests; the burst member draws from the SAME table (indexed by `(w * iters + i) % len(scenarios)`) and asserts the engine's runtime verdict matches `scen.wantAllow / scen.wantReason` for the SPECIFIC tuple THIS goroutine drew. A cross-write under the race that swapped two goroutines' tuples would yield a verdict that matches the OTHER goroutine's prediction (not THIS one's), and the per-iteration assertion would fail with the offending scenario name. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose closed-set scenario table feeds both a coverage table AND a contention burst: derive both from the same builder, key the burst's per-iteration assertion on the BURST's own tuple's expectation (not the aggregate count), and surface the worker + iteration index + scenario name on every per-iteration failure so an operator can correlate the regression to a specific cross-write. (3) `ASYMMETRIC AUTHORIZE-ERROR REDACTION CONTRACT — REASON CODE IN MESSAGE ONLY FOR FORBIDDEN, NOT FOR UNAUTHENTICATED` — policy.Engine.Authorize maps ReasonDeniedNoPrincipal to apierr.Unauthenticated (HTTP 401) with a fixed `"authentication is required for action <action>"` message that does NOT echo the reason code; every other deny reason maps to apierr.Forbidden (HTTP 403) with `"not authorized for action <action> (<reason>)"`. The tenant-isolation coverage member's redaction sub-assertion therefore checks the action substring on every denial but only checks the reason substring on the Forbidden branch (skipping ReasonDeniedNoPrincipal). A regression that started echoing principal/tenant ids into either message would still surface — the per-secret loop covers both branches. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose authorization chokepoint surfaces multiple error envelopes: enumerate which fields are guaranteed in which envelope and pin the assertions per-branch, not as a single message-shape check.
ralph/progress.txt:424:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, pagination-stability instance (BE-0397 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_pagination_stability_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/pagination/pagination_stability_test.go's new canonical pair TestPaginationStabilityCoversCallSites + TestPaginationStabilityPreservesPagesUnderInserts + SECURITY.md "## Pagination Stability Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: pagination stability tests (BE-0397)" subsection; NINETEENTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0396 verification-suite mega-pattern, canonical command `go test -run TestPaginationStability ./...`, CI step name `Pagination stability tests`, verify.sh prefix #22 inserted between BE-0396's #21 and the trailing optionals renumbered 22->23/23->24/24->25/25->26 in the same edit. Per-story knobs unchanged from BE-0396's bullet — (a) canonical command from PRD, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (next number after the previous required gate), (d) SECURITY.md section substring set (here 19 substrings: internal/controlplane/pagination, pagination_stability_test.go, deterministic, fakeStore, concurrentPaginationStore, EncodeCursor, DecodeCursor, base64url, no duplicates, no drops, request_id, yalla.output.v1, yalla.error.v1, tenant isolation, the two function names, Every push and PR, redacted, and the self-locating static-test filename), (e) AGENTS.md subsection mirroring SECURITY.md but speaking to maintainers. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six. Three BE-0397-specific extensions to the verification-suite template, all forward-applicable to ANY future verification story whose subject is a list-pagination / cursor-encoding / wire-shape invariant in a Go package with an existing single-call test fixture (BE-0398 tenant isolation, BE-0399 backup-restore rehearsal): (1) `DUAL-FIXTURE REUSE — EXISTING ZERO-MUTEX SINGLE-CALL FIXTURE FOR THE COVERAGE MEMBER, A FILE-LOCAL MUTEX-GUARDED FIXTURE FOR THE BURST MEMBER` — the canonical pair lives in `package pagination` (the same internal package as the existing `page_test.go`) so the coverage member reuses the existing `fakeStore` / `row` / `positionByID` / `rowID` helpers verbatim. The contention burst introduces a SECOND in-file store, `concurrentPaginationStore`, with a `sync.RWMutex`-guarded rows slice and `insert` / `delete` / `list` / `listAsc` methods — declaring it in the new test file (not in `page_test.go`) keeps the existing `fakeStore`'s zero-mutex shape for the single-call unit tests that rely on it. (2) `WIDEN THE EXISTING FIXTURE WITH AN ASCENDING SIBLING METHOD WHEN THE COVERAGE SCENARIO TABLE NEEDS BOTH DIRECTIONS — KEEP THE DESCENDING-DEFAULT METHOD UNCHANGED` — the existing `fakeStore.list` hard-codes descending order (matches the audit-log / deployment-list canonical case). Rather than refactor it (and risk regressing the existing TestCursor* tests), add a sibling `listAsc` method on the same receiver in the new test file so the closed-set coverage member can iterate both directions through a `storeLike` interface (`list` + `listAsc`) without forking the descending fixture. The interface lives in the new test file so the existing `fakeStore` definition file is untouched. (3) `CLOSED-SET SCENARIO TABLE COVERS EVERY DISTINCT PAGINATION SHAPE — EMPTY / SINGLE-ROW / EXACT-PAGE / MULTI-PAGE-NO-REMAINDER / MULTI-PAGE-PARTIAL-TRAILING / SMALL-PAGE / BOTH DIRECTIONS` — the scenario-table-rejects-duplicate-names self-check at the head of the coverage member guards against a future addition that quietly drops the closed-set property. Each scenario walks BuildPage end-to-end with a safety-bound terminator (1000-page bound) so the test fails loudly if a regression introduces a non-terminating cursor stream; the diagnostic surfaces both the scenario name AND the page index AND the row id of the divergence so an operator can correlate the gate failure with a specific in-flight traversal without re-running the suite locally. The contention burst sizing (workers=4, iterations=8, seedRows=50, pageSize=5) is the smallest power-of-two-burst-table-pair where a non-thread-safe store fails under -race ~99% of the time while keeping the wall-clock under 1s on CI.
ralph/progress.txt:425:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, audit-completeness instance (BE-0396 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_audit_completeness_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/audit/audit_completeness_test.go's new canonical pair TestAuditCompletenessCoversCallSites + TestAuditCompletenessPreservesRecordedFieldsUnderContention + SECURITY.md "## Audit Completeness Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: audit completeness tests (BE-0396)" subsection; EIGHTEENTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0395 verification-suite mega-pattern, canonical command `go test -run TestAuditCompleteness ./...`, CI step name `Audit completeness tests`, verify.sh prefix #21 inserted between BE-0395's #20 and the trailing optionals renumbered 21->22/22->23/23->24/24->25 in the same edit. Per-story knobs unchanged from BE-0395's bullet — (a) canonical command from PRD, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (next number after last required step; renumber trailing optionals in same edit), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and substring set (BE-0396 substring count = 18; matches BE-0395 count — the scope swaps three idempotency-specific anchors `Idempotency-Replayed` / `Idempotency-Key` / `fakeIdempotencyStore` for three audit-specific anchors `Auditor.Record` / `policy.Action` / `policy.Decision`, and swaps `byte-identical` / `E_INVALID_INPUT` / `E_FORBIDDEN` / `E_NOT_FOUND` for `allowed` / `denied` / `concurrentAuditRecorder` / `fakeRecorder`), (e) AGENTS.md subsection mirroring SECURITY.md but speaking to maintainers. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six. Three BE-0396-specific extensions to the verification-suite template, all forward-applicable to ANY future verification story whose subject is a chokepoint-mediated event-recording surface (audit / metrics / structured-log / outbox emitter) in a Go package with an existing single-call test fixture: (1) `DUAL-FIXTURE REUSE — EXISTING SINGLE-CALL FIXTURE FOR THE COVERAGE MEMBER, A FILE-LOCAL CONCURRENT FIXTURE FOR THE BURST MEMBER` — the canonical pair lives in `package audit_test` (the same external test package as the existing `audit_test.go`) so the coverage member reuses the existing `fakeRecorder` / `newAuditor` / `ctxWith` / `orgID` helpers verbatim. The contention burst introduces a SECOND in-file recorder, `concurrentAuditRecorder`, with a mutex-guarded events slice and a `snapshot()` accessor — declaring it in the new test file (not in `audit_test.go`) keeps the existing `fakeRecorder`'s zero-mutex shape for the single-call unit tests that rely on it. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose contention burst would race the existing single-call fixture: don't retrofit a mutex onto the existing helper — add a sibling helper in the new test file. The BE-0395 pattern called for "extend, don't fork" the existing fixtures; BE-0396 refines that to "extend the coverage helper, add a sibling for the contention burst" when the existing helper's zero-mutex shape is part of its contract. (2) `CLOSED-SET SCENARIO TABLE WALKS ALLOWED *AND* DENIED VARIANTS PER ACTION SURFACE` — the audit-completeness contract is not "every recorded event is well-formed" — it's "every security-relevant decision IS recorded AND well-formed", and denied decisions are as load-bearing as allowed ones (an audit log that omits denials is incomplete by definition). The scenario table therefore enumerates the canonical policy.Action surface (organization.create allowed, project.create allowed, environment.create allowed, service.create allowed, project.delete denied cross-tenant, environment.delete denied out-of-scope, keys.manage denied no-capability, limits.write denied principal-disabled) and the per-scenario `wantDecision` field pins the recorded `store.AuditDecision` to `AuditDecisionAllowed` or `AuditDecisionDenied` so a regression that flipped the recorder's verdict literal trips per-scenario. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose subject is a chokepoint that records BOTH outcomes (audit / metrics counter / structured log emitter / outbox): the closed-set scenario table MUST cover both verdicts, not just the happy path. (3) `PER-EMITTER FIELD-FIDELITY UNDER CONTENTION VIA EXPECTED-SET / OBSERVED-SET DIFF` — the runtime burst seeds `auditCompletenessWorkers * auditCompletenessIterationsPerWorker = 32` concurrent goroutines, each emitting a recorded decision with a unique `req_audit_completeness_burst_<w>_<i>` request_id. After the burst, the test builds two maps (expected: the closed set of synthesized request_ids; observed: the set of request_ids on captured events) and reports both missing-in-observed (a dropped emission OR a cross-write that overwrote one emitter's RequestID with another's — both surface here) AND unexpected-in-observed (a spurious event OR a cross-write that produced a new request_id under contention). Asserting on the SET (not just the count) is what catches the cross-write regression — a recorder that swapped two emitters' RequestIDs under a race would pass the count assertion but fail the set diff. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose contention burst's contract is "every emitter's identifying field is preserved on its recorded event": build expected/observed sets keyed on the emitter-unique identifier, then diff both ways. The burst's auditWorkers=4 × auditIters=8 sizing matches the BE-0395 burst sizing (32 concurrent calls is the smallest power-of-two-burst-table-pair where a non-thread-safe recorder fails under -race ~99% of the time, while keeping the wall-clock under 1s on CI).
ralph/progress.txt:429:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, load-smoke instance (BE-0392 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_load_smoke_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/httpapi/load_smoke_test.go's new canonical pair TestLoadSmokeContractCoversCoreEndpoints + TestLoadSmokeContractRunsBurstWithStableEnvelopes + SECURITY.md "## Load Smoke Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: load smoke tests (BE-0392)" subsection; FOURTEENTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0391 verification-suite mega-pattern, canonical command `go test -run TestLoadSmoke ./...`, CI step name `Load smoke tests`, verify.sh prefix #17 inserted between BE-0390's #16 and the optionals — optionals renumbered #17→#18 govulncheck, #18→#19 staticcheck, #19→#20 golangci-lint, #20→#21 goreleaser in the SAME edit; TENTH consecutive required-step insertion with optional renumbering since BE-0380's #6). Two BE-0392-specific extensions to the template, all forward-applicable to ANY future verification story whose load-bearing subject is an HTTP handler under burst load: (1) `CANONICAL PAIR ENCODES DETERMINISTIC × DETERMINISTIC DUAL — NO POSTGRES MEMBER` — unlike BE-0389/BE-0390 whose canonical pair splits across deterministic-fixture-only + Postgres-required substrates, BE-0392's BOTH pair members are deterministic. The bootstrap surface (`/healthz`, `/readyz`, `/version`) is the one part of the public HTTP API that needs no backing infrastructure — no Postgres, no fake Dokploy fixtures, no quota counters mutated, no scope-resolution chain. The closed-set member walks an in-memory endpoint table; the burst member spins the test handler in-process via httptest.NewServer and fires 16 workers × 32 iterations = 512 concurrent requests per endpoint. Neither member calls t.Skip on missing env vars — the gate trips on every developer machine and every CI runner without setup. Forward-applicable to ANY future verification story whose subject is a stateless HTTP surface or a pure-in-process invariant (BE-0407 desired-state goldens against in-memory reconciler, BE-0398 tenant isolation matrix at the policy layer alone). Critical implication: the BE-0389/BE-0390 dual-substrate shape is NOT mandatory for the canonical-pair template — when both invariants are deterministic, the pair can stay deterministic-only and the SECURITY.md section's "skips when YALLA_TEST_DATABASE_URL is unset" language is OMITTED (BE-0392's section instead explicitly says "no Postgres, no live Dokploy server"). The shared `YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY` opt-in-external substring stays in the substring set as the cross-suite escape hatch reference, but does NOT bind to any code path the canonical pair member exercises. (2) `BURST OBSERVATION COLLECTION SHAPE — channel buffered to total request count, observation struct includes endpoint + status + schema + ok + requestID + bodyBad + headerBad, then post-burst aggregation in main goroutine` — the burst test must avoid testing.T calls from worker goroutines (race-detector hostile and stdlib-discouraged); instead each goroutine pushes a typed `observation{}` onto a buffered channel sized to `len(endpoints) * workers * iterations` and the main goroutine drains the channel after wg.Wait()+close. Aggregation builds (a) a per-endpoint count map asserted against the expected burst total, (b) a per-request-id map for uniqueness assertion, (c) per-observation envelope/status/redaction assertions. Failure messages quote the offending endpoint AND the observed request_id (or "<missing>" sentinel + a synthetic "<request-construction-failed:...>" / "<request-do-failed:...>" id when the round-trip itself failed) so the operator correlates against logs without re-running. The burst total (16 × 32 × 3 = 1536 requests) completes in ~600ms on a developer laptop — safely under the flaky-test threshold. Forward-applicable to BE-0393 (chaos Dokploy timeouts — same channel+aggregation shape, but with a fault-injection step before each iteration), BE-0394 (chaos Postgres disconnects — same shape but with t.Skip on YALLA_TEST_DATABASE_URL), BE-0395 (idempotency replay — same shape but each iteration writes the same Idempotency-Key and asserts every response after the first is the cached envelope). The closed-set bootstrap endpoint table includes a `wantStatus` field per entry so a future endpoint that legitimately returns 503 under some condition can be added without breaking the burst member's status assertion. Per-story knobs for siblings: same as BE-0390's bullet — (a) canonical command from PRD, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (next number after last required step; renumber trailing optionals in same edit), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and substring set (BE-0392 substring count = 17; matches BE-0389's count), (e) AGENTS.md subsection mirroring SECURITY.md but speaking to maintainers. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
ralph/progress.txt:431:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, migrations-from-empty-DB instance (BE-0389 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_migrations_empty_db_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/store/migrate/migrate_test.go's new canonical pair TestMigrationsEmptyDBContractCoversAllNumberedFiles + TestMigrationsEmptyDBContractAppliesEmbeddedLadderToEmptyDatabase + SECURITY.md "## Migration Tests From Empty DB" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: migration tests from empty DB (BE-0389)" subsection; ELEVENTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0388 verification-suite mega-pattern, canonical command `go test -run TestMigrationsEmptyDB ./...`, CI step name `Migration tests from empty DB`, verify.sh prefix #15). Four BE-0389-specific extensions to the template: (1) `CANONICAL PAIR ENCODES DETERMINISTIC × POSTGRES-REQUIRED DUAL` — unlike every prior verification-suite story whose canonical pair pinned two invariants on the same execution substrate, BE-0389's pair splits across two SUBSTRATES: TestMigrationsEmptyDBContractCoversAllNumberedFiles runs on the embed.FS alone (no Postgres dependency, runs on every developer machine and every CI runner deterministically); TestMigrationsEmptyDBContractAppliesEmbeddedLadderToEmptyDatabase requires a real Postgres (creates a throwaway database via crypto/rand-named DSN, runs the embedded ladder, asserts schema_migrations is clean, skips with t.Skip when YALLA_TEST_DATABASE_URL is unset). The deterministic member is the FRONT LINE — it trips on every machine on any closed-set coverage regression (new NNNN_*.up.sql file the loader misses, numbered file renamed to non-numbered form, version gap). The Postgres member is the DEPTH gate — it asserts the runtime contract that no migration can fail on an empty DB. Forward-applicable to ANY future verification story whose contract spans deterministic-fixture-only AND requires-infrastructure invariants: BE-0390 (migration downgrade safety — deterministic Reversible check + Postgres Down test), BE-0392 (load smoke — deterministic harness contract + Postgres load run), BE-0394 (chaos Postgres disconnects — deterministic injection-point coverage + Postgres reconnect test). The Postgres member MUST use t.Skip on missing env var so the suite stays green on machines without Postgres — never t.Fatal, never silently pass. (2) `CLOSED-SET COVERAGE WALKS embed.FS, NOT RUNTIME.CALLER` — unlike BE-0388 (which read its own source via runtime.Caller to enumerate FuzzXxx targets), BE-0389's closed-set coverage walks the embedded migrations directory via fs.ReadDir(embeddedMigrations, migrationsDir) and parses each filename against a regex that MIRRORS the loader's grammar (`^(\d{4,})_([a-z0-9_]+)\.(up|down)\.sql$`). The matcher then cross-references against LoadMigrations(embeddedMigrations) to assert closed-set parity (same count, same versions in order, same names, non-empty bodies, 64-char checksums). The duplication of the filename grammar is INTENTIONAL — a future grammar change in the loader (e.g. permissive `.sql` form) MUST update both the loader AND the test in the same edit. Forward-applicable to any verification story whose closed-set surface is a directory tree (BE-0390 down-script coverage, BE-0398 tenant-isolation test coverage per repository method, BE-0407 desired-state goldens). (3) `SUBSTRING SET INCLUDES BOTH "schema_migrations" AND "NNNN_"` — the matcher binds to TWO load-bearing literals the migrations-from-empty-DB contract pivots on: `schema_migrations` (the ledger table — every applied migration MUST surface as one clean row) and `NNNN_` (the canonical filename grammar — closed-set coverage pivots on the four-digit-zero-padded version prefix). The substring set also includes `YALLA_TEST_DATABASE_URL` (the deterministic-skip env var) AND `YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY` (the shared opt-in-external escape hatch) so the matcher pins BOTH the per-suite skip contract and the cross-suite external contract. Substring count = 17 (matches BE-0388's count). The full FuzzXxx-style explicit-listing rule from BE-0388 carries over: when the matcher's substring set names the canonical function pair, the SECURITY.md section MUST quote each full function name verbatim — TestMigrationsEmptyDBContractCoversAllNumberedFiles AND TestMigrationsEmptyDBContractAppliesEmbeddedLadderToEmptyDatabase — so a reader sees the gate from the doc alone. (4) `VERIFY.SH RENUMBER OF OPTIONALS BLOCK — NINE INSERTIONS NOW (#6→#15)` — inserting required step #15 between #14 (fuzz validator tests) and the optionals renumbered 15→16/16→17/17→18/18→19 in the same edit (govulncheck/staticcheck/golangci-lint/goreleaser). The previous count was EIGHT consecutive insertions (BE-0380..BE-0388). Per-story knobs for siblings: same as BE-0388's bullet — (a) canonical command from PRD, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (next number after last required step; renumber trailing optionals in same edit), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and 15-17-entry substring set (per-suite invariants — preserve closed-set discipline; list canonical function pair verbatim), (e) AGENTS.md subsection mirroring SECURITY.md but speaking to maintainers. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six. GOTCHA: when the PRD's `-run` filter is NOT already in verificationLoop.requiredBackendCommands (BE-0389's `go test -run TestMigrationsEmptyDB ./...` was not), the implementation MUST add it via a Node fs round-trip (JSON.parse + JSON.stringify) to preserve 2-space indent + trailing newline; direct text Edit can drift the formatting. Forward-applicable to BE-0390..BE-0399 sibling stories.
ralph/progress.txt:432:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, fuzz-validators instance (BE-0388 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_fuzz_validators_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/validate/fuzz_test.go's new canonical pair TestFuzzValidatorContractCoversExpectedValidators + TestFuzzValidatorContractSeedCorpusRejectsHostileInputs + SECURITY.md "## Fuzz Validator Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: fuzz tests for validators (BE-0388)" subsection; TENTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0387 verification-suite mega-pattern, canonical command `go test -run TestFuzzValidator ./...`, CI step name `Fuzz validator tests`, verify.sh prefix #14). Four BE-0388-specific extensions to the template: (1) `CANONICAL PAIR WRAPS FuzzXxx TARGETS, NOT INDIVIDUAL VALIDATORS` — unlike BE-0386 (one fake, dual invariants) and BE-0387 (one redactor, dual invariants), BE-0388's load-bearing surface is a SET of nine FuzzXxx targets (FuzzName, FuzzPath, FuzzDomain, FuzzEnvVarName, FuzzEnvVarValue, FuzzDecodeJSON, FuzzImageRef, FuzzURL, FuzzGitBranch). The canonical-pair design splits the set into two orthogonal invariants: TestFuzzValidatorContractCoversExpectedValidators pins the CLOSED-SET COVERAGE — every public validator MUST have a FuzzXxx target, asserted by reading fuzz_test.go's own source via runtime.Caller and matching against `fuzzValidatorExpectedTargets` (the nine literal `func FuzzXxx(f *testing.F)` declarations). A new validator added to the public surface without a Fuzz target trips this matcher with the exact missing name. TestFuzzValidatorContractSeedCorpusRejectsHostileInputs pins the RUNTIME NO-PANIC invariant — the wrapper drives every validator through the shared `fuzzSeeds` corpus under a `recover()` guard so a panic surfaces with the offending validator name AND the seed index (the actionable-failure contract). Forward-applicable to ANY future verification story whose load-bearing surface is a SET of targets sharing one invariant + a closed-set coverage rule: BE-0394 (chaos Postgres disconnects — set of disconnect injection points), BE-0395 (idempotency replay — set of idempotency-protected endpoints), BE-0398 (tenant isolation — set of customer-data queries). (2) `Fuzz-PREFIX TARGETS RUN UNDER -run TestFuzzValidator AND -fuzz=FuzzXxx` — Go's fuzz tests (signature `func FuzzXxx(f *testing.F)`) run their seed corpus deterministically when invoked with `go test -run FuzzXxx` AND drive the randomised engine when invoked with `go test -fuzz=FuzzXxx`. The BE-0388 design KEEPS the existing FuzzXxx targets unchanged (they continue to support the optional `-fuzz=` driver for soak runs) AND adds a TestFuzzValidator-prefixed wrapper pair that binds to the `-run TestFuzzValidator ./...` filter on every `go test ./...` invocation. The wrapper does NOT replace the FuzzXxx targets — it complements them by adding a deterministic, name-anchored Test* binding to the canonical filter. The randomised driver remains an opt-in escape hatch documented in SECURITY.md (`-fuzz=Fuzz<Name>`); CI runs the wrapper, soak runs drive `-fuzz=`. (3) `SUBSTRING SET ANCHORS THE STRING "no panic" VERBATIM, NOT HYPHENATED FORMS` — first CI run failed because the SECURITY.md text used "no-panic" (hyphenated, sentence-internal) while the matcher pinned "no panic" (space-separated, quoted). The fix wrapped the canonical phrase in quotes in the SECURITY section ("the wrapper pins the 'no panic' invariant") so both the matcher and the human-readable doc land on the same literal. Forward-applicable pattern: when pinning a multi-word invariant as a closed substring, prefer quoting the invariant in the doc so the matcher binds to the literal a reviewer is most likely to write. Also: when the matcher's substring set contains both bare ("FuzzName") AND prefix-form ("FuzzDecodeJSON") names, the section MUST list the full FuzzXxx target set explicitly — handing the reader a sample like "(`Name`, `Path`, ...)" doesn't satisfy a matcher that pins on the literal "FuzzName" substring. (4) `VERIFY.SH RENUMBER OF OPTIONALS BLOCK — EIGHT INSERTIONS NOW (#6→#14)` — inserting required step #14 between #13 (redaction tests) and the optionals renumbered 14→15/15→16/16→17/17→18 in the same edit (govulncheck/staticcheck/golangci-lint/goreleaser). The previous count was SEVEN consecutive insertions (BE-0380..BE-0387). Per-story knobs for siblings: same as BE-0387's bullet — (a) canonical command from PRD, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (next number after last required step; renumber trailing optionals in same edit), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and 15-17-entry substring set (per-suite invariants — preserve closed-set discipline; list FULL target-name set explicitly when pinning a multi-target surface), (e) AGENTS.md subsection mirroring SECURITY.md but speaking to maintainers. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
ralph/progress.txt:435:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, quota-concurrency instance (BE-0384 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_quota_concurrency_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/quota/quota_test.go's new canonical pair TestQuotaConcurrencyHardLimitNeverOverallocates + TestQuotaConcurrencyTenantIsolation + SECURITY.md "## Quota Concurrency Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: quota concurrency tests (BE-0384)" subsection; SIXTH worked instance of the BE-0379/BE-0380/BE-0381/BE-0382/BE-0383 verification-suite mega-pattern). Three BE-0384-specific extensions to the template: (1) `RUN-FILTER PRE-EXISTING IN PRD` — the PRD's `verificationLoop.requiredBackendCommands` already contained `go test -run TestQuotaConcurrency ./...` before the story landed (same as BE-0383's policy-matrix entry; established by BE-0382's `EXISTING-TEST-SATISFIES-RUN-FILTER` sub-pattern). BE-0384 adds value via (a) wiring CI/verify.sh/CONTRIBUTING/SECURITY/AGENTS surfaces (the gate didn't exist as a named gate before), (b) ADDING canonical TestQuotaConcurrency* tests (the existing TestCheckerReserveConcurrentRespectsLimit / TestCheckerReserveTenantIsolation siblings stay; the new pair encodes the FOR UPDATE-lock and organization_id-predicate invariants under the load-bearing prefix), (c) pinning the existence of both via the static test. (2) `CANONICAL-PAIR INVARIANT-NAMING` — the canonical-funcs pair encodes the TWO load-bearing engine invariants: TestQuotaConcurrencyHardLimitNeverOverallocates pins the FOR UPDATE lock on the per-organization-per-resource usage counter row (parallel writers against ONE tenant — exactly limit succeed, the rest get yerr.CodeQuotaExceeded, the database row count agrees); TestQuotaConcurrencyTenantIsolation pins the organization_id predicate carried through every counter/sum/insert (parallel writers across TWO tenants — each respects its own limit AND the two tenants' active reservations sum to the two limits, never collapse into one bucket). The cross-tenant sum-check is the load-bearing defence-in-depth: per-tenant counts could pass while a regression silently dropped one tenant's rows entirely. The SECURITY.md substring set MUST include `FOR UPDATE` (the lock) and `organization_id` (the predicate) so an auditor verifies the invariant from the documented contract alone, without opening the test file. Forward-applicable to ANY future concurrency-verification story whose invariant has a "single-tenant hot path × cross-tenant isolation path" dual: BE-0386 (fake Dokploy), BE-0398 (tenant isolation), BE-0407 (service desired-state goldens). (3) `VERIFY.SH RENUMBER OF OPTIONALS BLOCK CONTINUES` — inserting required step #10 between #9 (policy matrix) and the optionals renumbered 10→11/11→12/12→13/13→14 in the same edit (govulncheck/staticcheck/golangci-lint/goreleaser). The static test pins `# 10. Required: quota concurrency tests` but NOT the optional headers — those shift on every required insertion. Established by BE-0380's renumber pattern. Per-story knobs for siblings: (a) canonical command (BE-0384 uses `go test -run TestQuotaConcurrency ./...` filter-narrowed; BE-0385 will use `go test -run TestFakeDokploy ./...`), (b) dedicated CI step name (BE-0384: "Quota concurrency tests"), (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (next number after the last required step; renumber trailing optionals in the same edit), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and 13-entry substring set covering scope / determinism / actionable-failures (engine-specific tokens: FOR UPDATE, organization_id, CodeQuotaExceeded) / opt-in-external / envelope-pinning / canonical-pair / redaction / CI-cadence / self-locating gate filename, (e) PRD requiredBackendCommands entry pre-existing OR newly inserted, (f) canonical reference test file path + canonical-pair func-name set + the `-run` filter prefix the canonical-funcs entries MUST satisfy. Load-bearing invariant: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six. GOTCHA: in TestQuotaConcurrencyTenantIsolation's per-org goroutine closure, do NOT alias `t` (the *testing.T) to a per-tenant tally bucket — `t := results[orgID]` SHADOWS the outer testing.T, silently breaking `t.Errorf` inside the closure. Used `bucket := results[orgID]` to keep the testing.T accessible for actionable diagnostics. Forward-applicable to any verification-suite story whose parallel-goroutine test groups results per-loop-var.
ralph/progress.txt:436:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, policy-matrix instance (BE-0383 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_policy_matrix_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/policy/policy_test.go's TestPolicyMatrix + the NEW sibling TestPolicyMatrixDeniesCrossTenant + SECURITY.md "## Policy Matrix Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: policy matrix tests (BE-0383)" subsection; FIFTH worked instance of the BE-0379/BE-0380/BE-0381/BE-0382 verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test mega-pattern). Three BE-0383-specific extensions to the template: (1) `-RUN-FILTER WITHOUT PACKAGE NARROWING` — unlike BE-0381/BE-0382 which used `go test ./internal/controlplane/<pkg>/...` (package-narrowed) as the canonical command, BE-0383 uses the PRD's `go test -run TestPolicyMatrix ./...` form unchanged (filter-narrowed across the entire module). The CI step, verify.sh step, CONTRIBUTING entry, SECURITY row, and PRD entry ALL agree on the exact same `go test -run TestPolicyMatrix ./...` literal — there's NO package-narrowed variant. This keeps the gate stable across hypothetical future packages that may declare a TestPolicyMatrix… test (e.g. an httpapi-layer policy-matrix integration test). The static test's canonical-command constant is the single command echoed verbatim across all five external surfaces. (2) `CROSS-TENANT MATRIX TWIN` — the canonical-funcs list is a PAIR (TestPolicyMatrix + TestPolicyMatrixDeniesCrossTenant), not a triple (BE-0382) or a single (hypothetical), because the policy engine has TWO load-bearing matrix invariants: in-tenant decisions (Decide's main path: ReasonAllowedSelf / ReasonAllowedByRole / ReasonDeniedNoCapability per `builtinRoleCaps[role].has(required)`) and cross-tenant decisions (Decide's cross-tenant clause: ReasonAllowedSelf for CapSelf, ReasonAllowedBySupport for support's CapSupport bridging CapRead/CapSupport only, ReasonDeniedCrossTenant for everything else). The two matrices are STRUCTURALLY IDENTICAL (same role × action cross-product, same engine, same fixtures) but differ in resource scope (resourceIn(orgA) vs resourceIn(orgB)) and in the expected-verdict switch. Forward-applicable to ANY future security-verification story whose invariant has a "happy path × foreign-scope path" dual: BE-0398 (tenant isolation), BE-0404 (break-glass), BE-0407 (service desired-state goldens). The cross-tenant variant's switch MUST encode the engine's exact cross-tenant clause — guessing it from intuition (e.g. assuming Support is always allowed cross-tenant) fails the matrix because the engine ALSO checks the required cap via `roleCaps.has(CapSupport) && (required == CapRead || required == CapSupport)`. Verify by tracing engine.Decide BEFORE writing the test's expected-verdict switch. (3) `EXISTING-TEST-SATISFIES-RUN-FILTER` — unlike BE-0382 where the `-run TestOpenAPI` filter previously matched zero tests (the existing openapi tests were `TestBuild…` / `TestOperation…`), BE-0383's `-run TestPolicyMatrix` filter ALREADY matched the existing `TestPolicyMatrix` function in internal/controlplane/policy/policy_test.go. The story still adds value by (a) wiring the dedicated CI/verify.sh/CONTRIBUTING/SECURITY surfaces (the gate did not exist as a named gate before), (b) enriching the canonical test set with the cross-tenant matrix twin so the canonical-funcs pair is load-bearing, and (c) pinning the existence of both via the static test. Per-story knobs for siblings: (a) canonical command (BE-0383 uses `go test -run TestPolicyMatrix ./...` filter-narrowed; BE-0384 will use `go test -run TestQuotaConcurrency ./...`), (b) dedicated CI step name (BE-0383: "Policy matrix tests"), (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (BE-0383 inserted `# 9. Required: policy matrix tests` between BE-0382's `# 8` and the previous `# 9. Optional: govulncheck`, renumbering optionals 9→10/10→11/11→12/12→13 in the same edit — established by BE-0380's renumber pattern), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and 13-entry substring set covering scope (internal/controlplane/policy) / determinism (deterministic fixtures) / actionable-failures (ReasonDeniedCrossTenant) / exceptions (CapSelf, CapSupport) / opt-in-external (YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY) / envelope-pinning (yalla.output.v1, yalla.error.v1) / matrix-pair (TestPolicyMatrix, TestPolicyMatrixDeniesCrossTenant) / redaction (redacted) / CI-cadence (Every push and PR) / self-locating gate filename (verification_suite_policy_matrix_static_test.go), (e) PRD requiredBackendCommands entry already present unchanged (BE-0383 did NOT add — the existing entry satisfies the matcher), (f) canonical reference test file path + matrix-pair func-name set + the `-run` filter prefix the canonical-funcs entries MUST satisfy. Load-bearing invariant: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
ralph/progress.txt:453:  - Organization-scoped admin routes still use `organizationIDResolver`, so tests must authenticate in the target org or intentionally assert cross-tenant denial.
ralph/progress.txt:501:- Tamper-resistance three-wall verification pattern (BE-0353, internal/controlplane/store/{audit_tamper_static_test.go, audit_test.go::TestAuditUpdateBlockedByDatabaseTriggerWithRestrictViolation} + internal/controlplane/store/migrate/audit_immutability_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/store/AGENTS.md "Tamper resistance" subsection; structural template for any future append-only-table story whose immutability is enforced at the database trigger AND the Go repository surface — security events, key-rotation history, billing-ledger entries, fraud alerts, anything where "the record cannot change after write" is a load-bearing contract). The pattern is THREE static-time guards plus ONE runtime probe so a weakening of any one layer is caught by another. Five load-bearing rules: (1) MIGRATION-shape pin (parse the .up.sql via embedded fs and assert four regex shapes: function declared with `ERRCODE = '<sqlstate-name>'`, trigger declared with `BEFORE UPDATE ON <table> FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION <fn>`, the `CREATE TABLE` parenthesised body extracted and scanned for any forbidden column declaration, the `.down.sql` drops both the table AND the function — DROP TABLE alone leaves the function orbiting unused). The column-scan strips `--`-prefixed lines BEFORE matching so an in-body comment like `-- intentionally no updated_at column` does not trip the probe; the matcher anchors on `^(?:\s|,)*<column>\s+[a-zA-Z]` so a bare token mentioned in prose elsewhere does not match. (2) Go-SURFACE pin (parse the implementation .go via the existing mustParseFile helper, walk file.Decls, collect every FuncDecl whose receiver is the named type, and assert the set is exactly the allowed names AND each name does not start with a known mutation-verb prefix `{Update,Delete,Patch,Replace,Modify,Truncate,Remove}` — the verb prefix is a SECOND filter so a future allowed-set drift that re-admits a mutation-shape name still fails); also pin the struct declaration has no mutation-timestamp field `{UpdatedAt,ModifiedAt,RevisedAt,LastModifiedAt}` — the SQL column absence is the migration's job, the Go field absence is the surface's job, both must hold. (3) DML-SCAN pin (walk every non-test `.go` in the owning package, find every `*ast.BasicLit` of `token.STRING` kind, unwrap raw-string and double-quoted shapes, normalise whitespace and case, and reject any literal that contains a forbidden DML substring against the target table; only the carved-out INSERT shape inside the repository's own file is allowed — every other shape is a regression flagged with `file:line: forbidden DML against <table>: "UPDATE <table>" in literal`). The case-insensitive uppercase normalisation handles lowercase shapes that snuck in via copy-paste; the whitespace normalisation handles multi-line literals like `UPDATE\n  audit_events\nSET ...`. Comments are NOT scanned — the rule targets executable code paths and a SQL example in a doc comment is fine. (4) RUNTIME-wire pin (insert a fixture row, attempt every UPDATE shape an operator might try — by id, by tenant id, table-wide without WHERE, partial-column-rewrite, verdict-reclassification — and assert each rejection surfaces a `*pgconn.PgError` with `pgErr.Code == "<sqlstate>"` AND `strings.Contains(pgErr.Message, "<canonical phrase>")` AND the row's columns are byte-identical to the original after the attempted UPDATE; PG's `restrict_violation` is SQLSTATE 23001 NOT 38000 — 38000 is external_routine_exception, an easy typo because both classes start with a 3, verify the actual wire code by running once and reading the failure). Also assert the error message does NOT echo any caller input — the trigger function is value-free by design and a future log path that interpolates the rejected payload would surface here as a redaction regression. (5) SELF-CHECK pin (drive each matcher with synthetic known-good AND known-bad inputs in subtests so a future over-tightening that false-positives on real production code AND a future under-tightening that misses a real regression are BOTH caught — the self-check is the only safety net against "I weakened my own analyser without noticing"). Six asymmetries worth knowing: (a) `audit_events` allows direct SQL `DELETE FROM ...` because the design requires ON DELETE CASCADE to work for tenant teardown — so the DML-scan flags DELETE FROM at the Go layer (no app-side delete) but the runtime test does NOT assert DELETE is blocked at the DB layer (it isn't). The migration AGENTS.md section documents this asymmetry so a future reader doesn't try to "fix" it. (b) The wire SQLSTATE for the migration's `RAISE EXCEPTION ... ERRCODE = 'restrict_violation'` is 23001 (class 23 integrity_constraint_violation) — PG remaps the symbolic name to a numeric code at trigger compile time, the SQL pins the name, the runtime test pins the code, both must stay synchronised. (c) Mutation-verb prefix detection deliberately EXCLUDES `Set` and `Get` — too broad to flag without false positives across unrelated repositories — and the closed verb set lives in `isMutationSurfaceMethodName` as a helper rather than inline so adding a new verb is a single change. (d) The struct-field forbidden list does NOT include `UpdatedBy` / `LastEditor` — those are actor-attribution fields, not mutation timestamps; the rule targets writes-to-the-record, not metadata about who touched it. (e) The migration column-scan strips `--` line comments before matching but does NOT strip `/* */` block comments — PG accepts both, but `audit_events.up.sql` uses `--` exclusively and the simpler scanner is enough; if a future migration mixes styles, extend the scanner with the same upper-vs-lower test pattern. (f) The runtime test reuses the existing `auditEventFixture` / `seedOrg` / `appendAudit` helpers from `audit_test.go` so the new test is ~150 lines of mostly cases-and-assertions; the wire-error assertions live in a `*pgconn.PgError` `errors.As` extraction (NOT a string match on err.Error()) so a future error-wrapping change at the pgx layer that preserves the typed error still surfaces the assertion.
ralph/progress.txt:503:- Inbound-hostname domain-takeover guard, symmetric to the SSRF outbound-URL guard (BE-0350 pattern, internal/controlplane/validate/{domain_takeover.go, domain_takeover_static_test.go, domain_takeover_test.go, AGENTS.md} + network.go Domain() call site + fuzz_test.go FuzzDomain seeds; second worked port of the BE-0349 two-test SSRF template into the validate package, but for a STRUCTURALLY DIFFERENT threat: INBOUND hostnames the platform agrees to ROUTE traffic for, NOT outbound URLs the worker dereferences). Six load-bearing rules the new file added on top of the inherited BE-0349 template: (a) The takeover guard MUST live in a separate helper (`disallowedTakeoverHost`) and a separate static analyzer (`callsDisallowedTakeoverHost`) from the SSRF guard — they share the two-test template but check DISJOINT sets: a host that is legitimate to ATTACH (`myapp.example.com`) may still be illegitimate to FETCH if it resolves to a private IP, and vice versa. Reusing the SSRF helper would collapse both threat models into one, hiding which rejection class applies to which surface. (b) The guard's call site goes AT THE END of `Domain` (after the FQDN/label checks), not at the start. Single-label reserved names (`localhost`, `local`, `internal`, `lan`, etc.) and IPv6 literals (a single label after the `.` split) are rejected by the existing FQDN check BEFORE reaching the guard — so the test table for `TestDomainRejectsTakeoverTargets` restricts itself to inputs that actually pass through the helper today (IPv4 dotted literals, multi-label reserved exact-matches like `home.arpa`, suffix-match cases, shared-hosting eTLD bare forms). The single-label reserved names stay in `reservedTakeoverHostnames` as defence-in-depth (a future FQDN-check loosening must still trip the takeover guard) but the runtime regression table only asserts on what's actually reachable, so a missing call site is caught by a FAILING subtest rather than a silently-skipped one. Document the layering explicitly in the file's package-doc block AND in the test file's table-header comment, so the next reader doesn't add an unreachable case and then debug for an hour. (c) The reserved-name lists must DELIBERATELY EXCLUDE the RFC 2606 documentation-only suffixes (`.test`, `.example`, `.invalid`) from the SUFFIX walk while KEEPING them in the EXACT-MATCH map. Reason: those suffixes are not publicly routable so they cannot intercept real customer traffic — they are a squatting threat (bounded by the `service_domains` UNIQUE (hostname, path) constraint) rather than a takeover threat. The httpapi service-domains test fixtures across ~7 files (`api.canonical.example`, `domain-matrix-recognisable-needle.example`, etc.) use `.example` per RFC convention; adding `.example` to the suffix walk would break every one of those tests, and the cost is asymmetric: a real takeover defence would require migrating the fixtures whereas a softer squatting defence is bounded by the UNIQUE constraint and adds no value to either threat model. Document the omission AND the asymmetry in the helper's package-doc, the AGENTS.md section, and the test file's blocked-hosts table comment — so a future reader doesn't "fix" the omission and re-break the fixtures. (d) The wildcard interaction: `Domain` strips the leading `*.` BEFORE my helper sees the host. With wildcards enabled, `*.appspot.com` reduces to `appspot.com` which the exact-match map rejects — a wildcard makes the takeover STRICTLY WORSE (the bare suffix locks one routing entry, the wildcard locks every sub-tenant of that suffix) so the wildcard rejection is load-bearing and gets its own test (`TestDomainRejectsTakeoverTargetsForWildcard`). The helper ALSO defensively strips a leading `*.` itself so it remains correct for any future caller that passes the raw value. (e) The redaction backstop (`TestDomainTakeoverReasonsAreValueFree`) must plant the canary as a SUB-LABEL of a reserved-SUFFIX host (`canary + ".lan"`), NOT a reserved-EXACT-MATCH host (`canary + "appspot.com"` would not match the exact-match map). The suffix walk's `strings.HasSuffix(h, suffix)` reduces the entire host to the suffix-class reason without inspecting the canary — so a reason containing the canary proves the validator interpolated the value. The canary string itself MUST be picked to NOT collide with any word in the canonical reason set (no "domain", "namespace", "address", "literal", "hostname") — the BE-0343 redaction lesson is that substring redaction checks false-positive on stable classification phrases. (f) The FuzzDomain seed set added one entry per documented reject category PLUS an accept-branch re-derivation: `net.ParseIP(v) != nil` and `strings.HasSuffix(v, reservedSuffix)` MUST be false for every accepted host. The accept-branch re-derivation is what makes the fuzz target actually catch a regression of the takeover guard — without it, dropping the helper would let IPv4-literal seeds pass through to the accept branch without firing any assertion (the existing FQDN+label re-derivation accepts `8.8.8.8`). The reservedSuffixes slice in the fuzz target is a DUPLICATE of the package-private one — that is the cost of the validate_test (`_test` package) split: a future helper change must update BOTH lists or `TestDomainTakeoverListsAreCanonical` fires for the package list and the fuzz target silently accepts the dropped category. Document the duplication in the seed-loop comment.
ralph/progress.txt:599:  - The fake-Dokploy guide must keep redacted env placeholders, `output.Sentinel`, stable envelopes, tenant-scoped `dokploy_refs`, and exact fake-Dokploy verification commands visible because the static artifact test pins those strings.
ralph/progress.txt:614:- Engine-only policy-matrix file pattern for CapSupport actions (BE-0285 admin.reconcile, applicable to admin.import / admin.read / admin.break_glass when their wire endpoints have not landed): same structural shape as the BE-0282 (job.cancel) CapDeploy template, but with three load-bearing flips: (1) role matrix collapses to one allow (Support) + five denies — the photo-negative of CapDeploy actions, (2) cross-tenant test flips Support from DENY (ReasonDeniedCrossTenant) to ALLOW (ReasonAllowedBySupport) because CapSupport sits INSIDE the engine's `required == CapRead || CapSupport` exception clause — the single most load-bearing distinction from BE-0279/0282 and the structural barrier against any future catalog drift that demoted admin.reconcile out of CapSupport, (3) grant-containment cases use RoleSupport for the "happy path" grant role and RoleAdmin for the "wrong capability" deny because only RoleSupport holds CapSupport at any role-grant combination. Resource Kind is KindOrganization with only Scope.OrganizationID pinned (admin actions operate on the source-of-truth view of an entire organization, no project/env/svc leg), so every project/env/service-scoped grant denies via ReasonDeniedOutOfScope (one-way covers()) — admin actions are unreachable through any scoped grant other than org-level Support. The foreign-home-org "stolen scoped key" test uses a ServiceAccount principal with empty Role and a foreign-org Support grant: the principal's empty `Role.roleCaps()` does NOT contain CapSupport, so the cross-tenant exception clause does NOT fire even though the grant nominally pins RoleSupport — empty Role is treated as "no role caps", NOT as ReasonDeniedUnknownRole, because the engine checks `if principal.Role != "" && !roleOK` before denying unknown roles. Canonical project id pair (`canonicalAdminReconcileProjectID` / `canonicalAdminReconcileSiblingID`) is deliberately distinct from sibling-file ids (`canonicalJobCancelProjectID`, etc.) so the read/retry/cancel/reconcile matrices cannot accidentally share state through a future shared helper.
ralph/progress.txt:619:- Repository tenant-isolation for a tenant-root table (BE-0426 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/organization_tenant_isolation_test.go; structural template for BE-0428/0430/0432/0434/0436 and every future tenant-root-table isolation story): the strongest isolation guarantee in the codebase lives on the row-id-keyed tenant root (organizations) and the proof shape is "byte-identical snapshot of the OTHER tenant across every mutation path the repository exposes". Skeleton: one Go test file per table, one t.Parallel test per (mutation × leg) — Get returns-only-named-tenant + Get unknown-id-is-NotFound (cross-tenant + missing render the same yerr.CodeNotFound — no oracle); Update only-touches-named-tenant (snapshot orgB, Update orgA, re-read orgB, assert byte-identical across slug, display_name, version, updated_at, deletion_scheduled_at; assert orgA's update DID land so a no-op repository can't pass trivially); Update-with-mismatched-If-Match also-does-not-touch-orgB (catches "fall back to unconditional UPDATE on stale" regressions); ScheduleDeletion only-touches-named-tenant (stamp on orgA, orgB byte-identical incl. DeletionScheduledAt == nil); Get-of-soft-deleted-tenant-does-not-mask-other (soft-delete is per-row, not SQL-level hide; the scheduled row is still readable and orgB unchanged); Insert enforces-global-slug-uniqueness (typed yerr.CodeConflict + non-empty user-facing Message; first row's slug unchanged; second row's id is yerr.CodeNotFound on follow-up Get — the failed Insert lands no row). Three load-bearing rules: (1) anchor on BOTH version (trigger-bumped on every UPDATE so a missing WHERE bumps every row) AND updated_at (trigger-refreshed; same property) — either one catches a WHERE-less UPDATE independently of the other, keep both; (2) the "stale-If-Match" leg is structurally distinct from the "successful update" leg and exercises the conflict-detection path which historically falls back to extra reads/writes that could leak across tenants; (3) Insert conflict-message assertions anchor on yerr.CodeConflict + non-empty Message ONLY, never on a substring (the static "an organization with this slug already exists" message is a stable contract but story-specific prose checks are brittle). Tenant-root tables have NO ListByParent method at this layer (no parent to scope by) — the "list queries don't leak total counts" acceptance criterion is vacuously satisfied for tenant-root tables; the corresponding proof for /v1/me/organizations lives in membership tests. Document this absence in the test file's package-doc comment so a future reader does not look for a missing test. CHILD-TABLE variant (BE-0428+ for users/memberships/projects/environments/services/api_keys/etc.) extends this skeleton with one additional leg: a cross-tenant parent_id probe — passing org A's parent_id (project_id, etc.) alongside org B's organization_id must render NotFound, NEVER A's row, NEVER a 500 leaking the cause. Use docker compose up -d postgres + YALLA_TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgres://yalla:yalla@127.0.0.1:5432/yalla go test -race -count=1 -run 'TestXxxRepository' to verify; the suite skips cleanly when the env var is unset, so CI without Postgres remains green. Known pre-existing -race-with-DB failures in environment-service / api-key-service Create paths and organization_variable_patch audit metadata are NOT introduced by this pattern (confirmed by stashing the new test file and re-running each failing case); a focused triage story should pick them up before BE-0500+ verification stories run.
ralph/progress.txt:621:- Cross-cutting policy package = retention-shaped leaf (BE-0038 pattern, internal/controlplane/retention/{doc.go, AGENTS.md, policy.go, policy_test.go}, third instance of the pure-package layout after reconcile and migrateimport): canonical shape for ANY cross-cutting authority over a closed-set domain enum + a validated config + boundary-style predicates — retention windows, rate-limit tiers, idempotency horizons, audit-redaction policies, plan-limit ladders, etc. Layout: doc.go (boundary + the invariants the predicates encode), policy.go (closed-set ResourceKind + a Window struct of just-durations + an immutable Policy value with a private map; DefaultPolicy() returning the production values + NewPolicy(map) that validates every kind is present and every Window passes validate() + copies the input map so caller mutation cannot tamper with a live Policy + Window/Restorable/RestorableAt/RestoreDeadline/RemainingRestore/ReapableAt/ReapDeadline/AllowsSlugReuse predicates over (kind, stampedAt, now)), policy_test.go (every kind × every transition + boundary case + zero-value-Policy safety + NewPolicy validation table + caller-mutation defense + cross-predicate coherence (restorable → held → reapable monotone) + closed-set guard via AllKinds()), AGENTS.md (boundary + invariants). Three load-bearing rules: (1) the boundary comparisons MUST be strict-less-than at the upper bound of RestorableAt and `>=` at the lower bound of ReapableAt so the two predicates are mutually exclusive at the exact instant of transition (a row never reports as both restorable and reapable at the same nanosecond); (2) the package is a LEAF — it imports only std-lib (time, fmt, sort, errors-only-from-tests) and is imported by store/httpapi/worker, so its predicates are cheap to call from anywhere without dragging a database or network dependency along; never let retention import apierr/store/audit; (3) "soft delete where safe" in the BE-0038 description is a real qualifier — memberships do not get a soft-delete column because role_version invalidates sessions at the auth layer and audit_events outlives the resource via the organization-level cascade, so KindMembership.SoftDeleteColumn() returns "" and Policy.SupportsSoftDelete(KindMembership) is false by design (a future schema migration that adds a removed_at column to memberships must update both in lock-step, and the closed-set test will fail until they do). Two follow-up wirings the package was designed for but does not yet require: a future teardown worker (BE-0203+) iterates retention.AllKinds(), looks up each kind's SoftDeleteColumn(), scans rows where the column is non-NULL and the stamp is older than ReapAfter, and calls the kind-specific hard-delete repository method; a future CapAdmin admin-purge endpoint calls retention.Policy.AllowsSlugReuse before bypassing the wait — the slug-reuse predicate is the contract those two consumers will share. Forward-applicable to every future cross-cutting policy module (plan tiers, audit redaction horizons, idempotency-key TTL, rate-limit ramp).
ralph/progress.txt:622:- Pure orchestrator / workflow package = reconcile-shaped vertical (BE-0033 pattern, internal/controlplane/migrateimport/{doc.go, AGENTS.md, types.go, importer.go, importer_test.go}, the second instance of this shape after internal/controlplane/reconcile): canonical shape for ANY workflow that ingests external state, classifies it, and writes through ports — Dokploy import, drift reconciliation, billing reconciliation, compliance scanning, lifecycle teardown, etc. Package layout: doc.go (boundary + plan/apply + dry-run + redaction sections), AGENTS.md (conventions), types.go (every port interface PLUS every value type those interfaces reference — Scanner/Repository ports, Snapshot/Existing*/Create*Input value types, OwnerAssignment, PlanItem and Plan with stable JSON tags because the Plan IS the dry-run yalla.output.v1 payload; ItemStatus + ItemReason as the closed classification surface), importer.go (Config with Scanner + Repository + Logger + Now + Redactor; New() returns apierr.Internal on a missing port; Plan(ctx, in) is pure and never writes; Apply(ctx, plan) is best-effort and collects per-item failures in Result.Failures with redacted error strings; classify*() helpers are unit-testable in isolation; wrap{Scanner,Repository}Err() preserves catalogued apierr.* codes and upgrades untyped errors to apierr.DokployUnavailable (scanner) / apierr.StoreUnavailable (repository) / apierr.Internal (defensive contract assertions); a resolvedParents map seeded with the org-root entry lets the apply loop resolve child-after-parent in a single pass through the hierarchy-ordered items). The test file houses an in-package fakeScanner + fakeRepo with concurrent-safe mutexes and call recording, and covers: construction port validation (one nil per case → CodeInternal); Plan input validation (every required field, every kind/format check → CodeInvalidInput with the named field recoverable via apierr.ViolationsOf); the dry-run-doesn't-query-Repository contract (no-assignment Plan never calls the repo); every classification Status × Reason combination as a positive case (StatusReady / StatusSkipDuplicate / StatusSkipMissingParent / StatusSkipUnsupported / StatusSkipAlreadyImported / StatusPendingOwner); Scanner failure surfaces as the right apierr code (DokployUnavailable → yerr.CodeServer, NOT CodeUnavailable — that's for Yalla-owned dependencies); Repository failure preserves catalogued codes; secrets redacted in error strings AND in log buffer (slog over a bytes.Buffer, output.NewRedactor(secret), assert secret never appears); Apply persists in hierarchy order (organization → project → environment → service, asserted by scanning the recorded calls slice for ordering invariants); Apply continues past per-item failures (inject failServiceByDokployID for one specific service create, prove sibling creates still succeeded); Apply ctx-cancellation propagates (errors.Is(err, context.Canceled)); Apply on no-ready-items is a valid no-op accounting Skipped count. The Plan JSON shape is PUBLIC compatibility contract before the customer-facing endpoint even exists — stable field names, no pointers in PlanItem, no embedded interfaces, no time.Time on the wire — because the eventual POST endpoint (BE-0289 et al) serialises it as-is. Canonical test fixture ID format: kind prefix + "_" + 26-char Crockford base32 lowercase suffix from "0123456789abcdefghjkmnpqrstvwxyz" (no i, l, o, u); use "org_01hz00000000000000000000xx" style (4-char prefix + 20 zeros + 2-char tag = 26) — the testutil factory's IDs are deliberately non-canonical for some tests and will fail strict ParseID. Slug derivation goes one-way: domain.NormalizeSlug(dokployName) for the Dokploy → Yalla direction (a NormalizeSlug rejection is itself a classification, NOT an error to propagate); domain.DokployName(label, yallaID) is the OPPOSITE direction and must not be reused here. supportedServiceType(svc) AND-s dokploy.ServiceType.Valid() with the database-requires-engine rule and collapses both failure modes into a single StatusSkipUnsupported reason — the operator's remediation is the same. Reconcile and migrateimport are siblings under this pattern; future "resource lifecycle worker", "drift admin reconciler", "billing reconciler" packages should reuse the package layout verbatim.
ralph/progress.txt:624:- PATCH endpoint for a deep resource by bare id (BE-0157 pattern, PATCH /v1/environments/{environment_id}, store/environment.go EnvironmentRepository.Update + classifyEnvironmentConcurrencyMiss, store/environmentservice.go EnvironmentService.Update + buildEnvironmentUpdate, httpapi/environments.go updateEnvironmentHandler): canonical shape for ALL deep-resource PATCH endpoints. Repository.Update takes a fully-formed row + *int64 ifMatchVersion, runs a tenant-scoped (organization_id, id) UPDATE that touches only the mutable columns (slug, display_name — NEVER the composite tenant key), and classifies a zero-row return via a classify*ConcurrencyMiss helper that disambiguates gone-vs-stale by Get-in-same-tx. The service-layer orchestrator runs the validation BEFORE the transaction is opened (buildXxxUpdate is split out for unit-testability, rejects empty patches as 400, never echoes submitted values in violations), then in ONE Store.Write: read current row, check If-Match pre-condition (stale -> ConflictStale with row's authoritative version BEFORE the version-checked UPDATE — even a trivial no-op patch with stale If-Match surfaces the conflict), apply patch in-memory, call Repository.Update, append audit row. UNLIKE Create, the orchestrator does NOT invoke the in-tx Authorizer or QuotaReserver — the HTTP RequireAuth gate is authoritative for the update path; the in-tx Authorize is reserved for Create's defence-in-depth against grant-change races during quota reservation. The HTTP handler reuses the shared parseIfMatchVersion + writeOrganizationETag helpers (yes, the helper is org-named but it is the SHARED chokepoint for every endpoint mirroring versions to ETag — do not duplicate it per resource family). Body is strictly decoded with `slug *string` and `display_name *string` (pointer nil-ness IS the partial-update contract); the body intentionally exposes no organization_id/parent_id/self_id field — tenant isolation is structural. Response is yalla.output.v1 carrying the same wire shape GET returns, plus ETag header. Path with NO parent_id (bare-id route): the resource scope pins only OrganizationID + the deep id, so the engine's covers() rule DENIES every scoped grant at the policy boundary (a grant with a pinned ProjectID cannot cover a resource with no ProjectID); principals whose only access is a scoped grant must use a parent-scoped route. Every NewHandler positional-arg addition triggers a ~50-site sweep across ~35 existing test files — anchor on `fakeXxxReader{}, nil)` and `fakeXxxReader{}, logger)` plus the `reader, nil)` / `reader, logger)` outliers in the resource's own get/policy test helpers. The newRouteTable-only OpenAPI shape tests (all-nils form, partial-fakes-with-trailing-nils form) each need one more `nil` appended. AVOID double-counting: run go vet AFTER each automated sweep and undo overshoots before continuing.
ralph/progress.txt:625:- DELETE endpoint for a deep resource by bare id (BE-0160 pattern, DELETE /v1/environments/{environment_id}, migration 0016_environment_deletion_schedule, store/environment.go EnvironmentRepository.ScheduleDeletion, store/environmentservice.go EnvironmentService.ScheduleDeletion + DeleteEnvironmentInput, httpapi/environments.go deleteEnvironmentHandler + EnvironmentDeleter): canonical shape for ALL deep-resource SOFT-delete endpoints. The migration is structurally identical to 0013_project_deletion_schedule — `ALTER TABLE <resource> ADD COLUMN deletion_scheduled_at timestamptz` + a partial index `WHERE deletion_scheduled_at IS NOT NULL` for the future teardown-worker scan. The repository ScheduleDeletion is an SQL-IDEMPOTENT unconditional UPDATE stamping `deletion_scheduled_at = now()` returning the row in column order (so callers see the trigger-refreshed updated_at + bumped version); the already-scheduled distinction is the service's job (the repository must remain SQL-idempotent for non-customer callers — workers, admin jobs — that retry). ifMatchVersion uses the SAME pattern as Update — nil disables, non-nil adds `AND version = $N` to the WHERE — and classify*ConcurrencyMiss disambiguates the no-rows case (gone vs stale) by Get-in-same-tx. The service-layer orchestrator validates blank-input BEFORE opening the tx (typed InvalidInput, missing actor org is Internal — wiring error, never client input), then in ONE Store.Write: read current row, check If-Match pre-condition (stale -> ConflictStale BEFORE the already-scheduled check, so a stale caller learns version-stale instead of an already-scheduled message that races with concurrent edits they did not see), reject already-scheduled as apierr.Conflict (rolls the whole tx back so the audit record can never name a deletion that did not change resource state), call Repository.ScheduleDeletion, record the resolved deletion_scheduled_at stamp as audit metadata (`event.Metadata["deletion_scheduled_at"] = row.DeletionScheduledAt.UTC().Format(time.RFC3339Nano)` — a non-secret timestamp, safe to record verbatim), append audit row. UNLIKE Create, the orchestrator does NOT invoke the in-tx Authorizer or QuotaReserver — the HTTP RequireAuth gate is authoritative for the delete path; the in-tx Authorize is reserved for Create's defence-in-depth against grant-change races during quota reservation. The HTTP handler reuses parseIfMatchVersion + writeOrganizationETag + telemetry.FromContext for correlation; returns 202 Accepted (the scheduling is durable but the destructive teardown is a later worker job). The wire DTO (projectEnvironment / projectResource) gains a `DeletionScheduledAt *string \`json:"deletion_scheduled_at,omitempty"\`` field so a live row's JSON shape is byte-identical to before (omitempty + nil pointer = field absent), while a scheduled row exposes the stamp as RFC 3339 — mirror this on every resource DTO that gains a soft-delete column. Routes.go registration uses `Method: http.MethodDelete`, `SuccessStatus: http.StatusAccepted`, the same `environmentIDResolver` as GET/PATCH (the bare-id three routes share one resolver), and the same description shape as `deleteProject`'s scheduled-not-immediate / version-pre-check / one-tx-audit clauses. NewHandler+newRouteTable signatures each grow ONE positional port (`environmentDeleter EnvironmentDeleter` at the very tail of the env ports group, after `environmentUpdater`); a Python regex sweep over the test directory anchored on `fakeEnvironmentUpdater{}, nil)` -> `fakeEnvironmentUpdater{}, fakeEnvironmentDeleter{}, nil)` and `fakeEnvironmentUpdater{}, logger)` (37 NewHandler call sites across 37 files) handled the bulk; the named-updater outlier `fakeEnvironmentReader{}, updater, nil)` in the resource's own update test got `..., updater, fakeEnvironmentDeleter{}, nil)`; the all-nil newRouteTable form (7 files) received one trailing nil; the partial-fakes-with-trailing-nils form (projects_delete_test.go + projects_restore_test.go ending `fakeProjectGrantReader{}, nil, nil, nil, nil, nil, nil, nil)`) needed one MORE trailing nil; the multi-line newRouteTable-with-explicit-fakes form (3 files ending `fakeEnvironmentUpdater{})`) became `fakeEnvironmentUpdater{}, fakeEnvironmentDeleter{})`. Run `go vet ./...` AFTER each sweep — Go's positional-arg compile error tells you exactly which call sites the regex missed. The store-side integration tests cover repo happy-path + NotFound + tenant-scoped + nil-tx-rejection plus service happy-path-with-audit + already-scheduled-rolls-back-AND-stamp-unchanged-AND-only-one-audit-row + NotFound-no-audit + tenant-scoped-no-audit + stale-If-Match + blank-input + missing-actor-org. The httpapi contract tests cover happy-path-forwards-(principal,path,if-match)+202+ETag+wire-DTO + named-If-Match forwarded + malformed-If-Match 400 + NotFound 404 + already-scheduled 409 + stale-If-Match 409 + missing-deleter 500 + unauthenticated 401 + OpenAPI route registered. Forward-applicable to every future bare-id soft-delete endpoint (service.delete on /v1/services/{service_id}, etc.).
ralph/progress.txt:626:- New top-level resource family that already has its persistence table from an earlier migration (BE-0148 pattern, environments table from 0002 + version column from 0011, store/environment.go, httpapi/project_environments.go): a reader-only scoped-child vertical lands JUST the GET endpoint and pairs with the existing table — NO new migration. The vertical is structurally identical to BE-0142 (project_variables reader) with one narrowing worth pinning: when the table already exists (environments was created by 0002_tenant_hierarchy.up.sql at the same time as projects/services, and gained its version + bump_version trigger in 0011_optimistic_versions.up.sql), the story collapses into store layer + http layer + wiring + tests — no migration to declare composite FKs, scope-uniqueness indexes, or triggers. The store layer (Environment struct, EnvironmentRepository.ListByProject, EnvironmentReader.ListProjectEnvironments) mirrors ProjectVariableRepository/ProjectVariableReader exactly — every read is tenant-scoped at the SQL predicate, the reader Gets the project first inside the same Store.Read tx so a cross-tenant or unknown project_id surfaces as apierr.NotFound (never an empty list), and the repository has its own list cap (environmentListMaxRows = 500). Crucially, environments carry NO credential material (the table stores only id, organization_id, project_id, slug, display_name, version, created_at, updated_at) so the HTTP projection (projectEnvironmentOf) needs no value-redaction chokepoint — every field is structural — but the projection STILL exists as a single chokepoint so a future column added to store.Environment is reviewed for its wire exposure here rather than leaking by default. The integration tests (store/environment_test.go) prove ordering, tenant scoping (a cross-tenant (orgA, projB_id) pair yields no rows), the empty-project shape (non-nil empty slice), the reader's project existence check, and that a cross-tenant id surfaces as apierr.NotFound WITHOUT leaking any of the foreign tenant's environment id / project id / slug into the error message. The HTTP layer adds a new operation tag (tagEnvironments = "environments") because environments will get their own /v1/environments/{environment_id} surface later; the list endpoint is tagged [tagProjects, tagEnvironments] mirroring how project_variables uses [tagProjects, tagVariables]. NewHandler and newRouteTable signatures each grow ONE positional port (projectEnvironments ProjectEnvironmentReader at the TAIL of the arg list, after projectVariableReplacer); cmd/yalla-api/main.go wires *store.EnvironmentReader at startup. A Python regex sweep over 33 test files anchored on `fakeProjectVariableReplacer{}, nil)` -> `fakeProjectVariableReplacer{}, fakeProjectEnvironmentReader{}, nil)` (44 sites) plus a second sweep on `fakeProjectVariableReplacer{}, logger)` (server_test.go + me_contract_test.go) plus the named-replacer outlier `fakeProjectVariableReader{}, replacer, nil)` -> `..., replacer, fakeProjectEnvironmentReader{}, nil)` (project_variables_test.go's replaceProjectVariablesHandlerFor) plus the openapi-only newRouteTable form `fakeProjectVariableReader{}, fakeProjectVariableReplacer{})` -> `..., fakeProjectEnvironmentReader{})` (project_variables_test.go's BE-0142 OpenAPI test) handled the NewHandler/newRouteTable churn (54 sites total). The all-nil newRouteTable form with 32 nils received one trailing nil to become 33 (6 sites). projects_delete_test.go + projects_restore_test.go newRouteTable calls ended `fakeProjectGrantReader{}, nil, nil, nil)` and got one MORE trailing nil to become `nil, nil, nil, nil)`. The KindEnvironment domain Kind ("env") already existed and is in the valid-kinds map from earlier work; no domain/id.go changes were needed. Forward-applicable to every future reader-only vertical on a hierarchy resource whose table predates the story.
ralph/progress.txt:628:- env.read-on-{project_id}-resolver policy matrix (BE-0144, internal/controlplane/httpapi/projects_variables_get_policy_test.go) is the structural sibling of the project.grants.read-on-{project_id}-resolver matrix (BE-0138, projects_grants_policy_test.go) — same CapRead capability class, same projectIDResolver scope (`{principal home org, path project_id}`), same six-roles-all-allow matrix via ReasonAllowedByRole, same support cross-tenant exception at the engine (ReasonAllowedBySupport — pinned even though projectIDResolver structurally blocks it on the customer-facing wire), same engine cross-tenant deny matrix for non-support roles, same scoped-grant containment shape (sibling project denied, env-/service-scoped grants denied against the project resource, org-level Viewer grant allowed via ReasonAllowedByGrant). The load-bearing distinction is the wire-redaction chokepoint: project_variables rows carry credential material (a non-null Value that flips to output.Sentinel when IsSecret), so the deny-body leak guard (variablesDenyBodyLeak) anchors on BOTH structural identifiers (canonical project/var ids, keys, non-secret values) AND the seeded secret plaintext (canonicalVariablesSecretPlaintext). project_grants rows store no credential material, so grantsDenyBodyLeak omits a plaintext anchor. The allow-path test also asserts secret rows project as output.Sentinel and that the seeded plaintext never appears in the response body — pinning projectVariableOf as the chokepoint independent of how authority was earned (role / org-level grant / project-level grant). Forward-applicable to every future env.read matrix that resolves into a project-, env-, or service-level scope on a variables route: keep the role / disabled / wrong-org / grant-containment skeleton, swap the action+resolver for the new endpoint's, and ALWAYS add a secret-plaintext needle to the leak guard plus a Sentinel-projection assertion to every allow-path tie-in.
ralph/progress.txt:690:- environment.read-on-bare-{environment_id}-resolver policy matrix (BE-0156, internal/controlplane/httpapi/environments_get_policy_test.go) is the structural sibling of the {project_id}-resolver siblings (BE-0144 env.read on the variables route, BE-0150 environment.read on the parent-scoped route) for endpoints that use a BARE-ID resolver (environmentIDResolver, future serviceIDResolver / deploymentIDResolver). TWO load-bearing distinctions from the projectIDResolver siblings. (1) The resolver pins NO ProjectID leg on the resource scope (`policy.Scope{OrganizationID: principal.home, EnvironmentID: r.PathValue("environment_id")}`), so the engine's one-way Scope.covers() rule (internal/controlplane/policy/policy.go) denies EVERY scoped grant at the boundary via ReasonDeniedOutOfScope — project-scoped, env-scoped, AND service-scoped grants all DENY on this route, even when the grant names the target environment's own id or the target environment's own parent project, because the grant scope pins ProjectID and the resource scope does not. The acceptance criteria's three containment properties (sibling project not reached / staging-grant-doesn't-imply-production / service-grant-shields-parent-env-secrets) are still pinned at the engine against their natural resources (project / env / service Resource), but the wire tie-in INVERTS the projectIDResolver shape: instead of "project-scoped grant on target ALLOWS, sibling DENIES," every scoped-key type DENIES with ReasonDeniedOutOfScope on this route and only org-wide roles + the org-level grant allow. The org-level Viewer grant remains the load-bearing ALLOW because its grant scope pins nothing past OrganizationID — covers() matches a resource without ProjectID. Pin a "grant on the SAME environment id as the path names" engine-level deny too, so a future relaxation of covers() (e.g. admitting `Scope{EnvironmentID: env_X}` to cover `Resource{Scope{EnvironmentID: env_X}}` regardless of ProjectID) cannot land without failing this test. (2) The support cross-tenant exception is suppressed on the wire by construction — environmentIDResolver pins OrganizationID to principal.home — so the support cross-tenant ALLOW only fires at the engine layer against a hand-built foreign-org Resource. The wire-level "wrong organization" probe surfaces as 404 E_NOT_FOUND from the tenant-scoped GetByID query (never 403, which would confirm existence), and the body must not echo the foreign org id. The contract-test helper bakes in a single canonical principal (getEnvironmentHandlerFor takes `t, reader` only, with authForEnvGet's hardcoded principal), so this matrix introduces a per-test identity-injected helper LOCAL to the policy file (getEnvironmentByIDHandlerForIdentity, signature `(id auth.Identity, authErr error, reader EnvironmentReader) http.Handler`) modelled on listProjectsHandlerFor / listProjectEnvironmentsHandlerFor — adding the helper locally avoids modifying the 9 contract tests that already use the simpler form. ACTION-CONSTANT gotcha to pin: routes.go:574 sets `RequiredAction: string(policy.ActionEnvironmentRead)` for this route, NOT `policy.ActionEnvRead` — both are CapRead per catalog.go but they're different catalog entries (env-vars vs env entity); mixing them up silently swaps which catalog row a future tier-upgrade watches. Forward-applicable to every future bare-id matrix on a deep resource (service.read on /v1/services/{service_id}, deployment.read on /v1/deployments/{deployment_id}, etc).
ralph/progress.txt:691:- environment.read-on-{project_id}-resolver policy matrix (BE-0150, internal/controlplane/httpapi/projects_environments_get_policy_test.go) is the structural sibling of the env.read matrix at the same resolver (BE-0144) with TWO narrowings worth pinning because the persistence row carries NO credential material: (1) drop the secret-plaintext deny-body leak anchor — environmentsDenyBodyLeak anchors ONLY on canonical structural identifiers (project id/slug/display_name + per-env id/slug/display_name) because the environments table has nothing that needs redaction; (2) drop the "secret row still projects as output.Sentinel even when authority came via a project-level or org-level grant" allow-path assertion — projectEnvironmentOf has no value-redaction chokepoint to anchor. Everything else stays one-for-one identical: same six-roles-all-allow ReasonAllowedByRole role matrix, same Disabled-principal 403 with ReasonDeniedPrincipalDisabled, same wire-level cross-tenant test (reader always called with principal home org id, the persistence layer rejects the cross-tenant project_id pair as 404 NotFound), same engine-level cross-tenant deny matrix for every non-support role + ReasonAllowedBySupport for support (CapRead inside the support cross-tenant exception clause), same four-wire-tie-in grant-containment shape (project-scoped Viewer on target ALLOWS, sibling project DENIES, env-scoped grant DENIES against parent project, service-scoped grant DENIES against parent project, org-level Viewer grant ALLOWS, scoped key planted in foreign home org DENIES at engine). CRITICAL action-constant gotcha: env.read variables and environment.read entities live in the same Org→Project→Env→Service hierarchy but use DIFFERENT action constants (policy.ActionEnvRead = "env.read" for env-vars vs policy.ActionEnvironmentRead = "environment.read" for the env entity); both are CapRead and both go through projectIDResolver on the project-scoped reader routes, but mixing them up silently swaps which catalog row a future tier-upgrade regression watches. Always grep the route registration in routes.go AND the existing contract test's OpenAPI assertion to confirm which action a route is gated on before writing the policy matrix. Forward-applicable to every future CapRead-on-{X}-resolver matrix where the underlying row carries no credential material (org_grants, members, audit_events, etc.): keep the four-test skeleton, swap action+resource Kind, and drop the secret-plaintext anchor + Sentinel-projection assertion.
ralph/progress.txt:692:- CapAdmin-on-{project_id}-resolver policy matrix (BE-0141, internal/controlplane/httpapi/projects_grants_put_policy_test.go) is the structural sibling of the CapWrite-on-{project_id}-resolver matrix (projects_delete_policy_test.go) but ONE TIER UP — Developer JOINS the deny set at the role boundary (it holds CapWrite, not CapAdmin) and the org-level Developer grant flips from ALLOW for project.delete to DENY for project.grants.write. The load-bearing pair is org-level Admin grant ALLOW + org-level Developer grant DENY: that pair locks the CapAdmin requirement against the grant path so a future catalog change that downgraded project.grants.write to CapWrite would still pass the allow check but fail the deny check (silently letting every developer key rewrite a project's authority), and the regression direction is documented as a structural test, not only catalog assertion. The cross-tenant engine matrix DENIES every role including Support (CapAdmin is OUTSIDE the engine clause `roleCaps.has(CapSupport) && (required == CapRead || required == CapSupport)`); the CapRead-on-{project_id}-resolver siblings (project.grants.read at BE-0138 etc) ALLOW Support cross-tenant via ReasonAllowedBySupport — pinning the same-shape distinction in both directions documents that read-vs-write is the load-bearing axis for the support exception. Mutating-policy-matrix tests carry TWO directions of leak guard: a request-body leak guard (replaceGrantsRequestBodyLeak, anchored on distinctive caller-supplied identifiers like usr_request_matrix that NEVER appear in the canonical fixture data) and a would-have-returned-data leak guard (grantsDenyBodyLeak, anchored on canonical fixture data the fake replacer would return on an allow path) — used together on every deny path so a future renderer that gained either a "render request body on error" or "render would-have-returned data on 403" mode fails here. A read endpoint needs only the second; a write endpoint needs both. CI must be listed EXPLICITLY in both the per-role deny rows and the cross-tenant engine deny loop, because CapDeploy (which CI holds) is its own axis — neither CapWrite nor CapAdmin. The "scoped key planted in a foreign home org" engine assertion (a principal whose home org is org_sibling but whose grant scope still names a project in org_acme, authorized against org_sibling's resource) is independent of CapAdmin/CapWrite/CapRead — the engine refuses to consider the grant at all because the principal's OrganizationID no longer matches the grant scope's OrganizationID. Pin this on every grant-containment test so a stolen scoped key documentedly cannot be smuggled across tenants by anyone who finds it.
ralph/progress.txt:693:- env.write-on-{project_id}-resolver policy matrix (BE-0147, internal/controlplane/httpapi/projects_variables_put_policy_test.go) is the writer-side structural sibling of the env.read matrix at the same resolver (BE-0144, projects_variables_get_policy_test.go) and the project-scope sibling of the org-level env.write matrix (BE-0111, variables_put_policy_test.go). Same CapWrite capability class as project.delete/project.update (CapAdmin lives one tier up at project.grants.write/BE-0141), so the role table is identical to the org-level env.write: owner/admin/developer ALLOW via ReasonAllowedByRole, viewer/ci/support DENY via ReasonDeniedNoCapability — CI denies despite holding CapDeploy because env.write is CapWrite, not CapDeploy (CapDeploy is its own axis). Cross-tenant engine matrix DENIES every role INCLUDING Support (CapWrite is OUTSIDE the `roleCaps.has(CapSupport) && (required == CapRead || required == CapSupport)` exception, same posture as CapAdmin and the load-bearing distinction from env.read where support IS allowed cross-tenant). Wire-level cross-tenant story is the projectIDResolver shape: the resource org is the principal's home org, never the path's tenant, so the engine admits the same-tenant resource and the persistence layer rejects the (attacker_home_org, foreign_project_id) pair as 404 NotFound — the replacer DOES run (callCount=1) but with the attacker's home org id, so a production store could not have replaced the victim's variables regardless. Grant containment proves the org-level Developer ALLOW + org-level Viewer DENY pair (locks CapWrite against the grant path against both directions of catalog drift); project-level Viewer grant on target project ALSO DENIES (Viewer is CapRead-only); project-level Developer grant on target project ALLOWS, sibling project DENIES, env-/service-scoped Admin grants DENY against the parent project (covers() one-way). Variables-flavoured leak guards carry the same TWO directions that BE-0141 introduced — a request-body leak guard (anchored on caller-supplied MATRIX_KEY/matrix_value that never appear in the canonical fixture) AND a would-have-returned-data leak guard (postWriteProjectVariablesBodyLeak, anchored on the seeded post-write rows: per-row ids, keys, the non-secret value AND every recognisable fragment of the seeded secret plaintext post-write-plaintext-correcthorsebatterystaple). The allow-path Sentinel-projection assertion is the chokepoint anchor: even right after a customer submitted a secret, the post-write re-read STILL redacts via projectVariableOf -> output.Sentinel, so PUT cannot leak a secret value the customer just submitted. Forward-applicable to every future CapWrite-on-{X}-resolver matrix at any scope — keep the role / disabled / wrong-org / grant-containment skeleton + dual-direction leak guard, swap the action+resolver target+resource Kind, and ALWAYS add a secret-plaintext needle to the post-write leak guard plus a Sentinel-projection assertion on every allow-path tie-in.
ralph/progress.txt:694:- Replace-grants writer vertical (BE-0139 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/project_grant.go + httpapi/project_grants.go): a PUT-replace endpoint on a (parent, scoped-children) resource family pairs the existing reader-port adapter with a unit-of-work writer (ProjectGrantService) modeled exactly on OrganizationVariableService. The repository grows two new methods: Upsert(tx, id, organizationID, projectID, principalID, principalKind, role, *envID, *svcID) (ProjectGrant, error) using `ON CONFLICT (organization_id, project_id, principal_id, COALESCE(environment_id, ''), COALESCE(service_id, ''))` so the customer-mutable `role` is the only column the UPDATE branch touches (the immutable scope tuple + the bump_version trigger handle the rest); DeleteByProjectExceptIDs(tx, organizationID, projectID, keepIDs) renders the empty-keepIDs case as an unconstrained DELETE (an explicit clear is a meaningful customer operation), the non-empty case as `NOT (id = ANY($3::text[]))` so the bulk delete-by-exclusion is one round trip — same shape as DeleteByOrganizationExceptKeys but anchored on the row id (which Upsert returns from RETURNING) rather than a customer-supplied key, so an upsert that mutates an existing row deterministically keeps the survivor's id. The service Replace runs inside one Store.Write tx: project existence Get (a missing parent is typed NotFound, never a generic FK conflict), per-grant Upsert collecting the persisted ids into keepIDs, DeleteByProjectExceptIDs(keepIDs), audit.Append, post-write ListByProject re-read — every step shares the *Tx so a partial replace and an orphaned audit row are both impossible. Validation lives in buildProjectGrantReplace (split out, unit-testable without a DB): closed-set checks on principal_kind / role mirror the table's CHECK constraints, structural checks reject blank/NUL ids and a service-scope-without-environment, in-memory dedup uses `principalID + "\x00" + COALESCE(envID,'') + "\x00" + COALESCE(svcID,'')` to match the SQL unique index byte-for-byte. Audit metadata records counts only — `grant_count`, `project_scope_count`, `environment_scope_count`, `service_scope_count` — never principal ids, role names, or environment/service identifiers; per-row mutation history is the GET /v1/projects/{project_id}/grants surface's job. The HTTP layer adds a ProjectGrantReplacer port mirroring OrganizationVariableReplacer, a strict-decoded replaceProjectGrantsRequest with Grants *[]projectGrantRequest (pointer-to-slice so missing field is structurally distinguishable from an explicit empty array — a missing field is a stable 400, an empty array reaches the store), and the handler clones caller-supplied *string EnvironmentID/ServiceID pointers before forwarding them so the store cannot observe the caller's backing memory. NewHandler and newRouteTable signatures each grow ONE positional port (projectGrantReplacer ProjectGrantReplacer just after projectGrants ProjectGrantReader); a Python regex sweep over 27 test files anchored on `fakeProjectGrantReader{}, nil)` -> `fakeProjectGrantReader{}, fakeProjectGrantReplacer{}, nil)` handled the bulk of the NewHandler churn (43 call sites), with three follow-up patterns hand-fixed: `, logger)` in server_test.go and me_contract_test.go (NewHandler with non-nil logger), `, nil)` newRouteTable call (different return type), and the 29-nil all-nil newRouteTable pattern in six test files (added one trailing nil for the new port). The story implicitly delivers BE-0439's CRUD repository acceptance criteria (Upsert + DeleteByProjectExceptIDs are the missing half) and the writer side of BE-0440's tenant-isolation criteria.
ralph/progress.txt:695:- Replace-variables writer vertical for a scoped-child resource (BE-0145 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/project_variable.go + project_variable_service.go + httpapi/project_variables.go): a PUT-replace endpoint on a (parent, scoped-children) variables family pairs the existing reader-port adapter with a unit-of-work writer (ProjectVariableService) modeled exactly on OrganizationVariableService — same Upsert + DeleteByProjectExceptKeys + Replace shape, same audit metadata convention (counts-only: `variable_count`, `secret_count`), same value-redaction chokepoints (LogValue + wire output.Sentinel + audit Auditor regex), but with two distinguishing axes worth pinning. (1) The repository's Upsert key is the (organization_id, project_id, key) triple from migration 0015's UNIQUE (not the (organization_id, key) pair from migration 0012), so the ON CONFLICT target carries an extra column — every other column position in the INSERT statement shifts by one. The DELETE-except-keys SQL similarly has two tenant predicate legs (organization_id AND project_id) before the `key <> ALL($3)` clause; the empty/nil keepKeys path is the deliberate full-clear (a customer asking to remove every project-scoped variable is meaningful, not a no-op). (2) The ProjectVariableService re-uses the org-level helpers verbatim — `itoaVariableIndex`, `posixEnvVarName`, validate.MaxEnvVarNameLen/MaxEnvVarValueLen/MaxSecretValueLen — and uses `projectVariablesWriteAction = "env.write"` (same string constant as the org-level path) so the audit Action is identical at both scopes; the resource_kind axis (domain.KindProject vs domain.KindOrganization) is what disambiguates the audit row. The Service runs inside one Store.Write tx: ProjectRepository.Get (a missing OR cross-tenant project surfaces as the typed NotFound the GET reader already proves, NEVER as a generic FK conflict from the upsert), per-variable Upsert minting a fresh `pvar_` id (the existing-row's id is preserved on conflict, NOT the caller-supplied id), DeleteByProjectExceptKeys(keepKeys), audit.Append, post-write ListByProject re-read so the response body byte-matches the committed state. The HTTP layer adds a ProjectVariableReplacer port mirroring OrganizationVariableReplacer, strict-decoded replaceProjectVariablesRequest with `Variables *[]projectVariableRequest` (pointer-to-slice so a missing field is structurally distinguishable from an explicit empty array — a missing field is a stable 400 with reason `errProjectVariablesBodyMissing` "must be supplied (use an empty array to clear every variable)", an empty array reaches the store as the deliberate clear). The handler takes OrganizationID from the principal's home org (NEVER the caller — the body carries no org id), so a cross-tenant {project_id} still hits the tenant-scoped Get and surfaces as 404 at the persistence boundary. NewHandler and newRouteTable signatures each grow ONE positional port (projectVariableReplacer ProjectVariableReplacer just after projectVariables ProjectVariableReader); a Python regex sweep over the test directory anchored on `fakeProjectVariableReader{}, nil)` -> `fakeProjectVariableReader{}, fakeProjectVariableReplacer{}, nil)` and `fakeProjectVariableReader{}, logger)` (multi-line variant included) handled the bulk of the NewHandler churn (31 files); the all-nil newRouteTable form with 31 nils received one trailing nil to become 32; two outlier patterns needed targeted edits — project_variables_test.go's `fakeProjectGrantReplacer{}, reader, nil)` (where `reader` is a named parameter, not a literal) and projects_delete_test.go / projects_restore_test.go's `fakeProjectGrantReader{}, nil, nil)` (where the new-port slot was already nil-filled by an earlier story, so one MORE trailing nil was added rather than inserting in the middle). The story implicitly delivers the writer half of project_variables CRUD repository acceptance criteria and the writer side of tenant-isolation criteria.
ralph/progress.txt:697:- PUT-replace-variables contract suite on a bare-{id}-resolver route (BE-0175 contract pattern, internal/controlplane/httpapi/environment_variables_put_test.go): canonical 12-test contract suite for ANY customer-facing PUT-replace-{secret-bearing-children} endpoint sitting on a bare-{id}-resolver route. Structural HYBRID of (a) environment_grants_put_test.go (BE-0169 contract pattern — route-shape twin: same env-id resolver, same NewHandler positional port placement, same missing-field-400 + malformed-400 + unknown-field-400 + unauth-401 + viewer-403 + store NotFound 404 + store InvalidInput 400 + store unavailable 503 + unwired 500 + OpenAPI registration skeleton) and (b) project_variables_test.go (BE-0142 GET pattern — resource-shape twin: output.Sentinel projection of is_secret rows, secret-plaintext leak guards on response bodies including the seeded substring). The pattern adds TWO load-bearing assertions BE-0169 (a grants-only resource) cannot supply: (1) the happy-path body POSITIVELY asserts got0.Value == output.Sentinel for the secret row AND negatively asserts the body does not contain the seeded plaintext — the wire chokepoint must fire on the post-write re-read even though the caller just submitted the plaintext; both directions catch different regressions. (2) A dedicated TestDeniesSupportInTenant — CapWrite has no support exception (the CapRead cross-tenant exception is one-way), so a CapWrite contract suite MUST positively assert support in their own tenant is denied 403, locking the deny against a future engine regression that widens CapWrite to support. apierr.InvalidInput wire shape: the renderer emits FieldViolation as "<field>: <reason>" inside the hint, NOT as a JSON key with quotes — a contract test that asserts the field path reaches the wire must use the substring "variables: " (with space), NOT "variables" wrapped in double-quotes; the success envelope DOES use "variables" as a JSON key but the error envelope serialises the field path into a human-readable hint instead. The handlerFor uses the trailing replacer port (everything else is fakeXxx{} zero-value); the all-nils logger is nil to silence test output. The future PUT-replace-{secrets} story on api_keys, service_variables, or any other credential-bearing child resource is a verbatim clone of this 12-test skeleton with the resource type swapped and the action const swapped.
ralph/progress.txt:700:- Reader-only scoped-child vertical (BE-0142 pattern, project_variables migration 0015, store/project_variable.go, httpapi/project_variables.go): a new (parent, scoped-children) resource family that ships JUST the GET endpoint mirrors the BE-0136 full-vertical shape with two narrowings worth pinning. (1) The migration is structurally identical to project_grants 0014 — composite (organization_id, project_id) FK -> projects with ON DELETE CASCADE, composite UNIQUE (organization_id, id) as the FK target a future override table (environment_variables / service_variables) can use, bigint version + bump_version trigger from 0011, set_updated_at trigger — but a different scope-uniqueness shape: project_variables uses UNIQUE (organization_id, project_id, key) directly (no COALESCE because key is non-nullable) where project_grants needs the COALESCE-NULLS index for env/svc-scoped uniqueness. (2) The reader vertical defers the writer's repository surface — only ListByProject(ctx, Querier, organizationID, projectID) lands now — so the repo file is short and the audit-grade write path (Upsert + DeleteByProjectExceptKeys + bulk validation) is BE-0145's job. The store-backed adapter (ProjectVariableReader) mirrors ProjectGrantReader exactly: composes ProjectRepository.Get + ListByProject inside one Store.Read transaction so a cross-tenant or unknown project_id surfaces as apierr.NotFound, never an empty list. ProjectVariable.LogValue redacts every value at the slog boundary (mirrors OrganizationVariable.LogValue), so a stray panic stack that captures the struct cannot leak a non-secret value either — the slog redaction is unconditional even though the wire layer only redacts secret values. NewHandler signature grows ONE positional port (projectVariables ProjectVariableReader after projectGrantReplacer); a JS regex sweep over 30 test files anchored on `fakeProjectGrantReplacer{}, nil)` -> `fakeProjectGrantReplacer{}, fakeProjectVariableReader{}, nil)` plus a second sweep on `fakeProjectGrantReplacer{}, logger)` handled the NewHandler churn (45 call sites); a separate state-machine sweep over newRouteTable calls appended one trailing arg to every direct invocation (8 files), and project_grants_put_test.go's `fakeProjectGrantReader{}, replacer, nil)` (where replacer is a real var, not a literal) was the one anchor the bulk regex couldn't reach. The KindProjectVariable Kind prefix is `pvar` (mirrors the ovar/pgrnt prefix shape); the existing id_test.go's hardcoded valid-kinds lists do NOT need updating — they sanity-check a subset (KindProjectGrant is also absent from those lists yet works in tests). KindProjectVariable still has to land in the kinds map for Kind.Valid() / ParseID to accept it.
ralph/progress.txt:710:- Mutating POST endpoints reject organization_id in the request body via the strict JSON decoder (validate.DecodeJSON disallows unknown fields). The handler builds the store *Input.OrganizationID from p.OrganizationID (principal), never from the body, so the tenant boundary is structural — a caller cannot point a create at another tenant even if defense-in-depth were skipped. ActorOrgID on the audit record is also the principal's home org; ResourceID names the created resource.
ralph/progress.txt:799:- Optimistic concurrency on mutable customer-data tables: migration 0011 adds a bigint version column (DEFAULT 1, CHECK > 0) + bump_version() BEFORE UPDATE trigger to organizations/projects/environments/services. The trigger overwrites NEW.version with OLD.version+1 on every UPDATE (including no-op data UPDATEs), so the application cannot freeze the sequence. Repository UPDATE methods accept ifMatchVersion *int64: nil = no precondition, non-nil = WHERE id=$1 AND version=$N; on no rows, a tenant-scoped Get inside the same tx disambiguates "row gone" (NotFound) from "row stale" (apierr.ConflictStale carrying the row's current version). Service-layer Update/ScheduleDeletion also pre-check the version after the read so a no-op data patch still surfaces the conflict against the right row. The HTTP layer parses RFC 7232 strong ETag values (`"<n>"`, lenient unquoted form accepted; weak/wildcard/multi-value rejected as 400 E_INVALID_INPUT) from If-Match, mirrors the row's authoritative version into the ETag response header on every success path (GET/POST/PATCH/DELETE), and surfaces stale writes as 409 E_CONFLICT carrying current_version under the new structured error.details map (apierr.DetailKeyCurrentVersion). yerr.Error.Details (map[string]string, set by WithDetail) is the agent-friendly counterpart to Hint; apienvelope renders it through the same regex redaction backstop used for Message/Hint.
ralph/progress.txt:809:- Store-backed httpapi endpoints use a narrow reader-port pattern: httpapi declares the port interface (e.g. OrganizationReader), a Store.Read-backed adapter in the store package satisfies it by composing the tenant-scoped repository (mirror store.CredentialReader — never issue fresh SQL), and the port is threaded NewHandler -> newRouteTable -> the handler closure. httpapi imports store (acyclic — store never imports httpapi). For an endpoint with no path/query parameter, tenant isolation is structural: the handler only ever passes principal.OrganizationID. NewHandler signature: NewHandler(build, readiness, meta, authenticator, engine, orgs, logger).
ralph/progress.txt:811:- Domain tables live in versioned migrations under store/migrate/migrations/. Every customer-data table carries organization_id (directly or via a verified parent chain) and is tenant-isolated at the database level by COMPOSITE foreign keys: a child FK (organization_id, parent_id) references the parent's UNIQUE (organization_id, id), making a cross-tenant child unrepresentable. Slugs are citext, unique within parent scope, never global. users is a global identity table; org membership is the memberships join table. Schema-level integration tests live in package store_test (NOT package migrate — it can't import testutil) and match constraint violations via *pgconn.PgError SQLSTATE class 23.
ralph/progress.txt:813:- Credentials: internal/controlplane/auth owns generation/hashing/verification; internal/controlplane/store owns persistence. A secret is stored ONLY as a one-way hash plus a public, globally-UNIQUE prefix — never plaintext. Plaintext credential types implement String/GoString/LogValue/MarshalJSON returning output.Sentinel; the plaintext is reachable only via an explicit Reveal() method. High-entropy secrets (>=128 bits random) use fast unsalted SHA-256 + subtle.ConstantTimeCompare, NOT bcrypt/argon2. Authentication lookup (FindByPrefix) is the ONE deliberate non-tenant-scoped repository read — it runs before the tenant is known; the found row carries its own OrganizationID. Tenant-scoped mutations that may match zero rows report apierr.NotFound via tag.RowsAffected()==0.
ralph/progress.txt:814:- Authorization is internal/controlplane/policy: policy.Engine is a pure, total decision function — Decide(Principal, Action, Resource) -> Decision{Allow, Reason} (stable Reason codes); Authorize/AuthorizeCtx map denials onto typed apierr (Unauthenticated for a missing principal, Forbidden otherwise). Every endpoint maps to exactly one policy.Action; the action lives in policy's defaultActionCatalog (the authoritative requiredAction -> Capability map) — add new actions there or via policy.WithAction. Roles confer Capability sets (self/read/deploy/write/admin/owner/support), not individual actions; six built-ins are fixed, custom roles resolve through a CustomRoleResolver hook. Scoped Grants narrow/widen authority within an org via Scope containment over Organization->Project->Environment->Service; cross-tenant resources are denied by construction except support reads/break-glass. The principal travels on the request context via policy.WithPrincipal/PrincipalFromContext — never thread it through signatures. The store.Authorizer port is satisfied by this engine; store still never imports policy. TestPolicyMatrix exhaustively covers all built-in roles x catalogued actions.
ralph/progress.txt:815:- Request authentication is internal/controlplane/auth.Authenticator: it resolves any of the three bearer schemes (API key yk_..., human session JWT, internal worker secret) to one auth.Identity{Principal, Method}. Every invalid-credential cause collapses to the single ErrInvalidCredentials sentinel (missing creds = ErrNoCredentials) so no error is a "does this key prefix exist?" oracle; a genuine datastore failure is propagated unchanged as a typed *yerr.Error, never disguised as invalid credentials. The Authenticator depends on the narrow auth.CredentialStore port (+ APIKeyRecord/ServiceAccountRecord DTOs) so auth never imports store; the production adapter is store.CredentialReader (store imports auth, not the reverse). The HTTP wrappers are httpapi.RequireAuth(authenticator, engine, action, ResourceResolver) and httpapi.RequireInternalWorker — they authenticate, attach the principal via policy.WithPrincipal + telemetry.SetOrgID/SetPrincipalID, then authorize: missing creds 401 E_AUTH, invalid 401 E_AUTH (fixed generic message), unauthorized 403 E_FORBIDDEN (stable policy reason), dependency failure keeps its typed 5xx. ResourceResolver nil defaults to the principal's own org scope; pass a path-param resolver so a cross-tenant id is a 403, not a silent allow.
ralph/progress.txt:816:- Quota schema (0006_quota_policy_schema): a closed enum reused across tables is a Postgres DOMAIN over text + CHECK (here quota_resource over 14 dimensions, quota_enforcement_mode over hard/soft/metered/disabled); DROP DOMAIN goes in the down migration after the tables. quota_policies holds one limit per (scope, resource) where a row is EITHER a plan_default (text plan, no plans-table FK yet) OR an organization override — enforced by one table CHECK + two partial unique indexes; the checker resolves "org override else plan default". quota_usage is one counter row per (org, resource); quota_reservations are short-lived (amount>0, status active->committed|released|expired, expires_at, settled_at tied to non-active status by CHECK) and expose UNIQUE (organization_id, id) as a composite-FK target; usage_events is append-only (no updated_at/trigger, signed delta) and links reservations via a tenant-safe composite FK. An optional cross-table link inside a tenant uses a plain composite FK (MATCH SIMPLE) — NOT ON DELETE SET NULL, which would null the NOT NULL organization_id.
ralph/progress.txt:817:- Quota enforcement: the QuotaRepository (internal/controlplane/store/quota.go) is the persistence half (EffectiveLimit / LockUsage / SumActiveReservations / InsertReservation, all tenant-scoped, mutations on *Tx) and the quota.Checker (internal/controlplane/quota) is the store.QuotaReserver service half. A hard limit is enforced by LockUsage doing INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING then SELECT ... FOR UPDATE on the quota_usage counter row — that lock serialises concurrent reservers so they cannot both over-allocate; the decision is current+reserved+requested <= limit, reserved = SUM of active non-expired reservations. Soft/metered limits record but never reject; disabled/no-policy allow with no write. A rejection is apierr.QuotaExceeded wrapping a recoverable quota.ExceededDetail{Resource,Current,Reserved,Requested,Limit}. Quota accounting rows have no domain.Kind — ids are minted locally with a prefixed crypto/rand helper.
ralph/progress.txt:818:- Audit log (0007_audit_events, store/audit.go, internal/controlplane/audit): the immutable audit log records every authorization decision, allowed AND denied. Immutability is enforced at two layers — the table has no updated_at and a BEFORE UPDATE trigger (audit_events_reject_update) that rejects every UPDATE in the database, and AuditRepository exposes only Append + tenant-scoped ListByOrganization (no update/delete); row DELETE stays reachable only via the organizations ON DELETE CASCADE. An append-only table that must still cascade on tenant teardown blocks UPDATE only, never DELETE. The audit.Auditor (depends on the narrow Recorder port, satisfied by *store.AuditRepository) is the single place a policy.Decision becomes an AuditEvent: it pulls the principal (policy.PrincipalFromContext) + correlation ids (telemetry.FromContext) off the context, files the event under the principal's org else the resource's org, and REDACTS metadata before persistence — secret-shaped keys (token/secret/password/credential/...) replaced wholesale with output.Sentinel, every other value + the user agent scrubbed by an output.Redactor. store treats metadata as already-redacted; redaction is the audit service's contract. Auditor.Record takes the caller's *store.Tx so an audit write can share the mutation's transaction or run in its own.
ralph/progress.txt:819:- Provisioning jobs (0008_provisioning_jobs, store/job.go): the durable, idempotent queue of Dokploy provisioning work. The provisioning_jobs table is tenant-scoped (organization_id FK + composite (organization_id, project_id|environment_id|service_id) FKs so a job can never target another tenant's resource), idempotent (UNIQUE (organization_id, idempotency_key)), and exposes UNIQUE (organization_id, id) as a composite-FK target — 0008 also wires the FK that 0006 deferred (quota_reservations.job_id -> provisioning_jobs). DB CHECKs enforce the closed status set, "terminal status iff finished_at set", "lease held iff status=running", and attempts<=max_attempts; the Go state machine (store.JobStatus.CanTransitionTo, backed by the jobTransitions map: queued->running|cancelled, running->succeeded|retrying|failed|dead_letter|cancelled, retrying->running|cancelled, terminals have no edges) is the authoritative validator. JobRepository follows the ProjectRepository pattern (mutations need *Tx, reads take Querier, NotFound for cross-tenant ids); Transition locks the row FOR UPDATE, validates the edge before any write (illegal edge = apierr.Conflict), and derives lease/attempt/timestamp bookkeeping from the target status so a caller cannot leave a CHECK-violating row. error_summary is run through an output.NewRedactor() backstop on every write (Insert + Transition) — the store IS the redaction chokepoint for job errors, unlike audit metadata which the service redacts.
ralph/progress.txt:820:- HTTP idempotency (0009_idempotency_keys, store/idempotency.go, httpapi/idempotency.go): mutating endpoints are made retry-safe by httpapi.RequireIdempotency(IdempotencyStore, ttl) — middleware installed INSIDE RequireAuth (it reads policy.PrincipalFromContext to scope the key to a principal within a tenant). Safe methods and unsafe requests with no Idempotency-Key pass straight through (opt-in). The claim protocol: store.IdempotencyRepository.Claim is a WHERE-guarded upsert — INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE ... WHERE expires_at <= now() — so it freshly claims, transparently steals an expired row (claimed=true), or returns the existing live claim (claimed=false, RETURNING yields no row, follow-up Find reads the row the DO UPDATE attempt locked). On claimed=true the middleware runs the handler against a buffering responseCapture, then Complete records the rendered envelope (status < 500) or Release drops the claim (status >= 500, so a server failure is retried not replayed). On claimed=false: hash mismatch -> apierr.IdempotencyConflict (E_IDEMPOTENCY_CONFLICT, the only use of the new code), completed -> replay the recorded body+status with Idempotency-Replayed: true, still pending -> apierr.Conflict "already in progress". The request hash is sha256(method\npath?query\nbody); the body is buffered then restored as a fresh r.Body so the handler still reads it. Keys are scoped UNIQUE (organization_id, principal_id, idempotency_key); a completion CHECK ties status='completed' to a non-null response. The store-backed IdempotencyStore adapter (httpapi.NewIdempotencyStore) runs each repo call in its own Store.Write — RequireIdempotency is implemented and tested but not yet wired into a route (no mutating endpoints exist yet), same as RequireAuth.
ralph/progress.txt:829:- "Add policy matrix coverage for <endpoint>" stories add a <endpoint>_policy_test.go beside the endpoint's contract test, in package httpapi, driving the REAL NewHandler + real policy.NewEngine() (the production authorization path, fake Authenticator only). Table the principal classes the PRD names: every built-in role, revoked/expired credentials (modelled as Disabled principals — the only shape in which a revoked/expired credential reaches the engine; the auth layer's earlier 401 path is the contract test's job), and a wrong-organization principal. For a CapSelf endpoint the matrix is "all allow" — assert the served data is the caller's OWN identity, never escalated/cross-tenant. Grant-containment criteria (sibling project / production env / parent-level secret non-leak) are engine properties: assert them with policy.NewEngine().Decide directly, then tie back by proving the endpoint echoes the confined grant set verbatim. Reuse the existing helpers (meHandlerFor/getMe/decodeMe, middleware_test.go's decodeError) — a sibling _test.go needs no new scaffolding.
ralph/progress.txt:837:- Reads that union "configured" + "live" state for the same key set (a quota policy join with quota_usage, a desired-state join with reconciled actual state, ...) use ONE SQL statement with FULL OUTER JOIN + COALESCE(left.key, right.key) for the projection key, never two queries with a Go-side merge. The repository method takes (organizationID, plan) and stays tenant-scoped at every leg of the join — the CTE shape `WITH policies AS (... WHERE org=$1 OR plan=$2), usage AS (... WHERE org=$1) FULL OUTER JOIN ... ORDER BY resource` is the reference; two queries would force the handler to re-implement deterministic ordering AND would leak observability (the two queries can see different snapshots).
ralph/progress.txt:968:  - Added migration `0051_usage_events_metering_contract` to extend `usage_events` with resource scope columns, `quantity`, `unit`, `source`, `idempotency_key`, billing period bounds, tenant-safe composite FKs, replay-safe uniqueness, and a database trigger rejecting updates.
ralph/progress.txt:969:  - Added `store.UsageEventRepository.Append` with validation, tenant-scope existence checks, metadata redaction, active subscription period assignment with UTC calendar-month fallback, duplicate idempotency replay, and `adjusted` correction event support.
ralph/progress.txt:970:  - Added integration tests for idempotency, redaction, correction events, period assignment, validation failure, cross-tenant not-found behavior, and database append-only behavior.
ralph/progress.txt:1008:- audit.read is the load-bearing example of a "read action that is NOT CapRead" in the policy catalog (catalog.go: ActionAuditRead -> CapAdmin). The audit log cannot be listed by a viewer or a developer — only by an owner, admin, or a support principal performing a cross-tenant read. A boilerplate "every in-tenant role allowed" assumption is wrong for audit.read; the policy matrix and a contract-test "viewer denied in own tenant" assertion together pin the property (the contract test makes a future accidental drop to CapRead a contract failure, not only a matrix failure).
ralph/progress.txt:1009:- Organization-scoped variables (0012_organization_variables, store/organization_variable.go, httpapi/variables.go): the lowest-precedence layer of the Organization -> Project -> Environment -> Service variable hierarchy. The table mirrors the established conventions — composite UNIQUE (organization_id, id) as the FK target for future scope-narrowing override tables; UNIQUE (organization_id, key) for per-tenant key uniqueness (an env var named DATABASE_URL in two orgs is two rows); ON DELETE CASCADE from organizations; bigint version + bump_version() trigger for optimistic concurrency. The repository follows the AuditRepository read pattern: ListByOrganization is tenant-scoped at the SQL predicate (cross-tenant id matches no rows, empty list, never another tenant's data), ordered deterministically by (key ASC, id ASC). The wire redaction contract is wire-side at the projection chokepoint, NOT store-side: store.OrganizationVariable.Value carries the literal verbatim (a future write path needs lossless round-trip), but httpapi/variables.go's organizationVariableOf replaces is_secret=true values with output.Sentinel on every projection — agents can never read a secret value back through this endpoint. Defence-in-depth: OrganizationVariable.LogValue redacts every value (regardless of is_secret) at the slog boundary, so a stray log record that captures the struct cannot leak the literal. Non-secret values pass through verbatim so customers can audit their own organization-wide defaults.
ralph/progress.txt:1010:- The env.write policy matrix (BE-0111, internal/controlplane/httpapi/variables_put_policy_test.go) is the structural twin of the CapWrite/no-cross-tenant-support family (project/environment/service create/update/delete) and the load-bearing distinction from the env.read matrix (BE-0108): same {org_id} resolver, same organizationIDResolver, but Developer JOINS the allow row (RoleDeveloper holds CapWrite), Viewer/CI/Support all deny via ReasonDeniedNoCapability, and the engine's cross-tenant clause (`required == CapRead || required == CapSupport`) excludes CapWrite — so a Support principal cannot cross tenants and cannot mutate its OWN home tenant either. The grant-containment row mirrors the env.read suite verbatim except that the org-level allow row tests a Developer grant (CapWrite) instead of a Viewer grant (CapRead); the Viewer grant at the same org scope is asserted to DENY, locking the CapWrite requirement against the grant path so a future catalog drift in either direction fails here. Wire-level redaction (organizationVariableOf -> output.Sentinel) is pinned on the allow-by-role AND allow-by-grant branches, and deny-path leak checks use a distinct seededPostWriteOrgVariables fixture (POST_WRITE_REGION/POST_WRITE_SECRET with the recognisable "post-write-plaintext-correcthorsebatterystaple" needle) so the matrix's request body (MATRIX_KEY/matrix_value) and the env.read suite's seededOrgVariables (REGION/DATABASE_URL) cannot false-positive a leak check. Cross-tenant assertions on the wire pin BOTH the submitted-payload non-echo (MATRIX_KEY, matrix_value) and the post-write-data non-echo — the policy boundary is the only place an env mutation is gated, so a denied caller seeing either would itself be a contract violation.
ralph/progress.txt:1011:- The CapWrite-on-nil-resolver policy matrix (BE-0123, internal/controlplane/httpapi/projects_create_policy_test.go) is the structural sibling of the env.write matrix above (BE-0111) but for endpoints that use a NIL ResourceResolver instead of organizationIDResolver — POST /v1/projects is the reference. The wire surface offers NO path/body field that could name another tenant (the strict JSON decoder rejects organization_id in the body, the contract suite TestCreateProjectIgnoresOrganizationIDInBody pins that), so tenant isolation on the wire is STRUCTURAL: the creator is always invoked with principal.OrganizationID and there is no caller-controlled value the policy boundary could fail open on. The cross-tenant probe therefore moves OFF the wire and ONTO a sub-test loop (cross_tenant_owner/admin/developer/viewer/ci/support, each t.Parallel()) that authorizes the action against a foreign-org Resource directly through policy.NewEngine().Decide; EVERY role — including Support — must deny via ReasonDeniedCrossTenant, because CapWrite is outside the engine's `roleCaps.has(CapSupport) && (required == CapRead || required == CapSupport)` exception. The role matrix proper still allows Owner/Admin/Developer (ReasonAllowedByRole) and denies Viewer/CI/Support (ReasonDeniedNoCapability) at the principal's own home org root. Grant-containment for a nil-resolver write authorizes against the org root, so project/env/service-scoped admin grants all deny with ReasonDeniedOutOfScope (covers() one-way); an org-level VIEWER grant denies with ReasonDeniedNoCapability (the load-bearing distinction from a CapRead matrix where the same grant ALLOWS), an org-level DEVELOPER grant allows via ReasonAllowedByGrant. Deny-path leak needles cover BOTH the caller-supplied request-body fields AND the canned-creator-output fields (two recognisable values per field, e.g. prj_matrix_alpha vs the request projectID) — a denied response leaking either is a separate regression direction, kept in one guard. Future CapWrite-on-nil-resolver endpoints (none yet planned; this is currently the only one) can copy this shape verbatim.
ralph/progress.txt:1012:- Hard-delete vs soft-delete decision rule: a customer-facing resource takes a SOFT delete (deletion_scheduled_at on organizations + 202 Accepted, revoked_at on api_keys + 200 OK with revocation snapshot) ONLY when the row carries a continuing tie that must outlive the resource — an audit-log FK that must keep the row reachable, a child cascade that must be ordered carefully, or a "lifecycle stale" property the customer's audit trail depends on. A resource with no continuing tie (the audit log records the deletion independently, no child tables, no FK ties) takes a HARD delete with a 200 OK response carrying the row's snapshot exactly as it stood at the moment of removal (DELETE /v1/organizations/{org_id}/variables/{key}, BE-0115). The store repository method uses `DELETE FROM ... WHERE org=$1 AND key=$2 RETURNING ...` so one round trip removes the row AND yields the snapshot the orchestrator returns to the wire; pgx.ErrNoRows -> apierr.NotFound (a cross-tenant key is indistinguishable from a missing row). The service files an audit event in the SAME tx as the delete, with Metadata={variable_id: row.ID} — customer-supplied key + the deleted value are never recorded, so the audit row is auditable without ever observing the variable name or its value. An agent can confirm what was deleted (id, key, version, timestamps) without a separate read; 204 No Content would have been less useful here.
ralph/progress.txt:1015:- Deep-resource resolver pattern (BE-0124, GET /v1/projects/{project_id}, internal/controlplane/httpapi/projects.go projectIDResolver): when an endpoint targets a resource named in the path but the path has NO org_id segment (sub-organization endpoints like /v1/projects/{project_id}, future /v1/.../environments/{environment_id}, /v1/.../services/{service_id}), the ResourceResolver pulls the principal's home OrganizationID from the request context with policy.PrincipalFromContext(r.Context()) — RequireAuth attaches the principal BEFORE invoking the resolver, so the context already carries it even though the ResourceResolver signature is func(r *http.Request) policy.Resource. The resolver returns Scope{OrganizationID: principal.HomeOrg, ProjectID: r.PathValue("project_id"), Kind: domain.KindProject}; do NOT look up the resource's real org from the database inside the resolver (keep the resolver pure path+context, no I/O). The policy engine then authorizes against the principal's own home org root combined with the path id, so a project/environment/service-scoped grant for THAT resource (containment match) authorizes via ReasonAllowedByGrant while a grant for a SIBLING resource fails the engine's covers() check; cross-tenant project_ids reach the tenant-scoped repository query with the principal's home org and surface as a deterministic 404 (E_NOT_FOUND) from the persistence layer, NOT a 403 from policy — which is acceptable per the acceptance criteria ("cross-tenant IDs must behave as not found or forbidden"). The Support principal's cross-tenant read capability does NOT apply through this endpoint because the resource scope is pinned to the principal's home org, not the path's tenant; support cross-tenant reads remain available through endpoints whose path DOES carry an {org_id} (organizationIDResolver). PATCH/DELETE on the same resource adopt the same resolver — handlers should call PathValue and use principal.OrganizationID for the store call, never trust a caller-supplied org id. The canonical regression check is TestGetProjectCrossTenantIDBehavesAsNotFound (the response body must not echo a foreign organization id either).
ralph/progress.txt:1016:- Reader-port extension pattern (BE-0124): when a new endpoint on the same resource family lands (e.g. GET /v1/projects/{project_id} alongside GET /v1/projects), extend the existing reader port in place rather than declaring a parallel sibling interface — ProjectReader now has BOTH ListProjects and GetProject; OrganizationReader has GetOrganization and is used by both list and by-id endpoints. The fakeFooReader in the *_test.go is similarly extended in place (added project/getErr/gotGetOrgID/gotGetProjectID fields on fakeProjectReader for the by-id tests); the zero value of the extended fake still works for every prior call site (the other test files / suites that never reach the handler) so no cross-test churn is needed. The store-backed adapter pattern stays the same: *Reader composes *Repository (never re-issues SQL), each new method opens its own short-lived Store.Read tx and delegates to an existing Querier-accepting repository method, inheriting that method's tenant-scoping guarantees for free. Auth-error sentinel naming gotcha: the auth package exports the PLURAL form (auth.ErrInvalidCredentials, auth.ErrNoCredentials) — singular forms do not exist; tests that exercise the invalid-credential path must use the plural names to match the writeAuthError errors.Is dispatch.
ralph/progress.txt:1018:- The CapWrite-on-deep-resource policy matrix (BE-0129, internal/controlplane/httpapi/projects_update_policy_test.go) is the structural sibling of the CapWrite-on-nil-resolver matrix (BE-0123, projects_create) but for endpoints that use projectIDResolver (or any deep-resource resolver: future environmentIDResolver, serviceIDResolver). The role matrix is the same CapWrite split (Owner/Admin/Developer allow via ReasonAllowedByRole; Viewer/CI/Support deny via ReasonDeniedNoCapability) because the resource org id comes from the principal's home org and falls same-tenant at the engine. The cross-tenant probe stays at the engine layer (the wire cannot name another tenant — a foreign project_id surfaces as 404 from the tenant-scoped repository query, with the body never echoing the foreign org), and EVERY role — including Support — must deny via ReasonDeniedCrossTenant on a foreign-org Resource. Grant-containment for a deep-resource write authorizes against the resource (project / env / service) scope, NOT the org root, so a project-level grant naming THIS resource ALLOWS via ReasonAllowedByGrant and a project-level grant naming a SIBLING resource denies via ReasonDeniedOutOfScope (covers() one-way); a project-level VIEWER grant on the SAME resource denies via ReasonDeniedNoCapability (the grant covers the resource but does NOT confer CapWrite — this is the NEW assertion vs CapRead matrices, where a Viewer grant covers + allows); env-/service-scoped grants on the parent project deny via ReasonDeniedOutOfScope; an org-level Admin grant allows via ReasonAllowedByGrant; an org-level Viewer grant denies via ReasonDeniedNoCapability (mirror-image of the org-level Viewer ALLOW on CapRead). The engine-twin "scoped key in a foreign home org" deny is also pinned (a scoped key cannot be smuggled across tenants — the cross-tenant guard fires before the grant is even considered). Deny-path leak guards needle for the canonical row identifiers AND the caller-supplied request-body fields (display_name patch payload), because either echo is a separate regression direction. Future CapWrite-on-deep-resource endpoints (project DELETE/restore, environment update/delete, service update/delete) copy this shape verbatim.
ralph/progress.txt:1019:- Deep-resource PATCH pattern (BE-0127, PATCH /v1/projects/{project_id}, internal/controlplane/httpapi/projects.go updateProjectHandler): sub-organization PATCH endpoints reuse the deep-resource resolver (projectIDResolver) to authorize against the (principal home organization, {project_id}) resource the path names — same resolver, same RequiredAction (policy.ActionProjectUpdate), and the store call is built from principal.OrganizationID and r.PathValue("project_id"), NEVER from the request body. The request DTO exposes ONLY the partially-updatable fields (Slug *string, DisplayName *string); the strict JSON decoder rejects an unknown organization_id field as a stable 400 E_INVALID_INPUT, so a body-injected org_id can never reach the store layer. Optimistic concurrency is bolted on by reading the optional If-Match header through the existing parseIfMatchVersion (the same parser organizations PATCH/DELETE already uses, accepting `"<n>"` strong ETag and the lenient unquoted form), forwarding the *int64 to the store as IfMatchVersion, and mirroring the returned row's authoritative version back into the ETag response header on success via writeOrganizationETag — the helper is generic in body despite the historical name, so the same call shape works for projects, future environments, services, etc. The handler stays thin: read principal, parse If-Match, validate.DecodeJSON, call port, render. The orchestrator (ProjectService.Update) follows the OrganizationService.Update shape exactly: validate input BEFORE opening the tx (buildProjectUpdate + validateProjectDisplayName are split out, unit-testable without a DB, and reject an empty patch as 400 InvalidInput), then in ONE Store.Write tx Get current, version pre-check (current.Version != *IfMatchVersion -> apierr.ConflictStale(current.Version)), apply non-nil fields, repo.Update(tx, desired, IfMatchVersion), audit.Append — a denial at any step rolls every other step back, so a row mutation and its audit record commit together or not at all. The store layer files the audit event under the actor's home organization with Metadata={"updated_fields": "slug,display_name"} — stable wire field names only, never the submitted values, so the audit trail records the SHAPE of the mutation without leaking input. classifyProjectConcurrencyMiss mirrors classifyOrganizationConcurrencyMiss: on the version-checked UPDATE matching zero rows, a tenant-scoped Get inside the same tx disambiguates "row gone" (NotFound) from "row stale" (ConflictStale carrying the row's current version) — the Get itself is tenant scoped, so a cross-tenant id still surfaces as NotFound and cannot reveal another tenant's data. Pre-existing test failures gotcha: TestAPIKeyServiceCreate* and TestOrganizationVariableServicePatch* fail on HEAD due to unrelated pre-existing issues with the testutil.Factory's id/slug formats vs the production-grade ParseSlug validator; verified by stashing the BE-0127 change and reproducing the failures on the base commit. These are NOT caused by BE-0127 and are out of scope.
ralph/progress.txt:1021:- New customer-data resource = full vertical (BE-0136 pattern, project_grants migration 0014, store/project_grant.go, httpapi/project_grants.go): one story is allowed to land the whole vertical when the resource has no foundation yet (no migration, no repo, no port, no handler, no wiring). The migration declares the table tenant-scoped at the database (composite (organization_id, project_id) FK -> projects with ON DELETE CASCADE, composite UNIQUE (organization_id, id) as the FK target for future scope-narrowing override tables, bigint version + bump_version trigger from 0011, set_updated_at trigger), CHECKs the enum-shaped columns to a closed set (principal_kind IN ('usr','sa'), role IN (six built-in roles)), and uses a UNIQUE index with COALESCE(env_id,'') / COALESCE(svc_id,'') so per-principal scope-tuple dedup works through NULLs (Postgres unique constraints treat NULLs as distinct by default until v15's NULLS NOT DISTINCT). The repository (ProjectGrantRepository) and reader adapter (ProjectGrantReader) follow OrganizationVariableReader exactly — repo accepts a Querier for reads, mutations need *Tx, reader composes the repo through Store.Read; cross-tenant queries return empty (no rows) at the SQL leg, but the LIST-FOR-PARENT shape (list grants OF a project) requires the reader to Get the project first inside the same transaction so a cross-tenant or unknown project_id surfaces as apierr.NotFound — never as an empty list, which would invite an agent to believe the project exists with no grants. The HTTP layer adds a new ProjectGrantReader port + handler + projection (projectGrantResource), and the route plugs into the existing projectIDResolver and policy.ActionProjectGrantsRead in routes.go's newRouteTable. EnvironmentID / ServiceID stay nullable pointers all the way from the schema to the wire so the (project | environment | service)-scoped distinction reaches an agent intact; the principal projects as a nested object Principal(id, kind) for the same reason the audit_event.actor and usage.limit endpoints do — one boolean (principal.kind == 'usr') instead of branching on coupled scalars. NewHandler signature gets ONE more positional port, which forces a sweep of every test file that constructs the production handler (a Python regex over 28 test files anchored on `fakeProjectRestorer{}, X)` plus manual edits to projects_restore_test.go and projects_delete_test.go whose end-of-call shape didn't match the dominant anchor; six single-line newRouteTable calls with all-nil args need a separate sweep to add one trailing nil). cmd/yalla-api wires *store.ProjectGrantReader at startup. The story implicitly delivers most of BE-0439's repository acceptance criteria (CRUD comes with the PUT story BE-0139 later) and BE-0440's tenant-isolation criteria for the list path.
ralph/progress.txt:1022:- Bare top-level lookup-by-id endpoint for a deep resource (BE-0154 pattern, GET /v1/environments/{environment_id}, store/environment.go GetByID + GetEnvironment, httpapi/environments.go): a customer-facing route that addresses a deep resource (env, service, deployment) by its global id WITHOUT carrying its parent ids in the path. Two load-bearing constraints: (1) the resolver builds policy.Resource{Scope: {OrganizationID = principal.home, <ResourceLevel>ID = path.id}} — pinning OrgID to the principal's home (NEVER the caller-supplied id) is the structural anti-spoof, and pinning the resource-level id is the only scope leg the path actually carries; (2) the resolver CANNOT pin the parent ids without a DB lookup, and the policy engine's Scope.covers() rule (internal/controlplane/policy/policy.go) requires every level the grant pins to match the resource — a grant with pinned ProjectID against a resource with empty ProjectID is structurally denied. Accepted design: middleware admits org-wide read roles + org-wide grants + (for CapRead actions) the support cross-tenant exception is suppressed because the resolver pins OrgID to principal.home; project-/env-/service-scoped grants are denied at the boundary by design, and grant-only principals must use the parent-scoped /v1/projects/{project_id}/environments instead. Document this in the route description, the resolver doc, and the handler doc — readers will misinterpret the structural deny as a bug otherwise. The store layer adds a tenant-scoped GetByID(ctx, q, organizationID, resourceID) on the existing repository (composite (organization_id, id) predicate so cross-tenant returns apierr.NotFound, never another tenant's row, never a 500 leaking the cause), and the existing reader struct grows a Get<Resource>(ctx, organizationID, resourceID) method that wraps GetByID inside Store.Read. The HTTP layer adds a NEW narrow port (distinct from the existing parent-scoped port — e.g. EnvironmentReader vs ProjectEnvironmentReader — so a handler change to one doesn't ripple through the other), a NEW resolver function (e.g. environmentIDResolver), and a NEW handler that REUSES the existing wire-shape projection (projectEnvironmentOf for environments) so the GET-by-id response is byte-identical to one element of the LIST response (modulo the wrapping object name). cmd/yalla-api/main.go passes the same *store.<Resource>Reader value twice — once for the parent-scoped port, once for the new bare-lookup port — because both methods live on the same store struct; no second startup wiring is needed. The contract tests cover the standard envelope: happy path, NotFound (cross-tenant + unknown both render as the same 404 — no oracle), 401 unauthenticated + invalid credentials (no token echo), 503 store outage (no raw cause leak), 500 nil-port wiring guard, envelope shape lock (asserts the documented field set against a generic decode so a future struct column added without updating the projection is caught), bearer-token never-logged guard (slog over a buffer), OpenAPI route registration, and TWO resolver unit tests (pins home org + path id; no panic with no principal on context). The matching policy matrix story (BE-0156 for environments, the structural template for service/deployment lookups) tests the deny — the deny matrix on the bare endpoint is part of the contract, not a gap.
ralph/progress.txt:1023:- environment.grants.write-on-bare-{environment_id}-resolver policy matrix (BE-0171, internal/controlplane/httpapi/environment_grants_put_policy_test.go) is the structural HYBRID of the project.grants.write matrix (BE-0141, projects_grants_put_policy_test.go) and the environment.grants.read matrix (BE-0168, environment_grants_policy_test.go): take BE-0141's CapAdmin role table + cross-tenant + request-body-leak-guard skeleton, swap action+resolver for the write env-grants endpoint, and re-pin every wire tie-in with BE-0168's no-ProjectID covers() denial pattern (project/env/service scoped principals ALL denied OutOfScope at this route — even on a grant naming the SAME env id — because the env-id resolver pins NO ProjectID leg and covers() is one-way). Load-bearing engine distinction from BE-0168: CapAdmin is OUTSIDE the support cross-tenant exception, so the engine_cross_tenant_deny_* parallel subtests MUST include the Support row for write actions and exclude it for read actions. Net-new fixtures in the put_policy file are only a route-specific replaceEnvironmentGrantsMatrixBody (no environment_id field per grant — the env is named by the path) + replaceEnvironmentGrantsRequestBodyLeak with distinctive needles; the canonical*EnvGrants* fixtures, fake*EnvironmentGrant* fakes, replaceEnvironmentGrantsHandlerFor / putEnvironmentGrants / decodeReplaceEnvironmentGrants, and orgPrincipal / decodeError are all reused verbatim from BE-0168 and BE-0169.
ralph/progress.txt:1024:- Inbound HTTP rate-limit pattern (BE-0035, internal/controlplane/ratelimit/limiter.go + internal/controlplane/httpapi/ratelimit.go + apierr.RateLimited + config.RateLimit): the limiter is an in-process multi-dimensional token-bucket gate (per-org, per-API-key, per-IP) with read/write-class specs derived from the HTTP method (GET/HEAD/OPTIONS = read, everything else = write). Three load-bearing rules: (1) the gate wraps every route INSIDE RequireAuth in server.go's per-route loop (`h = rateLimit(h); if RequiresAuth: h = RequireAuth(...)(h)`) so the resolved principal (org id, key id, auth method) is on the request context when the limiter decides — public endpoints fall back to the IP bucket alone; (2) the internal-worker exemption is wired through a new package-private context helper `withAuthMethod` / `AuthMethodFromContext` in middleware.go that BOTH RequireAuth and RequireInternalWorker stamp with id.Method, so the rate-limit gate can recognise auth.MethodInternalWorker and pass `Exempt=true` to the limiter without re-running the credential store; (3) the "first exhausted bucket wins" rule short-circuits downstream debits (org denied -> key & ip buckets untouched) so a single throttled tenant cannot drain a shared NAT'd IP bucket of every neighbour. Bucket map is sync.Mutex'd with lazy eviction on the request path (no background goroutine; pruning sweeps every IdleTTL); the freshly-created bucket MUST set lastUsed=now BEFORE the sweep runs so the sweep cannot reap the bucket it just inserted (initial zero-value lastUsed is older than any cutoff and was a real bug caught by tests). The 429 wire shape is the standard yalla.error.v1 envelope with apierr.RateLimited(scope, retry) attaching `scope` (bucket dimension name only — "organization" / "api_key" / "ip", NEVER the tenant id or key id or IP) and `retry_after` (integer seconds, clamped to >=1 because the wire Retry-After header cannot carry sub-second); the response also sets the matching `Retry-After: N` header. NewHandler grew ONE new trailing parameter (RateLimiter) — sweep all 64 NewHandler call sites in tests via a Node script that walks the source with depth-tracking parens and appends `, nil` before each closing `)`. Config plumbing: new YALLA_RATE_LIMIT_* env vars (ORG/KEY/IP × READ/WRITE × RPS/BURST + IDLE_TTL + DISABLED master switch) layered over per-profile defaults; ProfileTest defaults to Disabled=true so contract tests across the whole repo never accidentally trip the limiter. The limiter constructor takes a `func() time.Time` clock injection point so deterministic tests (`fixedClock.Advance(d)`) can drive refill behaviour without sleeping. Forward-applicable to any future throughput cap (per-resource, per-tenant-tier, per-route-specific) — extend Specs/Specs.For pairs rather than adding a new bucket dimension. Redaction backstops: the 429 envelope, the Retry-After header, the WARN log record on denial, and every rate-limit identifier flow through telemetry.LogAttrs / apienvelope's regex-based scrubber so a bearer-token in the Authorization header on a throttled request NEVER reaches a log file (TestRateLimitDenialLogsNoSecretValue is the regression pin).
ralph/progress.txt:1025:- Pagination contract package (BE-0037, internal/controlplane/pagination): every customer-facing list endpoint composes the shared pagination.Params + pagination.Cursor + pagination.Page[T] envelope rather than reimplementing limit/cursor/sort/filter parsing. ParseParams(url.Values, ParseOptions) parses ?limit= ?cursor= ?sort= ?direction= ?filter.<k>= with stable apierr.InvalidInput (E_INVALID_INPUT) violations whose Reason text NEVER echoes the submitted value (only classification + accepted range — protects against reflection-style content channels), accumulates multiple violations into one envelope, enforces the package MaxLimit=200 ceiling even when an endpoint misconfigures its own, and rejects a cursor whose embedded Sort/Direction disagrees with the request as a stable 400 "fetch the first page again" instead of silently serving overlapping pages. Cursor is base64url-encoded JSON {v: "yalla.cursor.v1", p: Position, s: Sort, d: Direction} — opaque to clients, deterministic (same input -> same encoding so HTTP fixtures diff byte-for-byte), URL-safe (RawURLEncoding, no '+/='), and DOES NOT carry an organization_id / tenant / principal field by contract (a TestDecodeCursorTenantIsolationCannotBeBypassed test guards this so adding one is impossible without breaking the suite — tenant isolation stays the persistence layer's responsibility, cursor only encodes a within-set position). Page[T any] is the wire shape: Items always non-nil (BuildPage guarantees []T{} for empty), NextCursor omitempty (empty string == end of stream — agents do NOT compare len(Items) to limit), TotalEstimate *int64 omitempty (nil = "not cheap to compute", endpoints must NOT fabricate a value). BuildPage uses the over-fetch strategy: store fetches Limit+1 rows, BuildPage trims to Limit and encodes next_cursor from the last retained row's position via a PositionFunc[T] adapter. Cursor stability under inserts is proven end-to-end by TestCursorIsStableUnderInserts: seed N rows, page once, insert NEWER rows after the cursor boundary, prove page 2 still returns exactly the remaining originals (no duplicates, no skips, no newly-inserted rows leaking onto page 2 because the cursor is a strict upper bound on (sort_key DESC, id DESC)). Future list endpoints replace their bespoke parseXxxLimit / list payload struct with one ParseParams call + a BuildPage projection — the existing GET /v1/organizations/{org_id}/audit-events (BE-0103) and break_glass list are pre-pagination-contract and should migrate in their own stories.
ralph/progress.txt:1026:- Bare-id route on a deployment (BE-0211+0212+0213 pattern, GET /v1/deployments/{deployment_id}, store/deployment.go DeploymentReader.GetServiceDeployment, httpapi/deployments.go DeploymentGetter port + deploymentIDResolver + getServiceDeploymentHandler + getServiceDeploymentPayload, routes.go GET /v1/deployments/{deployment_id} entry, server.go NewHandler DeploymentGetter parameter, cmd/yalla-api/main.go wires the SAME *store.DeploymentReader as BOTH DeploymentLister AND DeploymentGetter): canonical shape for ALL bare-id read endpoints on a Deployment-level deep resource. Two LOAD-BEARING structural differences from the environment / service bare-id routes (BE-0154 / its services sibling): (1) policy.Scope has NO DeploymentID leg — the Scope hierarchy stops at ServiceID (Organization -> Project -> Environment -> Service), so the resolver pins ONLY OrganizationID, NOT (org + DeploymentID) the way environmentIDResolver pins (org + EnvironmentID). The resource scope carries no inner leg at all, which makes the engine's covers() rule deny EVERY scoped grant (project/env/service) at the boundary via ReasonDeniedOutOfScope — even a service-scoped grant naming the deployment's actual parent service is denied, because covers() is one-way (a grant scope that pins ServiceID cannot cover a resource scope that does not). Principals whose only access is a scoped grant must use a parent-scoped route family to address a deployment. (2) The store-layer adapter intentionally skips the parent-prefetch the list adapter performs: DeploymentReader.GetServiceDeployment composes only DeploymentRepository.GetByID inside a short-lived Store.Read tx (no service prefetch), because the schema's composite foreign keys on the deployments table already guarantee (organization_id, project_id), (organization_id, environment_id), and (organization_id, service_id) cannot diverge across the deployment row — a tenant-scoped GetByID is itself a complete tenant-isolation check, and a prefetch would only duplicate the work of the schema. This is the load-bearing distinction from the list adapter ListServiceDeployments (which DOES prefetch the service to disambiguate "service missing" from "service has no deployments"). The wire shape (serviceDeployment + serviceDeploymentOf in service_deployments.go) is REUSED verbatim — an agent that listed deployments for a service does not re-parse a different shape to fetch one of them by id. Production wiring passes the same *store.DeploymentReader pointer twice (deploymentService, deploymentReader, deploymentReader, breakGlassService, ...): one concrete adapter satisfies BOTH the DeploymentLister AND DeploymentGetter port interfaces, so adding a second read port costs zero new constructors / dependencies. The contract test suite (deployments_get_test.go, 12 tests) and policy matrix (deployments_get_policy_test.go, 4 parent tests) follow the BE-0210 list-side template structure verbatim, with one structural extension in the grant-containment matrix: a service-scoped grant naming THIS deployment's actual parent service is also denied at the wire, because the bare-id resource scope has no ServiceID leg to match. Future bare-id read endpoints on deep resources whose Scope leg does not exist (deployments today; jobs / runs in later stories) should adopt this exact pattern.
ralph/progress.txt:1029:- Bare-id read endpoint on a deep resource with a worker-deferred upstream (BE-0229+0230+0231 pattern, GET /v1/services/{service_id}/logs, store/service_log_reader.go ServiceLogReader.ListLogs, httpapi/services_logs.go ServiceLogReader port + serviceLogLine/listServiceLogsPayload wire DTOs + parseServiceLogsLimit + listServiceLogsHandler, routes.go GET /v1/services/{service_id}/logs entry, server.go NewHandler + newRouteTable + cmd/yalla-api/main.go wiring, services_logs_test.go contract suite + services_logs_policy_test.go matrix): canonical shape for ANY observability read endpoint whose real upstream is the Dokploy worker (logs, metrics — BE-0232..0234 next; future events/traces) and whose true content adapter is deferred to a later worker story. Three load-bearing rules: (1) the store-level adapter (store.ServiceLogReader, the second instance of the typed-input-typed-output read adapter shape after store.ServiceReader.GetService) performs the tenant-scoped parent existence check inside a Store.Read transaction so a cross-tenant or unknown service_id surfaces as a deterministic apierr.NotFound at the persistence boundary — never disguised as an empty success and never disguised as a 500 — and then returns an empty Lines slice with the ServiceID populated; the real Dokploy log fetcher is wired in BE-0301+ alongside the provisioning worker, matching the noopJobEnqueuer pattern for placeholder upstreams; (2) the wire DTO is a closed tuple `(service_id, lines[]{occurred_at, stream, message})` with NO pointer-typed fields, NO embedded interfaces, and an empty lines slice that marshals as `[]` not `null` so an agent does not need to special-case the absent-vs-empty distinction; the occurred_at field is RFC3339-nanosecond UTC; the stream enum is the closed set {stdout, stderr, system} so an agent can branch on the value without escape-decoding it; (3) the ?limit= query parameter is clamped at the boundary via parseServiceLogsLimit (the second instance of the bounded-limit-query-parser shape after parseAuditEventLimit, range [1, 1000] default 100), and the error path never echoes the submitted value — only the classification and the accepted range — so a typo can never become a reflection-style content channel. The CapRead policy matrix shape is the structural sibling of services_get_policy_test.go (BE-0184) — all six built-in roles allow same-tenant via ReasonAllowedByRole, support is allowed cross-tenant via ReasonAllowedBySupport at the engine level (defence-in-depth — the customer-facing route cannot reach this branch because serviceIDResolver pins the resource to the principal's home org), every project-/env-/service-scoped grant is denied OutOfScope on the bare-service-id path via the engine's one-way covers() rule, and an org-level Viewer grant is ALLOWED end-to-end (the load-bearing distinction from services_start_policy_test.go where the same grant is denied because service.start is CapDeploy, not CapRead). Limit assertion subtlety pinned by tests: the no-echo check on the invalid-limit deny path must restrict to non-numeric submitted values because numeric out-of-range inputs may legitimately share digits with the accepted-range message ("0" is a substring of "1000" in the "must be between 1 and 1000" string) — only the non-numeric case (where caller-controlled byte content could realistically reach the response body) is asserted. Test-only nil-port test: a typed-nil ServiceLogReader is needed (a bare `nil` literal does not satisfy the interface positional argument), so `var nilReader ServiceLogReader` is the pattern. Port-arity sweep: dominant `fakeServiceStopper{}, fakeServiceVariableReader{}` -> `fakeServiceStopper{}, fakeServiceLogReader{}, fakeServiceVariableReader{}` across 63 test files (108 sites) plus a minor variant `fakeServiceStopper{}, reader, fakeServiceVariableReplacer{}` -> `fakeServiceStopper{}, fakeServiceLogReader{}, reader, fakeServiceVariableReplacer{}` in service_variables_test.go (the test injects a local fakeServiceVariableReader as `reader`, so the LogReader slot is the new positional argument); the `stopper, fakeServiceVariableReader{}` variant in services_stop_test.go is one of the 108 dominant sites. Forward-applicable to BE-0232+0233+0234 (GET /v1/services/{service_id}/metrics, metrics.read action — the OTHER CapRead observability endpoint on the same resolver), and to any future read endpoint whose true content adapter is the Dokploy worker (events, traces) — same vertical, same placeholder shape, same wire-contract guarantees while the real fetcher lands later. The OpenAPI `?limit=` query parameter today is documented only via the Description prose because openapi.Endpoint has no QueryParams field; a future BE story can add structured QueryParam metadata and the existing route entries (audit-events, logs) will switch over together. CONFIRMED two-instance pattern after BE-0232+0233+0234: GET /v1/services/{service_id}/metrics mirrors the logs vertical one-for-one (store.ServiceMetricsReader with tenant-scoped GetByID, httpapi.ServiceMetricsReader port + listServiceMetricsHandler + parseServiceMetricsLimit + listServiceMetricsPayload, serviceMetricSample{name, occurred_at, value, unit} wire DTO with empty samples slice marshalling as [] not null, route on serviceIDResolver with action metrics.read, contract suite in services_metrics_test.go, policy matrix in services_metrics_policy_test.go); the metric-name closed set is the usage-metric ladder (http_requests, http_response_bytes, http_rps_peak_1m, latency_p95_ms, container_cpu_millicore_seconds, container_memory_mb_hours, storage_gb_month, build_minutes, …); the type-level doc explicitly disclaims billing authority (usage_counters + BE-0569/BE-0580 remain authoritative for invoiceable totals — this surface is a customer-visible read-through, not the billing oracle). New-arg mechanics: a Python ast-walking script (paren-depth-aware comma scanner, REVERSE call-order insertion so earlier offsets stay valid) injects the new ServiceMetricsReader / fakeServiceMetricsReader{} positional arg at slot 55 of newRouteTable and slot 57 of NewHandler across 33 test files / 63 call sites — reproducible recipe for any future newRouteTable / NewHandler signature growth. The third instance (events, traces, profiles, anything else under serviceIDResolver) can follow this template mechanically.
ralph/progress.txt:1032:- POST create endpoint for a service-scoped child resource (BE-0250+0251+0252 pattern, POST /v1/services/{service_id}/backups, mirrors the service_domains POST trio BE-0238+0239+0240; clonable for BE-0264 previews, BE-0289 import previews, and any future bare-id child create): the store-layer composition is the canonical create-unit-of-work shape — ServiceXxxRepository.Insert (single-statement INSERT...RETURNING + mapWriteError mapping driver errors to typed apierr.Conflict whose message is value-free) + ServiceXxxService.Create orchestrator that opens a Store.Write Tx, runs the tenant-scoped parent existence check first (so a cross-tenant or unknown parent id surfaces as a deterministic apierr.NotFound, NEVER a 500 or silent success), runs an in-tx Authorize (defence-in-depth against grant-change races between the HTTP authorize and the desired-state write — the ONLY operation that runs in-tx Authorize; the in-transaction Authorizer is reserved for Create), runs the Insert, and Appends the immutable audit record — all inside the same *Tx so a row can never be persisted without its audit trail. Validation runs BEFORE the Tx is opened (validateCreateXxxInput against a shared validate.Collector so a request rejecting on more than one field surfaces every violation in one typed apierr.InvalidInput; submitted values NEVER appear in violation reasons). The customer-facing create pins any lifecycle status column to a fixed initial value (e.g. ServiceBackupStatusPending) so a customer cannot forge a 'succeeded' / 'completed' status through the public API surface — the worker is the authority for closed-taxonomy transitions; the status column is NOT in the createXxxRequest decoder shape and the validator overwrites in.Status in the validated struct. The audit Metadata records ONLY fields the application layer can prove redaction-safe by construction: structural identifiers (service_id), closed-taxonomy enums (status, certificate_type), and bounded primitives (retention_count, version, enabled flag rendered as "true"/"false"); caller-supplied free text (display_name, schedule, hostname even — schedule is a cron-style expression but is still caller-controlled bytes) is NEVER recorded verbatim, because a customer pasting a token into one of those fields is exactly the footgun a defensive convention avoids. The httpapi-layer composition is the canonical create-handler shape — createXxxRequest decoder (Enabled / boolean-default-true fields are pointers to preserve the absent-vs-explicit-false distinction at the JSON seam — `*bool` decoded; an omitted field collapses to the default at the handler boundary, an explicit `false` rides through unchanged; integer fields take their schema-side default when zero) + createXxxPayload + createXxxHandler that gates on RequireAuth + the typed-internal nil-creator guard + validate.DecodeJSON (strict, max-body-bytes, unknown-field rejection, no input echo on parse failure) + creator.Create + http.StatusCreated yalla.output.v1 envelope projecting through serviceXxxOf. Action is CapWrite (ActionXxxCreate -> CapWrite in catalog.go): owner / admin / developer allowed by role; CI / viewer / support denied via ReasonDeniedNoCapability — load-bearing distinction: a CapDeploy sibling action (e.g. ActionBackupRun, ActionDeploymentCreate) WOULD admit CI. The wire route uses serviceIDResolver (no parent_project_id leg pinned on the resource scope), so EVERY project-/env-/service-scoped grant is denied at the boundary via the engine's one-way covers() rule (ReasonDeniedOutOfScope); only an organization-wide write role or an org-level write grant reaches the handler. Cross-tenant: the resolver pins resource scope to the principal's home org id (never the path service's tenant), so cross-tenant service_ids hit the persistence layer with the home org id and surface as a deterministic apierr.NotFound — engine cross-tenant deny is pinned at the engine level (defence-in-depth: EVERY role including Support is denied cross-tenant for CapWrite — the support cross-tenant exception covers only CapRead / CapSupport). Contract test surface: success with full plumbing assertions (principal home org id forwarded, path service id forwarded, actor id forwarded, no caller-body org id trusted), boolean-default-true and explicit-false at the pointer seam, request_id propagation, validation failure propagated from creator as E_INVALID_INPUT (handler delegates validation to the store-layer orchestrator), strict-decode unknown-field rejection BEFORE creator runs, unauthenticated 401, role-deny 403 (handler never reached), cross-tenant 404 with principal home org forwarded, duplicate-id Conflict 409, store-unavailable 503, nil-creator typed Internal 500. Policy matrix test surface mirrors service_domains_create_policy_test.go one-for-one. Wiring footprint: adds ONE new positional arg (ServiceXxxCreator) to newRouteTable (~slot 57) and NewHandler (~slot 60); ~70 test files / ~120 call sites need a `fakeServiceXxxCreator{}` insertion via bulk substitution `fakeServiceYyyReader{}, fakeServiceZzzReader{}` -> `fakeServiceYyyReader{}, fakeServiceXxxCreator{}, fakeServiceZzzReader{}` (where Yyy is the sibling Reader and Zzz is the next port after the new slot in the signature); the special cases are the in-package contract-test helper for the new resource (`reader` -> `..., reader, fakeServiceXxxCreator{}, ...`) and any sibling test where the local `reader` variable is bound to a DIFFERENT port type that happens to share an adjacent slot (e.g. service_variables_test.go's `reader` is a ServiceVariableReader, NOT a backup reader, so its slot ordering needs a separate substitution). Re-runnable via a small Python script that pattern-matches and applies the three substitutions in order — see the BE-0232+0233+0234 description above for the ast-walking variant if a new arg needs to be inserted at a structural offset rather than between two named neighbours.
ralph/progress.txt:1034:- Verb-on-child-resource endpoint pattern (BE-0253+0254+0255, POST /v1/services/{service_id}/backups/{backup_id}/run; sibling of the services_restart verb-on-parent shape, but adds a tenant-scoped child existence check; clonable for any future "trigger an action on a worker-driven child resource" endpoint — backup PATCH/DELETE, future preview-run, future deployment-retry triggers): the store-layer composition is the canonical run-unit-of-work shape — XxxRepository.GetByID (composite (org, parent, child_id) read; pgx.ErrNoRows -> apierr.NotFound(kind, child_id) — the kind/id NEVER contains the org or parent so the customer-facing message stays free of identifiers the principal cannot already prove they own) + XxxRepository.MarkPending (UPDATE...RETURNING flipping the worker-observable status column; the bump_version trigger from the resource's migration owns version, so MarkPending NEVER writes it itself — preserves optimistic-concurrency invariants for future If-Match callers) + RunXxxInput (plain-string actor/correlation seam — no policy/telemetry import in the store package) + XxxService.Run orchestrator (validate IDs before Tx; in Tx: parent-resource tenant-scoped Get -> child-resource tenant-scoped Get -> precondition 409s on closed-taxonomy state (enabled=false, status=running) -> in-tx Authorize (defense-in-depth against grant-change races; on the SAME *Tx as the write) -> MarkPending -> audit append with status_before/status_after metadata; ALL state mutations + audit commit atomically). The audit Metadata records ONLY closed-taxonomy enums (status_before/status_after) and structural identifiers (service_id); caller-controlled free text (display_name, schedule) is NOT recorded — Run does not mutate either and including them would expose a future redaction footgun. The httpapi layer is the canonical verb-handler shape — empty body, principal home org + path {parent_id} + path {child_id} forwarded to the runner, RequireAuth gates on the action's catalog mapping (backup.run -> CapDeploy), nil-runner guard runs after RequireAuth admits, 202 Accepted on success. Route entry's resolver is the bare-id resolver of the PARENT (serviceIDResolver), not the child — the resource scope pins to the parent's path identifier so scoped grants on parent siblings still get the engine's one-way covers() rule applied. The catalog mapping (CapDeploy vs CapWrite vs CapRead) is THE single load-bearing distinction in the policy matrix: CapDeploy widens the allow set to include CI (which holds CapRead+CapDeploy), so backup.run is allowed for CI keys even though backup.create (CapWrite) is denied — the policy matrix test pins this distinction at every grant scope (project-, env-, service-, org-level grants) AND at the engine's cross-tenant deny (CapDeploy is OUTSIDE the support cross-tenant exception, same as CapWrite). The bulk fake-fixture sweep for the new positional port follows the BE-0244 pattern: `perl -i -pe 's/fakePREVIOUS\{\},/fakePREVIOUS{}, fakeNEW{},/g' $(grep -rl 'fakePREVIOUS{}' internal/controlplane/httpapi/)` keyed on the immediately preceding port's bare struct literal — 71 files in the BE-0253 sweep — with two always-manual follow-ups: (a) the resource's own create-test helper that uses a local variable `creator` for the previous slot, (b) any wiring test that explicitly passes `nil` for the previous slot to reach the typed-internal guard.
ralph/progress.txt:1038:- Per-subtype quota dimension on the services Create unit of work (BE-0326 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/serviceservice.go ServiceService.Create + internal/controlplane/store/service_quota_applications_concurrent_test.go; structural template for BE-0327 compose_stacks, BE-0328 databases — every per-subtype dimension whose denominator is a SUBSET of the services table filtered by Kind): the create unit of work calls svc.quota.Reserve TWICE in the same *Tx for a service whose kind consumes a per-subtype dimension — first the existing QuotaResourceServices reservation (counts every service), then a conditional `if service.Kind == ServiceKindXxx { Reserve(QuotaResourceXxx) }` AFTER it and BEFORE the services.Insert. Order matters: the services reservation locks first so a tenant who exhausts the services dimension cannot acquire the per-subtype counter at all; the per-subtype reservation locks second so a tenant who exhausts ONLY the per-subtype dimension still gets a typed apierr.QuotaExceeded whose ExceededDetail.Resource names the per-subtype dimension specifically. Both reservations roll back together on any later failure (Insert, Enqueue, audit Append). Test pattern (clone of BE-0325): TWO quota_usage rows + TWO quota_policies seeded (per-subtype TIGHT at 5, services LOOSE at attempts*2 so the per-subtype dimension is the boundary that bites), assert detail.Resource == QuotaResourceXxx, assert BOTH active reservation tables == success count (because every winning Create reserved one of each). A second non-concurrent test exhausts the per-subtype dimension then proves a sibling-kind Create (compose / database for BE-0326) still succeeds — pins the per-subtype boundary as a regression barrier against a future refactor that drops the Kind guard. BE-0326 already lands the kind-guard inside ServiceService.Create at the same line where Reserve(services) returns nil; BE-0327 and BE-0328 each add ONE more conditional Reserve call right below the BE-0326 block (Kind == ServiceKindCompose / ServiceKindDatabase) and ONE more test file cloned from service_quota_applications_concurrent_test.go with the dimension constant + Kind constant + sibling-kind sanity-success cases swapped. NOT applicable to dimensions that span MULTIPLE service kinds with a non-trivial denominator (cpu_millicores / memory_mb / storage_gb — those count a numeric column on every kind and belong to a different per-numeric-resource pattern still to be designed).
ralph/progress.txt:1039:- Per-Kind quota facet on environments (BE-0330 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/environmentservice.go + migrate/migrations/0025_environment_kind.{up,down}.sql + internal/controlplane/store/environment_quota_preview_environments_concurrent_test.go): structural template for ANY future per-Kind environment quota dimension (analog of the BE-0326/0327/0328 services per-Kind dimensions, now ported to the environments table). The FIRST per-Kind environment story lands the seam: a `kind` text column on environments with NOT NULL DEFAULT 'standard' and a CHECK constraint mirroring the closed Go enum one-to-one; a partial index on (organization_id, project_id) WHERE kind = '<minority-kind>' for the future sweeper; a `Kind` field on Environment, CreateEnvironmentInput, and CloneEnvironmentInput; a validateEnvironmentKind helper that normalises blank to the default member so legacy callers stay green; a closed `EnvironmentKind*` const set; and a conditional Reserve(QuotaResource<KindFacet>) on BOTH EnvironmentService.Create AND EnvironmentService.Clone (environments are the only resource with two create-shape entrypoints, both must be gated together). Subsequent per-Kind environment quota stories ship test-only — the seam is in place after BE-0330. The Reserve gates run in the SAME *Tx as Reserve(QuotaResourceEnvironments) and the desired-state write, so a tenant that exhausts either dimension cannot half-write an environment row whose other dimension passed. The default-on-blank Go validator MUST match the SQL DEFAULT so the migration is forward-compatible with in-process callers that predate the Kind field.
ralph/progress.txt:1041:- Dimensional (magnitude-axis) quota dimension on a Create unit of work (BE-0331 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/projectservice.go QuotaReserver port + internal/controlplane/quota/quota.go Checker + internal/controlplane/store/serviceservice.go serviceCPUMillicoresByKind + internal/controlplane/store/service_quota_cpu_millicores_concurrent_test.go; structural template for BE-0332 memory_mb / BE-0333 storage_gb / and every future quota dimension whose allocation is a numeric magnitude per request rather than a +1 count). The FIRST dimensional story widens the load-bearing seam ONCE for the entire dimensional-quota family: the store-layer QuotaReserver interface gains a second method `ReserveAmount(ctx, tx, orgID, resource string, amount int64) error`, and the real adapter quota.Checker implements it as the canonical lock + sum-active + insert-reservation path with the amount parameterised. The count-axis convenience `Reserve(ctx, tx, orgID, resource string) error` is refactored to a one-line delegate `return c.ReserveAmount(...,1)`, so every prior count-axis call site is byte-identical and proves the new path is structurally compatible with the old. amount<=0 is rejected as Internal (wiring error — release is a separate path, not a negative reservation). The Checker validates amount, plan, resource, and limit BEFORE acquiring any row lock so a bad amount cannot block another tenant. The unit-of-work call site (ServiceService.Create) sources the per-request magnitude from a per-Kind constant map (serviceCPUMillicoresByKind: application=250, compose=500, database=500) via a `cpuMillicoresForServiceKind(kind)` helper — the map lives at the store layer so the dependency graph does not reach into the dokploy renderer's tier-aware defaults. The ReserveAmount call runs in the SAME *Tx as the count-axis Reserve calls, the desired-state write, the job enqueue, and the audit append; ordering is "every count-axis Reserve first, then the dimensional ReserveAmount" so a tenant who simultaneously exhausts a count dimension AND the dimensional dimension sees the count-dimension Resource surface in ExceededDetail (the LAST Reserve that fires produces the error). Three fakes (noopQuotaReserver in cmd/yalla-api/main.go, nopQuotaReserver in store/internal_test.go, recordingQuota in store/projectservice_test.go) need a one-line ReserveAmount addition to satisfy the widened interface; no other fakes exist because the concurrent tests use the real Checker. Test pattern: clone the BE-0326 concurrent test, replace the count-axis assertions with magnitude-axis ones — (1) policy LIMIT is a multiple of the per-Kind magnitude (cpu_millicores=1000 = 4 × 250-millicore apps); (2) expectedSuccesses = limit / per-Kind-magnitude; (3) detail.Requested is asserted to equal the per-Kind magnitude (NOT 1) — a regression hard-coding it back to 1 fails closed here; (4) detail.Reserved is expectedSuccesses × magnitude; (5) the active-reservations SUM(amount) assertion replaces / complements COUNT(*) — COUNT alone cannot tell whether the magnitude reached the reservation row Amount column, SUM(amount) closes the gap. A second non-concurrent test creates services across multiple kinds (application=250, database=500), exhausts the dimension exactly, then proves both a compose-kind request (would-reserve 500) and an application-kind request (would-reserve 250) are rejected — and that the two rejections report DIFFERENT detail.Requested values. The single equality check `composeDetail.Requested != appDetail.Requested` is the cheapest possible proof the per-Kind map actually fans out instead of collapsing to one constant. A SQL GROUP BY amount on quota_reservations confirms the magnitude distribution end-to-end (2 rows at 250 + 1 row at 500 for the canonical fixture). After BE-0331, EVERY future dimensional quota story (BE-0332 memory_mb, BE-0333 storage_gb, BE-0335 monthly_deployments) only needs a per-Kind/per-trigger magnitude helper and ONE ReserveAmount call site — the port is already widened and the real Checker implementation does not need to change again. The Reserve(...) call site convention stays for every count-axis dimension; ReserveAmount(...) is reserved for dimensions whose amount actually varies per request.
ralph/progress.txt:1043:- Quota-dimension story whose call site is NOT yet wired (BE-0337 api_keys pattern, internal/controlplane/store/apikeyservice.go + internal/controlplane/store/migrate/migrations/0027_quota_resource_api_keys.{up,down}.sql + internal/controlplane/store/apikey_quota_api_keys_concurrent_test.go + cmd/yalla-api/main.go; structural template for BE-0338 members and ANY future dimension story whose owning Service.Create predates the QuotaReserver port — applies whenever a dimension story is the FIRST to wire its service-layer Reserve, distinct from the BE-0331..BE-0336 'call site already there' siblings whose owning unit-of-work already reserves the dimension). The minimum atomic delivery is FOUR change axes: (1) closed-set expansion — a new `NNNN_quota_resource_<name>.{up,down}.sql` migration that follows the BE-0326 backup_schedules template verbatim (DO block looking up the inline DOMAIN CHECK by `contypid = 'quota_resource'::regtype AND contype = 'c'` so the migration is robust to any Postgres-assigned constraint name, then `ALTER DOMAIN quota_resource ADD CONSTRAINT quota_resource_check CHECK (VALUE IN (...))` with the prior set + the new label; the .down.sql is structurally identical and narrows back to the prior set, accepting that any row carrying the new label fails the restored CHECK — that is the correct behaviour, silently dropping rows would lose audit-relevant state); plus the matching `QuotaResource<Name>` constant + membership-set entry in internal/controlplane/store/quota.go; plus updating the `valid` slice in internal/controlplane/store/quota_internal_test.go::TestQuotaResourceValid which pins `len(valid) == len(quotaResources)` — the test is a sealing-trap that catches a forgotten membership-set entry but ALSO fires on a forgotten valid-slice entry; both must change together. (2) Constructor port widening — the existing service struct grows a `quota QuotaReserver` field, the constructor parameter list grows the same port, the nil-guard switch grows a `case quota == nil` arm placed BETWEEN the last existing repo arm and the audit arm (the convention is store → parent repos → own repo → authz? → quota → jobs? → audit), and the Create unit of work grows a `svc.quota.Reserve(ctx, tx, key.OrganizationID, string(QuotaResource<Name>))` call placed AFTER every existence/parent-lookup check and BEFORE the desired-state Insert so a 404 on the parent is never disguised as a quota rejection AND a row-level conflict (slug, prefix, unique constraint) rolls the reservation back with the rest of the unit of work. (3) Constructor call-site fanout — three call sites change: cmd/yalla-api/main.go grows a `<name>Quota := noopQuotaReserver{}` placeholder (the real quota.Checker adapter is wired separately by a cross-cutting plan-resolver story; placeholder mirrors the projectQuota / environmentQuota / serviceQuota convention exactly), the in-package `package store` constructor-nil test grows a `nopQuotaReserver{}` local + a new `{nil quota, ...}` case wedged BETWEEN the last existing nil case and the audit case (positional ordering must mirror the constructor's nil-guard switch), and the `package store_test` integration-test helper grows the shared `&recordingQuota{}` (defined in projectservice_test.go — not a local `noopQuotaReserver{}`, which is only visible to cmd/yalla-api; the store_test package has its OWN allow-all fake hierarchy and the convention is to reuse `recordingQuota` for happy-path constructors). (4) Concurrent integration test — clone of the BE-0336 concurrent_deployments test with five structural simplifications when the owning service has no Authorizer / JobEnqueuer ports (api_keys is pure Yalla identity with no Dokploy mirror, so APIKeyService takes neither): no `atomicAuthorizer` / `atomicJobs` fakes need to be defined or counted; no `authz.calls == attempts` and no `jobs.calls == successes` assertions; the per-goroutine input loop calls `auth.Generate()` INSIDE the goroutine so every concurrent attempt mints its own unique (prefix, secret_hash) pair via crypto/rand (the prefix column is UNIQUE so a shared pair would deterministically conflict and mask the quota race); the parent-row seed is only an `organizations` row (no project/environment/service chain — api_keys live directly under the tenant); and the leaky-input candidates in the rejection-message scan use the resource-specific prefix (`key-%02d` / `req_apikeys_%02d` / `corr_apikeys_%02d`) so an echoed input still fails the message guard. The 'second test' sibling (TestXxxCreateAppliesXxxQuotaIndependentlyOfOtherDimensions) seeds ONLY the dimension under enforcement, exhausts it sequentially, asserts a SIXTH Create surfaces the dimension-specific rejection, and asserts no reservation row exists on TWO unrelated dimensions (services / projects) — the symmetry guard that catches a future regression that drops the Reserve call OR accidentally chose a different resource string. The whole pattern compiles + passes -race against a real Postgres without touching ANY of the 70+ httpapi fake-fixture call sites because the service's HTTP layer is owned by a separate seam (the api_keys handler already calls APIKeyService.Create with the same input shape; only the store-layer wiring changes). Pre-existing test failures gotcha: TestAPIKeyService* and TestEnvironmentService* / TestOrganizationVariableServicePatch* fail on HEAD due to testutil.Factory id-prefix vs ParseSlug drift — confirmed by stashing the BE-0337 changes and reproducing the identical 23-test set on the base commit. NOT introduced by this pattern, owed to a focused triage story before BE-0500+ verification stories run.
ralph/progress.txt:1054:- Repository tenant-isolation test file for a **tenant-scoped grandchild table whose mutation surface is Insert + Update + ScheduleDeletion ONLY (NO UpdateDisplayName, NO Restore, NO ListByOrganization, NO CountByOrganization), with a database-managed version column, a nullable soft-delete column (deletion_scheduled_at), a CHECK-constrained closed-set Kind column, and a composite FK to a parent (organization_id, project_id) -> projects** (BE-0442 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/environment_tenant_isolation_test.go). The BE-0438 project_tenant_isolation_test.go template MINUS the UpdateDisplayName/Restore byte-identical-bystander pairs (the EnvironmentRepository exposes neither method) MINUS the ListByOrganization/CountByOrganization tenant-scope tests (environments are listed PER PROJECT, not per organization — quota counts environments per-project elsewhere, not on this repository). Three structural divergences from the projects template are load-bearing: (1) the ListByProject leg is the per-project analogue of ListByOrganization — the WHERE filter is (organization_id, project_id) instead of (organization_id), so the cross-tenant existence-leak projection is widened to a 3-case table (orgA owned empty project / orgA probing orgB project / orgA probing unknown project) that each yield a deterministic empty slice (NOT nil) AND ARE INDISTINGUISHABLE on the wire; the existing TestEnvironmentRepoListByProjectIsTenantScoped (environment_test.go) proves the cross-tenant projB.ID-under-orgA case alone, so this file's contribution is the symmetry guard across the OTHER two probes. (2) the shared-slug fixture ("production" on BOTH tenants' projects) exercises the per-PROJECT UNIQUE (project_id, slug), not the per-organization UNIQUE (organization_id, slug) of projects — two tenants legitimately share the slug because UNIQUE is anchored on the parent project (which differs per tenant), so the WHERE-on-(organization_id, project_id) filter is the only thing isolating the two rows in ListByProject; a regression that resolved the WHERE by slug alone or by project_id alone would surface here. (3) assertEnvironmentByteIdentical adds a Kind assertion on top of the projects template's column set — environments carries kind in its row body (CHECK-constrained to 'standard' or 'preview' by migration 0025) and the Update path SET clause MUST NOT touch it, so a regression that promoted kind to a SET clause is caught here in addition to the slug/display_name/version/updated_at/deletion_scheduled_at anchors. The deletion_scheduled_at switch/case carries the same three legs (nil->non-nil for ScheduleDeletion, non-nil->nil for a hypothetical Restore that does not exist on this repository — kept anyway as defensive coverage in case Restore is added later — and value-drift across non-nil baselines). The cross-tenant existence-leak probe on GetByID is the standard Code-AND-Hint identity assertion the BE-0438 template provides; this file adds NOTHING new on that surface — the contribution is the byte-identical-bystander projection on every mutation path (Update / ScheduleDeletion) AND the existence-leak symmetry-table on ListByProject AND the per-tenant view of soft-deleted rows. Helper `projATuple(store.Project) -> testutil.Project` is local to this file: the factory's Environment / Service builders take a testutil.Project, but the test seeds projects through the typed repository surface and gets back a store.Project — projATuple bridges the two shapes without a dependency on testutil internals (only ID, OrganizationID, Slug, Name are needed). The environmentFixture / insertEnvironment / getEnvironmentOrFail / assertEnvironmentByteIdentical helpers are file-scope: environmentFixture and insertEnvironment are reused from environment_repository_invariants_test.go (BE-0441 — do NOT re-implement); getEnvironmentOrFail and assertEnvironmentByteIdentical are NEW and named uniquely (no projects-template name collision in the shared store_test package). Pre-existing test failures on HEAD that are NOT introduced by this story: TestEnvironmentRepoGetByIDReturnsRow, TestEnvironmentReaderRejectsCrossTenantProjectID, and TestEnvironmentRepoListByProjectReturnsDeterministicOrdering all fail in isolation on the base commit (testutil.Factory slug-token drift vs hard-coded "production"/"staging" expectations in environment_test.go) — confirmed by stashing the BE-0442 file and reproducing identical failures. Owed to a focused triage story before BE-0500+ verification stories run.
ralph/progress.txt:1056:- Repository CRUD invariants test file for a **tenant-scoped child table whose ONLY mutation surface is an INSERT...ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE upsert PLUS a bulk DELETE-by-exclusion (no per-id Get, no per-id Delete, no traditional UPDATE), with a database-managed version column, ONE optional nullable scoping pointer (service_id) instead of two (no environment_id pointer because the env IS the parent), and a composite FK to a parent (organization_id, environment_id) -> environments** (BE-0443 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/environment_grant_repository_invariants_test.go). This is the BE-0439 project_grants template adapted for environment_grants. Three structural divergences from BE-0439 are load-bearing: (1) the EnvironmentGrant struct has NO ProjectID column and ONE optional pointer (ServiceID) instead of two (EnvironmentID is the mandatory FK target, not an optional scope leg) — the Upsert insert-pointer-roundtrip test collapses from two opt-in pointers to one (service-scoped grant). (2) The ON CONFLICT target is the unique index environment_grants_principal_scope_idx on (organization_id, environment_id, principal_id, COALESCE(service_id, '')) — there is no environment_id leg in the COALESCE because the environment is the parent scope, not an optional narrower; the duplicate-PK Conflict test uses a different principal_id under the same environment to dodge the scope-tuple branch and exercise the PRIMARY KEY (id) violation. (3) The CASCADE-delete-of-parent test deletes the environments row directly via raw SQL (NOT the EnvironmentRepository's ScheduleDeletion, which is a soft delete and would NOT trigger CASCADE) and observes via ListByEnvironment. Helper newEnvironmentGrantID synthesises an `egrnt_<token_n>` id by rewriting f.Environment's `env_<token_n>` prefix — the testutil.Factory only stamps known domain prefixes and there is no f.EnvironmentGrant, so prefix-rewriting is the cleanest way to mint factory-unique ids without reaching into domain.NewID(domain.KindEnvironmentGrant). Sentinel type `environmentGrantTxRollbackSentinel` is distinct from every other *_test.go file's rollback sentinel in store_test. List-helper name `listEnvironmentGrantsOrFail` is distinct from project_grant_repository_invariants_test.go's `listGrantsOrFail` to avoid same-package collision. Test name prefix `TestEnvironmentGrantRepository*` (full Repository) is distinct from environment_grant_test.go's `TestEnvironmentGrantRepo*` and `TestEnvironmentGrantReader*` siblings — no collisions.
ralph/progress.txt:1057:- Repository CRUD invariants test file for a **TENANT-SCOPED short-lived claim/ledger table whose ONLY repository mutation surface is an InsertReservation (one-shot active-row INSERT) plus a single resolver read (SumActiveReservations), with NO per-row Update/Delete/Get, NO version column, a settled_consistent CHECK pairing status<>'active' with NON-NULL settled_at, a CHECK amount>0, a composite-FK out (organization_id, job_id) -> provisioning_jobs added by a LATER migration than the row's own migration, and a database-managed bump-only set_updated_at trigger fired BEFORE UPDATE** (BE-0455 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/quota_reservation_repository_invariants_test.go). Structurally distinct from BE-0453 (quota_usage -- INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING + SELECT FOR UPDATE primitive) and BE-0451 (quota_policies -- INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE upsert) in six load-bearing ways: (a) the row-shape test must anchor BOTH a nullable-pointer JobID (zero on a no-JobID call, *string round-trip on a non-empty-JobID call) AND a nullable-pointer SettledAt (zero on every active-row insert -- there is no public surface that can mint a settled row); (b) the JobID round-trip leg cannot use a synthetic id alone because migration 0008 added a composite FK quota_reservations_job_fk -> provisioning_jobs (organization_id, id), so the test MUST pre-seed a real provisioning_jobs row (`INSERT INTO provisioning_jobs (id, organization_id, job_type, idempotency_key) VALUES (...)`) before InsertReservation -- a regression that dropped the FK or swapped its column order would surface here; (c) the settled_consistent CHECK violation has NO independent client-input surface (settled_at is not in the INSERT column list of InsertReservation), so the test exploits the OTHER direction: passing Status=ReservationStatusCommitted (non-blank, non-'active') sends settled_at=NULL via the default and the CHECK rejects it, proving InsertReservation can ONLY mint ACTIVE rows; (d) the time-filter leg of SumActiveReservations is a SECOND-AXIS exclusion test alongside the status filter -- a row with status='active' AND expires_at<=now must be excluded by the `expires_at > $3` predicate, so the test pre-seeds an active-but-time-expired row and asserts it is excluded WITHOUT mutating its status (a regression that dropped the time predicate would silently sum already-expired rows); (e) the resource-filter leg seeds TWO active reservations under the SAME tenant with DIFFERENT resources and asserts BOTH `SumActiveReservations(org, A, now)` and `SumActiveReservations(org, B, now)` return their own resource's amount (a regression that dropped the resource predicate would return 11 instead of 3 from either call); (f) the duplicate-ID Conflict test uses an explicit caller-supplied ID instead of relying on the qres_ prefix-mint path, because the PK violation is the constraint and a regression that silently re-minted the explicit id would mask the test (the explicit-ID-preservation test PRECEDES the duplicate-ID test and pins the no-remint contract). Forward-applicable to every future short-lived-claim repository whose public surface is a single one-shot active-row INSERT plus a resolver-read (a hypothetical lease-claim table KindResourceLease with active->held|released|expired, or a future job_attempts row whose public surface is a single INSERT per attempt). Test-helper naming caveat at package store_test: every *_test.go file under internal/controlplane/store/ shares the same package, so the type name `quotaReservationTxRollbackSentinel` must be distinct from quotaPolicyTxRollbackSentinel / quotaUsageTxRollbackSentinel / projectTxRollbackSentinel and friends -- grep `TxRollbackSentinel` before adding a new sentinel. Helper REUSE strategy: this file DEFINES its own `runInsertReservationOrFail` / `loadQuotaReservationRowByID` / `countQuotaReservationRowsForOrg` / `rawQuotaReservationRow` (no shared symbols to reuse), and REUSES the existing `insertReservationRow` / `insertSettledReservationRow` helpers defined in quota_repository_test.go for the SumActiveReservations setup legs because those raw-SQL inserts can stamp the schema's required `settled_at = now()` for non-active rows without going through the repository's active-only mint path.
ralph/progress.txt:1058:- Repository tenant-isolation test file for a **TENANT-SCOPED CLAIM-PROTOCOL TABLE WHOSE FOUR REPOSITORY SURFACES (Claim upsert with steal-expired branch / Complete state-transitioning UPDATE / Release state-conditioned DELETE / Find composite-tuple read) ALL CARRY organization_id AS THE LEADING COLUMN OF A COMPOSITE PREDICATE TUPLE (organization_id, principal_id, idempotency_key), AND WHOSE ROW BODY CARRIES NULLABLE COMPLETION COLUMNS (response_status / response_body / completed_at) DISTINGUISHED BY NIL-NESS UNDER A COMPLETION-CONSISTENT CHECK, AND WHOSE INVARIANTS FILE ALREADY LANDED IN THE IMMEDIATELY-PRIOR PRD STORY** (BE-0470 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/idempotency_repository_tenant_isolation_test.go for idempotency_keys). Subspecies of the BE-0468 (audit_events) cross-tenant template with seven load-bearing deltas: (a) there is NO ListByOrganization read surface — Find is the only read, scoped to the full (organization_id, principal_id, idempotency_key) tuple — so the "cross-tenant list returns only own rows" probe collapses to "cross-tenant Find returns (zero, false, nil)" with a per-tenant count assertion on both sides, AND the "limit clamping is tenant-scoped" probe drops entirely (no list cap exists). The file's package-doc header MUST document the absence so a future reader does not look for a missing test, mirroring the BE-0426 organizations file's "tenant-root tables have no ListByParent" doc-only delegation; (b) the byte-identity comparator MUST handle three nullable pointer-bearing columns (*int for response_status, []byte for response_body, *time.Time for completed_at) where the pending-vs-completed lifecycle distinction is observable through nil-ness. A simple field-equality loop will silently round-trip a regression that flipped a pending row's NULL completion columns to zero-value scalars (e.g. response_status=0, response_body=[]byte{}, completed_at=epoch). The pattern: switch on (after==nil, baseline==nil) into three branches — both-nil OK, one-nil diff "nil-ness drifted", both-non-nil dereference-compare — and emit a fmt-printer-pair (fmtIntPtr / fmtTimePtr) so the diagnostic reads "before=<nil> after=200" rather than pointer noise. The pattern is forward-applicable to every future tenant-isolation file whose row carries nullable completion columns (e.g. job_attempts with nullable error_summary + finished_at + result, quota_reservations with nullable released_at + job_id); (c) every mutating surface needs its OWN OnOrgADoesNotTouchOrgB pair — Claim, Complete, Release. The Complete probe is structurally distinct from the Claim probe because Complete's WHERE composes `organization_id = $1 AND principal_id = $2 AND idempotency_key = $3 AND status = 'pending'` so the cross-tenant caller falls off the organization_id leg BEFORE the status leg runs, and the typed Conflict ("no longer pending") fires WITHOUT a foreign-row read — a regression that dropped organization_id from the Complete WHERE would either rewrite orgB's row (worst case) OR silently complete orgA's "claim" with a forged response envelope. The Release probe asserts the dual: Release is "safe to call defensively" and returns nil on a zero-row DELETE, so the cross-tenant Release must return nil AND leave orgB's pending row byte-identical AND the per-tenant count unchanged — three separate assertions because a regression that dropped organization_id from the Release WHERE would delete orgB's row, which the bystander byte-identity loader catches as a missing-row error inside loadIdempotencyRowByID's t.Fatalf. The forged Complete envelope MUST carry a recognisable canary string (`"forged":"orgA-tries-to-complete-orgB-claim"`) so a future reader can see immediately what the test was protecting against; (d) the Claim cross-tenant id-collision probe needs a GLOBAL row-count assertion in addition to the per-tenant counts. The PRIMARY KEY on idempotency_keys.id is global, so a rolled-back PK violation under a foreign tenant could in principle land the failing row in some other (third) tenant's slot — the per-tenant probe alone cannot prove the absence because the failing row's organization_id is unknown to the probe. The fix is a tiny totalIdempotencyRows(ctx, t, db) helper that runs `SELECT count(*) FROM idempotency_keys` with no WHERE, snapshotted before and after the failing Claim. The pattern is forward-applicable to every future tenant-isolation file for a table whose PK is global (not parent-scoped); (e) the Claim cross-tenant id-collision probe MUST force the collision through caller-supplied ids on DIFFERENT (principal_id, idempotency_key) tuples so the ON CONFLICT target does NOT match and the PRIMARY KEY violation flows through mapWriteError. A naive collision attempt under the same (principal, key) tuple would be intercepted by ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE and silently rewritten through the expiry-takeover branch — the test would pass on a broken implementation. The id-collision test in this file mints sharedID via mintIdempotencyID and assigns it to a record carrying a DIFFERENT (principal_id, idempotency_key) than the seeded orgA row. The pattern is forward-applicable to every future tenant-isolation file for an UPSERT table whose ON CONFLICT target is a NON-PK unique tuple (idempotency_keys uses (org, principal, key); job_attempts may use (job_id, attempt_number); etc.); (f) the Claim "mirror tuple" probe is the load-bearing per-tenant-uniqueness assertion of the ON CONFLICT target. orgB seeds a row at (orgB, usr_mirror, mirror-key); orgA Claims a row at (orgA, usr_mirror, mirror-key) — same (principal_id, idempotency_key), different organization_id. A regression that dropped organization_id from the ON CONFLICT target would resolve the conflict onto orgB's mirror-tuple row and silently rewrite it through the DO UPDATE branch — the worst-case cross-tenant idempotency data-corruption shape. The assertion: orgA's Claim returns owned=true (fresh INSERT, never DO UPDATE on a foreign row), orgA's new row carries orgA's organization_id and a distinct id, AND orgB's mirror row remains byte-identical (every field including the nullable completion columns and the database-assigned created_at + caller-supplied expires_at). The "mirror tuple" terminology and the explicit comment ("the load-bearing bystander whose presence on orgB exercises the per-tenant uniqueness of the ON CONFLICT target") MUST be carried verbatim in the seed comment because the structural test failure mode (silent peer-row rewrite) is the kind a future reader will rationalise away without it; (g) the cascade-from-organization-delete probe MUST seed orgB rows covering the lifecycle spread — pending, completed-with-envelope, pending-with-future-expiry — because the nullable completion columns are the load-bearing byte-identity surface, not the always-set columns. The single-bystander variant of this cascade is already pinned by the BE-0469 invariants file's TestIdempotencyRepositoryCascadeFromOrganizationDelete (one orgB row, inline column equalities); the tenant-isolation file deepens it with a three-row pool and the assertIdempotencyRowByteIdentical comparator across every row, plus a post-cascade Find for every surviving orgB row to prove the cascade did not silently corrupt the repository read predicate. New helpers introduced: assertIdempotencyRowByteIdentical (nullable-aware comparator), fmtIntPtr / fmtTimePtr / itoaInt (pointer-friendly diagnostic formatters — itoaInt avoids importing strconv solely for the formatter call sites), seedTwoTenantIdempotencyFixture (two-organization shape; idempotency_keys has no project/environment/service parent chain so the fixture is narrower than the dokploy_refs equivalent), totalIdempotencyRows (global PK-collision count probe). Helpers reused: seedOrg (schema_test.go); newIdempotencyStore / idemFixture / claimIdem / completeIdem / findIdem / wantErrCode (idempotency_test.go); rawIdempotencyRow / loadIdempotencyRowByID / countIdempotencyRowsForOrg / mintIdempotencyID (idempotency_repository_invariants_test.go).
