ralph/progress.txt:11816:- Added policy-matrix coverage for PUT /v1/projects/{project_id}/grants in internal/controlplane/httpapi/projects_grants_put_policy_test.go: role matrix (Owner/Admin allow via ReasonAllowedByRole; Developer/Viewer/CI/Support all deny via ReasonDeniedNoCapability — the load-bearing distinction from project.delete is that Developer ALSO denies here because project.grants.write is CapAdmin, one tier above CapWrite), revoked/expired credential 403, wrong-organization principal (wire-level 404 at persistence + engine-level cross-tenant deny for ALL six roles including Support since CapAdmin is OUTSIDE the engine's `CapSupport && (CapRead || CapSupport)` exception), and grant containment (project-Admin grant allows target/denies sibling; project-Developer grant on the same project denies via ReasonDeniedNoCapability — the load-bearing distinction from project.delete where the same grant would allow; env-scoped staging-Admin grant denies the parent project AND production env; service-scoped Admin grant denies parent env env.write, sibling service update, AND parent project; org-Admin allows; org-Developer denies via ReasonDeniedNoCapability — the org-admin-allow + org-developer-deny PAIR locks the CapAdmin requirement against the grant path; foreign-home-org with grant on canonical project denies as engine-level cross-tenant).
ralph/progress.txt:11818:- Verification: gofmt -l ., go mod tidy, go build ./..., go vet ./..., go test ./..., go test -race ./... all green. Focused: go test -run TestReplaceProjectGrantsPolicy -v ./internal/controlplane/httpapi/... passes (matrix roles 6/6, revoked+expired 2/2, wrong-org cross-tenant deny 6/6 including support, grant containment 1/1).
ralph/progress.txt:11819:- Remaining risks: none for this story. The store-backed integration tests for project_grant_test.go cover persistence-layer tenant isolation; this file covers the policy boundary. Both deny-direction leak guards (replaceGrantsRequestBodyLeak for caller-submitted request body needles, grantsDenyBodyLeak for canonical post-write data needles) are checked on every deny path so a future renderer that gained either a "render request body on error" mode or a "render would-have-returned data on 403" mode fails here.
ralph/progress.txt:11821:  - CapAdmin-on-{project_id}-resolver policy matrix (BE-0141, internal/controlplane/httpapi/projects_grants_put_policy_test.go) is the structural sibling of the CapWrite-on-{project_id}-resolver matrix (projects_delete_policy_test.go) but ONE TIER UP — Developer JOINS the deny set (CapWrite-holder, not CapAdmin-holder) and the org-level Developer grant flips from ALLOW (delete) to DENY (grants.write). Pair the org-Admin allow with the org-Developer deny: that pair locks the CapAdmin requirement against the grant path so a future catalog change that downgraded project.grants.write to CapWrite would still pass the allow check but fail the deny check, surfacing the regression. The cross-tenant engine matrix denies EVERY role including Support (CapAdmin is OUTSIDE `CapSupport && (CapRead || CapSupport)`); the CapRead siblings (audit.read, project.grants.read) ALLOW Support cross-tenant via ReasonAllowedBySupport — so this is the load-bearing distinction between read and admin matrices for the same path-shape.
ralph/progress.txt:11823:  - For a CapAdmin-on-resource matrix, the role matrix correctly fails when CI is omitted: CI holds CapDeploy (for service lifecycle) but neither CapWrite nor CapAdmin. List CI explicitly in BOTH the per-role deny rows AND the cross-tenant engine deny loop — the deploy capability is its own axis.
ralph/progress.txt:11824:  - Distinguish the "principal-with-grants-but-foreign-home-org" engine assertion from the "in-tenant role" matrix: the cross-tenant guard fires before the grants are even consulted because the principal's OrganizationID no longer matches the grant scope's OrganizationID. This is a structural property of the engine independent of CapAdmin vs CapWrite vs CapRead, and pinning it here documents that a stolen scoped key cannot be smuggled across tenants by anyone who finds it. Use a foreign-home-org principal whose grant is otherwise valid for the target resource, then resolve against the same project id under the foreign org's scope — the engine refuses even when the grant scope itself names the same project.
