ralph/progress.txt:11968:  - TestListProjectVariablesPolicyWrongOrganizationPrincipal pins the cross-tenant boundary: wire-level 404 E_NOT_FOUND from the persistence layer when an attacker hits a foreign {project_id} (reader called with attacker's home org id, error.message redacts the foreign org id), plus engine-level deny-matrix for owner/admin/developer/viewer/ci against a foreign-org Resource via ReasonDeniedCrossTenant, plus engine-level Support cross-tenant allow via ReasonAllowedBySupport (env.read is CapRead so it sits inside the support exception even though the customer-facing route is structurally blocked from reaching that branch by projectIDResolver).
ralph/progress.txt:11980:  - The customer-facing route under test cannot reach the engine's cross-tenant Support exception branch by construction (projectIDResolver pins resource scope to the principal's home org). The matrix pins the engine verdict anyway so a future admin/scoped variant that DOES resolve into a foreign-org scope inherits a working ReasonDeniedCrossTenant matrix AND a working ReasonAllowedBySupport exception. A future catalog change that upgraded env.read above CapRead would fail TestListProjectVariablesPolicyWrongOrganizationPrincipal/support before it could regress a real customer.
ralph/progress.txt:11987:    - The "stolen scoped key planted in foreign home org" engine assertion is identical-shape to the grants matrix's: a Principal with OrganizationID=org_sibling carrying a Grant whose Scope.OrganizationID=org_acme, authorized against a Resource in org_sibling, is denied at the engine because the principal's home org no longer matches the grant scope's org. CapRead/CapWrite/CapAdmin axis is irrelevant — the cross-tenant guard fires first. Always pin this on every grant-containment test.
