ralph/progress.txt:14597:  - TestReplaceEnvironmentGrantsPolicyWrongOrganizationPrincipal — wire-level cross-tenant attacker hits 404 via persistence layer (replacer called with attacker home org, never victim's), body never echoes victim org id or caller-supplied replacement set; engine-level cross-tenant pinning denies EVERY role (including Support) via ReasonDeniedCrossTenant since CapAdmin is outside the support cross-tenant exception (load-bearing distinction from BE-0168 GET, which DOES admit Support cross-tenant via ReasonAllowedBySupport).
ralph/progress.txt:14613:  - The "policy matrix for PUT {grants}" story is the structural sibling of the projects_grants_put_policy_test.go pattern (BE-0141) with ONE narrowing: the resolver pins NO ProjectID leg, so every scoped grant — including a grant on the SAME env_id named in the path — is denied OutOfScope at the engine AND at the wire. The matrix file is therefore a hybrid of (a) the projects_grants_put_policy_test.go CapAdmin role + cross-tenant + request-body-leak-guard skeleton and (b) the environment_grants_policy_test.go no-ProjectID covers() property (BE-0168) — copy the BE-0141 skeleton, swap CapAdmin role expectations as-is, and re-pin every wire tie-in with the BE-0168 OutOfScope denial pattern (project/env/service scoped principals ALL denied at this route even on same-id grants).
ralph/progress.txt:14615:  - Load-bearing engine-level distinction from BE-0168: GET is CapRead, so Support IS admitted cross-tenant via ReasonAllowedBySupport; PUT is CapAdmin, so Support is denied cross-tenant via ReasonDeniedCrossTenant. The cross-tenant engine deny matrix must INCLUDE the Support role for write actions and EXCLUDE it for read actions — getting this wrong silently allows or denies a support cross-tenant action. Always include the support row in the engine_cross_tenant_deny_* parallel subtests for CapAdmin write actions; that single row is the only difference between the read-cross-tenant matrix and the write-cross-tenant matrix.
ralph/progress.txt:14617:  - ReplaceEnvironmentGrantsInput field name is EnvironmentID (not ProjectID — that's the project-grants twin) and ActorOrgID (not ActorOrgID could easily get typed as ActorOrganizationID — copy from environment_grants_put_test.go's existing assertions to avoid drift). The captured input ALWAYS carries the PRINCIPAL'S home org as both OrganizationID and ActorOrgID — never a caller-controlled value, never the path tenant.
ralph/progress.txt:14624:  - internal/controlplane/store/migrate/migrations/0018_environment_variables.up.sql (new) — environment_variables table tenant-scoped at the DB via composite FK (organization_id, environment_id) -> environments ON DELETE CASCADE, UNIQUE (organization_id, environment_id, key) for per-env key uniqueness, UNIQUE (organization_id, id) for future service-override FK target, bigint version + bump_version + set_updated_at triggers from migration 0011/earlier.
ralph/progress.txt:14626:  - internal/controlplane/store/environment_variable.go (new) — EnvironmentVariable struct (with slog.LogValue redacting value at the slog boundary, mirroring ProjectVariable); EnvironmentVariableRepository.ListByEnvironment (tenant-scoped SELECT under (organization_id, environment_id), ORDER BY key, id, LIMIT environmentVariableListMaxRows = 500); EnvironmentVariableReader store-backed adapter composing EnvironmentRepository.GetByID + ListByEnvironment in one Store.Read tx so a cross-tenant or unknown environment_id surfaces as apierr.NotFound (never an empty list).
ralph/progress.txt:14627:  - internal/controlplane/store/environment_variable_test.go (new) — repo+reader integration tests: deterministic ordering, tenant scoping (cross-tenant (orgA, envB_id) pair yields zero rows), empty-environment case, reader happy path, reader rejects cross-tenant environment id without leaking foreign tenant data, reader unknown id surfaces NotFound, nil-store constructor rejection.
ralph/progress.txt:14649:  - The EnvironmentVariableReader adapter's two-step (Get env + List variables) inside one Store.Read transaction is load-bearing for the agent-friendly contract: a cross-tenant or unknown environment_id surfaces as apierr.NotFound, NEVER as an empty success — the empty-success failure mode would invite an agent to believe the environment exists with no variables (a structural lie). Repository.ListByEnvironment alone is NOT enough; always compose the parent-existence Get in the same tx.
ralph/progress.txt:14658:  - TestListEnvironmentVariablesPolicyWrongOrganizationPrincipal — wire-level cross-tenant attacker (org_attacker hitting env_victim_evar) hits 404 via persistence (reader called with attacker home org id + foreign env id, never victim org id), body never echoes victim org id nor the secret plaintext; engine-level cross-tenant deny matrix denies owner/admin/developer/viewer/ci via ReasonDeniedCrossTenant and ADMITS Support via ReasonAllowedBySupport (env.read is CapRead, inside the engine cross-tenant exception clause — the load-bearing distinction from CapAdmin write actions like BE-0171 env-grants-PUT).
ralph/progress.txt:14674:  - Cross-tenant engine matrix DIRECTION rule (CapRead actions): env.read is CapRead, so the engine cross-tenant clause ADMITS Support via ReasonAllowedBySupport AND DENIES every non-support role via ReasonDeniedCrossTenant. The cross-tenant assertion block must therefore (1) iterate owner/admin/developer/viewer/ci through engine_cross_tenant_deny_<role> subtests and (2) add a SEPARATE positive assertion that Support is allowed via ReasonAllowedBySupport. Forgetting (2) would silently let a future catalog change upgrade env.read to a non-CapRead capability (which would deny every support cross-tenant read) without failing here. Mirror-test for CapAdmin write actions inverts this: Support is DENIED cross-tenant via ReasonDeniedCrossTenant, and the support row joins the deny matrix (the load-bearing distinction the BE-0171 env-grants-PUT entry pinned).
ralph/progress.txt:14676:  - Cross-tenant deny status code rationale (BE-0174 wire-level): the env-id resolver pins the resource to the principal HOME org id (not the path tenant), so a cross-tenant attacker is admitted same-tenant at the engine and rejected at the persistence layer with apierr.NotFound("environment", id). Status MUST be 404 E_NOT_FOUND, NEVER 403 (which would confirm the environment existence in another tenant) and NEVER 200 with an empty list (which would invite the agent to believe the environment exists with no variables). The fakeEnvironmentVariableReader.err = apierr.NotFound(...) pattern mirrors the production store contract: the (org, env_id) tuple-miss surfaces as NotFound regardless of the caller role. This invariant is shared with environment-grants and is the cornerstone of cross-tenant defence on every bare-{environment_id}-resolver route.
ralph/progress.txt:14682:  - internal/controlplane/httpapi/environment_variables_put_test.go (new file, 12 contract tests covering: happy path with secret + non-secret variables proving wire redaction on the post-write re-read AND forwarded ReplaceEnvironmentVariablesInput; empty-array semantics = explicit clear; missing variables field = stable 400 naming the field path; malformed JSON = 400; unknown top-level field = 400; unauthenticated = 401 with replacer never running and no leak of seeded plaintext / id / key; viewer in tenant denied 403 — CapWrite has no read-only role admission; support in tenant denied 403 — CapWrite has no support exception either, unlike CapRead actions; store NotFound propagated as 404; store InvalidInput propagated as 400 with the field path on the wire; store unavailable propagated as 503 with no driver-cause leak; missing replacer = 500; OpenAPI route registered with stable operationId / RequiredAction / tags).
ralph/progress.txt:14699:  - PUT-replace-variables contract suite pattern (BE-0175 contract pattern, internal/controlplane/httpapi/environment_variables_put_test.go): the canonical contract suite for a customer-facing PUT-replace-{children} endpoint sitting on a bare-{id}-resolver route + secret-bearing child resource is the structural hybrid of (a) environment_grants_put_test.go (BE-0169 contract pattern, route-shape twin: same env-id resolver, same NewHandler positional port placement, same CapWrite vs CapAdmin difference noted in the role-deny tests) and (b) project_variables_test.go (BE-0142 GET pattern, resource-shape twin: same wire-level redaction chokepoint via output.Sentinel projection of is_secret rows, same leak-needle assertions on response bodies including the seeded secret plaintext). The new file lifts environment_grants_put_test.go's 12-test skeleton verbatim (handlerFor, putXXX, decode, missing-field 400 naming the field, malformed JSON, unknown field, unauth, viewer-in-tenant 403, NotFound, store InvalidInput, store unavailable, unwired = 500, OpenAPI registration) and adds TWO load-bearing additions: (1) the happy-path body POSITIVELY asserts got0.Value == output.Sentinel for the secret row AND negatively asserts the body does not contain the seeded plaintext — the chokepoint must fire on the post-write re-read even though the caller just submitted the plaintext; (2) a dedicated TestDeniesSupportInTenant — CapWrite has no support exception (the CapRead cross-tenant exception is one-way), so a CapWrite contract suite MUST positively assert support in their own tenant is denied 403, locking the deny against a future engine regression that widens CapWrite to support. Both directions are needed; either alone would silently regress.
ralph/progress.txt:14707:- Story scope: BE-0177 adds policy-matrix coverage for PUT /v1/environments/{environment_id}/variables. The structural twin of environment_grants_put_policy_test.go (BE-0171) at one capability tier lower: env.write = CapWrite (not CapAdmin), so Developer joins Owner/Admin in the role allow set, CI / Viewer / Support are denied via ReasonDeniedNoCapability, and the cross-tenant boundary still denies every role (Support included) because CapWrite is OUTSIDE the engine's `CapSupport && (CapRead || CapSupport)` exception. Distinct from env-grants, environment_variables rows store SECRET credential material, so the deny-path leak guards must cover BOTH the canonical post-write secret plaintext AND the caller-submitted secret plaintext in the request body — a denied PUT must not turn into a request-reflecting oracle for credentials.
ralph/progress.txt:14709:  - internal/controlplane/httpapi/environment_variables_put_policy_test.go (new file, 4 tests covering: full role matrix with allow set Owner/Admin/Developer and deny set Viewer/CI/Support — Developer in allow is the load-bearing distinction from BE-0171, CI in deny is the load-bearing distinction from a hypothetical "all writers allow" matrix since CI has CapDeploy but not CapWrite; revoked/expired key (Disabled=true) deny with replacer never running; wrong-organization principal — wire-level 404 via persistence, never 200/403, plus engine-level cross-tenant deny for ALL six roles INCLUDING Support since CapWrite is outside the support exception; grant containment — project-/env-/service-scoped grants all denied OutOfScope at the wire AND at the engine, the SAME-id env grant also OutOfScope, the sibling-project grant denied at the engine; org-level Developer grant ALLOWED end-to-end via ReasonAllowedByGrant with the wire-level redaction chokepoint still firing on the post-write re-read (secret value = output.Sentinel); org-level Viewer grant DENIED via ReasonDeniedNoCapability — the org-dev-allow / org-viewer-deny pair locks the CapWrite requirement against the grant path as a unit; stolen-key cross-tenant deny at the engine for a project-scoped grant whose home org has been swapped to a sibling org).
ralph/progress.txt:14713:  - go test -run TestReplaceEnvironmentVariablesPolicy ./internal/controlplane/httpapi/ — PASS (4 tests + 6 cross-tenant engine subtests).
ralph/progress.txt:14730:  - **Cap tier governs three load-bearing properties** at the policy matrix layer: (1) which roles allow in own tenant (CapWrite admits Developer / not CI; CapAdmin admits neither); (2) which roles allow cross-tenant via the support exception (CapRead / CapSupport admit Support; CapWrite / CapAdmin do not — the engine's `required == CapRead || required == CapSupport` is the gate); (3) which org-level grant roles allow via ReasonAllowedByGrant (CapWrite is admitted by Developer + Admin + Owner; CapAdmin is admitted by Admin + Owner only). The "org-developer allow paired with org-viewer deny" assertion in TestReplaceEnvironmentVariablesPolicyGrantContainment is the load-bearing CapWrite-vs-CapRead unit-test: a future catalog change that downgraded env.write to CapRead would still pass the org-developer allow check (Developer holds both CapRead and CapWrite) but fail the org-viewer deny check (Viewer holds CapRead but not CapWrite, so a CapRead downgrade would flip viewer to allow). Pair them per-endpoint to lock the cap tier as a unit.
ralph/progress.txt:14733:  - **CapWrite-vs-CapAdmin cross-tenant deny is universal across roles** — the engine's exception clause `CapSupport && (CapRead || CapSupport)` admits Support cross-tenant ONLY for CapRead and CapSupport actions. CapWrite, CapAdmin, CapDeploy, CapOwner are all outside it, so EVERY role (including Support) is denied cross-tenant for any of those capabilities via ReasonDeniedCrossTenant. The engine-level cross-tenant subtest sweep at the bottom of TestReplaceEnvironmentVariablesPolicyWrongOrganizationPrincipal (and the matching one at the bottom of TestReplaceEnvironmentGrantsPolicyWrongOrganizationPrincipal) is the structural lock: any future catalog change that moved env.write into the support cross-tenant exception (or widened the exception) would fail those subtests before it could ship.
