ralph/progress.txt:19249:- Bare-id read endpoint on a deep resource with a worker-deferred upstream (BE-0229+0230+0231 pattern, GET /v1/services/{service_id}/logs, store/service_log_reader.go ServiceLogReader.ListLogs, httpapi/services_logs.go ServiceLogReader port + serviceLogLine/listServiceLogsPayload wire DTOs + parseServiceLogsLimit + listServiceLogsHandler, routes.go GET /v1/services/{service_id}/logs entry, server.go NewHandler + newRouteTable + cmd/yalla-api/main.go wiring, services_logs_test.go contract suite + services_logs_policy_test.go matrix): canonical shape for ANY observability read endpoint whose real upstream is the Dokploy worker (logs, metrics — BE-0232..0234 next; future events/traces) and whose true content adapter is deferred to a later worker story. Three load-bearing rules: (1) the store-level adapter (store.ServiceLogReader, the second instance of the typed-input-typed-output read adapter shape after store.ServiceReader.GetService) performs the tenant-scoped parent existence check inside a Store.Read transaction so a cross-tenant or unknown service_id surfaces as a deterministic apierr.NotFound at the persistence boundary — never disguised as an empty success and never disguised as a 500 — and then returns an empty Lines slice with the ServiceID populated; the real Dokploy log fetcher is wired in BE-0301+ alongside the provisioning worker, matching the noopJobEnqueuer pattern for placeholder upstreams; (2) the wire DTO is a closed tuple `(service_id, lines[]{occurred_at, stream, message})` with NO pointer-typed fields, NO embedded interfaces, and an empty lines slice that marshals as `[]` not `null` so an agent does not need to special-case the absent-vs-empty distinction; the occurred_at field is RFC3339-nanosecond UTC; the stream enum is the closed set {stdout, stderr, system} so an agent can branch on the value without escape-decoding it; (3) the ?limit= query parameter is clamped at the boundary via parseServiceLogsLimit (the second instance of the bounded-limit-query-parser shape after parseAuditEventLimit, range [1, 1000] default 100), and the error path never echoes the submitted value — only the classification and the accepted range — so a typo can never become a reflection-style content channel. The CapRead policy matrix shape is the structural sibling of services_get_policy_test.go (BE-0184) — all six built-in roles allow same-tenant via ReasonAllowedByRole, support is allowed cross-tenant via ReasonAllowedBySupport at the engine level (defence-in-depth — the customer-facing route cannot reach this branch because serviceIDResolver pins the resource to the principal's home org), every project-/env-/service-scoped grant is denied OutOfScope on the bare-service-id path via the engine's one-way covers() rule, and an org-level Viewer grant is ALLOWED end-to-end (the load-bearing distinction from services_start_policy_test.go where the same grant is denied because service.start is CapDeploy, not CapRead). Limit assertion subtlety pinned by tests: the no-echo check on the invalid-limit deny path must restrict to non-numeric submitted values because numeric out-of-range inputs may legitimately share digits with the accepted-range message ("0" is a substring of "1000" in the "must be between 1 and 1000" string) — only the non-numeric case (where caller-controlled byte content could realistically reach the response body) is asserted. Test-only nil-port test: a typed-nil ServiceLogReader is needed (a bare `nil` literal does not satisfy the interface positional argument), so `var nilReader ServiceLogReader` is the pattern. Port-arity sweep: dominant `fakeServiceStopper{}, fakeServiceVariableReader{}` -> `fakeServiceStopper{}, fakeServiceLogReader{}, fakeServiceVariableReader{}` across 63 test files (108 sites) plus a minor variant `fakeServiceStopper{}, reader, fakeServiceVariableReplacer{}` -> `fakeServiceStopper{}, fakeServiceLogReader{}, reader, fakeServiceVariableReplacer{}` in service_variables_test.go (the test injects a local fakeServiceVariableReader as `reader`, so the LogReader slot is the new positional argument); the `stopper, fakeServiceVariableReader{}` variant in services_stop_test.go is one of the 108 dominant sites. Forward-applicable to BE-0232+0233+0234 (GET /v1/services/{service_id}/metrics, metrics.read action — the OTHER CapRead observability endpoint on the same resolver), and to any future read endpoint whose true content adapter is the Dokploy worker (events, traces) — same vertical, same placeholder shape, same wire-contract guarantees while the real fetcher lands later. The OpenAPI `?limit=` query parameter today is documented only via the Description prose because openapi.Endpoint has no QueryParams field; a future BE story can add structured QueryParam metadata and the existing route entries (audit-events, logs) will switch over together.
