ralph/progress.txt:19943:  - internal/controlplane/httpapi/service_backups_run_policy_test.go (new): policy matrix for backup.run on the bare-id serviceIDResolver route. Role table (owner/admin/developer/CI allowed via ReasonAllowedByRole; viewer/support denied via ReasonDeniedNoCapability) — CI allowed is the load-bearing distinction from backup.create. Revoked/expired CI key denied via ReasonDeniedPrincipalDisabled. Wrong-organization principal hits 404 at persistence with home-org id forwarded; engine cross-tenant deny pinned for every role including Support (CapDeploy outside the cross-tenant exception). Grant containment: project-level dev grant allowed at full-scope service / denied at sibling project / denied at bare-id route; project-level viewer denied; project-level CI grant allowed at full-scope service (LOAD-BEARING — CI cannot create but CAN run); env-staging grant allowed in staging / denied in production env / denied at bare-id; service-scoped grant allowed at full-scope / denied at unrelated sibling / denied at bare-id; org-level Admin allowed via ReasonAllowedByGrant; org-level Viewer denied via ReasonDeniedNoCapability. Wire-level proofs that scoped grants are denied at the boundary (runner never runs) and that the org-admin path reaches the runner with correct ids.
ralph/progress.txt:19957:  - **Verb-on-child-resource endpoint pattern (BE-0253+0254+0255, POST /v1/services/{service_id}/backups/{backup_id}/run, mirrors the services_restart trio for the verb-on-parent shape but adds a tenant-scoped child existence check):** The clean composition for ANY "trigger an action on a worker-driven child resource" endpoint is — store: serviceBackupRunAction const + ServiceBackupRepository.GetByID (composite (org, svc, child_id) read; pgx.ErrNoRows -> apierr.NotFound(kind, child_id) — the kind/id NEVER contains the org or parent because the customer-facing message stays free of identifiers the principal cannot already prove they own) + ServiceBackupRepository.MarkPending (UPDATE...RETURNING that flips the worker-observable status column; the bump_version trigger from the resource's migration owns version, so MarkPending NEVER writes it itself — this preserves the optimistic-concurrency invariant for future If-Match callers) + RunServiceBackupInput (plain-string actor/correlation seam — no policy/telemetry import in the store package) + ServiceBackupService.Run orchestrator (validate IDs before Tx; in Tx: parent-resource tenant-scoped Get -> child-resource tenant-scoped Get -> precondition 409s on closed-taxonomy state (enabled=false, status=running) -> in-tx Authorize (defense-in-depth against grant-change races; on the SAME *Tx as the write) -> MarkPending -> audit append with status_before/status_after metadata; ALL state mutations + audit commit atomically). The audit Metadata records ONLY closed-taxonomy enums (status_before/status_after) and structural identifiers (service_id) — caller-controlled free text (display_name, schedule) is NOT recorded because the action does not mutate it and including it would expose a future redaction footgun. The httpapi handler is the canonical verb-handler shape — empty body, principal home org + path {service_id} + path {child_id} forwarded to the runner, RequireAuth gates on the action's catalog mapping (backup.run -> CapDeploy), nil-runner guard runs after RequireAuth admits, 202 Accepted on success. Route entry's resolver is the bare-id resolver of the PARENT (serviceIDResolver), not the child — the resource scope pins to the parent's path identifier so scoped grants on parent siblings still get the engine's one-way covers() rule applied. The catalog mapping (CapDeploy vs CapWrite vs CapRead) is THE single load-bearing distinction in the policy matrix: CapDeploy widens the allow set to include CI (which holds CapRead+CapDeploy), so backup.run is allowed for CI keys even though backup.create (CapWrite) is denied — the policy matrix test pins this distinction at every grant scope.
ralph/progress.txt:20006:  - internal/controlplane/httpapi/service_backups_update_policy_test.go (new): policy matrix — drives owner/admin/developer/viewer/ci/support through the production request path; pins the engine verdict at ReasonAllowedByRole / ReasonDeniedNoCapability (CapWrite splits the matrix the same way as domain.update — viewer/ci/support all denied at the role boundary, support has no cross-tenant write exception); revoked + expired credentials (deny via ReasonDeniedPrincipalDisabled with no body leak); wrong-org cross-tenant principal (wire 404 + engine cross-tenant deny pinned for all six roles via ReasonDeniedCrossTenant); grant containment (sibling project / env-grant-not-prod / service-grant containment; project/env/service grants denied OutOfScope at the wire; org-Admin allowed end-to-end; org-Viewer denied at engine via ReasonDeniedNoCapability).
ralph/progress.txt:20022:  - The strict JSON decoder's unknown-field rejection is load-bearing for THREE distinct attacks on this endpoint: (1) status smuggling (forge a 'succeeded' status), (2) organization_id smuggling (redirect the write at another tenant), (3) service_id / id smuggling (redirect the write at another resource). All three are wire-level guarantees BEFORE the updater is called — the test suite has explicit cases for each.
ralph/progress.txt:20036:  - internal/controlplane/store/service_backup.go: added serviceBackupDeleteAction = "backup.delete" + ServiceBackupRepository.DeleteByID (tenant-scoped DELETE...RETURNING with optional If-Match version predicate; reuses classifyServiceBackupConcurrencyMiss from the PATCH path to disambiguate row-missing-vs-stale-version when an If-Match was supplied; pgx.ErrNoRows without precondition -> apierr.NotFound) + DeleteServiceBackupInput + ServiceBackupService.Delete orchestrator (validate org/service/backup IDs before opening Tx -> Write: DeleteByID -> audit append with service_id/status/enabled/retention_count/version metadata). Audit metadata is the closed taxonomy enums + bounded scalars only; display_name + schedule (caller-controlled free text) are NOT recorded, matching the create/update conventions. Also added "strconv" import for strconv.FormatInt of the int64 version (the existing strconvI helper takes int, not int64).
ralph/progress.txt:20042:  - internal/controlplane/httpapi/service_backups_delete_policy_test.go (new): policy-matrix suite. TestDeleteServiceBackupPolicyMatrixRoles drives every role through the production request path — owner/admin/developer allowed via ReasonAllowedByRole; ci/viewer/support denied via ReasonDeniedNoCapability (backup.delete is CapWrite, OUTSIDE the cross-tenant Support exception). TestDeleteServiceBackupPolicyRevokedAndExpiredKeys pins the disabled-principal deny path via ReasonDeniedPrincipalDisabled, asserts the deleter is never reached (the load-bearing destructive-op no-run invariant), and asserts no body leak of principal id / org / seeded backup data. TestDeleteServiceBackupPolicyWrongOrganizationPrincipal pins the cross-tenant boundary (wire 404 + handler forwards the attacker's home org id, never the path-controlled foreign org; foreign-org id never echoed in error.message) AND pins the engine-level cross-tenant deny for all six roles INCLUDING Support via ReasonDeniedCrossTenant (backup.delete is CapWrite, never in the support cross-tenant exception). TestDeleteServiceBackupPolicyGrantContainment pins the project-scoped developer grant allowed inside the target project / denied sibling project / denied at the bare-id route via ReasonDeniedOutOfScope (covers() one-way rule); project-scoped Viewer + CI grants denied at engine via ReasonDeniedNoCapability even inside the target project (CI grants do NOT confer CapWrite — automation keys cannot destroy backup policies); env-staging grant allowed inside staging / denied production / denied at the bare-id route; service-scoped grant allowed on full scope / denied sibling / denied at the bare-id route; org-Admin allowed end-to-end on the bare-id route (org-level grants don't pin ProjectID so covers() admits the bare-id scope, AND Admin holds CapWrite); org-Viewer denied at engine via ReasonDeniedNoCapability.
ralph/progress.txt:20051:  - The store-layer integration test for ServiceBackupService.Delete (verifying the tenant-scoped DELETE predicate, the If-Match precondition + classifyServiceBackupConcurrencyMiss disambiguation, and the audit metadata composition end-to-end against real Postgres) is not added in this iteration — a future Postgres-integration story should clone the BE-0252 pattern at store/service_backup_test.go. The BE-0252 progress note documents the pre-existing skip behavior with no DB.
ralph/progress.txt:20054:  - **DELETE-on-child-resource vertical with optional If-Match (BE-0259+0260+0261, DELETE /v1/services/{service_id}/backups/{backup_id}, exact structural twin of BE-0244+0245+0246 for service_domains DELETE):** the repo's canonical shape for a hard-DELETE of a bare-{id}-resolver child resource has stabilised — store-side `<Resource>Repository.DeleteByID(ctx, tx, parents..., childID, ifMatchVersion *int64) (<Resource>, error)` returning the deleted snapshot, reusing the SAME `classify<Resource>ConcurrencyMiss` helper that the PATCH path declared (so the staleness disambiguation is a single chokepoint per resource), no soft-delete column (a backup policy is desired-state configuration, never a continuing audit-trail tie that must outlive the row — the audit row itself outlives it); store-side `<Resource>Service.Delete(ctx, Delete<Resource>Input)` validates path identifiers BEFORE opening Tx, then Write -> DeleteByID -> audit append with the closed-set / structural-identifier metadata only (never caller-controlled free text); httpapi-side narrow Deleter port, errNo<Resource>Deleter sentinel, payload type that re-uses the SAME wire shape every other endpoint for that resource returns (no DELETE-specific projection), handler order: RequireAuth -> nil-principal Internal -> nil-deleter Internal -> parseIfMatchVersion -> deleter.Delete -> 200 yalla.output.v1; route registration with serviceIDResolver, RequiredAction=<action>.delete, two PathParams, multi-paragraph Description that explicitly states the no-soft-delete invariant + the If-Match contract + the cross-tenant 404 + the CapWrite Support suppression. **The reason this is one vertical and not three:** BE-0261 (priority=1, the policy-matrix story) is logically blocked on BE-0259 (priority=2, the endpoint) — the policy tests have no fakeServiceBackupDeleter wiring to call into until the deleter port exists — so the picker rule "land the highest-priority STORY GROUP that is doable, not the highest-priority story considered in isolation" applies, and the trio ships in one commit per the bundling convention.
ralph/progress.txt:20058:- Repository for an **APPEND-ONLY EVENT LOG table whose row body has NO updated_at column, NO version column, and is guarded by a BEFORE UPDATE trigger that rejects every UPDATE at the SQL level, AND whose tenant scope is enforced through a composite FK back to a DEEPER parent (deployments, not directly organizations)** (BE-0459 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/deployment_event.go + 0035_deployment_events migration + deployment_event_repository_invariants_test.go). Structurally distinct from BE-0455 (quota_reservations — append-only-from-the-customer-perspective but still has updated_at + settled_at lifecycle UPDATE surface from the worker; tenant-scoped directly to organizations) and from AuditRepository (also append-only with BEFORE UPDATE trigger but tenant-scoped DIRECTLY to organizations with NO composite FK to a deeper parent) in three load-bearing ways: (a) the table has only TWO timestamp columns (occurred_at + created_at), both stamped by now() at INSERT; occurred_at is caller-overridable to support a worker reconciling after a restart that persists the original wallclock the event was observed at, while created_at is always database-stamped. The repository's Append uses `COALESCE($N, now())` so a zero time.Time defaults to now() but a non-zero caller-supplied time round-trips verbatim. (b) The composite FK leg (organization_id, deployment_id) references deployments(organization_id, id), not organizations(id) directly — this means a cross-tenant Append surfaces as apierr.Conflict via mapWriteError (FK violation) BEFORE the row gets near the organizations table, and ON DELETE CASCADE chains through deployments rather than directly from organizations. The cascade-delete test must therefore DELETE the parent deployment (not the parent organization) to exercise the chain, and the FK rejection test seeds two orgs but only ONE deployment so the cross-tenant probe references alpha's deployment id with bravo's organization_id — the (bravo.ID, alphaDep.ID) tuple matches no row in deployments and the FK trips. (c) The repository surface is intentionally narrower than QuotaRepository's: Append + GetByID + ListByDeployment, no Update, no Cancel, no aggregation method. GetByID exists despite the table being internal-accounting (audit_events has no GetByID for the same reason) because the timeline endpoint's future read paths benefit from a tenant-scoped point lookup and the cost is a single short method. ListByDeployment orders occurred_at ASC + id ASC (chronological — the timeline endpoint renders the deployment unfolding forward in time, opposite to deployments' reverse-chronological list path).
ralph/progress.txt:20060:The 20-test shape pins: Append-mints-id-and-row-shape (depev_ prefix), Append-blank-fields-default (Message='' RequestID='' CorrelationID='' Metadata=nil), Append-respects-caller-supplied-id, Append-respects-caller-supplied-occurred-at (worker-reconciliation contract), Append-unknown-event-type-rejected (CHECK via mapWriteError -> Conflict), Append-blank-event-type-rejected (apierr.Internal at application boundary, NOT a database-CHECK pass-through — the empty string is a programming error caught before SQL, since a blank value would also pass the CHECK if the CHECK accepted '' which it does not but the application boundary catches it first), Append-unknown-deployment-rejected (composite FK -> Conflict), Append-cross-tenant-deployment-rejected (composite FK -> Conflict + countDeploymentEventRowsForDeployment proves no row persisted), Append-duplicate-id-Conflict (PRIMARY KEY collision via mapWriteError), Append-nil-Tx-Internal, Append-rolls-back-on-tx-rollback, Append-peer-row-byte-identity (NO updated_at column to drift, so the test asserts byte-identity on id+EventType+Message+OccurredAt+CreatedAt — the absence of bump_version + set_updated_at triggers MEANS the peer can never drift on a successful sibling Append), Update-rejected-by-trigger (raw `db.Exec UPDATE` bypasses the repository surface entirely so the trigger is the only thing that can stop the write), cascade-delete-on-deployment (raw `DELETE FROM deployments WHERE id = $1` cascades through the composite FK ON DELETE CASCADE), GetByID-returns-persisted-row / unknown-NotFound / cross-tenant-NotFound-with-no-leak-on-foreign-deployment-id, ListByDeployment-chronological-order (seed three rows OUT OF chronological order so the ASC ordering must reorder them on read), ListByDeployment-unknown-deployment-empty (returns empty slice not error — distinguishes "no events" from "no parent" is the caller's responsibility, mirrors deployment ListByService), ListByDeployment-cross-tenant-empty. The peer-row test gets simpler than quota_reservations or deployments because there is NO updated_at column to drift and NO version column to bump — append-only is structural in the column list, not just the trigger. Forward-applicable to every future append-only event-log surface (e.g. provisioning_job_events when that lands).
ralph/progress.txt:20087:  - The cross-tenant support exception is gated on `required == CapRead || required == CapSupport` (engine.go: Decide). CapDeploy is OUTSIDE the exception, so Support principals authorizing preview.create against a foreign-org resource are DENIED. This is the load-bearing distinction from preview.read (CapRead — Support is allowed cross-tenant via ReasonAllowedBySupport). Pinning the Support cross-tenant deny at the engine level is what prevents a future endpoint that resolved into a foreign-org scope from silently leaking deploy authority across tenants.
ralph/progress.txt:20112:  - The cross-tenant matrix is the SHARP distinction between a CapRead action and a CapDeploy action at this layer: for preview.read, Support is allowed cross-tenant via ReasonAllowedBySupport (the engine's `roleCaps.has(CapSupport) && (required == CapRead || required == CapSupport)` clause), while for preview.create / preview.delete Support is denied cross-tenant via ReasonDeniedCrossTenant. Pinning the Support cross-tenant ALLOW here is what keeps a future catalog change that downgraded preview.read out of the support exception detectable before it could regress incident-response read access.
ralph/progress.txt:20113:  - The deny-no-capability case in the project-grant containment test was DELIBERATELY omitted because all six built-in roles hold CapRead, so no first-class role can anchor a "scope OK, capability missing" deny against preview.read at the project-grant path. The closest equivalent (a custom role without CapRead) is already pinned by policy_test.go TestDecideCustomRole; adding a duplicate here would couple this story to the custom-role engine semantics. The org-grant capability matrix in this file therefore pins only the four CapRead allow rows (CI/Developer/Viewer/Support all allow); the create-side matrix pins the Viewer/Support DENY rows because those load-bearing rows do not exist on the read side. This asymmetry is intentional and is itself the load-bearing learning: a future read-action policy file (project_read_policy_test.go, environment_read_policy_test.go, service_read_policy_test.go, deployment_read_policy_test.go, backup_read_policy_test.go, job_read_policy_test.go, logs_read_policy_test.go, metrics_read_policy_test.go, limits_read_policy_test.go) should mirror this exact shape — six-row allow at home org, Support-allow cross-tenant, all-grant-roles allow at org/project scope, sibling/parent containment denials, and NO deny-no-capability case on the role-grant path.
ralph/progress.txt:20115:  - The foreign-home-org grant filter is identical across CapRead and CapDeploy actions: a scoped grant minted for orgA, carried by a principal whose home org is orgB, against a resource in orgB → the cross-tenant guard does NOT fire (resource org == home org), then the engine's `g.Scope.OrganizationID != principal.OrganizationID` filter skips the grant entirely, and the verdict is ReasonDeniedNoCapability (not ReasonDeniedOutOfScope, not ReasonDeniedCrossTenant). This is the engine-level twin of the wire-level "stolen scoped key" property: CapRead being inside the support cross-tenant exception does NOT save a stolen key, because the exception is gated on the principal's home-org role (which a stolen scoped key does not carry).
ralph/progress.txt:20120:- Added public-API contract coverage for GET /v1/organizations at internal/controlplane/httpapi/organizations_contract_test.go. organizations_test.go (BE-0046) already pins the success/error envelopes, the request_id propagation, the OpenAPI operation registration, the unauthenticated / invalid-credential / disabled-principal / home-organization-not-found / dependency-failure rejection space, and the "reader is scoped to the caller's own home organization" tenant invariant; this file closes the three remaining contract criteria the base file doesn't assert directly: (1) the HTTP server writes response data only through http.ResponseWriter — never to the process stdout/stderr; (2) the per-request structured log stays redacted on the success path AND the authorization-failure path so a bearer credential is never logged; (3) the 503 error envelope built from a wrapped dependency cause never leaks that cause onto the wire. Same shape as me_organizations_contract_test.go (BE-0044) and me_contract_test.go.
ralph/progress.txt:20179:  - The destructive-verb framing in the doc comments is itself a reusable substitution for any future destructive-verb engine pin (e.g. ActionEnvDelete, ActionDeploymentRollback when those land as engine-only pins). Specifically: (1) the role-matrix doc gains a "destructive customer-data operation" / "data-loss incident" framing for the CapDeploy split; (2) the disabled-key doc gains a "stale CI key whose lease has expired" framing distinct from create's "minting" framing; (3) the cross-tenant doc gains a "misrouted delete is unrecoverable without a backup" framing; (4) the foreign-home-org-grant doc gains a "stolen scoped key teardown vector" framing distinct from create's "stolen scoped key minting vector". The CapDeploy verdict is identical, the operator-facing risk surface is not, and the doc comments must reflect the operator-facing risk so an engine regression review (which catches "did this change widen permissions?") can also catch "did this change widen destructive permissions?" — a stricter bar.
ralph/progress.txt:20180:  - The destructive-verb cross-tenant doc explicitly calls out CapDeploy being OUTSIDE the engine's support cross-tenant exception (`roleCaps.has(CapSupport) && (required == CapRead || required == CapSupport)`). This is the same load-bearing distinction as preview.create but framed harder for the destructive verb: the support deny on a destructive cross-tenant resource is a defence against a privileged support credential being used to delete customer data, not just a defence against unauthorised resource minting. The wire-level test at httpapi/ for a future cross-tenant request should also enforce this distinction (the support-token-on-foreign-org test should expect 403, not 404, because Yalla's support role is allowed to enumerate the foreign org's projects via preview.read; on preview.delete the support principal hits the cross-tenant deny without reaching the row-existence check).
ralph/progress.txt:20190:    - TestJobReadPolicyWrongOrganizationPrincipal: cross-tenant matrix on a foreign-org resource. Five roles denied with ReasonDeniedCrossTenant; Support is the singular exception (CapRead + CapSupport -> ReasonAllowedBySupport). Pins the support cross-tenant exception so a future catalog change that downgraded job.read out of the CapRead+CapSupport pair would fail here.
ralph/progress.txt:20195:    - TestJobReadPolicyForeignHomeOrgGrant: stolen-scoped-key cross-tenant guard on the grant path. A principal whose home is orgB carrying a grant for orgA cannot authorize job.read against an orgB resource — the engine's grant-filter clause `g.Scope.OrganizationID != principal.OrganizationID` skips the grant entirely, and with no home-org role the verdict is ReasonDeniedNoCapability. CapRead is inside the cross-tenant support exception, but that exception is gated on the home-org role's caps — a stolen scoped key has no home-org role to satisfy the gate.
ralph/progress.txt:20208:  - The job-read engine pin assumes the wire endpoint will use Kind=KindJob for the resource. domain.KindJob is the only available kind for a provisioning-job resource (id.go:52 KindJob = "job"), so the natural resolver mapping is straightforward. If the future endpoint instead resolves Kind=KindOrganization for the org-level list (because the list endpoint surveys the tenant rather than a specific job), the wire-level policy matrix would need to keep Kind=KindOrganization for the bare list resource — but the engine.Decide() flow does not branch on Kind (only Scope), so the engine-level verdict pinned here would still apply.
ralph/progress.txt:20209:  - BE-0274 (POST /v1/jobs is N/A — jobs are not created by API; they are enqueued by mutating endpoints), BE-0275/0276 (GET /v1/jobs/{job_id} endpoint + contract + P=1 policy BE-0276), BE-0278/0279 (POST /v1/jobs/{job_id}/retry endpoint + contract + P=1 policy BE-0279), BE-0281/0282 (POST /v1/jobs/{job_id}/cancel endpoint + contract + P=1 policy BE-0282) follow the same picker-rule pattern: a P=1 policy story whose endpoint has not landed yet. When this iteration's pin is the template, the next iterations should clone job_read_policy_test.go's structure but flip the capability class (job.cancel + job.retry are CapDeploy — viewer + support denied at role matrix, denied at org-grant matrix, denied at cross-tenant; CI is the canary row that flips from deny to allow vs job.read because CI holds CapDeploy).
ralph/progress.txt:20212:  - **Resource Kind constants are decorative, not load-bearing in the engine.** The engine.Decide() flow at internal/controlplane/policy/engine.go:88+ does NOT branch on resource.Kind at any point — only Scope.OrganizationID (cross-tenant guard) and Scope.covers() (grant containment) participate in the verdict. Kind is for downstream consumers (audit metadata, error rendering, defense-in-depth invariants in handler tests). This means: (a) Engine-level policy pins for a list endpoint CAN pick any reasonable Kind for the canonical resource shape — Kind=KindJob is the natural choice for job.read but Kind=KindOrganization would yield the same engine verdict. (b) A future endpoint that changes its resolver's Kind choice will NOT break the engine pin — the wire-level policy file would need to update its Kind assertions but the engine verdicts pinned in this file would survive intact. (c) Wire-level policy tests should assert Kind separately (the audit-event resource kind and the policy-resource Kind are both observable wire properties); engine-level tests do not need to.
ralph/progress.txt:20213:  - **The "stolen scoped key" property has two engine-level facets that must BOTH be pinned in the engine policy test for read actions on cross-tenant resources.** Facet 1 (the cross-tenant role guard): when home_org == resource_org but the principal's home role is Support, the engine's CapRead+CapSupport exception fires — that's TestJobReadPolicyWrongOrganizationPrincipal, support row, allow. Facet 2 (the cross-tenant grant guard): when home_org == resource_org but the grant scope is in a different org, the engine's grant-filter clause `g.Scope.OrganizationID != principal.OrganizationID` skips the grant — that's TestJobReadPolicyForeignHomeOrgGrant, deny with ReasonDeniedNoCapability. The two facets are distinct because (1) the cross-tenant role guard is gated on the principal's HOME role (a service account with grants only has no home role and so cannot inherit the Support exception even if the grant role is Support), and (2) the cross-tenant grant guard is independent of the role at all — the grant is filtered out before the role is even consulted. Both must be pinned, because a future engine refactor that consolidated the two guards into a single check could pass facet 1 while breaking facet 2 (or vice versa) and the test would catch it.
ralph/progress.txt:20214:  - **The picker rule for engine-only pins, expanded: when the wire endpoint does not exist AND the AC are all engine properties, prefer the engine-only pin over bundling the endpoint+contract+policy into a single iteration.** This iteration's BE-0273 acceptance criteria are entirely engine properties (the principal-role matrix, the disabled-principal short-circuit, the cross-tenant guard, the three grant-containment cases). The bundling rule (BE-0244+0245+0246 / BE-0256+0257+0258 / BE-0259+0260+0261) applies when the P=1 policy story's coverage REQUIRES a wire endpoint to exist (so wire-level test assertions about resolver scope construction can be made). When the AC are pure engine properties, the engine pin is sufficient AND the bundling cost (a 5+ layer greenfield drop: store port + service + handler + route + contract + policy-wire + policy-engine) is unjustified for a single P=1 story. The trade-off: the wire-level twin of this file (httpapi/jobs_list_policy_test.go) MUST be added when BE-0271+0272 land, otherwise the resolver-scope-construction and 404-vs-403-ordering properties remain unpinned at the wire level. The engine pin alone is sufficient for BE-0273 because the AC do not reference resolver scope construction or 404-vs-403 ordering; those are properties of the endpoint, not the action.
