ralph/progress.txt:31627:  3. TestServiceAccountRepositoryInsertConflictOnOrgADoesNotTouchOrgB — duplicate-slug Insert in orgA must produce typed Conflict, leave orgA's existing row byte-identical, leave orgB's same-slug bystander byte-identical, AND preserve CountByOrganization == 1 for each tenant (the quota-reader observable).
ralph/progress.txt:31629:  5. TestServiceAccountRepositoryListByOrganizationIsolatesSharedSlug — same-slug-different-tenants returns exactly the per-tenant row, never the cross-tenant or merged row.
ralph/progress.txt:31630:  6. TestServiceAccountRepositoryCountByOrganizationIsTenantScoped — three-tenant fixture (orgA=2, orgB=3, orgC=0 empty bystander) pins the per-tenant Count read used by the quota layer.
ralph/progress.txt:31631:  7. TestServiceAccountRepositoryCrossTenantErrorIsIndistinguishableFromUnknownId — Get and Disable cross-tenant probes vs unknown-id probes must agree on Code AND Hint (not Message, which echoes the caller-supplied id by design); post-probe re-read confirms saB.disabled_at remained nil.
ralph/progress.txt:31641:- Remaining risks/follow-ups: none for BE-0432. BE-0433 (api_keys CRUD invariants) and BE-0434 (api_keys tenant-isolation) are the natural next two stories. BE-0434 reuses this exact pattern with two structural extensions called out in the new top-level pattern entry: (1) UNIQUE (organization_id, prefix) replaces UNIQUE (organization_id, slug) as the shared-key fixture surface; (2) api_keys carries a secret_hash, so every cross-tenant probe in BE-0434 must additionally assert no secret_hash needle reaches any observable surface.
ralph/progress.txt:31643:  - For a tenant-scoped table whose only mutations are Insert + a single lifecycle flip (like Disable), the byte-identical-bystander pattern still applies but the anchor selection differs from a generic-Update template: the BEFORE-UPDATE trigger fires on the lifecycle mutation, so the snapshot assertion catches a WHERE-on-slug mistake there; for the Insert-conflict path there is no UPDATE trigger to anchor on, so Count-per-tenant becomes the second observable surface that catches a row landing in the wrong tenant (a future refactor that switched Insert to a SAVEPOINT or autonomous transaction would silently inflate Count without ever surfacing on Get).
ralph/progress.txt:31644:  - The "shared identity column across tenants" angle that BE-0430 (memberships shares user_id with users) and BE-0428 (users.email globally unique) exploited does NOT translate to service_accounts — but the schema's UNIQUE (organization_id, slug) deliberately permits two tenants to share a slug, which is the structural counterpart and the only fixture that can trip a WHERE-on-slug-only mutation. The pattern is "find the schema-permitted shared-column overlap and seed the cross-row failure mode that overlap enables".
ralph/progress.txt:31645:  - apierr.NotFound("resource", id) builds Message containing the id verbatim but never sets Hint. So a cross-tenant-vs-unknown-id indistinguishability check that compared Message would always fail by design — only Code and Hint are the leak-surface fields. This already established convention in user_tenant_isolation_test.go and membership_tenant_isolation_test.go is the authoritative reference; replicate the same Code+Hint-only comparison shape in BE-0434 / BE-0436 / every future tenant-isolation story.
