ralph/progress.txt:32442:- Codebase Patterns: I'm NOT adding a new top-level pattern entry here because the structure is a direct mirror of BE-0440's pattern (already documented as the "Repository-layer tenant-isolation test file for a tenant-scoped child whose mutation surface spans Upsert ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE plus bulk DELETE-by-exclusion" pattern), with the three structural deltas (no ProjectID, ServiceID-only nullable pointer, 9-column byte-identical projection) documented here in the per-story learnings — if a future grandchild-grant story lands these become reusable; until then, story-specific notes are the right home.
ralph/progress.txt:32448:- Tests prove: (a) Insert returns the committed row with version=1, byte-equal CreatedAt/UpdatedAt (no UPDATE trigger has fired yet), nil DeletionScheduledAt, and round-trips identity (id/org/project/env/slug/name/kind) plus GetByID re-read agrees; (b) Update bumps version by exactly +1 via services_bump_version, refreshes updated_at, preserves created_at, preserves Kind (the SET clause must touch slug/display_name only — Kind is immutable Dokploy taxonomy enforced by the migration-0002 CHECK), preserves the composite (organization_id, project_id, environment_id) tenant key (Update is a PATCH, not a re-parenting), and leaves DeletionScheduledAt nil on a live row; (c) Update with matching If-Match succeeds (positive companion to the stale-If-Match conflict path through classifyServiceConcurrencyMiss); (d) ScheduleDeletion bumps version, refreshes updated_at, preserves created_at, preserves identity (slug/display_name/kind), preserves the composite tenant key, and stamps deletion_scheduled_at at or after baseline.UpdatedAt; (e) Restore is the inverse projected through the same dual-anchor — bumps version, refreshes updated_at, preserves created_at, preserves identity and the composite tenant key, and clears deletion_scheduled_at back to NULL (the load-bearing assertion since a missing `SET deletion_scheduled_at = NULL` would still trip the version/updated_at anchors); (f) Insert participates in the surrounding transaction as the unit of work — a closure error rolls the row back so GetByID surfaces NotFound AND ListByEnvironment does not see it; (g/h/i) nil-Tx guards on Insert, Update, and ScheduleDeletion render typed apierr.Internal — never a nil-pointer panic (Restore's nil-Tx guard is already covered by TestServiceRepositoryRestoreRejectsNilTx in service_restore_test.go).
ralph/progress.txt:32462:- Remaining risks/follow-ups: none for BE-0445. The next priority-1 story is BE-0446 (Repository tenant isolation: services), which will need a parallel cross-tenant byte-identical-bystander test file for the services table — a richer schema than grants (canonical kind from the Dokploy taxonomy CHECK, soft-delete via deletion_scheduled_at, the composite (organization_id, project_id, environment_id) parent FK to environments rather than a single-leg FK to a tenant-scoped parent).
ralph/progress.txt:32464:  - The services table is the FIRST table in the priority order whose mutation surface is BOTH Insert + Update + ScheduleDeletion AND Restore (environments stopped at ScheduleDeletion; projects has all four — services mirrors projects's lifecycle shape). The invariants matrix therefore replicates project_repository_invariants_test.go's structure — Insert version/timestamp / Update dual-anchor / Update If-Match / ScheduleDeletion dual-anchor / Restore dual-anchor / Insert tx rollback / nil-Tx guards — adapted to the services row's extra columns. Future tables that share this lifecycle (any tenant-scoped resource with soft-delete + restore) should reuse the same template.
ralph/progress.txt:32465:  - Services has TWO load-bearing immutability properties Update must protect that projects/environments do NOT: (1) the composite (organization_id, project_id, environment_id) tenant key — three columns, not two — must be byte-preserved, since a re-parenting through Update would silently move the service to another environment with no audit trail; (2) Kind is immutable Dokploy taxonomy (the kind CHECK in migration 0002 enforces the {"application","database","compose"} closed set, but the CHECK does not prevent moving WITHIN that set). The Update test deliberately seeds desired.Kind = testutil.ServiceKindDatabase against a baseline application row to prove the repository's SQL `SET slug = $3, display_name = $4` clause does not include kind and the persisted row keeps the baseline kind verbatim.
ralph/progress.txt:32469:  - Codebase Patterns: NOT adding a new top-level entry here. The pattern is a direct mirror of project_repository_invariants_test.go (the "Repository-layer CRUD-and-lifecycle invariants test file for a tenant-scoped resource with soft-delete + restore" pattern is already documented under BE-0407 / projects). The two services-specific deltas (three-column composite tenant key, immutable Dokploy taxonomy kind) are story-specific learnings here; if a future table lands with the same shape (services-like lifecycle + canonical-kind CHECK + composite parent FK), promote them then.
ralph/progress.txt:32472:## 2026-05-18 - BE-0446 - Repository tenant isolation: services
ralph/progress.txt:32473:- Added internal/controlplane/store/service_tenant_isolation_test.go modeled on environment_tenant_isolation_test.go (BE-0442). Proves the services table is tenant-scoped at every repository surface via byte-identical bystander assertions on (id, organization_id, project_id, environment_id, slug, display_name, kind, version, created_at, updated_at, deletion_scheduled_at). Thirteen test cases covering: GetByID correct row per tenant, GetByID cross-tenant indistinguishable from unknown id (Code + Hint identity), ListByEnvironment count isolation, ListByEnvironment shared-slug isolation, ListByEnvironment cross-tenant/unknown empty-slice symmetry, Update bystander byte-identical (own-tenant write + cross-tenant NotFound), ScheduleDeletion bystander byte-identical (both directions), Restore bystander byte-identical against BOTH a live peer (nil stamp) and a soft-deleted peer (non-nil stamp), Insert shared-slug-in-two-tenants both-succeed (UNIQUE (environment_id, slug) per-environment), and soft-deleted row tenant-scoped across GetByID + ListByEnvironment with owner still seeing own soft-deleted row.
