ralph/progress.txt:32817:- Implemented: internal/controlplane/store/quota_policy_repository_invariants_test.go — repository-level invariants for QuotaRepository's quota_policies write surface (UpsertOrganizationPolicy) and the ListEffectiveLimits resolver. The existing quota_repository_test.go already owned the per-resource EffectiveLimit semantics (PrefersOrgOverride / FallsBackToPlanDefault / NotFound / TenantScoped) and the reservation flows, so this file is additive only — it pins what the existing file does NOT: INSERT row shape (qpol_<token> id, scope_kind='organization', plan IS NULL, organization_id, timestamps), UPDATE branch identity preservation (id and created_at byte-equal, updated_at refreshed), plan-default rows untouched by org override upserts, FK violation -> Conflict on unknown organization_id, negative limit -> Conflict, unknown enforcement_mode -> Conflict, every EnforcementMode constant round-trips, ListEffectiveLimits deterministic alpha order with override-beats-default, ListEffectiveLimits tenant scoping (orgB cannot see orgA's overrides), ListEffectiveLimits plan isolation (a 'pro' default does not bleed into a 'starter' read), ListEffectiveLimits empty result when unconfigured, Store.Write closure rollback proven via a typed sentinel (errors.Is + EffectiveLimit reports unconfigured), and nil-tx guard -> yerr.CodeInternal.
ralph/progress.txt:32830:  - The three pre-existing failures are tracked separately; they are environment slug-salting drift (factory now stamps a token suffix the test still hard-codes as "production") and a cross-tenant error message leaking a parent project id. Out of scope for BE-0451 but worth a focused follow-up story.
ralph/progress.txt:32833:  - **Repository-level invariants for an EXISTING table whose CRUD surface is already partially covered (BE-0451 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/quota_policy_repository_invariants_test.go)**: quota_policies is NOT a "first story for this table" — migration 0006 already exists, UpsertOrganizationPolicy / EffectiveLimit / ListEffectiveLimits / ListOrganizationUsage already exist in store/quota.go, and internal/controlplane/store/quota_repository_test.go already owns EffectiveLimit's read semantics + reservation flows. The story's "Repository: <table> CRUD and invariants" line therefore reduces to "fill the invariants gap": pin what the existing test file does NOT cover (row-shape of an Upsert INSERT vs UPDATE, byte-equal preservation of id/created_at across UPDATE, sibling-row non-interference for plan defaults, FK/CHECK/DOMAIN -> Conflict mapping, every enum constant round-trips, ListEffectiveLimits tenant + plan isolation, Store.Write closure rollback, nil-tx -> Internal). Before writing anything, grep for `func Test<RepositoryName><MethodName>` to enumerate what is already pinned, and write tests with NEW names that only address the gap. Reuse the test names verbatim only when intentionally re-shaping coverage; never write a same-named test in another file (vet's "redeclared in this block" fires at package store_test).
ralph/progress.txt:32837:  - **ListEffectiveLimits plan isolation** is a distinct invariant from tenant isolation: even within one organization, a plan_default row keyed by plan='pro' must not appear when the caller passes plan='starter'. The single SQL statement's WHERE clause anchors this (`scope_kind='plan_default' AND plan=$2`), but a future regression that drops the plan predicate (e.g., during a "search across all plans" refactor) would slip past a pure tenant-isolation test. The BE-0451 file therefore probes both: a separate orgB test for tenant scoping AND an inlined 'pro' plan default in the deterministic-order test to prove plan scoping. The 'pro' row is asserted absent by row count + per-row resource equality; no extra fixture cost since both probes share the same setup.
ralph/progress.txt:32841:- Implemented: internal/controlplane/store/quota_policy_tenant_isolation_test.go — repository-layer tenant-isolation tests for the quota_policies table. quota_policies is a dual-scope table (plan_default OR organization) whose ONLY mutation surface is UpsertOrganizationPolicy and whose row body is only observable through the typed resolvers (EffectiveLimit / ListEffectiveLimits / ListOrganizationUsage). No per-id Get / Update / Delete surface exists, so the cross-tenant guarantee at this layer is exhaustively the projection invariant on every resolver leg AND the byte-identical-bystander invariant on the upsert. Coverage (9 tests): (1) EffectiveLimit per-resource is tenant-scoped — orgB's override does not appear when orgA queries the same resource; (2) ListEffectiveLimits rendered count is local to the queried tenant (orgA=1, orgB=3) — no count cross; (3) shared-resource isolation (both tenants override `projects` with distinct limit/mode — each sees its OWN); (4) plan isolation (orgA on 'starter' must not see 'pro' plan defaults); (5) ListOrganizationUsage is tenant scoped at BOTH the policies CTE and the usage CTE; (6) shared-counter isolation (both tenants have a quota_usage counter for the same resource with distinct used_value); (7) Upsert INSERT branch on orgA leaves orgB's bystander row byte-identical (the SAME resource on both tenants — the ON CONFLICT predicate is exercised); (8) Upsert UPDATE branch on orgA leaves orgB's bystander row byte-identical (both tenants pre-seeded with an override on the SAME resource — the maximally adversarial fixture for the partial-unique-index ON CONFLICT target); (9) same-resource upserts in two tenants both succeed (positive-direction pair for the bystander proofs — proves the partial unique index is per-tenant, not global).
ralph/progress.txt:32843:  - internal/controlplane/store/quota_policy_tenant_isolation_test.go (NEW)
ralph/progress.txt:32857:  - **Repository tenant-isolation tests for a DUAL-SCOPE table whose ONLY mutation surface is an INSERT...ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE upsert and whose row body is observable EXCLUSIVELY through typed resolvers (no per-id Get/Update/Delete, no traditional ListBy<parent>)** (BE-0452 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/quota_policy_tenant_isolation_test.go). quota_policies is structurally distinct from every prior tenant-isolation template: it has TWO scope_kind values (plan_default with NULL organization_id, and organization with non-NULL), partial unique indexes per scope (`quota_policies_org_resource_idx (organization_id, resource) WHERE scope_kind='organization'`), and NO version column. The byte-identical-bystander anchor therefore reduces to updated_at ALONE (no trigger-bumped version to anchor on), so the trigger-managed updated_at is the SOLE load-bearing column that surfaces a wrong-row UPDATE even when column writes look correct. The 9-test shape diverges from BE-0440 (project_grant) and BE-0436 (api_key_scope) in four load-bearing ways: (a) the tenant-isolation projection is observed through three resolver legs (EffectiveLimit / ListEffectiveLimits / ListOrganizationUsage), NOT through a single ListByParent — each leg has its own WHERE clause and warrants its own tenant-scoping test; (b) a NEW plan-isolation leg is added (orgA on 'starter' must not see 'pro' plan defaults, even though both rows live in the same table with NULL organization_id) — this is the tenant-isolation analogue of BE-0451's per-plan invariant; (c) the byte-identical-bystander proof is observed via a raw-SQL load of the underlying quota_policies row (loadOrganizationPolicyRow, reused from BE-0451's invariants file), because the typed reader projections deliberately omit id/created_at/updated_at/scope_kind/plan — a regression that stamped correct-looking limit_value and enforcement_mode onto orgB's row would otherwise slip past every typed-resolver projection; (d) the ListOrganizationUsage projection has TWO independent tenant predicates (one in the policies CTE on `organization_id`, one in the usage CTE on `organization_id`) — they need INDEPENDENT proofs because either dropping alone would surface a different leak (a missing policies-CTE predicate leaks peer limit_value; a missing usage-CTE predicate leaks peer used_value). Forward-applicable to any future ledger-style table whose physical realization is a dual-scope row body with two partial unique indexes (a hypothetical KindFeatureFlagDefault + KindFeatureFlagOverride pairing would inherit this template).
ralph/progress.txt:32858:  - **No per-id Get surface == no (Code, Hint) existence-leak projection.** The api_key_scope and variables tenant-isolation files include a `Get-cross-tenant-is-indistinguishable-from-unknown-id` test that anchors on (apierr.NotFound.Code, apierr.NotFound.Hint) being byte-identical between a cross-tenant probe and an unknown-id probe — this protects against an existence oracle in the Hint or Message. quota_policies has NO per-id Get, no per-id Update, no Delete — the cross-tenant probe surfaces as an EMPTY projection (resolver returns ok=false, or len(rows)==0), NOT a typed error. The (Code, Hint) projection therefore has NO analogue here; documenting the deliberate absence in the file's package-doc comment keeps a future reader from looking for a missing test. The empty-slice / ok=false projection IS the existence-indistinguishability proof: a probing caller cannot tell from `EffectiveLimit returns false` whether the resource is unconfigured for this tenant or configured for another, and cannot tell from `ListEffectiveLimits returns no row for projects` whether another tenant has a `projects` override.
ralph/progress.txt:32859:  - **Reuse the invariants file's helpers across files in the same package**. BE-0451 already defined `upsertOrganizationPolicy`, `loadOrganizationPolicyRow`, `effectiveLimitOrFail`, `listEffectiveLimitsOrFail`, and the `rawQuotaPolicyRow` struct in quota_policy_repository_invariants_test.go. All *_test.go files under internal/controlplane/store/ share the same store_test package, so BE-0452's tenant-isolation file inherits these symbols by package membership. The new file defines ONLY the symbols it introduces: `listOrganizationUsageOrFail`, `assertQuotaPolicyByteIdentical`, and per-file `stringPtrEqualQP` / `fmtStringPtrQP` nullable-pointer helpers with the `QP` suffix to avoid collisions with same-named helpers in sibling tenant-isolation files (BE-0440's project-grant file already defines a `stringPtrEqual` variant — naming conflict would block compilation).
ralph/progress.txt:32860:  - **Shared-conflict-tuple fixture for the Upsert UPDATE bystander test**. The maximally adversarial fixture for proving the ON CONFLICT branch of an `INSERT...ON CONFLICT (organization_id, resource) WHERE scope_kind='organization' DO UPDATE` is BOTH tenants pre-seeded with an override on the SAME resource — so the ON CONFLICT target tuple matches for both rows when the WHERE predicate is stripped, and the planner's row choice (which row the UPDATE lands on without the partial index's organization_id leg) becomes observable through updated_at drift on the bystander. The fixture must be CONSTRUCTED through the same `upsertOrganizationPolicy` helper the test exercises, not through `seedOrgPolicy` raw SQL — using the repository to build the fixture proves the repository can land the second (peer) row at all without colliding with the first, AND aligns the baseline.updated_at timestamps so a wrong-row UPDATE shows up as a sub-second drift. The corollary "same-resource-in-two-tenants-both-succeed" test is the positive-direction pair: without it, a regression that resolved the conflict by (resource,) alone would have prevented the bystander INSERT from landing in the first place — and every byte-identical assertion in the UPDATE-branch test would silently pass against a non-existent row.
ralph/progress.txt:32861:  - **ListOrganizationUsage has two CTE legs that each need independent tenant-scoping proof**. The policies CTE selects from quota_policies WHERE `(scope_kind='organization' AND organization_id=$1) OR (scope_kind='plan_default' AND plan=$2)`; the usage CTE selects from quota_usage WHERE `organization_id=$1`. The result is the FULL OUTER JOIN of the two. A regression that dropped the organization_id leg of EITHER CTE leaks a different column: the policies CTE leaks limit_value / enforcement_mode / Scope, the usage CTE leaks used_value. Two independent tests are warranted — one fixture isolates the policies CTE (orgA has an override, orgB has overrides on different resources, no counters on either side — surfaced through LimitValue), the other isolates the usage CTE (both tenants have a counter row on the SAME resource with DISTINCT used_value — surfaced through UsedValue). Collapsing them into one combined test would still pass against a regression that broke only one of the two CTE predicates.
ralph/progress.txt:32916:  - **Same-tenant disabled principal as the authorization-failure-leg fixture for redaction tests on a WRITE endpoint** (BE-0176 / BE-0146 pattern). The deny-leg actor MUST be a same-tenant principal (the read-sibling's RoleViewer doesn't work as cleanly because viewer is denied by role not by disabled flag; a disabled developer principal hits the policy denial in a single short-circuit that's not role-matrix specific). `denied.Principal.Disabled = true` is the cleanest way to force a 403 without depending on the policy matrix tests' role choices. The deny-leg replacer is `fakeEnvironmentVariableReplacer{err: stderrors.New("replacer must not be called")}` — proves the deny path short-circuits at policy AND proves any logged "replacer error" cannot be the leak source (so if either sentinel string appears in the log on the deny leg, the regression is in the redaction surface, NOT in the deny-path short-circuit).
ralph/progress.txt:32921:- Implemented: internal/controlplane/store/quota_usage_repository_invariants_test.go — repository-layer CRUD-and-lifecycle invariants for the quota_usage table. quota_usage is the per-tenant counter table; its ONLY public write surface in QuotaRepository is LockUsage, which INSERTs the counter row ON CONFLICT (organization_id, resource) DO NOTHING and then SELECTs FOR UPDATE. There is no per-row Update/Delete method, no version column, and no service_id pointer — the increment path is owned by quota.Checker and the delete-cascade is owned by schema CASCADE. The 9-test file pins what neither quota_repository_test.go (the original LockUsage happy-path test) nor the schema/tenant-isolation siblings already cover: (1) row-shape on the INSERT leg — qusg_ prefix, used_value defaults to 0, created_at/updated_at within 1s; (2) second-call byte-identity — id, created_at, updated_at, AND used_value all anchored across ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING (the set_updated_at trigger fires BEFORE UPDATE only; a non-UPDATE must not stamp updated_at — proved with a measurable wallclock gap between calls); (3) peer-row byte-identity — LockUsage(org, services) does not touch the (org, projects) counter row's id/created_at/updated_at/used_value, and the two rows have distinct ids; (4) ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING discards the minted qusg_ id when the row exists — proved by pre-seeding a row at used_value=17 with id `qu_seed_projects` and observing both the live used_value and the original id survive a LockUsage call; (5) FK violation (unknown organization) -> typed Conflict via mapWriteError; (6) DOMAIN violation by casting `"not-a-resource"` to store.QuotaResource -> typed Conflict; (7) transaction rollback — closure returns sentinel after a successful LockUsage, Write rolls back, no quota_usage row persists (counter probe = 0); (8) nil-tx -> yerr.CodeInternal. The 9th test is the pre-existing TestQuotaRepositoryLockUsageCreatesAndReturnsCounter that the file does NOT duplicate — it lives in quota_repository_test.go and remains the load-bearing two-call-idempotency probe.
ralph/progress.txt:32939:  - **Repository-layer CRUD-and-lifecycle invariants test file for a TENANT-SCOPED COUNTER table whose ONLY mutation surface is an INSERT...ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING + SELECT...FOR UPDATE primitive (LockUsage), with NO per-row Update/Delete, NO version column, NO service_id pointer, and a database-managed used_value mutated by an external code path (quota.Checker), set_updated_at trigger fires BEFORE UPDATE only** (BE-0453 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/quota_usage_repository_invariants_test.go). Structurally distinct from BE-0451 (quota_policies — single-table INSERT...ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE upsert) in four load-bearing ways: (a) the second-call byte-identity proof must anchor updated_at TOO, because ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING is not an UPDATE and the set_updated_at trigger must not fire — quota_policies's UPDATE leg actively REFRESHES updated_at via the trigger, so its byte-identity proof EXCLUDES updated_at; quota_usage's byte-identity proof INCLUDES updated_at as a load-bearing anchor (a regression that switched to ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE with even a no-op SET would silently bump updated_at and a missing anchor would let that slip past); (b) the peer-row proof for quota_usage is a SAME-TENANT-DIFFERENT-RESOURCE check (the UNIQUE (organization_id, resource) constraint isolates rows by resource within a tenant), distinct from quota_policies's cross-scope peer (plan_default vs organization within the SAME quota_policies table); (c) the "row already exists with non-zero value" leg has no quota_policies analog — for quota_usage we pre-seed a row at used_value=17 via direct SQL (seedQuotaUsage from usage_test.go) and prove BOTH the seeded id `qu_seed_projects` AND the seeded used_value survive the LockUsage call, which is the only way to prove the discarded INSERT's minted qusg_ id does not replace the row's id (a regression that switched to ON CONFLICT (...) DO UPDATE SET id=EXCLUDED.id would replace the row's id and reset used_value to 0 — a catastrophic data-loss path the typed reader projections would not catch); (d) the DOMAIN-violation test casts an arbitrary string to store.QuotaResource — `store.QuotaResource("not-a-resource")` — to bypass Go's compile-time check on the typed string and exercise the DOMAIN quota_resource CHECK at the database layer; this is the same casting pattern quota_policies uses for unknown enforcement_mode (`store.EnforcementMode("not-a-mode")`).
ralph/progress.txt:32942:  - Codebase Patterns: ADDED a new top-level pattern entry (this story's quota_usage shape is structurally distinct from BE-0451's quota_policies upsert shape; future ledger-style counter tables with the same INSERT-DO-NOTHING + SELECT-FOR-UPDATE primitive — e.g. quota_reservations is a different shape with status lifecycle, but a future per-tenant-per-resource counter would mirror this exactly — should follow BE-0453, not BE-0451).
ralph/progress.txt:32969:- Implemented: internal/controlplane/store/quota_usage_tenant_isolation_test.go — repository-layer tenant-isolation tests for the quota_usage table. quota_usage's only public mutation surface is LockUsage (INSERT ... ON CONFLICT (organization_id, resource) DO NOTHING + SELECT ... FOR UPDATE) and there is no per-row Update/Delete/Get; the typed read resolver ListOrganizationUsage's cross-tenant projection is already covered by quota_policy_tenant_isolation_test.go (BE-0452), so this file is additive and focused on LockUsage cross-tenant invariants + the cascade-delete tenant-isolation that TestQuotaCascadeDeleteOnOrganization (quota_schema_test.go) only proves on the deleted side. Coverage (6 tests):
ralph/progress.txt:32970:  (1) TestQuotaRepositoryLockUsageInsertOnOrgADoesNotTouchOrgB — orgB owns a pre-seeded counter for the same resource at used_value=42; orgA's LockUsage on that resource lands as a fresh qusg_<token> INSERT and orgB's row is byte-identical (id, used_value, created_at, updated_at, organization_id, resource); orgA returns 0 (orgB's 42 must not leak); per-tenant row counts are 1+1.
ralph/progress.txt:32971:  (2) TestQuotaRepositoryLockUsageReturnValueIsTenantScoped — both tenants have a pre-seeded counter on projects with distinct used_value (7 vs 42); each tenant's LockUsage observes its OWN value through the SELECT ... FOR UPDATE leg. A regression that dropped the organization_id predicate from the SELECT would surface as a swap or merge.
ralph/progress.txt:32972:  (3) TestQuotaRepositoryLockUsageOnOrgADoesNotChangeOrgBRowCount — orgB has 3 counters on distinct resources; orgA LockUsages a resource orgB has NOT seeded; orgB's count stays at 3 and orgA goes 0->1. Catches a cross-tenant FK swap that would either bump or shrink orgB's count.
ralph/progress.txt:32974:  (5) TestQuotaRepositoryLockUsageSameResourceInTwoTenantsBothSucceed — load-bearing positive-direction pair: both LockUsage(orgA, projects) and LockUsage(orgB, projects) succeed via the repository surface (NOT a raw INSERT), two rows persist with distinct ids and distinct organization_id, each tenant has exactly one row on projects. Without this anchor, a regression that resolved the ON CONFLICT by (resource,) alone would have prevented orgB's seed in every other test and the byte-identical assertions would silently pass against rows that never existed.
ralph/progress.txt:32975:  (6) TestQuotaUsageOrganizationDeleteCascadeIsTenantScoped — orgA has 2 quota_usage rows, orgB has 3 (including one on the SAME resource as orgA); deleting orgA cascades only orgA's rows; orgB's three rows survive byte-identically (id, used_value, created_at, updated_at). Complements TestQuotaCascadeDeleteOnOrganization which proves the cascade fires for the targeted tenant; this proves it does NOT bleed into another tenant.
ralph/progress.txt:32979:  - internal/controlplane/store/quota_usage_tenant_isolation_test.go (NEW, ~310 lines)
ralph/progress.txt:32994:  - **Repository tenant-isolation tests for a TENANT-SCOPED COUNTER table whose ONLY public mutation is INSERT ... ON CONFLICT (organization_id, resource) DO NOTHING + SELECT ... FOR UPDATE (LockUsage), where the row body's used_value is increment-managed BY AN EXTERNAL CODE PATH (quota.Checker, not the repository), with NO per-id Get/Update/Delete surface, NO version column, NO service_id pointer, and the cascade-delete tenant isolation NOT covered by the existing single-tenant cascade test** (BE-0454 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/quota_usage_tenant_isolation_test.go). Structurally distinct from BE-0452 (quota_policies, dual-scope UPSERT) in four load-bearing ways: (a) NO typed-resolver coverage is added here — ListOrganizationUsage's two-CTE-leg tenant scoping is already proved in quota_policy_tenant_isolation_test.go (BE-0452) AND in usage_test.go (TestQuotaRepositoryListOrganizationUsageTenantScoped), and re-asserting would cause same-named tests to collide at compile time across the shared store_test package; the cross-tenant id-leak surface to defend is LockUsage's INSERT/SELECT primitive, which the typed projection cannot expose (the projection omits id by construction) — so the bystander proofs use raw-SQL row loads instead; (b) the bystander invariant has TWO peer classes — same-resource and cross-resource — both anchored on the same baseline tenant in ONE test (test 4), because both classes are governed by the SAME ON CONFLICT predicate (organization_id, resource) and a regression that dropped either column would surface in both; this is structurally simpler than BE-0452's split insert/update bystander tests (quota_policies has TWO upsert branches — DO NOTHING never happens, only DO UPDATE — so insert-on-orgA-doesn't-touch-orgB and update-on-orgA-doesn't-touch-orgB are distinct probes; quota_usage's ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING means orgA's INSERT against orgB's existing row is the ONLY upsert branch worth proving and the test surface collapses to one); (c) a NEW probe is added on top of BE-0452's template: a "per-tenant row-count invariance" test (test 3) that does NOT load any row — only counts rows owned by orgA and orgB before and after — to catch a cross-tenant FK swap that the byte-identity probes would miss (the byte-identity probes only observe rows that still exist; a regression that DELETED orgB's row and INSERTED an orgA-owned replacement at the same id would pass byte-identity if id and used_value coincidentally matched, but would fail this count probe); (d) the cascade-delete tenant-isolation test (test 6) is a NEW class of probe quota_policy_tenant_isolation_test.go did NOT carry because TestQuotaCascadeDeleteOnOrganization (quota_schema_test.go) proves single-tenant cascade only — it never checks that a different tenant's rows survive. quota_usage is the right place to add this probe because the cascade fires at the schema level (FK CASCADE on quota_usage.organization_id) and is per-table; quota_policies's cascade is asymmetric (plan_default rows have NULL organization_id and survive deletes by construction, so a "different tenant's rows survive" probe there would conflate two distinct invariants — the plan-default-survives invariant from quota_schema_test.go AND the organization-bystander-survives invariant — and the asymmetry would obscure the test's purpose). For quota_usage there is no plan_default scope, so the cascade-isolation probe is unambiguous: orgB's rows survive byte-identically (id, used_value, timestamps) when orgA is deleted.
ralph/progress.txt:32996:  - **The "positive-direction pair" anchor (test 5)** is the load-bearing complement of every bystander byte-identity proof in tenant-isolation tests, but for quota_usage it has a different mechanical purpose than for quota_policies: in BE-0452 the positive-direction pair (`SameResourceInTwoTenantsBothSucceed`) anchors the bystander proofs because the partial unique index `quota_policies_org_resource_idx (organization_id, resource) WHERE scope_kind='organization'` is partial AND keyed by both columns — so a regression to a global UNIQUE would have made BOTH the insert-bystander and the update-bystander silently pass against missing rows; in BE-0454 the equivalent positive-direction pair anchors the bystander proofs because `quota_usage_org_resource_idx (organization_id, resource)` is full (not partial — quota_usage has no scope_kind column) but is per-tenant, so the same anchor mechanic applies even though the partial-index angle drops out. Going through the repository surface on BOTH tenants — not via raw INSERTs — is the load-bearing detail: a regression that hard-coded a specific organization_id inside the LockUsage SQL itself would surface here too.
ralph/progress.txt:33013:  - BE-0456 (tenant-isolation for quota_reservations) is the paired follow-up and will exploit the helpers (`loadQuotaReservationRowByID`, `runInsertReservationOrFail`, `countQuotaReservationRowsForOrg`) introduced here.
ralph/progress.txt:33053:- Implemented repository-layer tenant-isolation tests for the quota_reservations table.
ralph/progress.txt:33054:- New file: internal/controlplane/store/quota_reservation_tenant_isolation_test.go (7 tests).
ralph/progress.txt:33060:  - Tenant-isolation for a table with no UNIQUE constraint on (organization_id, resource) — only PRIMARY KEY (id) and composite-FK target UNIQUE (organization_id, id) — needs a DIFFERENT positive-direction pair than quota_usage's LockUsage-both-succeed: it becomes InsertReservation-and-both-persist (no conflict to defeat). The same-resource-two-tenants-both-persist test is STILL load-bearing because it proves orgA's INSERT actually wrote a row — otherwise the bystander byte-identity tests above could silently pass against unmutated rows.
ralph/progress.txt:33061:  - Global PRIMARY KEY columns on tenant-scoped tables need a NEW cross-tenant probe: an explicit-id collision from orgA against orgB's existing row MUST surface as typed Conflict AND MUST leave orgB's row byte-identical AND MUST NOT insert a row for orgA. This guards against a regression that swapped the bare INSERT for ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE.
ralph/progress.txt:33085:  - DeploymentRepository.Cancel disambiguates zero-row UPDATE outcomes (NotFound vs ConflictStale vs already-terminal Conflict) through a tenant-scoped GetByID against the SAME *Tx. Tests must verify all three branches: nil expectedVersion + terminal row -> Conflict (no current_version attached), non-nil expectedVersion + version mismatch -> ConflictStale (current_version attached via apierr.CurrentVersionOf), unknown id -> NotFound.
ralph/progress.txt:33086:  - Cross-tenant Cancel byte-identity proof: when bravo asks to cancel alpha's deployment_id, the response must be NotFound AND alpha's row must read back byte-identical (status unchanged, finished_at still NULL, version not bumped). Without the byte-identity leg, a regression that scoped the UPDATE by id alone and the disambiguation by organization_id would silently mutate the foreign tenant's row before failing the lookup.
ralph/progress.txt:33091:## 2026-05-18 21:55 - BE-0458 - Repository tenant isolation: deployments
ralph/progress.txt:33092:- Implemented internal/controlplane/store/deployment_tenant_isolation_test.go: nine cross-tenant invariant tests covering every DeploymentRepository surface (Insert / GetByID / FindByIdempotencyKey / Cancel / ListByService) PLUS the organizations DELETE cascade — proving deployments rows cannot cross tenant boundaries even when the underlying columns (idempotency_key, source_ref, status, service_id-shape) deliberately collide between two tenants.
ralph/progress.txt:33094:  - internal/controlplane/store/deployment_tenant_isolation_test.go (new, 9 tests)
ralph/progress.txt:33105:  - The global PRIMARY KEY on deployments(id) means two tenants CANNOT share a deployment id, even though every OTHER per-row anchor is per-tenant (UNIQUE (organization_id, idempotency_key), the closed-set source/status taxonomies, source_ref shape). A naive cross-tenant test that uses the SAME suffix for both orgA's and orgB's `seedQueuedDeployment` calls trips the global PK as a Conflict surfaced through mapWriteError's generic "a deployment with this idempotency key already exists" message — which LOOKS like an idempotency-key conflict but is actually an id collision because mintDeploymentID hashes `t.Name() + suffix`. Use distinct suffixes per tenant for ID-minting helpers; preserve the "looks like a peer" property by colliding only the columns that ARE per-tenant unique (idempotency_key, source_ref) via the Insert-test branch (TestDeploymentRepositoryInsertSameIdempotencyKeyInTwoTenantsBothPersist).
ralph/progress.txt:33106:  - The not-found payload for GetByID(orgB, alphaDep.ID) must name ONLY the deployment_id the caller supplied — never the foreign source_ref, idempotency_key, or service_id. The substring no-leak assertions in TestDeploymentRepositoryGetByIDCrossTenantReturnsNotFoundAndDoesNotEcho guard against a regression that attached cross-tenant details to apierr.NotFound.
ralph/progress.txt:33107:  - The acceptance criterion "List queries return stable pagination without leaking total counts from other tenants" is pinned by asserting (a) the cross-tenant list response is an empty slice AND (b) the per-tenant COUNT(*) for the foreign tenant is unchanged after the read. Reading must not leak into a write.
ralph/progress.txt:33120:  - internal/controlplane/store/deployment_event_repository_invariants_test.go — 20 invariants tests covering row-shape, id contracts, CHECK/FK violations, append-only enforcement at the SQL trigger level, cascade-delete on parent deployment, GetByID tenant-scoped reads (unknown + cross-tenant NotFound with no foreign-id leak), ListByDeployment chronological ordering + cross-tenant empty slice.
ralph/progress.txt:33133:  - BE-0460 (Repository tenant-isolation for deployment_events) is the paired sibling story and is the next pick in the priority=1 backlog. The composite FK to deployments(organization_id, id) means the tenant-isolation positive-direction shape is "two orgs each with their own deployment can each have events with the same depev_ prefix and the same event_type, and neither leaks into the other's ListByDeployment / GetByID" — distinct from BE-0456 (quota_reservations isolation, which is org-scoped not deployment-scoped) and from BE-0458 (deployments isolation, where the global PRIMARY KEY on id makes deployment ids strictly per-tenant-distinct). For deployment_events the depev_ ids are minted by crypto/rand so they are practically distinct per-tenant; the test must still encode the per-tenant ListByDeployment positive shape and the cross-tenant zero-leak negative shape.
ralph/progress.txt:33136:  - **Append-only repositories in this codebase carry NO updated_at column, NO version column, AND a BEFORE UPDATE trigger.** All three are load-bearing: omitting updated_at + version means peer-row byte-identity tests are simpler (only id + content columns can drift, and the trigger forbids drift); adding the trigger means the immutability holds even when an operator bypasses the repository surface with raw SQL through a console. The pattern is shared with audit_events; deployment_events extends it with a composite FK to a deeper parent (deployments) so the cascade chain runs through two ON DELETE CASCADE legs (deployments -> events, then organizations -> deployments -> events on tenant teardown).
