ralph/progress.txt:34185:## 2026-05-18 - BE-0470 Repository tenant isolation: idempotency_keys
ralph/progress.txt:34186:- Added internal/controlplane/store/idempotency_repository_tenant_isolation_test.go (eight Postgres-backed integration tests) proving cross-tenant isolation of every IdempotencyRepository surface (Claim, Complete, Release, Find) AND ON DELETE CASCADE tenant scoping for idempotency_keys.
ralph/progress.txt:34200:  - The idempotency_keys CHECK constraint `idempotency_keys_completion_consistent` is the load-bearing reason the byte-identity comparator MUST switch on nil-ness FIRST and dereference value SECOND. A naive field-equality loop would silently round-trip a regression that flipped a pending row's NULL completion columns to zero-value scalars (response_status=0, response_body=[]byte{}, completed_at=epoch) — the schema would reject this on INSERT but the test would not catch it on a peer-row drift. Forward-applicable to any future tenant-isolation file whose row carries nullable completion columns: job_attempts (nullable error_summary / finished_at / result), quota_reservations (nullable released_at / job_id), break_glass_sessions (nullable revoked_at).
ralph/progress.txt:34201:  - The Claim ON CONFLICT target IS the per-tenant uniqueness contract. The "mirror tuple" probe — orgB seeds (orgB, usr_mirror, mirror-key), orgA Claims (orgA, usr_mirror, mirror-key) — is the load-bearing assertion that organization_id is part of the conflict target, NOT inferred. A regression that dropped organization_id from ON CONFLICT would resolve the conflict onto orgB's mirror row through the DO UPDATE branch and silently rewrite it; the test would otherwise pass on a broken implementation if the probe only used distinct (principal, key) per tenant. The seed comment for the mirror row MUST explicitly call out the rationale ("the load-bearing bystander whose presence on orgB exercises the per-tenant uniqueness of the ON CONFLICT target") because the failure mode is the kind a future reader will rationalise away ("of course they're isolated").
ralph/progress.txt:34202:  - The Claim cross-tenant id-collision probe MUST use DIFFERENT (principal_id, idempotency_key) tuples across the two tenants. If both records share the same (principal, key), the orgB attempt would be intercepted by the ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE branch under the steal-expired WHERE (which fires only if expires_at <= now()) — the row is locked, Find inside the same tx returns the orgB-existing row's data, and the test would pass on a broken implementation that silently swapped tenants. The PK violation only flows through mapWriteError when the INSERT branch is taken (no row matches ON CONFLICT target), so the test ID-collision fixture MUST live at (orgB, distinct-principal, distinct-key) with the SAME id as orgA's row.
ralph/progress.txt:34203:  - Global PK + nullable completion columns require BOTH a per-tenant count probe AND a global count probe in the id-collision test. A per-tenant probe alone (countIdempotencyRowsForOrg) cannot prove the failing row's absence because the failing INSERT's organization_id is the same as the colliding tenant — a regression that landed the row in a third unrelated tenant's slot would pass the per-tenant probe. totalIdempotencyRows (SELECT count(*) WITH NO WHERE) closes that gap. The same pattern applies to any future table with a global PK and tenant-scoped read predicates.
ralph/progress.txt:34204:  - The Complete cross-tenant test should embed a recognisable canary string ("forged":"orgA-tries-to-complete-orgB-claim") in the response_body argument so a future reader sees immediately what the test was protecting against. A bland `[]byte("body")` argument would obscure the worst-case shape ("orgA silently completes orgB's claim with arbitrary bytes"). The canary also doubles as a redaction-leak probe — if a future error-formatter regression embeds the rendered envelope in the typed Conflict's Error() / Message / Hint, the canary surfaces in a grep across the test output.
ralph/progress.txt:34206:  - The `go test ./...` run from the repo root surfaces pre-existing flaky concurrent-quota tests AND the pre-existing TestProjectVariableServiceReplaceCrossTenantProjectIsNotFound failure. The right verification cadence for a tenant-isolation story is `go test -race -count=1 -run '^Test<Subsystem>' ./internal/controlplane/store/` for the focused suite, plus a confirming `go test -count=1 -p 1` of the failing tests in isolation to prove they pass when sequential — which separates real regressions from flakes. Do NOT attempt to "fix in passing" the apierr.NotFound message format leak — the message format is load-bearing across every NotFound test in the suite.
ralph/progress.txt:34215:- The repository surface is intentionally narrow: Append (worker writes a finding inside a tx), GetByID (operator reads one finding tenant-scoped), ListByOrganization (operator triage queue, newest first, capped at 200), and MarkResolved (the one mutation — a finding's only legal state-transition is to be resolved). There is no per-row Delete; row removal is reachable only through ON DELETE CASCADE.
ralph/progress.txt:34217:- Optional resource legs (project_id / environment_id / service_id / service_domain_id) use MATCH SIMPLE composite FKs referencing the parents' UNIQUE (organization_id, id). An organization-level finding with all legs NULL is legal; a scoped finding is pinned to its tenant. The repository serialises blank Go strings as SQL NULL via nullIfEmpty(s string) any so a cross-tenant or empty-string leg never engages the composite FK with an unmatched row.
ralph/progress.txt:34218:- Lifecycle precondition for MarkResolved: SQL predicate filters on resolved_at IS NULL so the UPDATE updates zero rows when the finding is already resolved. The method then distinguishes "missing" (typed apierr.NotFound, caller-supplied id) from "already resolved" (typed apierr.Conflict) via a tenant-scoped follow-up Get inside the same transaction. drift_findings_resolved_consistent CHECK ties resolved_at to a non-empty resolved_by_actor_id so a future writer cannot leave the row half-resolved.
ralph/progress.txt:34219:- Verification run: gofmt -w on the new files; TMPDIR=/tmp go build ./... (no output); TMPDIR=/tmp go vet ./... (no output); TMPDIR=/tmp go mod tidy (no module changes); TMPDIR=/tmp YALLA_TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgres://yalla:yalla@127.0.0.1:5432/yalla go test -race -count=1 -run TestDriftFinding ./internal/controlplane/store/... PASS (20 sub/cases including byte-identical peer-row probes, cascade delete, limit clamping, transaction rollback, cross-tenant NotFound, already-resolved Conflict, validation matrix, nil-Tx Internal). TMPDIR=/tmp go test -count=1 -run "TestMigrations|TestSchemaMigration|TestSQLInjection" ./internal/controlplane/store/... PASS.
ralph/progress.txt:34221:  - BE-0472 (Repository tenant isolation: drift_findings) is the immediate sibling. The reusable helpers landed here (rawDriftFindingRow, loadDriftFindingRowByID, countDriftFindingRowsForOrg, driftFindingFixture, seedDriftFinding, assertDriftFindingByteIdentical, assertStringPtrEqual, assertTimePtrEqual) are forward-applicable verbatim. Note that BE-0471's TestDriftFindingMarkResolvedCrossTenantIsNotFound + TestDriftFindingAppendCrossTenantParentIsConflict + TestDriftFindingGetByIDCrossTenantIsNotFound already exercise three of the most load-bearing tenant-isolation surfaces; BE-0472 should focus on List-by-organization indistinguishability and a more exhaustive bystander-byte-identity matrix across List + MarkResolved with a tenant-shared text shape (e.g. same dokploy_resource_id across two tenants on different unmanaged findings).
ralph/progress.txt:34232:- Repository CRUD-and-invariants for a TABLE WHOSE MIGRATION + REPOSITORY DO NOT EXIST YET, WITH A NARROW APPEND + READ + ONE-MUTATION (RESOLVE) LIFECYCLE AND OPTIONAL POLYMORPHIC NULLABLE RESOURCE LEGS PINNED BY MATCH-SIMPLE COMPOSITE FKs (BE-0471 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/drift_finding.go + drift_finding_repository_invariants_test.go + migrate/migrations/0037_drift_findings.{up,down}.sql). Subspecies of the BE-0463 (job_attempts) new-table template with five load-bearing deltas: (a) the lifecycle has TWO writes (Append + MarkResolved) rather than one (Append) and NO append-only trigger — the resolved_at + resolved_by_actor_id transition is a legal UPDATE, paired by a drift_findings_resolved_consistent CHECK that ties the two columns nil-state-together. The migration uses set_updated_at trigger (not bump_version + version column) because there is no measurable optimistic-concurrency contention on operator-driven resolution; the file header documents the version-column-omission as load-bearing so a future bulk-resolve story knows it must add a new migration + version column rather than retrofit one. (b) The optional resource legs are NULLABLE composite FKs on (organization_id, project_id|environment_id|service_id|service_domain_id) referencing the parents' UNIQUE (organization_id, id) — MATCH SIMPLE so the FK fires only when the column is set. The repository serialises a blank Go string to SQL NULL via a private nullIfEmpty(s string) any helper (so a `f.ProjectID = ""` Append lands as project_id IS NULL on disk, and the composite FK does not engage with a non-existent empty-string id). scanDriftFinding reads *string into scratch and projects nil onto "" so the public DriftFinding struct collapses NULL onto the zero value (matching JobAttempt / DeploymentEvent — the customer never observes the distinction). Forward-applicable to any future polymorphic-resource-leg table (a future cost_attributions_findings or capacity_breaches table). (c) MarkResolved's UPDATE filters on `resolved_at IS NULL` so an already-resolved row updates zero rows; the method then distinguishes "missing" (typed apierr.NotFound, caller-supplied id only) from "already resolved" (typed apierr.Conflict) via a tenant-scoped follow-up Get inside the SAME *Tx. The Get-on-zero-rows shape is the right pattern for ANY future state-transition repository method whose pre-state is a column predicate (vs a row-existence predicate). A regression that removed the `IS NULL` predicate would silently allow a second resolver to overwrite the first's stamp; TestDriftFindingMarkResolvedAlreadyResolvedIsConflict pins this by asserting BOTH the typed Conflict AND the raw-SQL re-read confirms the original resolved_by_actor_id did not move. (d) Closed taxonomies (DriftKind / DriftReason / DriftLevel) mirror reconcile.DriftKind / DriftReason / ResourceLevel verbatim; the repository's Valid()/String() shape is identical to JobAttemptStatus / DeploymentSource. The CHECK constraints are unreachable from the typed Append API (Valid() pre-rejects), but they remain authoritative for any future raw-SQL writer; the test file header documents this delegation. The application-layer validation matrix (TestDriftFindingAppendValidationRejectsBadInput) exercises every taxonomy column's empty-and-unknown legs as typed apierr.InvalidInput, plus zero-DetectedAt, plus the not-yet-resolved guards (ResolvedAt-set-on-new-finding + ResolvedByActor-set-on-new-finding) which are unique to a table with a state-transition. (e) Test name sanitisation: sanitizeTestNameForDriftID strips '/' from t.Name() so an id minted inside a subtest stays a single underscore-delimited token. Forward-applicable to any future mint helper that derives a caller-supplied id from t.Name() in a table-driven test with subtests. The 19-test shape this file lands: Append-row-shape (drft_ prefix + server-stamped created_at within [before, after] + DetectedAt round-trip + ResolvedAt/ActorID stay zero), Append-organization-level-legs-are-NULL (the polymorphic-FK shape probe), Append-caller-supplied-id-preserved, Append-duplicate-id-Conflict (PK), Append-validation-matrix (10 sub-cases covering missing + unknown taxonomy + zero timestamps + premature resolution), Append-nil-Tx-Internal, Append-unknown-organization-Conflict (FK), Append-cross-tenant-parent-Conflict (3 sub-cases: foreign project / environment / service via composite-FK), Append-rollback-removes-row, Append-peer-row-byte-identical, GetByID-round-trip, GetByID-unknown-NotFound, GetByID-cross-tenant-NotFound, List-orders-newest-first (detected_at DESC + id DESC tiebreaker), List-clamps-limit (4 sub-cases), List-unknown-org-empty-slice-not-nil, MarkResolved-happy-path (resolved_at + actor + UpdatedAt-after-CreatedAt + raw-SQL byte-equal), MarkResolved-already-resolved-Conflict + no-overwrite (the load-bearing state-transition pin), MarkResolved-unknown-NotFound, MarkResolved-cross-tenant-NotFound + bystander-byte-identity, MarkResolved-validation-matrix (4 sub-cases), MarkResolved-nil-Tx-Internal, MarkResolved-peer-row-byte-identical, Cascade-delete-from-organization. New helpers introduced (per-file DriftFinding-suffix to avoid store_test collisions): rawDriftFindingRow (with *string + *time.Time fields for the nullable legs + resolved_at), loadDriftFindingRowByID, countDriftFindingRowsForOrg, mintDriftFindingID + sanitizeTestNameForDriftID, driftFindingTxRollbackSentinel, driftFindingFixture, seedDriftFinding, assertDriftFindingByteIdentical, assertStringPtrEqual, assertTimePtrEqual. Reused verbatim from sibling files: seedOrg, seedProject, seedEnvironment, seedService (schema_test.go); newStore (store_test.go); wantErrCode (idempotency_test.go). NEW migration added: 0037_drift_findings.up.sql + .down.sql, both with the standard DROP TRIGGER / DROP INDEX / DROP TABLE shape; the up.sql includes a service_domains composite FK so service_domains must remain a composite-FK target — a future migration that dropped service_domains' UNIQUE (organization_id, id) would break this FK and surface as a TestMigrations failure. The byte-identical-bystander tenant-isolation gap-fill is the paired BE-0472 story.
